BEGIN;
UPDATE glossary SET category='Footstep performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making bare feet too loud or heavy for a soft action','Choosing a surface that does not match the on-screen floor','Missing the wet, slapping skin texture of bare feet','Faking it with light shoes and EQ instead of true bare-foot performance']::text[], definition='Foley footstep performance in which the artist works without shoes, using bare feet on a chosen surface to recreate the soft, quiet footfalls of a barefoot character in sync with picture.', plain_english='This is Foley done with bare feet. The artist steps without shoes on a surface. It matches an actor who is barefoot on screen. Bare feet make soft, slappy sounds. The steps are timed to the picture.', practical_application='The Foley artist removes shoes and steps on a suitable pit surface such as wood, tile, or sand while watching picture, adjusting pressure to match the actor''s gait.', purpose_function='Provides realistic soft footfalls for barefoot characters, giving weight and presence to quiet movement that production sound often fails to capture.', related_terms=ARRAY['Footstep Foley','Foley Walker','Foley Performer','Foley Shoe','Cloth Foley']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A barefoot beach or poolside scene','A character walking barefoot through a house at night','A child running barefoot across a floor']::text[] WHERE id='a791339e-52fc-47c0-954c-508fa362553d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Impact performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: dropping onto a hard floor without padding, risking bruising or joint injury to the performer','Recording the impact too clean with no cloth layer','Using the wrong weight for the character''s size','Ignoring the surface, such as wood versus concrete']::text[], definition='The Foley performance and recording of the sound of a human body falling or impacting a surface, created by dropping weighted props, bags, or padded weights in sync with an on-screen fall.', plain_english='This is Foley for a body hitting the ground. The artist makes the thud of a fall. They may drop weighted bags or props. The impact is layered with cloth sounds. It is matched to the picture.', practical_application='Using sandbags, phone books, or padded weights, the artist drops the object onto a matching surface and layers in clothing rustle to build a full-body fall.', purpose_function='Gives falls, tackles, and collapses a convincing sense of weight and impact, which a real filmed fall rarely records cleanly.', related_terms=ARRAY['Footstep Foley','Cloth Foley','Foley Prop','Hero Foley','Foley Take']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A fight-scene knockdown','A character fainting to the floor','A stunt fall in an action sequence']::text[] WHERE id='c2ecf9f9-7f7f-4038-a3b1-75bc02756a3b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Effort performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing breath Foley with recorded ADR dialogue','Assuming all breaths are Foley when many belong to vocal or ADR work','Over-performing breaths so they distract from the scene','Poor sync to the actor''s exertion']::text[], definition='The performance and recording of a character''s breathing and effort sounds such as inhales, gasps, and exertions, timed to picture; on many productions this overlaps with vocal or effort work rather than sitting strictly within footsteps, moves, and specifics.', plain_english='This is recorded breathing that matches the picture. It covers gasps, sighs, and hard breaths. It adds effort to running, lifting, or fighting. It makes a character feel alive. Some studios treat it as voice work.', practical_application='A performer watches picture and voices matching breaths and grunts close to a microphone, timing each one to the actor''s exertion.', purpose_function='Adds human effort and physical presence, filling gaps where production breaths are missing or masked by noise.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Acting','Foley Performer','Foley Session','Hero Foley','Footstep Foley']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A character sprinting in a chase','Heavy lifting or strained effort','A tense moment where breathing sells the exertion']::text[] WHERE id='5156fcbe-e4c0-408e-b56d-027022f38b2a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Movement performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording cloth louder than it would be in real life','Using the wrong fabric type for the costume','Skipping cloth passes entirely','Letting cloth movement mask the footsteps']::text[], definition='The Foley performance of clothing and fabric movement sounds such as rustles, swishes, and friction, produced by manipulating garments and cloth in sync with an actor''s body movement.', plain_english='This is the sound of clothes moving. The artist rubs and shifts fabric by hand. It matches how an actor moves on screen. It adds soft rustles to walking and gestures. These sounds make bodies feel present.', practical_application='The artist holds and moves matching garments such as a jacket, denim, or leather near the mic, brushing and folding cloth to follow the actor''s motion.', purpose_function='Supplies the constant, subtle fabric movement that grounds characters physically and smooths the gaps between footsteps and prop sounds.', related_terms=ARRAY['Footstep Foley','Foley Walker','Foley Performer','Hand Foley','Foley Dirt']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A character in a leather jacket turning','Someone dressing or removing a coat','A robed figure walking down a hall']::text[] WHERE id='114d9a0f-6a92-4b77-95f4-f6ef7206b941';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Stylized performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Exaggerating in a project meant to feel realistic','Applying exaggeration inconsistently across scenes','Pushing so far the sound reads as fake','Ignoring how the mix will treat the cue']::text[], definition='Foley performed larger, sharper, or more pronounced than natural physics would produce, used deliberately for stylistic, comedic, or dramatic emphasis rather than strict realism.', plain_english='This is Foley made bigger than real life. Sounds are louder, sharper, or punchier. It is used for comedy or drama. It makes action feel exciting or funny. Realism is traded for effect on purpose.', practical_application='The artist chooses bolder props and heavier gestures, such as an oversized punch or a snappy whoosh, pushing performance beyond natural loudness to sell a stylized moment.', purpose_function='Heightens tone and draws attention to action, matching the stylized feel of comedies, cartoons, and action films.', related_terms=ARRAY['Hero Foley','Foley Sweetening','Foley Prop','Foley Acting','Foley Reinforcement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['An animated comedy','A slapstick pratfall','A heightened superhero punch']::text[] WHERE id='53b32326-c642-49b6-9bc8-772f1e22b1ed';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Prop building', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming the real object always sounds best','Over-building when a found prop would work','Not testing the rig on the mic before the take','Making a prop too fragile for multiple takes']::text[], definition='A sound-making object built or assembled specifically to produce a required Foley effect, as opposed to a found or everyday object, often combining materials to imitate something impractical to record directly.', plain_english='This is a prop built just to make a sound. The artist assembles it from parts. It stands in for something hard to record. For example, celery snapped for breaking bones. It is made on purpose for the effect.', practical_application='To get a bone crack, the artist builds a rig from celery stalks, or assembles a creature body from gloves and gel, testing until the sound matches picture.', purpose_function='Lets the Foley team recreate sounds that are dangerous, unavailable, or impractical to source from the real object.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Prop','Foley Prop Collection','Foley Prop Room','Hero Foley','Foley Specific']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Bone breaks in a fight scene','A fantasy creature''s body movement','A squishing effect for a horror scene']::text[] WHERE id='ea9e8aa5-e912-4c42-8bed-ed4a5c99e1fd';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Performance craft', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Focusing only on sync and ignoring character','Copying steps mechanically without feeling','Not watching the actor''s emotional state','Performing with energy that mismatches the scene']::text[], definition='The performance discipline within Foley in which the artist physically embodies an on-screen character''s movements and timing so recorded sounds match the actor''s weight, mood, and rhythm.', plain_english='This is acting through sound. The Foley artist mimics the actor''s moves. They match the walk, mood, and timing. Good acting makes the sound feel real. It is performance, not just noise-making.', practical_application='Watching an actor''s tired, heavy walk, the artist steps with matching drag and weight, channeling the character''s state into each footfall and prop handle.', purpose_function='Ensures Foley conveys character and emotion, not just mechanical sync, so footsteps and moves feel like they belong to the person on screen.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Performer','Foley Walker','Footstep Foley','Cloth Foley','Foley Take']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Matching a limping character','A nervous character''s fidgeting','An angry stomp across a room']::text[] WHERE id='407b9c97-8c4b-4867-98d4-aedc8834f44c';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley crew role', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Leaving props disorganized during a session','Slow resets that waste stage time','Failing to anticipate the next cue','Mishandling delicate or one-off props']::text[], definition='A crew member who supports the Foley artist and recordist by preparing props and surfaces, laying out cues, managing the pit, and handling logistics so the session runs efficiently.', plain_english='This person helps the Foley team work fast. They set up props and surfaces. They keep the pit organized. They fetch and reset items between takes. Their work keeps the session moving.', practical_application='Between cues the assistant swaps surfaces, brings the right shoes, cues the next prop set, and clears used items so the artist can stay focused on picture.', purpose_function='Keeps sessions productive by offloading setup and reset tasks from the artist and recordist, protecting the creative flow.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Performer','Foley Recordist','Foley Supervisor','Foley Setup','Foley Prop Room']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A busy episodic TV session','Resetting a water surface between takes','Prepping shoes for a scene change']::text[] WHERE id='28cbd65e-a690-4158-942b-8409f491280b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Workflow consistency', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Changing a character''s footstep sound mid-film','Forgetting which surface was used earlier','Mismatched levels between reels','Poor note-keeping across sessions']::text[], definition='The consistency of Foley choices such as surfaces, footwear, prop types, and level across shots, scenes, and reels, so a character''s sonic footprint stays believable throughout a project.', plain_english='This means keeping Foley consistent. A character keeps the same footstep sound. Surfaces and props stay matched across scenes. It prevents jarring changes for the audience. It keeps the world believable.', practical_application='The artist notes the shoe, surface, and cloth used for a character early on, then reuses those choices whenever the character reappears.', purpose_function='Maintains believability by ensuring the same character and setting sound alike from shot to shot, avoiding distracting mismatches.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Coverage','Foley Preparation','Foley Spotting','Foley Walker','Foley Supervisor']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A character crossing between scenes','Matching reels recorded on different days','Keeping a villain''s boots consistent']::text[] WHERE id='2e41610d-7fdd-44bb-9266-e0a25874b774';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Workflow planning', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming full coverage is always required','Under-covering key actions','Over-covering background the mix will bury','Not tracking what has been done']::text[], definition='The extent to which on-screen physical actions are supported by recorded Foley, describing how completely footsteps, moves, and specifics have been performed for a scene or project.', plain_english='This is how much of the action has Foley. Full coverage means every move has sound. Light coverage means only key actions do. It shows what is done and what is missing. It guides how much work remains.', practical_application='Reviewing a scene, the supervisor marks which actions still need Foley, deciding whether to cover every step or only the featured moves.', purpose_function='Helps supervisors gauge completeness and budget effort, ensuring important actions are covered while managing time and cost.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Spotting','Foley Continuity','Foley Supervisor','Foley Session','Incidental Foley']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A low-budget schedule needing selective coverage','A feature aiming for full coverage','Deciding coverage for a crowd scene']::text[] WHERE id='bf9e8965-0316-4200-bcb5-c47e96abd62c';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Movement performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Leaving Foley too clean and lifeless','Adding so much dirt it becomes noise','Using the wrong texture for the surface','Ignoring dirt in quiet, exposed scenes']::text[], definition='The small, incidental movement sounds such as scuffs, scrapes, shifts, and settles added to make Foley feel natural and lived-in rather than clean and mechanical.', plain_english='This is the little messy sounds in Foley. Think small scuffs and shifts. They fill the gaps between big sounds. They make movement feel real. Without them, Foley can sound too clean.', practical_application='Between main footfalls the artist adds toe scuffs, weight shifts, and small drags, sprinkling in dirt so the walk sounds real rather than robotic.', purpose_function='Adds naturalism and texture, filling the spaces between deliberate footsteps and prop hits so movement feels human.', related_terms=ARRAY['Footstep Foley','Incidental Foley','Cloth Foley','Foley Acting','Foley Sweetening']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A slow walk across a room','A character shifting in a chair','A tense pause with small movements']::text[] WHERE id='9103e083-1978-4d0c-832d-99b08627266a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley crew role', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the performer with the recordist or mixer','Assuming only footsteps are performed','Poor sync to picture','Ignoring character in the performance']::text[], definition='The artist who physically creates Foley sounds by walking, moving, and handling props in sync with picture; in most usage synonymous with Foley artist, and with Foley walker for footsteps.', plain_english='This is the person who performs Foley. They walk, move, and handle props. Everything is timed to the picture. They turn action into sound. They are also called a Foley artist.', practical_application='Standing in the pit, the performer watches picture and steps, rustles cloth, and works props, take after take, to build a scene''s movement track.', purpose_function='Provides the human performance at the heart of Foley, translating on-screen action into believable, synced sound.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Walker','Foley Acting','Footstep Foley','Foley Recordist','Foley Assistant']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A two-artist team splitting cues','A solo performer on a TV show','Performing a complex fight scene']::text[] WHERE id='65eba110-151c-4d69-bae6-9842d9d181ea';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Workflow planning', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Skipping prep and improvising on the clock','Missing a needed prop or surface','Not testing surfaces beforehand','Laying out cues in a disorganized order']::text[], definition='The planning and setup work done before a Foley session, including spotting cues, gathering props and shoes, choosing surfaces, and readying the stage so recording proceeds smoothly.', plain_english='This is the prep before recording. The team lists what sounds are needed. They gather props, shoes, and surfaces. They set up the stage. Good prep makes the session run fast.', practical_application='Before a session, the artist reviews the spotting notes, pulls the right shoes and props, wets a water tray, and lays out surfaces in cue order.', purpose_function='Saves costly stage time and prevents interruptions by ensuring everything needed is ready before recording begins.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Spotting','Foley Setup','Foley Prop Room','Foley Session','Foley Assistant']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Prepping for a full feature day','A rushed episodic turnaround','Readying water and gravel trays']::text[] WHERE id='076e30ad-ebdd-44ca-b743-eb11c911da5b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley equipment', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming the on-screen object is always the best sound source','Not keeping backup props for retakes','Using a prop that sounds wrong on the mic','Damaging an irreplaceable one-off prop']::text[], definition='Any object used on the Foley stage to generate a required sound effect, from everyday items to purpose-built rigs, performed in sync with picture.', plain_english='This is any object used to make a Foley sound. It can be a cup, a door, or keys. The artist handles it in time with picture. Some props are everyday items. Others are built for one sound.', practical_application='To match a character setting down a mug, the artist uses a real mug on a matching surface, performing the place-down in sync with the picture.', purpose_function='Provides the physical source of specific, prop-based sounds, letting the artist recreate handling, impacts, and object noises to match action.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Prop Collection','Foley Prop Room','Fabricated Foley Prop','Foley Specific','Hero Foley']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Keys jingling in a pocket','A phone set down on a desk','A sword drawn from a scabbard']::text[] WHERE id='46027ccf-3c86-4398-8ec2-218e1de26431';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley equipment', common_mistakes=ARRAY['A disorganized collection that wastes search time','Letting the collection go stale or incomplete','Not cataloging items','Discarding useful oddball objects']::text[], definition='The organized assortment of objects, materials, and surfaces a Foley team keeps on hand to produce a wide range of sound effects on demand.', plain_english='This is the team''s stock of sound props. It holds many objects and materials. The team keeps them ready to grab. A big collection covers many sounds. It saves time during sessions.', practical_application='Facing a scene needing glass, metal, and cloth sounds, the artist pulls the right items from the collection instead of hunting for them.', purpose_function='Enables fast, flexible sound creation by keeping a broad variety of proven sound sources within reach.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Prop','Foley Prop Room','Fabricated Foley Prop','Foley Setup','Foley Studio']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Building a collection for a new studio','Sourcing unusual materials for a project','Restocking after a big feature']::text[] WHERE id='489c5c0c-dee2-4b04-811d-83f8abf3559f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley facility', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: stacking heavy props or shelving them unstably so items can fall and injure crew','Poor organization that slows retrieval','Blocking access to the room during sessions','Letting clutter build up over time']::text[], definition='The dedicated storage space, adjoining or near the Foley stage, where props, materials, and surfaces are kept organized and accessible for sessions.', plain_english='This is the room that stores Foley props. It sits near the stage. Items are organized for quick access. The team pulls props from here during sessions. It keeps the stage clear and tidy.', practical_application='During a session the assistant runs to the prop room to grab a specific pane of glass or pair of boots, then returns them after the cue.', purpose_function='Keeps a large prop inventory organized and close to the stage so needed items can be found and returned quickly.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Prop Collection','Foley Prop','Foley Studio','Foley Setup','Foley Assistant']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Organizing a new prop room','Retrieving a bulky prop mid-session','Storing seasonal or specialty surfaces']::text[] WHERE id='ef541ce8-6ed0-4361-b4b3-18cd3363fdba';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley crew role', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the recordist with the performer','Poor microphone placement','Clipping loud impacts','Inconsistent perspective between cues']::text[], definition='The engineer responsible for capturing Foley performances by choosing and placing microphones, setting levels, and recording clean takes; often also called the Foley mixer.', plain_english='This is the person who records the Foley. They pick and place the mics. They set levels and capture clean takes. They work with the performer to get good sound. They are also called the Foley mixer.', practical_application='The recordist positions a mic near the pit, rides gain on a punchy prop hit, and rolls record while the artist performs to picture.', purpose_function='Ensures Foley is captured cleanly, at proper level, with appropriate mic choice and perspective for the picture.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Performer','Foley Session','Foley Take','Foley Setup','Foley Supervisor']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting mic distance for footsteps','Capturing a loud crash without clipping','Matching perspective to a wide shot']::text[] WHERE id='9d5d17d1-5c9d-4977-8fe1-ce67b85f2124';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound editorial approach', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing reinforcement with full replacement','Adding so much Foley it fights the production sound','Phase problems when layering with location audio','Mismatched perspective between layers']::text[], definition='The use of Foley to strengthen or support existing production sound or cut effects, adding weight, detail, or presence rather than fully replacing them.', plain_english='This is Foley used to back up other sound. It adds weight to what is already there. It fills in missing detail. It works with production sound, not against it. The goal is support, not replacement.', practical_application='Under weak production footsteps, the artist adds matching Foley steps to reinforce them, blending with rather than replacing the location track.', purpose_function='Enhances thin or incomplete production audio and effects, adding realism and impact without discarding the original.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Replacement','Foley Sweetening','Foley Coverage','Footstep Foley','Foley Session']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Thin location footsteps needing support','Backing up a weak door slam','Adding body to a filmed impact']::text[] WHERE id='3caa6116-24d4-48ee-a67b-c2ed9c42bf87';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound editorial approach', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing replacement with reinforcement','Missing cues that existed in the production sound','Mismatched perspective from the picture','Incomplete coverage for a music-and-effects (M&E) deliverable']::text[], definition='The practice of recreating on-screen action sounds entirely with Foley to replace unusable, absent, or undesirable production audio, common when location sound is noisy or a fully rebuilt effects track is needed.', plain_english='This is Foley that replaces the original sound. The location audio may be too noisy. So the team rebuilds every move sound. Footsteps and props are all re-created. The new Foley stands in fully.', practical_application='For a music-and-effects mix, the artist re-creates every footstep, cloth move, and prop sound so the film can be dubbed without losing action audio.', purpose_function='Provides clean, controllable movement audio when production sound cannot be used, and is essential for building foreign-language music-and-effects versions.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Reinforcement','Foley Coverage','Footstep Foley','Foley Session','Foley Sweetening']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A noisy location requiring full replacement','Building a music-and-effects track','Replacing a scene shot near traffic']::text[] WHERE id='fc872268-1181-4701-aefd-448c97ceabcb';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Under-booking the time needed','Poor prep that wastes session hours','Losing track of covered cues','Not slating takes clearly']::text[], definition='A scheduled recording period on the Foley stage during which the artist and recordist perform and capture Foley cues for a project, working through spotted actions reel by reel.', plain_english='This is a Foley recording session. The team works on the stage. They record sounds for the film. They go through the action cue by cue. Sessions are booked by the day or hour.', practical_application='Booked for a day, the team runs the session reel by reel, recording footsteps first, then moves, then specifics for each scene.', purpose_function='Provides the dedicated, organized time and space to capture a project''s Foley efficiently against a schedule.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Take','Foley Setup','Foley Preparation','Foley Recordist','Foley Performer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A one-day episodic session','A multi-day feature','A pickup session for missed cues']::text[] WHERE id='2aa70df6-edf8-4168-91d8-3d7264be18b9';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Placing microphones badly','Leaving props out of reach','Failing to secure loose surfaces','No clear sightline to the picture']::text[], definition='The physical arrangement of the Foley stage for recording, including positioning surfaces, props, microphones, and monitoring so the performer can work efficiently to picture.', plain_english='This is how the stage is set for recording. Surfaces, props, and mics are placed. The screen and monitors are ready. A good setup lets the artist work fast. It is done before takes begin.', practical_application='The team lays the walking surface in front of the mic, sets prop tables within reach, and confirms picture playback before the first cue.', purpose_function='Creates an efficient, repeatable working arrangement so performances sync well and recordings stay consistent.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Preparation','Foley Session','Foley Studio','Foley Recordist','Foley Prop Room']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting up for a rain scene','Arranging multiple walking surfaces','Positioning mics for footsteps']::text[] WHERE id='0dcca41e-f4d3-4dd5-ad93-04e514f564b5';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley equipment', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using one shoe for every character','Choosing the wrong shoe for the surface','Worn soles that change the sound','Not maintaining a varied shoe stock']::text[], definition='Footwear kept and used specifically for Foley footstep performance; artists maintain a range of shoes such as dress shoes, boots, heels, and sneakers to match on-screen characters and surfaces.', plain_english='This is a shoe used just for Foley. Artists keep many kinds. They pick shoes to match the character. Boots, heels, and sneakers all sound different. The right shoe helps sell the walk.', practical_application='For a scene with a character in heels on tile, the artist selects heeled shoes and steps on a tile surface to match the sound.', purpose_function='Lets the performer match a character''s footwear and produce the correct footstep timbre for the surface and scene.', related_terms=ARRAY['Footstep Foley','Foley Walker','Barefoot Foley','Foley Prop Collection','Foley Performer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Matching cowboy boots on wood','A character walking in high heels','Swapping shoes between characters']::text[] WHERE id='49ee2ae4-fab4-43f1-9c35-657a5283b374';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Skipping slates and losing track of takes','Using vague or wrong cue names','Inconsistent take numbering','Announcing the slate unclearly']::text[], definition='A spoken or visual identifier recorded at the head of a Foley take that names the cue, take number, and details so recordings can be logged and found later.', plain_english='This is a label at the start of a take. The recordist says the cue and take number. It helps sort recordings later. It keeps takes organized. It works like a slate in filming.', practical_application='Before rolling, the recordist announces the cue and take number so the editor can later identify and pull the right take.', purpose_function='Enables accurate logging and retrieval of takes, keeping a session''s many cues organized for editorial.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Take','Foley Session','Foley Recordist','Foley Continuity','Foley Spotting']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Logging many cues in a long session','Naming pickup takes','Organizing takes for an editor']::text[] WHERE id='57e3e7c8-55aa-49b6-b0cc-2e49bbdff3c5';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Prop performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Lumping specifics in with general moves','Poor sync on precise object hits','Using a prop with the wrong timbre','Missing small but audible specifics']::text[], definition='A single ''specific'' — one sound from the third Foley category (after footsteps and moves), a category known as specifics; it is a distinct prop or object sound performed for a particular on-screen action, such as a set-down, a switch, or a handled object.', plain_english='This is one specific prop sound. Foley has three types of sound. Footsteps and moves are two. Specifics are the third: object sounds. A specific is one such prop cue, like a cup set down.', practical_application='For a scene where a character flips a light switch and sets down keys, the artist performs each as a separate specific, matching timing exactly.', purpose_function='Covers the distinct object sounds an action needs beyond footsteps and cloth, giving props and handling their own precise cues.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Prop','Footstep Foley','Cloth Foley','Hero Foley','Foley Coverage']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A light switch click','Keys dropped on a table','A handled weapon or tool']::text[] WHERE id='44e92810-abab-4c2f-b70b-4418924a1dfd';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Workflow planning', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Missing subtle actions during the review','Over-spotting background the mix will bury','Writing vague notes','Not timestamping cues']::text[], definition='The review process in which the supervisor, editor, or artist watches picture to identify and note every action requiring Foley, producing a cue list that guides the session.', plain_english='This is finding what needs Foley. The team watches the picture closely. They list every action needing sound. This becomes the cue list. The list guides the recording session.', practical_application='Watching a reel, the supervisor pauses at each action, noting cues such as footsteps, a door, or a prop into a spotting list for the session.', purpose_function='Produces the roadmap for Foley work, ensuring nothing important is missed and time is planned around the actual needs.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Coverage','Foley Preparation','Foley Supervisor','Foley Session','Foley Continuity']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Spotting a newly cut reel','Planning a tight schedule','Flagging featured hero cues']::text[] WHERE id='3a9f3e53-804e-4c26-8643-4a5a0266554c';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley facility', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Expecting good Foley in an untreated room','Ignoring the value of varied pit surfaces','Poor acoustic isolation','Inadequate picture playback']::text[], definition='A facility designed for Foley recording, typically an acoustically controlled stage fitted with varied walking surfaces or pits, prop storage, microphones, and picture playback.', plain_english='This is the place built for Foley work. It is a quiet, treated room. The floor has different walking surfaces. It holds mics, props, and a screen. The team records Foley here.', practical_application='Booking a Foley studio, the team uses its built-in pits, mic setups, and prop room to record a project''s movement track.', purpose_function='Provides the controlled acoustic environment and built-in surfaces and gear needed to record clean, flexible Foley.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Setup','Foley Prop Room','Foley Session','Foley Recordist','Foley Performer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Booking a stage for a feature','Working in a small in-house Foley room','Recording on a purpose-built pit stage']::text[] WHERE id='199cf0b3-15d2-4ca0-99fb-7c16aaf21d95';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley crew role', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Giving the artist unclear direction','Poor scheduling of the work','Skipping careful spotting','Not communicating with editorial']::text[], definition='The person who oversees a project''s Foley by spotting needs, hiring and directing the artist and recordist, setting the creative approach, and ensuring cues meet the picture and schedule.', plain_english='This person runs the Foley for a project. They decide what needs Foley. They guide the artist and recordist. They set the creative goals. They keep the work on schedule.', practical_application='The supervisor spots the film, briefs the artist on the desired feel, reviews takes, and coordinates with the sound editorial team.', purpose_function='Provides creative and logistical leadership so Foley serves the film''s needs and finishes on time and budget.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Spotting','Foley Coverage','Foley Recordist','Foley Performer','Foley Continuity']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Leading a feature''s Foley','Briefing a new artist','Balancing coverage against schedule']::text[] WHERE id='6a9dd3e8-b10f-43dc-ad10-ad6eaa708733';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound editorial approach', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing sweetening with full replacement','Over-sweetening until it sounds unnatural','Phase problems when layering','Sweetening unimportant moments']::text[], definition='The addition of Foley to enhance, enrich, or polish an existing sound, adding detail, texture, or impact to production sound or effects so a moment feels fuller.', plain_english='This is adding Foley to improve a sound. It gives extra detail and richness. It polishes a moment that feels thin. It layers with what is already there. The result feels fuller.', practical_application='To make a punch land harder, the artist adds a layered impact and cloth hit, sweetening the cut sound effect with performed detail.', purpose_function='Improves the richness and impact of key moments by layering performed detail onto existing audio.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Reinforcement','Exaggerated Foley','Hero Foley','Foley Replacement','Foley Dirt']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Beefing up a punch','Enriching a door slam','Adding detail to a handled prop']::text[] WHERE id='301ee577-6cea-4505-b9fa-e21d7865a9bf';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording only one take with no backup','Not slating takes','Letting poor sync go unnoticed','Keeping no notes on the preferred take']::text[], definition='A single recorded pass of a Foley performance for a given cue; several takes are usually recorded so the best performance can be selected in editing.', plain_english='This is one recorded attempt at a cue. The artist performs it once and it is captured. The team often records several takes. Later they pick the best one. Each take gets a number.', practical_application='The artist performs footsteps three times; the recordist logs the takes, and the editor later picks the tightest-synced one.', purpose_function='Provides selectable options for a cue, letting editorial choose the best-synced, best-sounding performance.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Slate','Foley Session','Foley Recordist','Foley Performer','Foley Continuity']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Getting several footstep takes','A pickup take for a missed cue','Choosing between takes in the edit']::text[] WHERE id='0fcd78a6-6ef8-497b-8b43-3e288ef790d9';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley crew role', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Treating footsteps as mechanical rather than character-driven','Using the wrong shoe or surface','Poor sync to the actor''s stride','Ignoring the character''s weight and mood']::text[], definition='A Foley artist who specializes in performing footsteps and body movement to picture; a widely used term, especially in British practice, for the person who walks a character''s steps.', plain_english='This is a Foley artist who does footsteps. They walk the character''s steps to picture. The name is common in Britain. They match the actor''s gait and weight. Footwork is their specialty.', practical_application='The walker watches the actor''s stride and steps in matching shoes on the right surface, syncing each footfall to picture.', purpose_function='Provides the specialized footstep and movement performance that grounds characters physically in a scene.', related_terms=ARRAY['Footstep Foley','Foley Performer','Foley Acting','Foley Shoe','Barefoot Foley']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Walking a character across gravel','Matching a run','Syncing a limping gait']::text[] WHERE id='2077feac-57a8-45c7-8690-f7bf50cae192';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Footstep performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using the wrong surface or shoe','Mechanical timing without character','Ignoring the character''s weight','Inconsistent level across scenes']::text[], definition='The performance and recording of characters'' footsteps in sync with picture, the most fundamental category of Foley, matched to footwear, surface, gait, and weight.', plain_english='This is Foley for footsteps. The artist walks in time with the picture. Shoes and surface are chosen to match. Gait and weight are matched too. It is the core of Foley work.', practical_application='Matching a character crossing a wooden floor, the artist wears matching shoes and steps on a wood pit, following the actor''s rhythm.', purpose_function='Grounds characters in their environment and gives movement weight, forming the backbone of a scene''s Foley track.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Walker','Foley Shoe','Barefoot Foley','Foley Performer','Foley Acting']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Footsteps on gravel','A character running upstairs','Quiet steps in a tense scene']::text[] WHERE id='a0ad6f94-44cc-4ea9-8c26-42f21d58e87a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Movement performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Overlooking subtle hand sounds','Using the wrong material for the contact','Poor sync to fast gestures','Playing hands too loud for the scene']::text[], definition='The Foley performance of hand-generated sounds such as pats, taps, grabs, claps, and object handling made with the hands, synchronized to an actor''s gestures.', plain_english='This is Foley made with the hands. It covers pats, taps, and grabs. It matches an actor''s hand moves. Think of a hand slapping a table. The sounds follow the gestures on screen.', practical_application='For a character patting a dusty coat, the artist claps and pats matching materials by hand, timed to the on-screen gesture.', purpose_function='Supplies the many small hand-contact sounds such as handling, gestures, and touches that add realism to a character''s actions.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cloth Foley','Foley Prop','Foley Specific','Footstep Foley','Foley Acting']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A handshake','Patting down pockets','Clapping or gripping a rail']::text[] WHERE id='c84d0f9a-f678-4302-b4cd-357731fce512';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Performance emphasis', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Treating a featured sound as routine background','Under-preparing the hero prop','Recording too few takes on a critical cue','Using a prop with the wrong timbre']::text[], definition='A prominent, featured Foley element that carries dramatic weight in a scene and must be performed with special care because it will be heard clearly in the mix.', plain_english='This is a standout Foley sound. It is important to the scene. The audience will clearly hear it. So the team performs it with extra care. Everything must sound just right.', practical_application='For a close-up of a gun being cocked, the artist treats it as hero Foley, testing props and takes until the sound is perfect.', purpose_function='Ensures key, exposed sounds that the story leans on are performed and recorded to a higher standard than background detail.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Specific','Foley Prop','Foley Sweetening','Exaggerated Foley','Foley Coverage']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A featured weapon handle','A key door lock in close-up','A signature object sound']::text[] WHERE id='b5d1c6bf-7e83-4b5e-89a8-f13473e11009';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Movement performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making incidental sounds too loud','Adding so many they clutter the mix','Omitting them and leaving scenes lifeless','Using the wrong texture for the space']::text[], definition='Minor, background movement sounds performed to add realism, such as small shifts, settles, and passing actions that support a scene without drawing attention.', plain_english='This is small background Foley. It covers minor shifts and settles. It supports the scene quietly. It is not meant to stand out. It makes the world feel full and real.', practical_application='In a dialogue scene the artist adds quiet chair shifts and small prop touches so the space feels lived-in behind the words.', purpose_function='Fills a scene with subtle life and realism, supporting the main action without competing for attention.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foley Dirt','Foley Coverage','Cloth Foley','Footstep Foley','Foley Sweetening']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Background settling in a room','A character shifting during dialogue','Small movements in a quiet scene']::text[] WHERE id='64203273-91a0-4c88-82d8-14c3a0166e7b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley performance technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming loose sync means sloppy or careless timing rather than a deliberate, motion-matched feel','Using loose sync for hard contact sounds such as footfalls or door slams that the audience expects to hit exactly on picture','Over-nudging clips in the edit until natural performance rhythm is lost']::text[], definition='Foley performed and recorded to picture without frame-tight synchronization, where the sounds follow the general timing and feel of an action rather than locking precisely to each on-screen contact.', plain_english='This is Foley that follows the action loosely. The sounds match the general timing, not every exact frame. Artists use it for cloth and broad movement. Tight sync is saved for clear hits like footsteps.', practical_application='A Foley artist records a coat rustle or a person shifting in a chair by matching the motion''s overall rhythm, then nudges the clip in the DAW only if a specific accent needs to land.', purpose_function='Lets performers capture natural, flowing movement and cloth sounds without the stiffness that comes from chasing exact frames, keeping recordings believable and unforced.', related_terms=ARRAY['Synchronized Foley','Cloth Pass','Clothing Movement','Wild Foley','Effect Sync Point']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a cloth pass for a character walking through a room','Covering general body movement where no single frame-accurate hit is needed','Laying in restless bedsheet movement under a dialogue scene']::text[] WHERE id='ae337650-08cb-42d8-8642-ab8d1c397e23';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley performance category', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing mouth Foley with ADR or dialogue, which cover spoken lines instead','Recording mouth sounds too loud or too close, making them unnaturally wet and distracting','Forgetting that mouth Foley should sit under, not compete with, the dialogue']::text[], definition='Foley that covers non-verbal mouth-related sounds such as chewing, swallowing, sipping, kissing and lip smacks, performed in sync to picture and kept separate from dialogue and ADR.', plain_english='This is Foley for sounds the mouth makes. Think chewing, sipping, kisses, and lip smacks. It does not include spoken words. Artists record it in sync with the picture.', practical_application='For a dinner scene, a Foley artist eats similar food in sync to match each bite and chew, giving the editor clean mouth sounds to place under the picture.', purpose_function='Adds realistic eating and mouth detail that the production mic often misses, giving the mixer clean, controllable elements that do not disturb the dialogue track.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Foley','Synchronized Foley','ADR','Production Foley','Contact Detail']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding chewing and swallowing to a character eating during dialogue','Covering a kiss where the production sound is unusable','Building sipping and cup detail for a coffee-shop scene']::text[] WHERE id='47cd3de0-f77f-4b32-a46c-0522c6a76eb5';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley prop', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: Using a functional firearm, blade, or other dangerous item as a practical prop instead of a safe non-firing or blunted version handled under proper precautions','Assuming the real object always sounds best on mic, when a substitute may read better','Damaging or wearing out the actual prop through repeated takes when a stand-in would do']::text[], definition='A real, functional object, ideally the same type shown on screen, used on the Foley stage to generate an action''s sound directly, as opposed to a substitute prop chosen only because it sounds similar.', plain_english='This is a real, working prop used to make a sound. It usually matches the object seen on screen. A Foley artist handles the actual item. It differs from a stand-in that only sounds close.', practical_application='To cover a character loading a revolver, the Foley artist uses an actual safe, non-firing revolver of the same type so the cylinder, latch and hammer sounds match the picture.', purpose_function='Delivers authentic, matched sound and handling detail for a specific on-screen object, reducing the need to fake or heavily process the effect later.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Foley','Contact Detail','Synchronized Foley','Character Pass','Costume Movement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording the exact latch and hinge of the same briefcase used on set','Handling a matching set of keys to sync a door-unlock moment','Using the real prop phone to capture its specific button and flip sounds']::text[] WHERE id='aa25e6e4-302e-4d11-abcc-f8af4faac222';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Post-production sound source', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing production Foley with stage Foley recorded in a controlled Foley room','Relying on production movement that is buried under dialogue or noise and cannot be split out','Assuming production sound will always match the final cut''s perspective and edit']::text[], definition='Sync movement, footstep and handling sounds present in the production (on-set) recordings that can fulfill Foley''s role in the final track, potentially reducing the amount of Foley recorded on a stage.', plain_english='This is Foley-type sound already caught by the set microphones. It includes footsteps and handling caught during filming. Editors can sometimes keep it instead of recording new Foley. It saves time when the sound is clean.', practical_application='A sound editor auditions the production tracks and finds clean footsteps and cloth in a wide shot, keeping them in the effects edit rather than tasking the Foley stage to recreate them.', purpose_function='Uses usable on-set movement sound to save Foley-stage time and keep a natural bond between action and sound, when the production recording is clean enough.', related_terms=ARRAY['Production Effects','Production Sound','Synchronized Foley','Wild Foley','Effects Continuity']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Salvaging clean footsteps from a location take to avoid a Foley pass','Deciding whether on-set handling sound is usable in the effects edit','Splitting usable movement from dialogue during editorial']::text[] WHERE id='62b1ad94-b8b1-4b0a-9b28-b3d6bf052ea4';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley performance category', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Treating prop Foley as less important than footsteps, when object detail often sells the shot','UNSAFE: Handling real weapons, breakable glass, or sharp props without safe substitutes and protection','Recording props with the wrong surface or mic distance so they do not match on-screen scale']::text[], definition='The Foley category covering the sounds of handling and using objects such as cups, keys, weapons, tools and furniture, as distinct from the footsteps and cloth-movement categories.', plain_english='This is Foley for handling objects. It covers cups, keys, tools, and doors. It is one of the three main Foley types. The others are footsteps and cloth moves.', practical_application='In a prop pass, the Foley artist syncs every object a character touches, such as setting down a glass, cocking a gun or flipping a switch, matching each contact to picture.', purpose_function='Provides the specific object-interaction sounds that sell physical action and detail, filling gaps the production track missed and giving the mixer clean, controllable elements.', related_terms=ARRAY['Practical Foley Prop','Contact Detail','Cloth Pass','Character Pass','Synchronized Foley']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Covering a character preparing a meal with clinks, chops and pours','Syncing a gun handling sequence with cylinder, hammer and holster sounds','Adding paper, pen and desk sounds to an office scene']::text[] WHERE id='da25153d-e0ce-4802-90a5-d3d8f2719ff6';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley performance technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming small sync errors do not matter, when audiences readily notice off footsteps','Chasing perfect sync in performance instead of fixing minor slips in the edit','Confusing synchronized Foley with wild Foley, which is recorded free of picture']::text[], definition='Foley performed and recorded in tight synchronization with the on-screen action, so each footstep, movement or object contact lands on its matching picture frame; this frame-accurate sync is the defining trait of Foley.', plain_english='This is Foley recorded in tight sync with the picture. Each step or contact lands on the right frame. Watching sync is the heart of Foley work. It is named after Jack Foley.', practical_application='Watching the screen, the Foley artist walks in real time so each footfall hits exactly with the actor''s, capturing sound already locked to picture.', purpose_function='Ties the recorded sound precisely to the picture so the audience accepts the action as real, giving the mix elements that already sit in place.', related_terms=ARRAY['Loose-Sync Foley','Wild Foley','Effect Sync Point','Cadence Matching','Prop Foley']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Performing footsteps in real time against the locked picture','Syncing a sword draw exactly to the on-screen motion','Matching a hand slap to the frame of contact']::text[] WHERE id='23b81e98-4aca-4fc0-98ac-9fd1c248356b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley performance technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Over-performing every movement so the track feels noisy and unnatural','Confusing underplayed with inaudible, and leaving needed detail out entirely','Setting record levels so hot that subtle performance still sounds exaggerated']::text[], definition='Foley performed at a deliberately restrained level of intensity and detail so that it supports the picture naturally without drawing the audience''s attention to itself.', plain_english='This is Foley kept subtle on purpose. The artist performs it softly and simply. It supports the scene without standing out. Good Foley often goes unnoticed.', practical_application='For a quiet conversation, the Foley artist keeps cloth and small moves gentle and sparse, avoiding busy or exaggerated sounds that would pull focus from the actors.', purpose_function='Keeps sound believable and unobtrusive, matching real-life loudness so movement and handling do not distract from dialogue and story.', related_terms=ARRAY['Loose-Sync Foley','Clothing Movement','Contact Detail','Synchronized Foley','Cadence Matching']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Keeping cloth light under an intimate dialogue scene','Softening footsteps for a character trying to move quietly','Restraining prop handling so it stays behind the performances']::text[] WHERE id='24f3eb3b-4bc9-4318-9c0f-580e08f0d793';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley recording method', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming wild Foley skips editing, when it usually needs careful syncing afterward','Recording too few variations, leaving the editor without options to match picture','Confusing wild Foley with synchronized Foley performed to picture']::text[], definition='Foley recorded without watching or locking to picture, captured as free-running takes that are later edited and synchronized to the action in the DAW.', plain_english='This is Foley recorded without the picture. The artist makes the sounds freely. An editor lines them up later. It is the opposite of watching sync while performing.', practical_application='A Foley artist records many clean gravel steps wild, then the editor cuts and places individual footfalls to match each on-screen step.', purpose_function='Lets performers get the best-sounding takes of a texture or action without the pressure of exact timing, leaving sync to be handled in editing.', related_terms=ARRAY['Synchronized Foley','Loose-Sync Foley','Wild Track','Effect Sync Point','Controlled Effects Recording']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Capturing a library of clean footstep variations on one surface','Recording cloth textures wild for later placement','Grabbing prop handling sounds without chasing exact frames']::text[] WHERE id='66065a67-6b3b-4c56-9fb6-4be4781bdff9';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley moves subcategory', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Overloading a shot with accessory sounds that real gear would not make','Ignoring accessory detail that the audience expects from visible gear','Using items that jingle at the wrong pitch or weight for the costume']::text[], definition='A cloth-and-moves Foley subcategory covering the sounds of worn accessories in motion, such as belts, bags, holsters, straps and jewelry, as a character moves.', plain_english='This is Foley for accessories that move. Think belts, bags, holsters, and jewelry. The sounds come as a character moves. It is part of the cloth and moves work.', practical_application='For a soldier walking, the Foley artist adds the creak and jingle of a belt, holster and buckles, performed with matching items in sync to the movement.', purpose_function='Adds specific, believable detail to a character''s movement and costume, reinforcing weight, gear and period without competing with footsteps or dialogue.', related_terms=ARRAY['Clothing Movement','Costume Movement','Armor Movement','Cloth Pass','Coat Movement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding a duty belt''s creak and rattle for a police character','Covering a handbag''s straps and buckles as a character walks','Layering jewelry sounds under a formal-dress scene']::text[] WHERE id='752f91f9-9b90-4732-8be9-cafc38935cbb';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley moves subcategory', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using the wrong metal or mass so the armor sounds tinny or toy-like','Making armor rattle constantly when stillness needs quiet','Forgetting to match sword, scabbard and buckle sounds to the same character']::text[], definition='A Foley moves subcategory covering the sounds of armor in motion, such as the rattle, creak and clank of metal plate, chain mail or leather as an armored character moves.', plain_english='This is Foley for armor that moves. It covers clanks, rattles, and creaks. Metal plate and chain mail make these sounds. Artists perform it with real or stand-in armor.', practical_application='For a knight walking, the Foley artist moves metal plates and chain against a body rig, syncing rattles and clanks to each step and gesture.', purpose_function='Sells weight, material and period for armored characters, adding menace or grandeur and covering movement the production track cannot supply.', related_terms=ARRAY['Accessory Movement','Costume Movement','Clothing Movement','Prop Foley','Cloth Pass']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Covering a knight''s approach with plate and chain rattle','Adding armor movement to a battle scene''s hero pass','Syncing armor clanks to a character mounting a horse']::text[] WHERE id='40f9fcb0-a4c9-4768-94c6-a390de6b0656';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley movement effect', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: Dragging a real person or heavy dead weight without safe lifting technique, padding and clear communication, risking back strain or injury','Making the drag too smooth and losing the catches, stutters and effort of real weight','Forgetting cloth and accessory sounds that move with the dragged body']::text[], definition='A Foley or effects cue reproducing the sound of a body being dragged across a surface, combining scrape, cloth friction and the shifting weight of a person pulled along the ground or floor.', plain_english='This is the sound of a body being dragged. It has scraping and cloth friction. The weight shifts as the body moves. Artists perform it to match the picture.', practical_application='The Foley artist drags a weighted cloth bundle or their own body across the matching floor surface, syncing the scrape and friction to the on-screen drag.', purpose_function='Provides the visceral, grounded sound of a helpless or unconscious body being moved, adding weight and effort the picture alone cannot convey.', related_terms=ARRAY['Body Slide','Body Drop','Body Roll','Clothing Movement','Contact Detail']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Covering a character pulling a body out of danger','Syncing a drag across gravel in a crime scene','Adding effort and friction to a hostage being moved']::text[] WHERE id='f7514a0f-cf5a-463e-9595-086efc09fdc3';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley movement effect', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: Dropping your own body or heavy props to perform impacts without padding and safe technique, risking injury','Using an impact that is too light for the character''s weight and fall height','Forgetting the cloth rustle and settle that follow the main impact']::text[], definition='A Foley or effects cue reproducing the sound of a body falling and landing on a surface, combining the impact, cloth and settle of a person dropping to the ground.', plain_english='This is the sound of a body hitting the ground. It has a heavy impact and cloth. A small settle follows the landing. Artists perform it to match the fall.', practical_application='The Foley artist drops a weighted bag or padded stand-in onto the matching surface, timing the impact to the frame the body lands and adding cloth and settle.', purpose_function='Delivers the weight and finality of a fall or collapse, giving a body impact the punch and realism the picture needs.', related_terms=ARRAY['Body Drag','Body Roll','Body Slide','Contact Detail','Clothing Movement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Covering a character collapsing to the floor','Syncing a body hitting the ground after a fall','Adding a knockout drop in a fight scene']::text[] WHERE id='08be5a1a-bf94-4c41-820a-76ca615ec30d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley movement effect', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making the roll one smooth sound instead of separate weight shifts and catches','Losing the cloth friction that rolls with the body','Mismatching the roll''s speed to the picture']::text[], definition='A Foley cue reproducing the sound of a body rolling across a surface, with the continuous, shifting contact, cloth friction and weight of a person turning over the ground.', plain_english='This is the sound of a body rolling on the ground. Contact shifts as it turns. Cloth rubs and weight moves. Artists match it to the picture.', practical_application='The Foley artist rolls a weighted, clothed bundle or their body across the matching surface, following the on-screen roll''s speed and contact points.', purpose_function='Adds motion and continuity to a body on the ground, bridging a drop or impact into further movement.', related_terms=ARRAY['Body Drop','Body Drag','Body Slide','Body Shift','Clothing Movement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Covering a stunt fall that continues into a roll','Syncing a character rolling to dodge an attack','Adding a body rolling down a slope']::text[] WHERE id='484faad5-9fdd-4133-8c7b-9d14dc0f2d88';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley movement effect', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Overplaying shifts so a still character sounds restless','Ignoring the seat or surface sounds that accompany a shift','Making cloth too loud for a small movement']::text[], definition='A Foley cue for a small change in body position or weight, such as a character shifting on a seat, leaning or resettling, captured through subtle cloth, contact and support sounds.', plain_english='This is the sound of a small body move. Someone shifts in a chair or leans. It is mostly cloth and light contact. Artists keep it subtle.', practical_application='For a character fidgeting in an interview, the Foley artist adds soft cloth and chair sounds each time they shift weight, keeping it under the dialogue.', purpose_function='Adds quiet, living presence to still characters, signaling discomfort, tension or attention through small movement sounds.', related_terms=ARRAY['Body Slide','Body Roll','Clothing Movement','Cloth Settle','Underplayed Foley']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Covering a character resettling in a chair during dialogue','Adding a lean-in at a tense table moment','Signaling discomfort through small seat movement']::text[] WHERE id='c47740cb-604c-4bff-bafd-dc57a32f60dc';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley movement effect', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a slide''s steady friction with a drag''s stop-start effort','Losing cloth texture under the friction','Mismatching the slide length to picture']::text[], definition='A Foley cue reproducing the sound of a body sliding along a surface, with the sustained friction, cloth drag and shifting weight of a person sliding down or across the ground, a wall or an object.', plain_english='This is the sound of a body sliding. It has steady friction and cloth drag. Weight shifts along the surface. Artists match it to the slide on screen.', practical_application='The Foley artist slides a clothed, weighted bundle or their back down the matching surface, syncing the friction and cloth to the on-screen slide.', purpose_function='Conveys a controlled or weak slide, such as sinking down a wall or sliding across a floor, with continuous, grounded texture.', related_terms=ARRAY['Body Drag','Body Roll','Body Shift','Contact Detail','Clothing Movement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Covering a wounded character sliding down a wall','Syncing a body sliding across a smooth floor','Adding a slow slump into a seated position']::text[] WHERE id='ef5e2274-e606-40c5-98c4-aaa1b3f820a8';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley footwear detail', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Adding constant creak when real boots creak only at stress points','Using a creak pitch that does not match the boot''s size or leather','Letting creak overpower the footstep it accompanies']::text[], definition='A Foley detail reproducing the creak of leather boots as a character walks, shifts weight or flexes the foot, treated separately from the footstep impact itself.', plain_english='This is the creak of leather boots. It comes as the boot flexes. It is separate from the step''s impact. Artists add it for detail and character.', practical_application='The Foley artist flexes a real leather boot by hand or foot near the mic, syncing creaks to the character''s weight shifts and steps.', purpose_function='Adds material and character detail to footwear, suggesting leather, weight and period beyond the plain footstep.', related_terms=ARRAY['Boot Footstep','Accessory Movement','Contact Detail','Prop Foley','Clothing Movement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding leather creak to a cowboy''s slow walk','Detailing a soldier''s boots as they shift stance','Giving weight to a heavy character''s footwear']::text[] WHERE id='f05ad5e5-d457-4963-ae75-2a1a29e4cb9e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley footstep type', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using the wrong surface so booted steps do not match the location','Ignoring boot creak and buckle detail that go with the footwear','Mismatching step weight to the character''s size and gait']::text[], definition='A footstep Foley cue performed wearing boots, producing the specific impact, weight and surface interaction of booted feet as distinct from softer footwear.', plain_english='This is a footstep made in boots. Boots hit harder than soft shoes. The sound depends on the surface too. Artists perform it in real boots to match picture.', practical_application='In a footstep pass, the Foley artist wears matching boots and walks the Foley pit surface in sync with the character, capturing each booted step.', purpose_function='Provides the correct footwear character for booted characters, matching weight, hardness and surface for believable footsteps.', related_terms=ARRAY['Boot Creak','Cadence Matching','Synchronized Foley','Contact Detail','Character Matching']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Walking a character across a wooden porch in boots','Syncing booted marching on pavement','Covering heavy boots on gravel']::text[] WHERE id='0b211d9c-1d27-4871-9b72-8d1e949dece0';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley performance technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Hitting sync frames while missing the overall walking rhythm','Ignoring changes in pace, limp or hesitation in the actor''s gait','Performing a generic walk instead of the character''s specific stride']::text[], definition='The Foley practice of matching the rhythm, spacing and weight of performed footsteps to the timing and gait of the actor''s walk on screen.', plain_english='This means matching your steps to the actor''s walk. You copy their rhythm and spacing. The footsteps then feel like theirs. It is key to believable footstep Foley.', practical_application='Watching the actor, the Foley artist walks in step with the same rhythm and weight, so the recorded footsteps carry the character''s natural cadence.', purpose_function='Makes footsteps read as the character''s own by locking the performance to the actor''s pace, weight shifts and stride, not just individual frames.', related_terms=ARRAY['Synchronized Foley','Boot Footstep','Character Matching','Effect Sync Point','Contact Detail']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Matching a character''s confident stride down a hallway','Following a limp so the footsteps carry the injury','Keeping pace with a character speeding up to a run']::text[] WHERE id='d4027cf8-6a1d-4459-adb6-5fbcc435c084';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley performance technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using the same generic footsteps and cloth for every character','Ignoring how mood and situation change a character''s movement sound','Matching costume but not the character''s weight or gait']::text[], definition='The practice of shaping Foley, including footstep weight, footwear, cloth and handling, to fit a specific character''s build, costume, personality and situation, so the sounds feel like that individual.', plain_english='This means fitting the Foley to the character. You match their weight and shoes. You match their mood and costume. The sounds then feel like that person.', practical_application='For a heavy, tired character, the Foley artist chooses heavier footwear, slower cadence and weightier cloth than for a light, quick character in the same scene.', purpose_function='Gives each character a consistent, believable sonic identity, helping the audience read weight, status and mood through movement sound.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cadence Matching','Character Pass','Costume Movement','Boot Footstep','Underplayed Foley']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Giving a large villain heavier, slower footsteps','Matching a nervous character''s quick, light movement','Keeping a character''s sound consistent across scenes']::text[] WHERE id='1a502116-88db-42d8-a905-677a2a4220ff';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Mixing two characters'' Foley into one pass and losing track separation','Letting a character''s weight or footwear drift between reels','Forgetting to cover every action for the character before moving on']::text[], definition='A Foley recording pass dedicated to a single character, capturing that character''s footsteps, moves and props in one focused set of takes before moving to the next character.', plain_english='This is a Foley pass for one character. You record all their sounds together. Then you move to the next character. It keeps each character organized and consistent.', practical_application='For a two-hander scene, the Foley team records all of character A''s footsteps and moves in one pass, then repeats the scene for character B.', purpose_function='Keeps a character''s Foley consistent and well organized, letting the artist stay in that character''s weight, footwear and rhythm across the scene.', related_terms=ARRAY['Character Matching','Cloth Pass','Cadence Matching','Prop Foley','Costume Movement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording all of the lead''s footsteps before the co-star''s','Keeping a character''s cloth on its own track for the mix','Organizing a crowd scene by principal character passes']::text[] WHERE id='9a853778-64f0-49f1-8254-a5f56ebc4db7';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording cloth so busy it distracts from dialogue','Using fabric that does not match the on-screen costume','Skipping cloth on the assumption footsteps are enough']::text[], definition='A Foley recording pass dedicated to clothing and body-movement sounds, capturing the rustle, friction and settle of garments as characters move, recorded separately from footsteps and props.', plain_english='This is a Foley pass for clothing sounds. It covers rustles and cloth friction. It is recorded apart from footsteps and props. It runs under most scenes with people.', practical_application='The Foley artist wears or handles matching fabrics and moves in sync with the characters, recording a steady cloth layer across the scene.', purpose_function='Provides a continuous bed of realistic movement sound that grounds characters physically, kept on its own tracks for mix control.', related_terms=ARRAY['Clothing Movement','Costume Movement','Coat Movement','Character Pass','Cloth Settle']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Laying a cloth bed under a walk-and-talk scene','Matching heavy coat movement in a winter exterior','Adding subtle cloth to a seated dialogue']::text[] WHERE id='ee45b53a-4e63-4081-8252-f2086615c2e0';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley cloth detail', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Cutting the cloth off abruptly with no settle','Making the settle too loud for a small movement','Forgetting settle after sits, lies and stops']::text[], definition='A Foley detail capturing the sound of fabric coming to rest, such as the final rustle and drape of a garment settling after a character stops moving, sits or lies down.', plain_english='This is the sound of cloth settling down. It comes after movement stops. The fabric drapes and goes quiet. Artists add it to finish a move.', practical_application='After syncing a character sitting, the Foley artist adds the soft rustle of clothing settling into the seat, completing the movement.', purpose_function='Gives movements a natural ending and adds realism, signaling that a character has come to rest.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cloth Pass','Clothing Movement','Cloth Tension','Body Shift','Contact Detail']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Finishing a character sitting down with a cloth settle','Adding a coat settling as someone leans back','Closing a movement as a character lies on a bed']::text[] WHERE id='1fd62fea-d752-4e3d-9b82-bda7d4945031';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley cloth detail', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing tension creaks with loose cloth rustle','Overusing tension where the garment would move freely','Using fabric that will not creak under strain']::text[], definition='A Foley detail capturing the sound of fabric under tension, such as the stretch, creak or tightening of a garment as it is pulled taut by a character''s movement or grip.', plain_english='This is the sound of cloth being pulled tight. The fabric stretches and creaks. It happens when a garment is strained. Artists add it for effort and detail.', practical_application='For a character straining against restraints, the Foley artist pulls matching fabric taut near the mic, syncing the tension creaks to the effort on screen.', purpose_function='Adds effort, force and material detail to movement, suggesting a tight or strained garment as a character reaches, grips or strains.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cloth Settle','Clothing Movement','Cloth Pass','Costume Movement','Contact Detail']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding strain to a tight jacket as a character reaches','Detailing a rope-bound character straining','Covering a stretched garment in a struggle']::text[] WHERE id='f61fa647-793b-41d7-897e-eacdc3636d4c';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley moves category', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making cloth too loud or busy for the scene','Using the wrong fabric for the costume','Leaving cloth out and losing physical presence']::text[], definition='The Foley moves category covering the general sound of clothing in motion, such as the rustle, friction and swish of everyday garments as characters move.', plain_english='This is the sound of clothes moving. It covers rustle and swish. It happens whenever a dressed character moves. It is a core part of Foley.', practical_application='The Foley artist wears or handles matching clothing and moves with the characters, recording the general cloth sound across the scene.', purpose_function='Grounds characters physically and fills the natural movement sound the production track often misses, on tracks the mixer can control.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cloth Pass','Costume Movement','Coat Movement','Accessory Movement','Cloth Settle']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Covering general clothing sound in a walking scene','Adding movement to a seated group','Matching everyday clothes in a street scene']::text[] WHERE id='d80a7347-8218-4830-bff0-34c735904670';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley moves subcategory', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using a light fabric for a heavy coat','Making coat movement constant when the character is still','Ignoring the coat''s buttons, buckles and accessories']::text[], definition='A Foley moves cue covering the sound of a coat in motion, capturing the heavier rustle, swish and friction of an overcoat or jacket as a character moves.', plain_english='This is the sound of a coat moving. Coats are heavier than shirts. They make a fuller rustle and swish. Artists match the coat on screen.', practical_application='The Foley artist moves a matching coat in sync with the character, capturing the heavier swish as they walk, turn or sit.', purpose_function='Adds the weight and texture of outerwear to a character''s movement, reinforcing season, formality or bulk.', related_terms=ARRAY['Clothing Movement','Costume Movement','Cloth Pass','Accessory Movement','Cloth Settle']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Covering a detective''s trench coat as he walks','Adding a heavy coat swish in a winter scene','Matching a cape or long coat turning']::text[] WHERE id='0940c0a4-14e1-467b-a900-09e4fe103a57';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley performance detail', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Focusing on volume while ignoring the texture of the contact','Using a flat contact that does not match the surface','Missing the small scuffs and details that make contact real']::text[], definition='The fine sound detail produced at the exact moment two surfaces or objects meet, such as the specific texture of a footfall''s contact, a hand touching a surface or an object being set down.', plain_english='This is the small sound where things touch. Think a foot meeting the floor. Or a hand landing on a table. Artists capture this fine detail for realism.', practical_application='When performing a footstep, the Foley artist controls how the foot rolls and lands so the contact texture of heel, ball and scuff matches the surface and shot.', purpose_function='Sells the physical reality of an action by capturing the precise texture and weight of the moment surfaces meet, which the audience feels more than notices.', related_terms=ARRAY['Synchronized Foley','Boot Footstep','Prop Foley','Effect Sync Point','Cadence Matching']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Detailing how a foot lands on a creaky floorboard','Capturing a hand pressing on a wooden table','Syncing the precise touch of a cup on a counter']::text[] WHERE id='0afbf0e8-4935-47f6-809d-9b96f65248a2';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Effects recording method', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming a controlled space is silent without checking for HVAC and floor noise','Recording too few takes and variations to cover the edit','Losing natural character by over-controlling the performance']::text[], definition='Recording sound effects under controlled conditions, such as a studio, stage or quiet isolated space, so the performer can manage noise, mic, level and repeatability, as opposed to capturing them wild in the field.', plain_english='This means recording effects in a controlled space. You manage noise, mic, and level. You can repeat takes cleanly. It differs from recording out in the field.', practical_application='To record a specific gun handling effect, the recordist works on a quiet stage, controls mic distance and does several clean takes free of traffic and wind.', purpose_function='Produces clean, isolated, repeatable effects with consistent perspective and no unwanted background, giving editors flexible, high-quality elements.', related_terms=ARRAY['Wild Foley','Field Recording','Prop Foley','Synchronized Foley','Effects Continuity']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording clean prop handling on a quiet stage','Capturing repeatable impacts for a fight scene','Building an isolated effect free of location noise']::text[] WHERE id='bad1812e-be0f-46b5-ba31-295b1d4eb1c9';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley moves subcategory', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using modern fabrics that sound wrong for a period costume','Ignoring the layers and accessories a costume includes','Making costume movement too loud for the scene']::text[], definition='A Foley moves cue covering the sound of a character''s costume in motion, matched to the specific garments, materials and period of that costume rather than generic clothing.', plain_english='This is the sound of a costume moving. It is matched to the exact outfit. Period and special costumes need special fabrics. Artists choose materials to match.', practical_application='For a period drama, the Foley artist uses matching heavy fabrics and layers so the costume''s movement sounds true to the era, not like modern clothes.', purpose_function='Reinforces character, period and world through costume-specific movement sound, from leather and armor to silk and heavy robes.', related_terms=ARRAY['Clothing Movement','Coat Movement','Accessory Movement','Armor Movement','Cloth Pass']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Matching heavy robes in a historical epic','Covering a leather-clad character''s movement','Adding period-correct cloth to a costume drama']::text[] WHERE id='e9c739de-85ec-43bd-b09b-0e36c862ec00';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound effects editorial', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing cut effects with continuous background or atmosphere tracks','Placing the effect a frame off the action it marks','Using a sound whose perspective does not match the shot']::text[], definition='A hard sound effect edited in to synchronize with a specific on-screen action or event, sometimes called a spot effect that is cut to picture, as distinct from continuous background or atmosphere tracks.', plain_english='This is a sharp effect cut to a picture moment. It marks a specific action. It is not a steady background sound. Editors place it to hit the action.', practical_application='The sound editor cuts a door-slam or gunshot effect to land exactly on the picture event, placing it as a hard effect rather than part of the background.', purpose_function='Marks and reinforces specific on-screen events with precise, punctuating sounds, separate from the continuous ambience beds.', related_terms=ARRAY['Effect Sync Point','Effects Continuity','Hard Effect','Background','Cut-to-Cut Matching']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Cutting a gunshot to the exact frame of fire','Placing a door slam on the picture event','Syncing a hard impact in an action beat']::text[] WHERE id='f62e70d3-a9fc-4510-9283-94e15bc6b681';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound editorial continuity', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Letting ambience jump in level or tone at every cut','Ignoring how perspective should change with the shot','Matching content but not level across the edit']::text[], definition='Ensuring that effects, ambience and Foley match believably across a picture cut, so levels, perspective and content stay continuous or shift naturally as the shot changes.', plain_english='This means matching sound across a picture cut. The sound should stay smooth or shift naturally. Levels and perspective must fit each shot. It keeps a scene seamless.', practical_application='Across a shot change, the editor matches ambience and adjusts effect perspective so the sound feels continuous even though the camera angle changed.', purpose_function='Preserves the audience''s sense of a continuous space across edits, preventing jarring jumps in sound when the picture cuts.', related_terms=ARRAY['Effects Continuity','Cut Effect','Effect Sync Point','Character Matching','Effect Tail Extension']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Keeping room tone smooth across a dialogue edit','Matching traffic ambience across street shots','Shifting effect perspective naturally on a cut to close-up']::text[] WHERE id='151135e3-6533-4ea1-8c33-ee0df9ef3f47';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound effects editing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Extending past the available handle into silence or a glitch','Adding a fade so long the effect loses its attack','Forgetting that extending the head can uncover unwanted recorded noise']::text[], definition='Extending the start (head) of a sound-effect clip by revealing recorded material that lies before its in-point, using the clip''s handles to create room for a fade-in or to reach an earlier sync point.', plain_english='This means pulling the start of an effect earlier. You reveal recorded audio before the clip''s edit point. It makes room for a fade-in. Editors use the clip''s handle. A handle is the extra recorded audio kept past the edit point.', practical_application='To ease in a rising engine effect, the editor drags the clip''s head left into its handle, exposing more recorded audio and adding a fade so it enters smoothly.', purpose_function='Provides material to build a smooth fade-in or to move an effect''s start earlier, avoiding an abrupt entrance and helping the effect align with picture.', related_terms=ARRAY['Effect Tail Extension','Handle','Effect Sync Point','Crossfade','Effects Continuity']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Building a fade-in on a sustained effect','Moving an effect''s start earlier to catch a sync point','Smoothing an abrupt effect entrance']::text[] WHERE id='00d076f4-b6e5-4a7c-bb09-444fdadbfeb5';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound editorial reference', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Syncing to the visual start of a motion instead of the true impact frame','Ignoring that an effect''s attack may sit slightly inside the file','Placing the file''s start on the frame rather than its impact']::text[], definition='The specific picture frame at which a sound effect must line up with the on-screen action it accompanies, serving as the hit point the editor uses to place and sync the effect.', plain_english='This is the frame where an effect must land. It marks the exact on-screen action. Editors line the effect up to this point. It anchors the effect to picture.', practical_application='The editor marks the frame a fist lands, then slides the punch effect so its impact sits exactly on that sync point.', purpose_function='Gives the editor a precise anchor for placing effects so sound and picture events coincide, which the audience reads as real.', related_terms=ARRAY['Synchronized Foley','Cut Effect','Effect Head Extension','Effect Tail Extension','Cadence Matching']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Landing a punch effect on the frame of contact','Syncing an explosion to the visual flash','Placing a footstep effect on the foot''s contact frame']::text[] WHERE id='5adb7f59-88b9-4dd5-909a-6d7e39a436f2';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound effects editing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Extending past the handle into silence, noise or the next take','Cutting the tail too short and losing a natural decay','Adding a fade that removes the effect''s natural ring-out']::text[], definition='Extending the end (tail) of a sound-effect clip by revealing recorded material past its out-point, using the clip''s handles to create room for a fade-out or to let the sound ring out naturally.', plain_english='This means pulling the end of an effect later. You reveal recorded audio past the clip''s edit point. It makes room for a fade-out. It lets the sound ring out.', practical_application='To let a gunshot ring out, the editor drags the clip''s tail right into its handle, exposing the recorded decay and adding a fade so it ends naturally.', purpose_function='Provides material to build a natural decay or fade-out, avoiding an abrupt cut-off and letting effects like impacts or engines die away smoothly.', related_terms=ARRAY['Effect Head Extension','Handle','Effects Continuity','Crossfade','Cut-to-Cut Matching']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Letting an explosion decay naturally into a scene','Extending a reverberant tail on an impact','Smoothing an effect''s ending into the next shot']::text[] WHERE id='4a7cf575-0d2d-47a2-a46f-0abd9794e0a6';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound editorial continuity', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Letting ambience change level or tone between shots of the same scene','Forgetting continuity of effects that carry across a cut','Matching one element while others jump']::text[], definition='The consistency of sound effects and ambience across a scene or reel, so that content, level, texture and perspective stay believable and unbroken from shot to shot and cut to cut.', plain_english='This means keeping effects consistent across a scene. Sounds should not jump between shots. Levels, tone, and space must match. It keeps the world believable.', practical_application='The editor keeps a scene''s background traffic and room tone steady across every shot, matching level and tone so the location feels like one continuous space.', purpose_function='Maintains a stable, believable sound world across edits and shots, preventing distracting jumps in ambience, effects or perspective.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cut-to-Cut Matching','Cut Effect','Effect Tail Extension','Effect Sync Point','Controlled Effects Recording']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Keeping room tone consistent across a dialogue scene','Matching exterior ambience across a sequence','Ensuring a running effect carries smoothly across cuts']::text[] WHERE id='adfcd7cd-e408-4591-8e14-4c599bc04977';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - moves/contact', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making the contact so loud it distracts from dialogue','Ignoring the surface material so the sound does not match the shot','Recording it out of sync with the visible movement']::text[], definition='A Foley moves sound created when a performer''s elbow touches or presses against a surface such as a table, wall, or armrest, adding a subtle body-contact detail synchronized to on-screen action.', plain_english='This is the small sound an elbow makes touching a surface. A Foley artist makes it to match the picture. It fills in body movement the camera shows.', practical_application='A Foley artist watches a shot of someone leaning on a bar, then taps their own elbow on a matching surface in sync while recording.', purpose_function='Adds realism and physical presence to characters who lean or rest, filling gaps where production sound has no usable body-contact detail.', related_terms=ARRAY['Knee Contact','Hand Plant','Moves Pass','Handling Detail','Footstep Body']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A character leans an elbow on a bar during quiet dialogue','Someone props an elbow on a desk while thinking','A fighter''s elbow presses against a mat on the ground']::text[] WHERE id='c505eed0-55b8-49d2-8b90-d02d9f3e3a68';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - moves (cloth)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using cloth heavier or lighter than the on-screen garment','Overplaying the flap so it feels unrealistic','Forgetting to match the wind or speed shown in the shot']::text[], definition='A Foley cloth (moves) sound of loose fabric flapping through the air, such as a coat, cape, or sheet caught by fast movement or wind, recorded to match on-screen garment motion.', plain_english='This is the flapping sound loose cloth makes when it moves fast. Think of a coat or cape in the wind. A Foley artist waves fabric to make it.', practical_application='The Foley artist snaps or waves a piece of similar cloth near the mic in time with a billowing coat on screen.', purpose_function='Gives weight and energy to fast garment motion and helps sell wind, running, or dramatic capes when production audio lacks the movement.', related_terms=ARRAY['Fabric Swish','Fabric Snap','Garment Rustle','Moves Pass','Material Character']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A superhero''s cape billows as they land','A long coat flaps while a character runs','Bed sheets snap as they are shaken out']::text[] WHERE id='d2a7f4cf-a555-4bd2-8326-1bac7f1a64dc';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - moves (cloth)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording friction so loud it masks dialogue','Using the wrong fabric weight for the costume','Playing constant friction where the character is still']::text[], definition='A continuous Foley cloth sound produced by fabric rubbing against itself or another surface, giving low-level movement texture beneath a character''s body motion.', plain_english='This is the soft rubbing sound cloth makes when it moves. It happens when clothes brush against skin or each other. Foley artists add it under body movement.', practical_application='While watching a walking or gesturing character, the Foley artist rubs matching fabric near the microphone to lay a bed of movement.', purpose_function='Provides the constant, subtle presence of clothing that keeps a character feeling physically real between larger sounds.', related_terms=ARRAY['Garment Rustle','Fabric Swish','Moves Pass','Material Character','Leather Movement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A detective shifts in a wool coat during questioning','Someone crosses their arms in a jacket','A character adjusts clothing while seated']::text[] WHERE id='6d519d6d-2b39-4662-b732-7b6761a42ee0';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - moves (cloth)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a fabric snap with a softer fabric swish','Making it too harsh for the garment shown','Placing the snap slightly off the visible motion']::text[], definition='A short, sharp Foley cloth sound made when fabric is pulled taut or snapped quickly, accenting abrupt garment movements or the flick of cloth.', plain_english='This is the quick snapping sound of cloth pulled tight fast. It is sharper than a soft rustle. Foley artists use it for fast clothing moves.', practical_application='The artist snaps a cloth like a whip or yanks it tight in sync with a character whipping off a jacket.', purpose_function='Adds a crisp accent to sudden clothing actions, making fast moves feel snappy and defined.', related_terms=ARRAY['Fabric Flap','Fabric Swish','Garment Rustle','Moves Pass','Fabric Friction']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A character whips a towel','Someone yanks a bedsheet tight','A cape snaps as a hero turns sharply']::text[] WHERE id='f83af796-1cb3-47a1-8504-63f54bab7c37';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - moves (cloth)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using stiff fabric for a soft, flowing garment','Overdoing the swish so every step sounds windy','Not matching the swish speed to the on-screen motion']::text[], definition='A soft, airy Foley cloth sound of fabric sweeping through the air, typically accompanying arm swings, turns, or walking in loose clothing.', plain_english='This is the light swishing sound clothes make when they swing. You hear it when arms or a skirt move. Foley artists sweep cloth to make it.', practical_application='The Foley artist sweeps a length of similar fabric past the mic in time with swinging arms or a turning dress.', purpose_function='Sells the motion of flowing garments and limbs, adding grace and continuity to movement.', related_terms=ARRAY['Fabric Flap','Garment Rustle','Fabric Friction','Moves Pass','Fabric Snap']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A dancer''s dress swishes with a turn','A robe sweeps as a character walks','Arms swing in a loose coat while striding']::text[] WHERE id='0af17b9a-4481-4dd2-b477-8d8a456406b9';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - workflow (feet)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording feet, cloth, and props together so they cannot be separated','Wearing shoes that do not match the character','Ignoring the surface the character walks on']::text[], definition='A dedicated Foley recording pass in which the artist performs only footsteps for a scene, isolating feet as one of the three main Foley elements so they can be edited and mixed separately.', plain_english='This is a recording take that covers only footsteps. Foley is split into feet, moves, and props. Doing feet alone keeps them easy to edit.', practical_application='The artist walks in matched shoes on the right surface while watching the scene, recording a full pass of just footsteps before moving to cloth or props.', purpose_function='Keeps footsteps on their own tracks for clean editing, level control, and international mixing needs.', related_terms=ARRAY['Moves Pass','Footwear Match','Gait Matching','Heel-Toe Articulation','Hard-Sole Footstep']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording all footsteps for a walk-and-talk scene','Laying feet before adding cloth movement','Replacing noisy production footsteps for an international mix']::text[] WHERE id='b132b707-052f-4fe1-991b-e59d4503633d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - feet', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making a clean footstep where a drag is needed','Dragging on the wrong surface texture','Overusing drags so the walk feels unnatural']::text[], definition='A Foley footstep variation in which the foot is pulled or scraped along the ground rather than lifted cleanly, producing a dragging sound used for tired, injured, or shuffling characters.', plain_english='This is the sound of a foot sliding along the floor. The foot is dragged, not lifted. It suits tired or hurt characters.', practical_application='The artist scrapes the shoe across the recording surface in sync with a character who limps or trudges.', purpose_function='Conveys weakness, exhaustion, injury, or a heavy mood through the way feet move.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foot Scuff','Foot Shuffle','Limp Performance','Gait Matching','Feet Pass']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['An exhausted survivor trudges down a hall','A wounded character drags one leg','A sleepy person shuffles to the kitchen']::text[] WHERE id='5e1d98dc-aad2-460e-a448-4361fe2e31af';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - feet', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Stamping the whole foot flat so the step sounds fake','Rolling too slowly for a brisk walk','Ignoring the roll on hard-sole shoes where it is clear']::text[], definition='The rolling motion of the foot from heel to toe within a single footstep, which the Foley artist reproduces so each step has a natural weight shift rather than a flat, single impact.', plain_english='This is how the foot rolls from heel to toe in one step. It makes a step sound smooth, not flat. Foley artists copy this motion.', practical_application='The artist plants the heel first, then rolls onto the toe while recording, matching the actor''s stride.', purpose_function='Gives footsteps a realistic two-part contact and natural weight, avoiding a stiff, stamped sound.', related_terms=ARRAY['Heel-Toe Articulation','Footstep Body','Gait Matching','Feet Pass','Heel Click']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A slow, deliberate walk across a wood floor','Matching a confident stride in dress shoes','Recreating a natural heel-to-toe pace']::text[] WHERE id='fd2d57de-267d-4fa4-93c8-70a488a7ae63';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - feet', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a scuff with a full footstep','Scuffing on a surface that does not match the shot','Adding scuffs where the feet are clearly still']::text[], definition='A brief scraping Foley footstep sound made when the sole grazes the ground, used for scuffing, pivoting, or careless steps.', plain_english='This is the scraping sound a shoe makes on the ground. It happens with a careless or turning step. Foley artists scrape the sole to make it.', practical_application='The artist grazes the shoe sideways on the surface in time with a character shuffling or pivoting.', purpose_function='Adds texture and casualness to footsteps and marks turns, stumbles, or restless feet.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foot Drag','Foot Shuffle','Foot Roll','Feet Pass','Hard-Sole Footstep']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A bored teen scuffs their shoes','A character pivots sharply on concrete','Nervous feet shift under a table']::text[] WHERE id='8c84f459-6fbc-49b0-951c-e65565c61b55';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - feet', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Lifting the feet fully when a low shuffle is needed','Shuffling at the wrong speed for the scene','Using a shuffle where a clean stride fits better']::text[], definition='A Foley footstep pattern of small, quick, low-lifted steps that slide slightly, used for hesitant, crowded, aged, or lazy movement.', plain_english='This is the sound of small, sliding steps. The feet barely lift off the floor. It suits slow, shy, or crowded walking.', practical_application='The artist takes short sliding steps on the surface, matching the on-screen pace and feel.', purpose_function='Communicates hesitation, laziness, age, or shuffling crowds through footstep rhythm.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foot Drag','Foot Scuff','Gait Matching','Limp Performance','Feet Pass']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['An elderly character shuffles across a room','A crowd shuffles forward in a line','A tired worker drags toward the exit']::text[] WHERE id='6b21ef78-f1d2-4921-a252-679091393ce3';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound descriptor - feet', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Cutting off the body so steps sound thin','Confusing the body with the sharp transient','Ignoring the surface that shapes the body']::text[], definition='The main, sustaining portion of a footstep sound that carries its weight and mass, sitting between the sharp initial transient and the fading tail.', plain_english='This is the main part of a footstep sound. It carries the weight of the step. It comes after the first sharp hit.', practical_application='When editing or performing, the artist shapes the body so a heavy boot sounds fuller than a light shoe.', purpose_function='Communicates how heavy the person is and what surface they walk on, giving the step its fullness.', related_terms=ARRAY['Footstep Transient','Footstep Tail','Foot Roll','Hard-Sole Footstep','Material Character']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A heavy boot lands on a wooden stage','Editing a step to feel weightier','Matching footstep weight to a large character']::text[] WHERE id='46170948-1c2e-4141-91e7-39037b373fd1';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound descriptor - feet', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Chopping the tail so steps sound dead','Leaving long tails that muddy quick walking','Adding a big tail in a small, dry room']::text[], definition='The trailing, decaying part of a footstep sound after the main impact, including surface resonance or debris, that lets a step ring out naturally.', plain_english='This is the fading end of a footstep sound. It is what you hear after the main hit. It can include a surface ring or grit.', practical_application='The artist keeps or trims the tail so steps in a large hall ring longer than steps in a small room.', purpose_function='Adds realism and a sense of space, showing the room and surface as a step settles.', related_terms=ARRAY['Footstep Body','Footstep Transient','Material Character','Foot Roll','Feet Pass']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Steps echo in a marble lobby','Trimming tails for a fast run','Gravel grit trails after each step']::text[] WHERE id='41b89ff4-0f12-4188-856b-f336eaabb50f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound descriptor - feet', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Softening the transient so steps lose sync clarity','Over-compressing and killing the natural attack','Letting transients drift off the picture']::text[], definition='The sharp initial attack of a footstep sound, the brief high-energy spike where the foot first strikes the surface, which defines the step''s clarity and impact.', plain_english='This is the sharp start of a footstep sound. It is the quick hit when the foot lands. It gives the step its punch.', practical_application='The artist keeps transients clean and in sync so each step reads clearly against dialogue and music.', purpose_function='Sets the timing and crispness of a step and helps it cut through a busy mix.', related_terms=ARRAY['Footstep Body','Footstep Tail','Heel Click','Heel-Toe Articulation','Hard-Sole Footstep']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A crisp heel strike on tile','Tightening step timing to the frame','Making footsteps punch through a loud mix']::text[] WHERE id='41fe2b23-3c0b-4f31-989b-0bc08fcfb4d8';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - technique (feet)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using stiff new shoes that squeak on the take','Assuming any shoe will sound natural right away','Ignoring how a worn sole changes the sound']::text[], definition='The practice of wearing and working shoes until they lose stiffness and squeaks, so their footstep sounds are natural and controllable for Foley.', plain_english='This means wearing shoes until they soften up. New shoes can squeak or sound stiff. Broken-in shoes make cleaner footstep sounds.', practical_application='A Foley artist keeps a collection of broken-in shoes and flexes or wears new pairs before using them on a pass.', purpose_function='Removes unwanted squeaks and gives predictable, natural footsteps.', related_terms=ARRAY['Footwear Match','Feet Pass','Hard-Sole Footstep','Leather-Sole Footstep','Gait Matching']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Prepping dress shoes before a formal scene','Silencing a squeak in new leather soles','Building a shoe kit for varied characters']::text[] WHERE id='409e0ef0-12b7-4577-a7f2-dc35734ba3a8';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - technique (feet)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using sneakers for a character in hard-soled boots','Ignoring the walking surface when choosing shoes','Matching the shoe but not the character''s weight']::text[], definition='Selecting shoes whose type, sole, and weight match the on-screen character and action, so Foley footsteps sound believable for that person and surface.', plain_english='This means picking shoes that fit the character on screen. A cowboy needs boots, not sneakers. The right shoe makes the steps believable.', practical_application='Before a feet pass, the artist chooses boots, heels, or sneakers to match the actor''s footwear and the floor they walk on.', purpose_function='Ensures footstep sounds match what the audience sees, keeping the illusion intact.', related_terms=ARRAY['Footwear Break-In','Feet Pass','Hard-Sole Footstep','High-Heel Footstep','Leather-Sole Footstep']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Choosing heels for a woman in a gown','Selecting work boots for a construction scene','Matching sandals for a beach walk']::text[] WHERE id='7c75417d-5d49-4d08-8adc-28ccb0e43c76';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Choosing a prop that looks right but sounds wrong','Overlooking common objects that sound perfect','Not testing the prop before recording']::text[], definition='An everyday or improvised object pressed into service as a Foley prop because it makes a useful sound, rather than being the exact item shown on screen.', plain_english='This is a random object used to make a sound. It is not the real item on screen. Foley artists grab whatever sounds right.', practical_application='The artist might crush a box or shake keys to voice an on-screen action, choosing by sound rather than appearance.', purpose_function='Lets artists build convincing sounds cheaply and creatively from objects at hand.', related_terms=ARRAY['Hand Prop','Hero Prop','Material Substitution','Handling Detail','Grip Sound']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Using cornstarch in a bag for snow crunch','Shaking a chain for a machine sound','Grabbing a stapler to voice a mechanism']::text[] WHERE id='a9b0695c-acf1-48ff-9949-df21c4edf13e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - performance (feet)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Watching only the feet instead of feeling the rhythm','Missing a limp or uneven stride','Letting steps drift out of sync over a long walk']::text[], definition='The Foley skill of matching the rhythm, weight, and timing of one''s own footsteps to the walk of the on-screen actor, so the steps land exactly with the picture.', plain_english='This means walking in time with the actor on screen. You copy their speed and weight. The steps must land with the picture.', practical_application='The artist studies the actor''s stride, then walks in place or across the pit to lock each footfall to the frame.', purpose_function='Keeps footsteps in perfect sync and true to the character''s movement and mood.', related_terms=ARRAY['Feet Pass','Heel-Toe Articulation','Limp Performance','Foot Roll','Footwear Match']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Matching a brisk, confident walk','Following an actor''s uneven limp','Syncing steps for a slow, sneaking approach']::text[] WHERE id='bf63c5e9-6320-4e06-be04-ed8f30a29f26';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - moves (cloth)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording rustle louder than real clothing','Using the wrong fabric for the costume','Adding rustle while the character is perfectly still']::text[], definition='The soft rustling Foley sound of clothing shifting with a character''s body movement, forming the core of the cloth or moves layer.', plain_english='This is the soft rustle clothes make as a body moves. It is the main cloth sound in Foley. It keeps characters feeling real.', practical_application='The artist handles or wears matching garments, moving them near the mic in sync with the actor''s gestures and walk.', purpose_function='Grounds characters physically by giving their every movement a quiet clothing presence.', related_terms=ARRAY['Fabric Friction','Fabric Swish','Moves Pass','Leather Movement','Material Character']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A coat rustles as a detective sits','Clothing shifts during a hug','A jacket moves with each arm swing']::text[] WHERE id='da1609c3-a761-4f41-8a53-cbb909095c9a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - moves (props)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making gear sound heavier or lighter than it looks','Forgetting metal clinks on tactical gear','Playing gear movement when the character is still']::text[], definition='A Foley moves sound of worn equipment shifting on a character, such as a backpack, tool belt, holster, or tactical gear, adding weight and function to their motion.', plain_english='This is the sound of gear moving on a person. Think of a backpack or tool belt. It shifts and clinks as they move.', practical_application='The artist moves a similar pack, belt, or strap set in sync with a soldier or hiker walking on screen.', purpose_function='Signals that a character carries equipment, adding believability and character detail.', related_terms=ARRAY['Leather Movement','Handling Detail','Moves Pass','Garment Rustle','Material Character']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A soldier''s webbing shifts as they crouch','A hiker''s backpack creaks up a trail','A duty belt clinks as an officer walks']::text[] WHERE id='1449af56-373f-49a9-967b-38453340e698';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - props/handling', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing crew ''grip'' with the Foley grip sound','Making the grip louder than the moment needs','Using a material that does not match the object']::text[], definition='A Foley detail capturing the sound of a hand grasping or tightening on an object or surface, such as leather, metal, or fabric under a firm hold.', plain_english='This is the small sound a hand makes when it grips something. You may hear leather or metal squeeze. Foley artists add it for close handling shots.', practical_application='The artist squeezes a matching material near the mic as a character grips a railing, weapon, or handle.', purpose_function='Adds tactile realism to handling, showing the effort and material of a grasp.', related_terms=ARRAY['Handling Detail','Hand Prop','Leather Movement','Hand Plant','Material Character']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A hand tightens on a leather steering wheel','Fingers grip a metal railing','A fist closes around a rope']::text[] WHERE id='416122c8-31bb-41c0-9ea0-662c9b9f56c4';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - moves/contact', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making the plant too soft to register the effort','Using a surface that does not match the shot','Missing the exact frame of contact']::text[], definition='A Foley contact sound of a hand being placed firmly on a surface, as when a character braces, vaults, pushes up, or slaps a hand down.', plain_english='This is the sound of a hand pressed down on a surface. It happens when someone braces or pushes up. Foley artists slap a hand to make it.', practical_application='The artist plants their own hand on a matching surface in sync with a character bracing or getting up.', purpose_function='Marks moments of contact and effort, adding physical impact to hand movements.', related_terms=ARRAY['Knee Contact','Elbow Contact','Handling Detail','Grip Sound','Footstep Body']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A runner plants a hand to vault a wall','A character pushes up off the floor','A palm slaps onto a table in anger']::text[] WHERE id='8b59c317-8733-4472-aa14-d32157c9710f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Ignoring small handling sounds of held objects','Using a prop that sounds unlike the on-screen item','Recording props mixed in with footsteps']::text[], definition='A prop that a character holds and manipulates on screen, whose handling sounds are voiced in the Foley props pass, such as a cup, phone, weapon, or tool.', plain_english='This is an object a character holds and uses. Think of a cup, phone, or gun. Foley artists record how it sounds when handled.', practical_application='In a props pass, the artist picks up and uses a matching object in sync with the character on screen.', purpose_function='Provides the specific handling sounds of objects characters touch, key to a believable props layer.', related_terms=ARRAY['Hero Prop','Found Prop','Handling Detail','Grip Sound','Material Substitution']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A detective sets down a coffee mug','A soldier handles a rifle','A character taps and pockets a phone']::text[] WHERE id='6bf0cb52-ed4e-4ac6-864d-7d017ddd6881';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - props/handling', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Skipping small handling sounds in close-ups','Making handling louder than the moment warrants','Using materials that do not match the prop']::text[], definition='The fine Foley sounds of fingers and hands touching, adjusting, or moving objects, adding intimate texture to how characters interact with props.', plain_english='These are the tiny sounds of hands touching objects. Think of fingers on a page or a cup. They make handling feel real and close.', practical_application='The artist performs small finger and hand movements on a matching object, close to the mic, in sync with the action.', purpose_function='Brings close-up realism to prop interactions, especially in quiet or intimate shots.', related_terms=ARRAY['Hand Prop','Grip Sound','Found Prop','Material Character','Hero Prop']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Fingers turn the pages of a book','A hand adjusts a necklace','Someone fidgets with a ring']::text[] WHERE id='82e86473-ec76-4dc8-be7d-4c3fc6cbc608';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - feet', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using a soft surface that dulls the hard sole','Choosing hard soles for a casual, quiet character','Ignoring the ring and tail on hard floors']::text[], definition='A footstep made by shoes with hard soles, such as dress shoes or boots, producing a crisp, resonant step often used for formal or authoritative characters.', plain_english='This is a footstep from a hard-soled shoe. Think of dress shoes or hard boots. The steps sound sharp and clear.', practical_application='The artist wears hard-soled shoes on a matching hard surface to record crisp, ringing steps.', purpose_function='Gives footsteps a clear, defined sound that suits formal, confident, or heavy characters.', related_terms=ARRAY['Leather-Sole Footstep','High-Heel Footstep','Heel Click','Footwear Match','Feet Pass']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A businessman crosses a marble lobby','An officer''s boots sound down a hall','Dress shoes click on a wood stage']::text[] WHERE id='1e08886e-a5a1-43ae-afc9-c87277a9a074';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - feet', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Producing a heel click on a soft or carpeted surface','Making every step click when only some should','Losing sync between the click and the picture']::text[], definition='The sharp clicking Foley sound of a hard heel striking a firm surface, characteristic of dress shoes and heels on tile, wood, or stone.', plain_english='This is the sharp click a hard heel makes on the floor. You hear it with dress shoes or heels. It sounds crisp on hard surfaces.', practical_application='The artist strikes the heel first on a hard surface, letting it click before the toe lands.', purpose_function='Adds a crisp accent to footsteps and can signal confidence, formality, or tension.', related_terms=ARRAY['High-Heel Footstep','Hard-Sole Footstep','Heel-Toe Articulation','Footstep Transient','Leather-Sole Footstep']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Heels click down a quiet corridor','A guard''s boots click to attention','Dress shoes click across a ballroom']::text[] WHERE id='b5ea167c-8819-41c2-9a66-744a2374b3dd';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - performance (feet)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Stamping the foot flat and losing the two-part feel','Reversing the natural heel-then-toe order','Ignoring articulation on soft shoes where it still matters']::text[], definition='The technique of performing a footstep in two parts, the heel strike followed by the toe, so each step has natural weight, timing, and realism.', plain_english='This means making a footstep in two hits: heel then toe. It copies how people really walk. It makes steps sound natural.', practical_application='The artist lands the heel, then rolls to the toe in sync with the actor''s stride during a feet pass.', purpose_function='Produces lifelike footsteps with a proper weight shift instead of flat, single impacts.', related_terms=ARRAY['Foot Roll','Heel Click','Gait Matching','Footstep Transient','Feet Pass']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A measured walk on a hardwood floor','Matching a slow, deliberate stride','Recording realistic footsteps for a close shot']::text[] WHERE id='b1310325-a890-4a1a-a19f-b20853747740';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Props terminology', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a hero prop with a stunt or background copy','Assuming the hero prop always makes the on-set sound','Ignoring the prop''s real material when creating Foley']::text[], definition='In production terms, the highest-detail, fully functional version of a prop made for close-up use, as opposed to stunt or background duplicates; its material and action often drive the Foley props work.', plain_english='This is the best, most detailed version of a prop. It is used for close-up shots. Cheaper copies are used for stunts or background.', practical_application='When a hero prop features in close-ups, the Foley artist studies its material and action to voice its handling accurately.', purpose_function='Provides a screen-accurate prop for close scrutiny, and it is usually the one whose sounds the Foley team must match.', related_terms=ARRAY['Hand Prop','Found Prop','Handling Detail','Material Substitution','Grip Sound']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A detailed pistol used in a close-up','A featured pocket watch a character opens','A hero sword shown in fine detail']::text[] WHERE id='2e4aebfd-9988-4d91-83f0-4009c647c2be';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - feet', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording heels on a surface that hides their sharpness','Missing the narrow, distinct heel strike','Using a flat-shoe rhythm for a heel walk']::text[], definition='A footstep produced by high-heeled shoes, marked by a sharp, narrow heel strike and a distinct two-point contact, used to characterize the walk of a character in heels.', plain_english='This is a footstep from high-heeled shoes. The thin heel makes a sharp, clear hit. It has a special two-part sound.', practical_application='The artist wears or handles high heels on a hard surface, striking the heel and toe to match the character''s stride.', purpose_function='Defines a distinctive walk and can convey elegance, confidence, or tension.', related_terms=ARRAY['Heel Click','Hard-Sole Footstep','Footwear Match','Gait Matching','Leather-Sole Footstep']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A woman crosses a marble floor in heels','Tense heels approach in a thriller','Elegant steps enter a party scene']::text[] WHERE id='eb28a26e-3b4b-425e-9231-6c070dfa898c';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - moves/contact', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making the knee contact too light for a hard fall','Using a surface unlike the one on screen','Missing the frame where the knee lands']::text[], definition='A Foley contact sound of a knee touching or dropping onto a surface, as when a character kneels, crawls, or falls to their knees.', plain_english='This is the sound of a knee hitting a surface. It happens when someone kneels or falls. Foley artists press or drop to match it.', practical_application='The artist presses or lightly drops a padded object or their own knee onto a matching surface in sync.', purpose_function='Adds physical weight and realism to kneeling, crawling, and falling actions.', related_terms=ARRAY['Hand Plant','Elbow Contact','Footstep Body','Handling Detail','Gait Matching']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A soldier drops to one knee','A character crawls across a floor','Someone kneels at a graveside']::text[] WHERE id='08fdbed5-3ae4-43ad-9fcb-7f3aeeb733fd';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - moves (cloth/leather)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Overplaying creaks so leather sounds fake','Using stiff new leather for a worn item','Adding leather sounds where the costume is cloth']::text[], definition='A Foley moves sound of leather flexing and creaking, such as a jacket, holster, saddle, or boots, adding a rich, tactile texture to a character''s motion.', plain_english='This is the creaking sound leather makes as it bends. Think of a leather jacket or saddle. It adds a rich texture to movement.', practical_application='The artist flexes a leather item near the mic in sync with a character in a leather coat or on horseback.', purpose_function='Signals leather garments or gear and lends a distinctive, weighty character to movement.', related_terms=ARRAY['Gear Movement','Garment Rustle','Grip Sound','Leather-Sole Footstep','Material Character']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A biker''s leather jacket creaks','A holster flexes as a gun is drawn','A saddle creaks during a ride']::text[] WHERE id='1590d193-2ead-461e-a86e-f9708dc3bc73';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - feet', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using rubber soles for a leather-soled character','Recording on a surface that hides the slide','Ignoring the smooth pivot leather soles allow']::text[], definition='A footstep made by shoes with leather soles, giving a smooth, slightly sliding, resonant step distinct from rubber or synthetic soles.', plain_english='This is a footstep from leather-soled shoes. The steps sound smooth and can slide a little. They differ from rubber soles.', practical_application='The artist wears leather-soled shoes on a matching hard floor to capture their smooth, sliding footsteps.', purpose_function='Provides the characteristic sound of leather-soled dress shoes, useful for period or formal characters.', related_terms=ARRAY['Hard-Sole Footstep','Heel Click','Footwear Match','High-Heel Footstep','Leather Movement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A gentleman walks in period dress shoes','Leather soles slide on a polished floor','Formal steps cross a ballroom']::text[] WHERE id='b27ab85f-2655-4405-850c-8349d80ea8eb';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - performance (feet)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making both feet sound identical for a limping character','Placing the limp on the wrong foot','Keeping an even rhythm where it should be uneven']::text[], definition='The Foley performance of an uneven, favoring gait to match a limping character, giving one foot a different weight, rhythm, or drag than the other.', plain_english='This means walking with a limp to match the actor. One foot sounds different from the other. The rhythm is uneven on purpose.', practical_application='The artist favors one leg, adding a drag or lighter step on the injured side while syncing to the picture.', purpose_function='Conveys injury, disability, or exhaustion truthfully through footstep rhythm and weight.', related_terms=ARRAY['Gait Matching','Foot Drag','Foot Shuffle','Feet Pass','Heel-Toe Articulation']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A wounded soldier limps to cover','An injured runner favors one leg','An elderly character walks with a hitch']::text[] WHERE id='80613518-51ab-49f1-aa00-6a64ead63c38';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - sound principle', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Ignoring how a material really sounds','Matching a prop by look but not by sound','Assuming similar-looking materials sound alike']::text[], definition='The distinctive sonic quality a given material lends to a Foley sound, such as the ring of metal, the dull thud of wood, or the softness of cloth, which guides prop and surface choices.', plain_english='This is the special sound a material has. Metal rings, wood thuds, cloth is soft. Foley artists pick materials by this quality.', practical_application='The artist selects objects and floors by their sonic character to match glass, metal, wood, or fabric on screen.', purpose_function='Guides the choice of props and surfaces so recorded sounds match what the material should sound like.', related_terms=ARRAY['Material Substitution','Found Prop','Footstep Body','Handling Detail','Leather Movement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Choosing metal for a ringing clang','Picking wood for a hollow knock','Selecting cloth for a soft impact']::text[] WHERE id='c35fa63d-4400-451b-ab23-defa1357eecc';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - technique (props)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Insisting on the real object when a substitute sounds better','Choosing a substitute whose sound does not match the action','Not testing several substitutes before recording']::text[], definition='The Foley technique of using a different material or object to create a sound that convincingly stands in for the on-screen source, such as snapping celery for breaking bones.', plain_english='This means using one thing to make the sound of another. A classic trick is celery for breaking bones. The stand-in often sounds better than the real thing.', practical_application='The artist tests substitutes, like snapping celery or crushing cornstarch, choosing whatever best sells the on-screen action.', purpose_function='Lets artists produce convincing or impossible sounds safely and practically when the real source is unavailable or weak.', related_terms=ARRAY['Found Prop','Material Character','Hand Prop','Hero Prop','Handling Detail']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Snapping celery for a bone break','Crushing cornstarch for snow steps','Using coconut halves for hoofbeats']::text[] WHERE id='89978941-b9be-4960-ae9c-e9f30cbb6176';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley - workflow (moves)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Mixing moves in with feet so they cannot be separated','Using fabrics unlike the on-screen costume','Overplaying cloth so it competes with dialogue']::text[], definition='A dedicated Foley recording pass covering a character''s body and clothing movement, one of the three main Foley elements alongside feet and props, kept on its own layer for editing and mixing.', plain_english='This is a recording take for body and cloth movement. Foley has three parts: feet, moves, and props. Moves are recorded on their own.', practical_application='After feet, the artist performs cloth rustles and body movements in sync, recording a full moves pass on separate tracks.', purpose_function='Isolates cloth and body movement so it can be edited, balanced, and used in international mixes.', related_terms=ARRAY['Feet Pass','Garment Rustle','Fabric Friction','Leather Movement','Gear Movement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording cloth for a walking scene','Adding body movement after the feet pass','Building the moves layer for an international mix']::text[] WHERE id='9f37aa5a-391c-4025-ad53-fc3cdb3fc84b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Moves', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording moves so loudly that cloth rustle distracts from dialogue','Confusing moves with props — moves are body and cloth, props are handled objects','Forgetting that moves fill holes when the production track is muted or replaced']::text[], definition='A Foley element made up of cloth and body-movement sounds — clothing rustle and the friction of limbs and fabric — performed and recorded separately from footsteps and props, and delivered on its own track or stem within the sound mix.', plain_english='A moves track holds cloth and body sounds in Foley. It is the rustle of clothes as a person moves. Foley artists record it apart from footsteps and props. It gives characters a sense of life.', practical_application='A Foley artist watches picture and handles matching fabrics, performing rustles in sync with each gesture; the editor keeps these on a dedicated moves track so the re-recording mixer can balance them under dialogue.', purpose_function='Provides the subtle clothing and movement sounds that make characters feel physically present, filling gaps left when production dialogue tracks are cleaned or replaced.', related_terms=ARRAY['Footsteps track','Foley stem','Specifics','Foley walker','Cloth pass']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding cloth movement to a dialogue scene shot with a boom only','Rebuilding a fight scene''s body sounds for an M&E deliverable','Giving a period costume its characteristic heavy fabric rustle']::text[] WHERE id='d3f815bf-2ba3-4b30-8e7f-c727f57f31e5';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Post Workflow — Mixing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming one pass finishes the mix — passes are layered per element','Setting music levels without checking them against dialogue intelligibility','Not saving automation before starting another pass']::text[], definition='A dedicated run through a scene or reel during which the re-recording mixer records and balances the music stem — setting music levels, fades, and automation relative to dialogue and effects.', plain_english='A music pass is one run through a scene for the music. The mixer sets music levels and fades during it. Dialogue and effects get their own passes too. This keeps each part of the mix in control.', practical_application='During re-recording, the mixer plays the reel and rides the music faders, writing automation on a music pass, then combines it with the dialogue and effects passes for the final mix.', purpose_function='Lets the mixer focus on the music balance in isolation, writing automation and fades so music supports the story without masking dialogue.', related_terms=ARRAY['Dialogue pass','Effects pass','Stem','Re-recording mix','Automation pass']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Balancing a score cue under a tense dialogue exchange','Building the music stem for a film''s final mix','Adjusting song levels in a montage during re-recording']::text[] WHERE id='63977e41-809b-44d3-9a65-53d44a0cc2b9';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Matching the sound to the real prop''s weight instead of the on-screen object''s','Using bright, thin sounds for objects that should feel heavy','Ignoring how low-frequency content signals mass']::text[], definition='The perceptual effect by which Foley and sound-design performance convince an audience of an object''s or character''s mass and weight — heavier, denser, more effortful sounds implying a heavier object — even when the item used to make the sound is light.', plain_english='Sound can make an object seem heavy or light. Foley artists shape hits and steps to suggest weight. A big low thud feels heavy to the listener. The real object making it may be small.', practical_application='A Foley artist adds a low, slow, resonant impact and a hint of strain to sell a heavy crate, or uses a light, quick tap for a hollow prop, matching the sound''s weight to the on-screen object.', purpose_function='Lets sound convey physical mass and effort that the picture alone cannot show, making actions feel believable and letting light stand-in objects represent heavy ones.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Substitution','Impact','Footsteps track','Sound design','Perceived mass']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Making a foam boulder prop sound like solid stone','Selling the weight of a heavy door closing','Giving a large creature''s footsteps a sense of mass']::text[] WHERE id='02cbf28b-0ccf-4f48-8224-bc4a5d8a5eea';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using a fist or fingertips when the action calls for a flat palm''s broader tone','Not cupping the hand to match a hollow contact sound','Slapping too hard so the contact reads as an over-strong hit']::text[], definition='A Foley performance technique in which the artist uses the flat of the hand to create body-contact and impact sounds — pats, slaps, catches, and hand-on-surface touches — matched to on-screen action.', plain_english='Palm contact means making sounds with the flat hand. Foley artists pat, slap, or press surfaces. It covers hand touches and body contact on screen. The hand shape changes the tone of the sound.', practical_application='Watching a handshake or a shoulder pat, the Foley artist claps or pats their own palm against a surface or their body in sync, cupping the hand to shape a hollow or sharp tone.', purpose_function='Reproduces the many hand-based contact sounds in a scene, from a pat on the back to catching an object, that a boom rarely captures cleanly.', related_terms=ARRAY['Body contact','Impact','Specifics','Moves Track','Slap']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Covering a pat on the back in a reunion scene','Adding the slap of a high-five','Performing a hand pressing against a door']::text[] WHERE id='b536a1b0-1fc6-4f1c-b26d-6c0f701f1b34';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Waiting to react to the picture, which lands the sound late','Over-anticipating so the sound arrives before the action','Ignoring the preparatory motion that precedes a real movement']::text[], definition='In Foley and ADR performance, the practice of preparing for and slightly leading the on-screen action — performing the small preparatory motion before a movement and timing the body so the recorded sound lands precisely on the picture''s beat.', plain_english='Anticipation means getting ready just before the action. The performer starts the motion a little early. This helps the sound hit exactly on the picture. It also adds a natural lead-in before the beat.', practical_application='A Foley walker begins shifting weight a beat before a character steps, so the footstep lands on frame; an ADR actor breathes in just before a line to match the on-screen wind-up.', purpose_function='Improves sync accuracy and realism by accounting for reaction time and the natural wind-up that precedes real movements.', related_terms=ARRAY['Performance Follow-Through','Sync','Performance Matching','ADR','Pre-roll']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Landing footsteps exactly on a character''s stride','Timing a prop pickup to the hand reaching','Matching a punch impact to the moment of contact']::text[] WHERE id='bc891edf-059b-49a5-a5cd-d3643f180006';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Cutting the sound off the instant the main action ends','Forgetting the cloth or object settle after a movement','Making the follow-through as loud as the main hit']::text[], definition='In Foley and ADR performance, the trailing motion and its sound that continue after the main action — the settle, decay, or secondary movement that completes a gesture and makes it feel physically finished.', plain_english='Follow-through is the sound after the main action. A motion does not stop all at once. Clothes settle and objects come to rest. Foley artists perform these tails to finish the movement.', practical_application='After a character sits, the Foley artist adds the small cloth settle and shift; after a door slam, the frame rattle and latch settle are performed as the follow-through.', purpose_function='Adds realism by including the secondary, trailing sounds real movements produce as they settle, avoiding an unnaturally abrupt cutoff.', related_terms=ARRAY['Performance Anticipation','Settle','Release Sound','Moves Track','Decay']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding the settle after a character drops into a chair','Performing the rattle after a door slams','Finishing a sword swing with its trailing whoosh']::text[] WHERE id='e040e932-107b-4f9d-89e6-8928612e7005';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Matching timing but missing the intensity or mood of the action','Ignoring the surface or footwear the character actually uses','Treating ADR match as sync alone, not energy and emotion']::text[], definition='The craft of matching a re-performed sound — Foley moves, footsteps, props, or ADR dialogue — to the original on-screen action in timing, intensity, rhythm, and character so the added sound reads as if it happened on set.', plain_english='Performance matching means making a new sound fit the picture. The timing, force, and feel must match the action. Foley and ADR both rely on it. Done well, the sound seems to come from the screen.', practical_application='A Foley artist matches the weight and pace of a character''s walk; an ADR mixer guides an actor to match the lip movement and energy of the original take.', purpose_function='Ensures replaced or added sounds are believable, preserving the illusion that the audio was captured with the image.', related_terms=ARRAY['ADR','Sync','Performance Anticipation','Foley walker','Lip sync']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Replacing footsteps for a scene reshot on a different floor','Matching ADR lines to an actor''s on-set delivery','Recreating a prop handling sequence in sync']::text[] WHERE id='9b51692d-ef53-466e-ad6e-e5b7333ffce5';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Post Workflow — Mixing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Allowing the pickup sections to differ in level and tone from the approved mix','Remixing more than needed and disturbing approved material','Skipping a check of the surrounding transitions']::text[], definition='A focused mixing session that integrates or revises specific sections — such as newly shot pickup scenes or fixes — into an existing mix, rather than remixing the whole program from scratch.', plain_english='A pickup mix fixes or adds small parts of a mix. It handles new pickup scenes or changes. The mixer does not redo the whole film. Only the affected sections are worked on.', practical_application='When a scene is reshot or a note requires a change, the re-recording mixer loads the affected reel, mixes just those sections, and conforms them to match the surrounding approved mix.', purpose_function='Saves time and cost by re-mixing only the changed or newly added material while keeping the approved mix intact elsewhere.', related_terms=ARRAY['Pickup Take','Re-recording mix','Reconform','Final mix','Fix']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Mixing a reshot dialogue scene into a locked reel','Addressing a director''s note on one sequence','Adding a late insert shot to the final mix']::text[] WHERE id='2ee9f92c-6e43-4863-bedf-93a17e32b788';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Post Workflow — Recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Not matching the mic, level, and tone of the original take','Failing to slate or log the pickup, causing edit confusion','Recording a pickup with different room sound than the master']::text[], definition='An additional take recorded to replace or repair a specific portion of a scene or line rather than the whole performance — capturing only the section that needs fixing for later editing into the master take.', plain_english='A pickup take fixes just part of a scene. Only the bad section is redone. The editor drops it into the main take. This saves redoing the whole performance.', practical_application='On an ADR stage or Foley session, when one line or action is off, the recordist rolls a pickup take of just that beat, which the editor edits seamlessly into the approved take.', purpose_function='Efficiently repairs a flawed moment — a fluffed word, a noise, a missed cue — without re-recording an entire usable take.', related_terms=ARRAY['Pickup mix','ADR','Take','Punch-in','Edit']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Fixing one mispronounced word in an ADR line','Re-recording a single footstep that missed sync','Capturing a clean prop hit to replace a noisy one']::text[] WHERE id='032673e6-da5b-4d20-aa55-5e0973a6b009';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Footsteps', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using a clean step where a scuffing twist is needed','Mismatching the surface so the scuff sounds wrong','Overdoing the grind so it distracts']::text[], definition='A Foley footstep in which the foot turns or twists on the spot as a character changes direction, producing a characteristic scuff or grinding accent distinct from a straight walking step.', plain_english='A pivot step is a turning footstep. The foot twists on the ground as the character turns. It makes a scuff sound, not a clean tap. Foley artists perform it to match a change of direction.', practical_application='Watching a character spin to face someone, the Foley walker plants a foot and twists it on the surface to create the pivot scuff in sync.', purpose_function='Reproduces the specific twisting scuff of a turn, adding realism that plain forward footsteps cannot convey.', related_terms=ARRAY['Plant Step','Scuff','Footsteps track','Foley walker','Surface']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A character turning sharply on a wooden floor','A dancer pivoting on stage','A guard spinning to check a noise']::text[] WHERE id='686cc038-33b8-45f7-a334-e7c75fb7c870';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Footsteps', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Performing it too lightly so the weight does not read','Missing the sync with the character''s commit point','Ignoring the surface''s contribution to the solid tone']::text[], definition='A Foley footstep performed with the foot set down firmly and the character''s full weight committed, giving a solid, grounded accent — as when a character stops, braces, or steps deliberately.', plain_english='A plant step is a firm, heavy footstep. The foot lands and the weight goes fully onto it. It sounds solid and grounded. Foley artists use it when a character stops or braces.', practical_application='For a character planting to throw a punch, the Foley walker stamps down firmly with full weight to create a solid, weighted step in sync.', purpose_function='Conveys weight, intent, and stopping power in movement, marking moments where a character commits their footing.', related_terms=ARRAY['Pivot Step','Object Weight Illusion','Footsteps track','Impact','Foley walker']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A fighter planting before a strike','A character stopping abruptly at a doorway','A soldier coming to attention']::text[] WHERE id='aeb6cc87-d025-40ff-a928-44e8cf7a485b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production Sound', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming production effects survive into the M&E — they usually must be replaced','Keeping a production effect that carries unwanted dialogue or noise','Forgetting they are often only a reference for cleaner replacements']::text[], definition='A sound effect captured within the production (location) recording during filming — such as a door slam, footstep, or gunshot occurring alongside the dialogue — that can be used or referenced in the final mix instead of being created in post.', plain_english='A production effect is a sound recorded on set during filming. It happens with the dialogue, like a real door slam. The mix can sometimes use it as is. Often it just guides the effects team.', practical_application='The production sound mixer records a scene''s live door slams and prop handling; in post, the editor may keep clean ones or use them as a guide for Foley and cut effects.', purpose_function='Provides authentic, in-the-moment effects tied to the scene, and serves as a sync and tonal reference for effects editors even when replaced.', related_terms=ARRAY['Production sound','M&E','Foley','Sound effects editing','Wild track']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Using a live door slam captured with the dialogue','Referencing on-set gunshots for cut effects','Deciding which prop sounds to replace for the music and effects (M&E)']::text[] WHERE id='05896660-ecec-466a-8b7e-70842d0aadd0';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Layering sounds that fight rather than blend','Losing sync when performing multiple objects at once','Over-layering so the result sounds cluttered']::text[], definition='A Foley technique of combining two or more objects or materials to build a single composite prop sound that no single object could produce convincingly.', plain_english='Prop combination means using several objects together. Two or more items make one blended sound. This builds sounds a single object cannot. Foley artists layer them to get the right effect.', practical_application='To build a mechanical creak-and-clunk, the Foley artist combines a leather twist with a metal hinge, performing them together or in separate layers.', purpose_function='Creates richer, more convincing, or more fantastical sounds by layering the qualities of multiple objects.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Substitution','Layering','Specifics','Prop Modification','Sound design']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Building a robot movement from several metal and cloth sounds','Creating a creature texture from layered props','Making a complex machine sound']::text[] WHERE id='71fe3833-6972-44c6-82ef-e874cc004581';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a creak with a squeak — a creak is lower and longer','Performing it out of sync with the movement','Making it too loud for the moment']::text[], definition='The creaking sound produced by flexing or stressing a prop — leather, wood, or rope — performed in Foley to accompany movement, tension, or the handling of an object on screen.', plain_english='A prop creak is a creaking sound from an object. It comes from bending leather, wood, or rope. Foley artists flex the prop to make it. It adds tension or age to a scene.', practical_application='For a character shifting on a leather chair, the Foley artist twists a leather item near the mic to produce the creak in sync.', purpose_function='Adds the tension, age, or strain a creak conveys, bringing objects like doors, saddles, or floorboards to life.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Squeak','Prop Resonance','Specifics','Leather','Wood creak']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A ship''s timbers creaking at sea','A leather saddle under a rider','An old door slowly opening']::text[] WHERE id='b30d566e-7551-44ae-8687-bf1a49d0c325';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: Dropping heavy, sharp, or breakable props without eye and hand protection and clear footing, risking injury from impact or flying shards','Matching the sound to the wrong surface or weight','Missing the bounce or settle after the first impact']::text[], definition='A Foley sound created by dropping a prop onto a surface to match an on-screen object falling or being set down hard, capturing the impact and any bounce or settle.', plain_english='A prop drop is the sound of an object falling. The Foley artist drops a prop onto a surface. It matches something dropped on screen. The hit and any bounce are recorded.', practical_application='To match a bag hitting the floor, the Foley artist drops a matching weighted object onto the correct surface in sync, catching the thud and settle.', purpose_function='Delivers the impact and settle of falling or dropped objects, adding weight and consequence to the action.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Set-Down','Impact','Object Weight Illusion','Prop Handling','Specifics']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A dropped suitcase in a hallway','A body falling to the ground','A tool dropped on a workshop floor']::text[] WHERE id='fcd6b834-46c9-49b3-9289-cd69d1dfad8a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using the wrong material so the handling sounds fake','Overplaying handling so it competes with dialogue','Forgetting small touches that make interaction believable']::text[], definition='The general Foley category of sounds made by touching, gripping, moving, and using handheld objects — the handling noises that accompany a character interacting with props on screen.', plain_english='Prop handling is the sound of using objects. It covers grabbing, moving, and setting down props. Foley artists perform these touches in sync. They make a character''s actions feel real.', practical_application='As a character makes coffee, the Foley artist handles a matching mug, spoon, and jar, performing each touch and set-down in sync.', purpose_function='Fills in the tactile, everyday object sounds that sell a character''s interaction with their environment.', related_terms=ARRAY['Specifics','Prop Pickup','Prop Set-Down','Moves Track','Props Pass']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A character preparing a meal','Someone loading a firearm','A clerk handling papers and stamps']::text[] WHERE id='5e760f5d-5217-462b-a2a7-2a10d98515a1';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using a mechanism that sounds nothing like the on-screen device','Missing the individual clicks and stages of the action','Ignoring how worn or new the mechanism should sound']::text[], definition='The sound of a prop''s working mechanical parts — latches, hinges, gears, springs, switches — performed or recorded in Foley to match a mechanism operating on screen.', plain_english='A prop mechanism sound comes from moving parts. Think latches, gears, or springs. Foley artists work a real mechanism to make it. It matches a device operating on screen.', practical_application='For a briefcase lock, the Foley artist operates a latch mechanism near the mic, matching each click to the on-screen action.', purpose_function='Gives mechanical devices believable operating sounds that convey how they work.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Combination','Specifics','Prop Rattle','Mechanism','Sound design']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A gun being cocked','A clockwork toy winding','A latch on an old chest']::text[] WHERE id='9553d142-7a3b-40ae-ae65-44b0b149cf95';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Modifying so far the prop no longer reads as the object','Not documenting a modification that must be repeated later','Damaging a borrowed prop while altering it']::text[], definition='Altering a prop — loosening, dampening, adding material, or otherwise changing it — so it produces the desired Foley sound rather than its natural one.', plain_english='Prop modification means changing an object to sound right. The artist might loosen, pad, or add to it. This tunes the sound to the scene. The prop then makes the exact noise needed.', practical_application='To make a squeak, the artist might tighten a hinge; to dull a clang, they pack a metal object with cloth before performing it.', purpose_function='Lets a Foley artist tune an object''s sound to fit the picture when the unmodified prop is wrong.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Substitution','Prop Combination','Sound design','Specifics','Dampening']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Tuning a hinge to squeak on cue','Dampening a bell to soften its ring','Adding grit to make a smooth surface scrape']::text[] WHERE id='da531f45-5307-4af7-af0e-fbf8d080f596';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing this handling sound with a re-recorded pickup take','Missing the small surface release as the object leaves','Using the wrong material for the object''s weight']::text[], definition='The Foley sound of a prop being lifted from a surface — the release and handling noise as a character takes hold of an object — matched in sync to the on-screen grab.', plain_english='A prop pickup is the sound of lifting an object. It is the noise of grabbing and raising a prop. Foley artists match it to the hand on screen. It pairs with the set-down sound.', practical_application='As a character lifts a phone, the Foley artist picks up a matching object from a surface, catching the slight scrape and release in sync.', purpose_function='Marks the moment a character takes hold of an object, adding tactile realism to handling.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Set-Down','Prop Handling','Specifics','Release Sound','Grab']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A detective lifting a piece of evidence','Picking a mug up off a table','Taking a weapon from a rack']::text[] WHERE id='93468c5e-4958-4291-b6b8-7944e42fbf6e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using contents that sound wrong for the object','Rattling out of sync with the visible shake','Overdoing the rattle so it masks dialogue']::text[], definition='A Foley sound of loose parts or contents shaking inside or against a prop, performed to match a rattling object such as a box of items, a chain, or a loose mechanism.', plain_english='A prop rattle is a shaking, loose sound. It comes from parts knocking together. Think a box of small items or a chain. Foley artists shake a matching prop in sync.', practical_application='For a pill bottle, the Foley artist shakes a matching container of small items in time with the on-screen movement.', purpose_function='Conveys looseness, contents, or disrepair through the shaking sound of an object.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Mechanism','Specifics','Prop Resonance','Prop Handling','Shake']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A box of nails being carried','A chain being handled','A loose part in a moving machine']::text[] WHERE id='8278bdca-1973-4159-8571-08fa6d04bf02';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Damping the object too soon and losing the ring','Using a material whose resonance is wrong for the object','Ignoring resonance where the picture implies a ringing object']::text[], definition='The ringing or sustaining tone a prop produces after it is struck or excited — the resonant decay of materials like metal, glass, or wood — captured or performed in Foley to give an object its characteristic voice.', plain_english='Prop resonance is the ring after a hit. Metal, glass, and wood keep sounding for a moment. Foley artists capture this tail. It gives an object its own tone.', practical_application='After striking a metal pan, the Foley artist lets it ring, capturing the resonance to match a bell or metal object on screen.', purpose_function='Provides the sustaining tone that tells the ear what material and size an object is.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Creak','Impact','Prop Combination','Decay','Specifics']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A struck bell','A dropped metal tray ringing','Tapping a wine glass']::text[] WHERE id='2507fa87-70d8-4146-be83-59ba5ae2cb2f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using the wrong surface so the roll sounds off','Rolling at a speed that does not match the picture','Missing where the object stops or hits something']::text[], definition='The Foley sound of an object rolling across a surface — a bottle, ball, or wheel — performed by rolling a matching prop to capture the continuous, evolving contact sound.', plain_english='A prop roll is the sound of something rolling. Think a bottle or ball moving across a floor. Foley artists roll a matching object. The sound changes as it moves.', practical_application='For a can rolling down a hall, the Foley artist rolls a matching object across the correct surface in sync with the picture.', purpose_function='Reproduces the moving, continuous contact of rolling objects, including their speed and surface.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Slide','Prop Scrape','Specifics','Surface','Prop Handling']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A bottle rolling across a floor','A ball rolling to a stop','A cart wheel on cobbles']::text[] WHERE id='6c2016ad-0031-420e-8222-0da1de09b52b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a light slide with a pressured scrape','Using a surface with the wrong texture','Losing sync during the drag']::text[], definition='A Foley sound made by dragging one surface against another under pressure — the grating or rasping of a prop moved across a surface — matched to on-screen scraping.', plain_english='A prop scrape is a grating, dragging sound. One surface rubs hard against another. Foley artists drag a matching object. It matches scraping seen on screen.', practical_application='For a chair dragged across a floor, the Foley artist scrapes a matching object over the correct surface in sync.', purpose_function='Delivers the harsh, friction-based sound of objects dragged with pressure.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Slide','Prop Roll','Specifics','Surface','Friction']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A heavy chair dragged across tile','A blade drawn across stone','A crate pushed over concrete']::text[] WHERE id='f02992d0-629d-42d9-924d-5bc4d7125ec4';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Setting it down too hard so it reads as a drop','Using the wrong surface or object weight','Missing the small settle after contact']::text[], definition='The Foley sound of placing an object onto a surface — the controlled contact and settle as a character sets a prop down — the counterpart to a prop pickup.', plain_english='A prop set-down is the sound of placing an object. A character puts a prop onto a surface. Foley artists set a matching object down. It pairs with the pickup sound.', practical_application='As a character sets a glass on a table, the Foley artist places a matching object on the correct surface, catching the contact and any settle in sync.', purpose_function='Marks where a character places an object, giving handling a clear, grounded end point.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Pickup','Prop Handling','Prop Drop','Specifics','Settle']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Placing a cup on a saucer','Setting a book on a desk','Laying a tool on a bench']::text[] WHERE id='d3d52cc1-353c-4070-b253-21c9fad5b58e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Adding too much pressure so it becomes a scrape','Using a surface with the wrong friction','Sliding at the wrong speed for the picture']::text[], definition='A Foley sound of an object sliding smoothly across a surface with light contact — distinct from a pressured scrape — matched to something gliding on screen.', plain_english='A prop slide is a smooth sliding sound. An object glides across a surface. It is lighter than a scrape. Foley artists slide a matching prop in sync.', practical_application='For a plate slid across a counter, the Foley artist slides a matching object over the correct surface in sync.', purpose_function='Reproduces the smooth gliding motion of objects moved lightly across a surface.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Scrape','Prop Roll','Specifics','Surface','Prop Handling']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A dealer sliding cards across a table','A plate slid down a bar','A drawer gliding open']::text[] WHERE id='c0a214bb-2a4d-4466-bd02-65fe9cde631a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a squeak with a lower, longer creak','Producing it out of sync with the movement','Making it too loud or too frequent']::text[], definition='A high, thin squeaking sound produced by a prop under friction — such as a rubber, hinge, or fabric squeak — performed in Foley to match a squeaking object on screen.', plain_english='A prop squeak is a high, thin squeak. It comes from friction on an object. Think a hinge or a rubber sole. Foley artists work a matching prop to make it.', practical_application='For a squeaky door, the Foley artist works a hinge or rubber item near the mic to produce the squeak on cue.', purpose_function='Provides the high friction squeak that signals rubbing, wear, or specific materials.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Creak','Prop Scrape','Specifics','Rubber-Sole Footstep','Friction']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A squeaky hinge on a gate','Rubber shoes on a gym floor','A balloon rubbed by hand']::text[] WHERE id='10fe58f7-2f91-47b9-8ac2-6f152814bd8c';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Choosing a substitute whose tone does not match the on-screen object','Assuming the real object always sounds most convincing','Forgetting to test the substitute against picture']::text[], definition='The classic Foley practice of using one object to create the sound of another — such as coconut shells for hoofbeats or celery for breaking bone — when the real object is unavailable, unsafe, or sounds wrong.', plain_english='Prop substitution means faking a sound with another object. A stand-in prop makes the needed noise. Coconut shells can sound like horse hooves. The real object may be unsafe or wrong.', practical_application='To create a body punch, the Foley artist strikes a raw chicken or a phone book; to make snapping bones, they twist celery stalks near the mic.', purpose_function='Lets Foley artists produce sounds that would be impractical, dangerous, or unconvincing to make with the real object.', related_terms=ARRAY['Prop Modification','Prop Combination','Specifics','Sound design','Object Weight Illusion']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Coconut shells for horse hooves','Celery snapped for breaking bones','Cornstarch squeezed for footsteps in snow']::text[] WHERE id='6d25f18d-c1b0-448a-8e24-ca5c34641bb0';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Mixing prop sounds into the footsteps pass and losing separation','Skipping a specifics prop the scene needs','Not keeping consistent mic and level across passes']::text[], definition='A dedicated Foley recording pass in which the artist performs only the prop and specifics sounds for a scene, separate from the footsteps and moves passes, so each layer can be edited and mixed independently.', plain_english='A props pass records only the object sounds. The Foley artist does props in their own run. Footsteps and cloth are done in other passes. This keeps each layer separate for the mix.', practical_application='After recording feet and moves, the Foley artist runs the reel again performing only the handled props and specifics, delivering a clean props layer.', purpose_function='Organizes Foley into separate element passes so props can be edited, replaced, and balanced without affecting footsteps or moves.', related_terms=ARRAY['Footsteps track','Moves Track','Specifics','Foley walker','Prop Handling']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording all handled objects for a kitchen scene','Building a clean props layer for the M&E','Re-doing only the props after a picture change']::text[] WHERE id='ab72e0d5-b73a-4463-a670-840174d89fed';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Forgetting the release and leaving only the initial contact','Placing the release out of sync with the let-go','Making the release louder than the action warrants']::text[], definition='The sound produced when a control or object is let go or returns to rest — a button springing back, a switch releasing, or a held object being released — the counterpart to the contact or attack sound of an action.', plain_english='A release sound is the noise of letting go. A button springs back or a switch clicks off. It is the opposite of the first contact. Foley and sound design both use it.', practical_application='For a light switch, the Foley artist records both the press and the release click; for a bowstring, the release is the moment of let-go.', purpose_function='Completes an interaction by giving the let-go or return phase its own sound, making controls and actions feel physical.', related_terms=ARRAY['Performance Follow-Through','Prop Pickup','Specifics','Attack','Contact']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A button springing back after a press','Releasing a drawn bowstring','Letting go of a spring-loaded latch']::text[] WHERE id='fa4985f7-1ac4-4793-ac07-894ca2ea1c6d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Footsteps', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using hard-soled shoes for a character clearly in trainers','Forgetting the characteristic rubber squeak on smooth floors','Mismatching the surface under the shoe']::text[], definition='A Foley footstep performed with rubber-soled footwear — such as trainers or work boots — giving the soft, slightly squeaking, low-click character distinct from hard leather soles.', plain_english='A rubber-sole footstep uses rubber shoes. Think trainers or sneakers. The sound is soft with a slight squeak. It is different from hard leather soles.', practical_application='For a character in trainers, the Foley walker uses rubber-soled shoes on the matching surface to get the soft, quiet step with the odd squeak.', purpose_function='Matches the specific footwear a character wears, since sole material strongly shapes a footstep''s sound.', related_terms=ARRAY['Sandal Footstep','Footsteps track','Prop Squeak','Surface','Foley walker']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A jogger on pavement','A nurse walking a hospital corridor','Sneakers squeaking on a gym court']::text[] WHERE id='36d76b2d-de31-4ac8-a730-4b91f1d2fe0a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Deliverables — Leader/Timing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Placing the tail pop at the wrong distance from the last frame','Confusing the head sync with the tail run-out','Trimming the run-out so no safe tail margin remains']::text[], definition='In deliverables and mastering, the tail section following the last frame of picture — trailing black carrying a tail sync pop placed two seconds after the final frame — that marks the program''s end and provides a safe margin for playback and duplication.', plain_english='A run-out is the tail after the picture ends. It is black with a sync pop near the end. The pop sits two seconds after the last frame. It helps line up sound and picture.', practical_application='When delivering a reel, the editor places a tail pop two seconds after the last frame of picture within the run-out, so anyone can verify sound-to-picture sync.', purpose_function='Provides a standardized tail with a sync reference so labs, mixers, and duplicators can confirm and maintain sync at the end of a program.', related_terms=ARRAY['Run-Up','Tail pop','Leader','Sync pop','2-pop']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Preparing a reel for the dub stage','Delivering a master with head and tail sync','Checking sync at the end of a reel']::text[] WHERE id='4a55eb4f-20a7-415f-89bd-844c99f9129f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Deliverables — Leader/Timing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Setting the run-up too short so systems have not locked','Confusing the run-up lead-in with the recorded take itself','Ignoring the run-up when timing a performer''s entry']::text[], definition='The lead-in before a program start or a sync point — the head leader run-up to the first frame, or the pre-roll during which transports run up to speed and lock before recording or playback begins.', plain_english='A run-up is the lead-in before the start. Machines get up to speed and lock during it. It also names the countdown before the first frame. It gives the system time to settle.', practical_application='Before a Foley or ADR take, the recordist rolls a run-up so the transport locks and the performer can anticipate the cue, then records on the action.', purpose_function='Gives recording and playback systems time to reach speed and lock to sync before the action, and gives performers a lead-in to prepare.', related_terms=ARRAY['Run-Out','Pre-roll','Leader','Performance Anticipation','Sync pop']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Rolling pre-roll before an ADR line','Locking machines before a mix pass','Counting down to the first frame of a reel']::text[] WHERE id='c1b18e82-52e0-4766-ac22-8907731ec943';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley — Footsteps', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Missing the sole slap and treating it like a closed shoe','Using the wrong surface under the sandal','Overdoing the flap so it distracts']::text[], definition='A Foley footstep performed with sandals or other open, loose footwear, characterized by a slap or flap of the sole against the foot and floor in addition to the ground contact.', plain_english='A sandal footstep uses open shoes like sandals. The sole slaps the foot and floor. It has a loose flapping sound. Foley artists copy this with matching footwear.', practical_application='For a character in flip-flops, the Foley walker uses sandals on the matching surface, letting the sole slap to produce the characteristic flap.', purpose_function='Captures the distinctive slap-and-flap of loose footwear, matching a character''s sandals or flip-flops.', related_terms=ARRAY['Rubber-Sole Footstep','Footsteps track','Surface','Foley walker','Slap']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A beach walk in flip-flops','A character shuffling in sandals','Sandals on a stone temple floor']::text[] WHERE id='e102d833-ee86-4fa7-abbe-68bac9125914';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Trying to perform every element in one pass, which muddies timing and control','Not watching the picture during the pass so the performance drifts out of sync','Failing to label passes, which makes later editing confusing']::text[], definition='In Foley and post sound, a scene pass is a single continuous performance take that covers one sound element for a whole scene, recorded in one run against the picture. A scene is usually built from several passes, each dedicated to one layer such as footsteps, then cloth, then props.', plain_english='A scene pass is one recording take for a whole scene. The artist performs the sound while watching the picture. You often do several passes, one per sound layer.', practical_application='A Foley artist walks a character''s footsteps for the entire scene in one pass, then does a separate pass for cloth and another for props while the recordist labels each.', purpose_function='It lets the artist keep timing and character consistent across a scene, and it organizes recording into clean, layered takes that are easy to edit.', related_terms=ARRAY['Stride Matching','Surface Match','Weight Matching','Sync Transient','Transport lock']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A Foley editor asks for a clean footsteps-only pass on a dialogue scene','Recording a chase in several passes to layer feet, cloth, and props']::text[] WHERE id='92918194-0d97-4f7d-91ad-b8b8c8ccbf60';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley moves', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making seat sounds so loud they distract from dialogue','Using a chair whose creak does not match the on-screen furniture','Ignoring the character''s clothing when choosing what fabric to rustle']::text[], definition='Seat movement is a Foley moves cue that reproduces the sounds a person makes while sitting, shifting, or settling in a chair or seat, including clothing rubbing the upholstery, body weight compressing the seat, and small creaks.', plain_english='Seat movement is the sound of a person moving in a chair. It includes clothes rubbing and the seat creaking. Foley artists add it so sitting looks real.', practical_application='As a character shifts during a tense conversation, the artist moves in a similar chair wearing similar cloth to create the matching rustle and creak.', purpose_function='It adds presence and realism to seated characters, filling the quiet gaps where the production recording has little movement detail.', related_terms=ARRAY['Trouser Movement','Sleeve Movement','Weight Shift','Skin Contact','Surface Match']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding subtle chair creaks as a detective leans back during an interrogation','Covering a restaurant scene where several seated characters shift and settle']::text[] WHERE id='faaa3c43-75db-4e6a-b083-2b8876c29208';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming one pair of shoes suits every character and every surface','Over-modifying so steps sound artificial or too clicky','Not testing the modified shoe against the picture before recording']::text[], definition='Shoe modification is the practice of altering footwear used for Foley, such as adding hard taps, sanding or building up soles, loosening heels, or attaching materials, in order to tune the tone, attack, and character of recorded footsteps.', plain_english='Shoe modification means changing shoes to get a better footstep sound. Artists add taps, sand the soles, or loosen the heel. This tunes how each step sounds.', practical_application='To brighten a dress-shoe step on marble the artist attaches a hard tap to the sole; for a softer step they sand the sole smooth.', purpose_function='It gives the Foley artist control over footstep tone and loudness so steps match the character, the surface, and the mood on screen.', related_terms=ARRAY['Shoe Prop','Sole Slap','Soft-Sole Footstep','Surface Match','Toe-Heel Articulation']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding metal taps for a confident, hard-heeled villain walk','Sanding rubber soles to soften a near-barefoot approach on wood']::text[] WHERE id='bf48f81b-7be7-45b5-936e-de9083dbc8a4';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley props', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Grabbing any shoe instead of matching the sole and heel to the character','Using worn shoes whose sound has drifted from the original','Ignoring the surface the character walks on when choosing shoes']::text[], definition='A shoe prop is any item of footwear kept in the Foley collection specifically for performing footsteps and shoe-related sounds, selected for its sole material, heel type, and fit to match on-screen characters and surfaces.', plain_english='A shoe prop is footwear kept just for Foley work. Artists pick shoes by their sole and heel. The right shoe helps match the character and floor.', practical_application='The artist keeps a rack of shoes such as dress shoes, boots, sneakers, and heels, then selects a pair that fits the character before the footstep pass.', purpose_function='It provides the physical tool for creating believable footsteps of many kinds, from heavy boots to soft slippers.', related_terms=ARRAY['Shoe Modification','Slipper Footstep','Soft-Sole Footstep','Sole Slap','Surface Match']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Choosing heavy work boots for a construction-site walk','Selecting quiet loafers for a librarian moving through a reading room']::text[] WHERE id='1cbe9d9c-13ab-4273-b710-a8fcf4e3b33b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley footsteps', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Adding squeaks where the surface would not realistically produce them','Overusing squeaks so a serious scene reads as comedic','Recording the squeak so hot that it masks the main footstep']::text[], definition='Shoe squeak is a Foley detail that reproduces the high rubbing squeal shoes make, typically rubber soles on a smooth hard floor or leather flexing, used to add texture, comedy, or tension to a character''s movement.', plain_english='Shoe squeak is the squeal shoes make on a floor. It often comes from rubber soles on smooth surfaces. Foley artists add it for texture or comedy.', practical_application='The artist twists a rubber-soled shoe against polished wood or vinyl to catch the squeak, timing it to a pivot or a stop on screen.', purpose_function='It adds a recognizable, sometimes comedic or tense detail that sells a character moving on a gym floor, hospital corridor, or similar hard surface.', related_terms=ARRAY['Sole Slap','Soft-Sole Footstep','Surface Texture','Surface Match','Shoe Prop']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Basketball players pivoting on a polished court','A nurse''s shoes squeaking down a quiet hospital hallway']::text[] WHERE id='f89de706-dc24-452b-8886-7a56f3ac66a0';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley specifics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making slaps too sharp or too loud so they sound unreal','UNSAFE: Striking your own body hard enough to bruise or injure when performing hits; use padding and controlled force','Ignoring how clothing over the skin changes the sound']::text[], definition='Skin contact is a Foley specific covering skin-on-skin sounds such as handshakes, pats, face touches, slaps, and body contact, usually performed by hand to add tactile realism to human interaction.', plain_english='Skin contact is the sound of skin touching skin. It covers handshakes, pats, and slaps. Foley artists often make these with their own hands.', practical_application='For a handshake the artist clasps and rubs their own hands; for a slap they strike a padded surface or forearm in sync with the picture.', purpose_function='It restores the small human contact sounds that production mics often miss, so touches, greetings, and fights feel physical.', related_terms=ARRAY['Sleeve Movement','Seat Movement','Weight Shift','Surface Texture','Trouser Movement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding pats and grips to a heartfelt hug between two characters','Building the impacts of a bare-knuckle fight with hand and body slaps']::text[] WHERE id='c8856124-3d13-4dd0-aa38-57479080e515';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley moves', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using fabric that sounds heavier or lighter than the on-screen costume','Making cloth moves so loud they compete with dialogue','Performing generic rustle that ignores the specific arm action']::text[], definition='Sleeve movement is a Foley moves cue that reproduces the rustle and swish of a garment''s sleeves as the arms move, gesture, or brush against the body.', plain_english='Sleeve movement is the rustle of sleeves as arms move. Foley artists make it by moving matching fabric. It helps arm gestures feel real.', practical_application='The artist handles fabric like the costume and swings or rubs the sleeve area in time with the actor''s arm movements.', purpose_function='It adds cloth detail to gestures and arm motion, supporting the moves layer that grounds a character inside their clothing.', related_terms=ARRAY['Trouser Movement','Seat Movement','Skin Contact','Weight Shift','Surface Match']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A character in a stiff raincoat reaching across a table','Adding robe rustle as a judge gestures during a ruling']::text[] WHERE id='ec147dc8-85ae-49ba-b83f-3c23a344c939';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley footsteps', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Adding too much heel attack so the step sounds like a hard shoe','Missing the shuffle or drag that defines slipper movement','Performing on the wrong surface for the room shown']::text[], definition='A slipper footstep is a Foley footstep performed to sound like soft house slippers, giving quiet, often shuffling steps with little heel attack and a light, padded contact.', plain_english='A slipper footstep is the soft sound of walking in slippers. Steps are quiet and often shuffle. There is little heel click.', practical_application='The artist wears slippers or soft-soled footwear and shuffles gently on a matching wood or carpet surface in the Foley pit.', purpose_function='It conveys a relaxed, domestic, or elderly quality and helps place a character at home or in a soft-footed state.', related_terms=ARRAY['Sock Footstep','Soft-Sole Footstep','Sneak Step','Sole Slap','Surface Match']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['An elderly character shuffling to answer the door at night','A character padding to the kitchen for a midnight snack']::text[] WHERE id='72d8d9e0-cb32-4700-8d23-e54c3f98fc3e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley footsteps', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making sneak steps fully silent, which removes tension the audience needs to feel','Adding a hard heel that breaks the illusion of stealth','Keeping a normal walking rhythm instead of slowing and varying it']::text[], definition='A sneak step is a Foley footstep performed quietly and deliberately to convey stealth, using soft, controlled contacts with reduced weight and careful heel-to-toe placement.', plain_english='A sneak step is a quiet, careful footstep. It shows a character moving in secret. The artist steps softly to keep it hushed.', practical_application='The artist rolls slowly from toe to heel with light pressure on a matching surface, keeping levels low and avoiding sharp attacks.', purpose_function='It communicates tension, stealth, and intent, telling the audience a character is trying not to be heard.', related_terms=ARRAY['Soft-Sole Footstep','Sock Footstep','Toe-Heel Articulation','Weight Shift','Stagger Step']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A burglar creeping across a bedroom while someone sleeps','A soldier moving quietly through an enemy building']::text[] WHERE id='77e7a206-8990-44d2-9aab-b3304119d330';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley footsteps', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Adding sole or heel sounds that socks would not make','Recording so quietly that nothing survives the mix','Using a surface that sounds wrong for the room']::text[], definition='A sock footstep is a Foley footstep performed in socks or soft coverings so the contact is muffled and nearly silent, with a faint fabric-on-floor sound and no hard sole.', plain_english='A sock footstep is the soft sound of walking in socks. It is quiet with no hard click. You mostly hear light fabric on the floor.', practical_application='The artist walks in socks on a matching wood or tile surface, using light pressure to catch the soft fabric contact.', purpose_function='It places a character indoors without shoes and adds quiet realism to domestic, barefoot-adjacent, or stealthy moments.', related_terms=ARRAY['Slipper Footstep','Soft-Sole Footstep','Sneak Step','Skin Contact','Surface Match']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A child sliding across a hardwood floor in socks','A character sneaking downstairs in socks to avoid waking others']::text[] WHERE id='fb260d42-4e58-408f-b909-1fe919838ffd';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley footsteps', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Adding a hard heel attack that suits dress shoes, not sneakers','Ignoring the squeak sneakers make on smooth floors','Choosing a surface that does not match the scene']::text[], definition='A soft-sole footstep is a Foley footstep made with soft-soled footwear such as sneakers or trainers, giving a cushioned contact with muted attack and rounded low end rather than a sharp heel click.', plain_english='A soft-sole footstep comes from soft shoes like sneakers. The step is cushioned with a soft attack. There is no sharp heel click.', practical_application='The artist wears sneakers and walks on a matching surface, adding pivots or squeaks where the action calls for them.', purpose_function='It matches casual or athletic characters and quieter surfaces, contrasting with hard-heeled dress shoes or boots.', related_terms=ARRAY['Sole Slap','Shoe Squeak','Slipper Footstep','Sneak Step','Surface Match']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A jogger crossing a gym on rubber-soled trainers','A teenager walking casually through a school hallway']::text[] WHERE id='11b4b922-7340-48f6-bc21-94f8111692ce';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley footsteps', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Performing a heel-to-toe roll when the footwear should slap flat','Making every step an identical slap instead of varying it','Mismatching the slap to the type of shoe shown']::text[], definition='A sole slap is a Foley footstep detail in which the flat of the shoe sole strikes the floor with a slapping report, as with sandals, flip-flops, loose shoes, or a flat-footed step, emphasizing a broad percussive contact rather than a heel-first roll.', plain_english='A sole slap is the flat smack of a shoe sole hitting the floor. You hear it with sandals or flip-flops. The whole sole lands at once.', practical_application='The artist lets a sandal or loose shoe slap flat against the surface, timing the smack to the foot plant on screen.', purpose_function='It characterizes loose or open footwear and flat-footed walks, adding a distinctive percussive texture to a step.', related_terms=ARRAY['Soft-Sole Footstep','Shoe Squeak','Toe-Heel Articulation','Slipper Footstep','Shoe Modification']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A vacationer walking a boardwalk in flip-flops','A character in loose sandals crossing a tiled kitchen']::text[] WHERE id='a0a70bd1-cbec-4ab9-a193-0eaa76736580';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound editing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Cutting all sound exactly on the picture edit, which makes transitions feel abrupt','Carrying a sound so far that it confuses which scene we are in','Forgetting to balance levels where the carried sound overlaps']::text[], definition='Sound carryover is the technique of letting a sound continue across a picture edit or scene change, so audio from one shot or scene bleeds into the next to smooth the transition or link the two ideas.', plain_english='Sound carryover is when a sound keeps playing across a cut. Audio from one shot flows into the next. It smooths the change or links scenes.', practical_application='An editor lets room tone or a line of dialogue continue past the visual cut so the transition feels seamless rather than abrupt.', purpose_function='It softens hard picture cuts and builds continuity, guiding the audience smoothly from one shot or scene to another.', related_terms=ARRAY['Sound-Effects Cut','Sync Transient','Scene Pass','Transport lock','Surface Match']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Holding a phone ring across a cut to a new location','Letting crowd noise bleed from a stadium into the following shot']::text[] WHERE id='5cc35b67-b788-4880-ad90-1f1e355ff390';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound editing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Placing the effect a frame early or late so it feels out of sync','Leaving unwanted head or tail noise on the cut effect','Choosing an effect whose character does not match the on-screen object']::text[], definition='A sound-effects cut is an individual edited placement of a sound effect against picture, the result of a sound editor cutting — that is, selecting, trimming, and syncing — an effect into the timeline at a specific point.', plain_english='A sound-effects cut is one edited effect placed against the picture. The editor trims and syncs the sound to the action. To cut here means to edit.', practical_application='A sound editor cuts a door slam so its impact lands on the frame the door shuts, trimming the head and tail to fit.', purpose_function='It builds the effects track one placement at a time, making sure each sound lands in sync and at the right length.', related_terms=ARRAY['Sound Carryover','Sync Transient','Scene Pass','Transport lock','Surface Match']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Cutting a gunshot to match a muzzle flash','Placing a glass-break effect on the frame a window shatters']::text[] WHERE id='a2841d0c-bc6c-4d1c-9592-bd9e52b7bb4f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley footsteps', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Keeping an even rhythm that reads as a normal walk','Overdoing the stumble so it looks comedic when it should be serious','Forgetting the scuffs and drags that sell instability']::text[], definition='A stagger step is a Foley footstep performed to sound unsteady, with irregular timing, uneven weight, scuffs, and drags, to portray a character who is drunk, injured, exhausted, or off balance.', plain_english='A stagger step is an unsteady footstep. The timing and weight are uneven. It shows a character who is drunk, hurt, or tired.', practical_application='The artist walks with uneven pacing, adds scuffs and drags, and shifts weight unpredictably to match a character stumbling across a room.', purpose_function='It communicates loss of balance or control, telling the audience about a character''s state through their gait.', related_terms=ARRAY['Weight Shift','Sneak Step','Toe-Heel Articulation','Stride Matching','Weight Matching']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A drunk character weaving home from a bar','A wounded soldier struggling across a battlefield']::text[] WHERE id='8c121088-9fc6-4a75-b921-5de618eb4117';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Walking a natural pace instead of matching the actor''s stride','Ignoring differences in stride length between the artist and the actor','Fixing sync only in editing rather than performing it well first']::text[], definition='Stride matching is the Foley skill of aligning performed footsteps to the length, rhythm, and timing of a character''s stride on screen, so each recorded step lands in sync with the visible foot plant.', plain_english='Stride matching means fitting your steps to the actor''s steps. Each Foley footstep must land when the foot hits the ground. It keeps the walk in sync.', practical_application='Watching the actor, the artist paces their own walk to match stride length and rhythm, adjusting speed so every plant hits on the frame.', purpose_function='It makes footsteps believable and locked to picture, which is the core requirement of any footstep pass.', related_terms=ARRAY['Weight Matching','Surface Match','Sync Transient','Scene Pass','Toe-Heel Articulation']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Matching a tall actor''s long, slow stride during a hallway walk','Syncing rapid steps to a character running upstairs']::text[] WHERE id='bf6557dd-85db-4296-998b-29450ff51074';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using one generic surface for every location','Matching the look of a surface but not its sound','Forgetting that shoe choice also shapes the surface sound']::text[], definition='Surface match is the practice of choosing and performing on a Foley surface whose material and acoustic character match the floor or ground seen on screen, so footsteps and moves sound as if made on that surface.', plain_english='Surface match means picking a Foley floor that matches the screen. The artist walks on gravel for gravel and wood for wood. This makes footsteps sound right.', practical_application='For a scene on gravel the artist works in a gravel pit; for tile they use a hard tiled surface, adjusting shoes to complete the match.', purpose_function='It grounds footsteps and movement in the correct environment, which is a key part of making Foley believable.', related_terms=ARRAY['Surface Substitution','Surface Texture','Surface Resonance','Stride Matching','Weight Matching']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Building footsteps for a forest walk on a leaves-and-dirt surface','Matching a marble lobby with a hard stone Foley surface']::text[] WHERE id='dec87c29-4d7a-4cb7-8498-f79b09a6b8ef';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley acoustics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using a boomy surface for a scene that should sound dead','Matching only surface texture and ignoring resonance','Letting excess low-end resonance muddy the mix']::text[], definition='Surface resonance refers to the way a Foley surface vibrates and rings when struck or walked on, such as the hollow boom of a wooden stage, the ring of metal, or the dead thud of concrete, which shapes the tone of footsteps and impacts.', plain_english='Surface resonance is how a floor rings or booms when you step on it. Hollow wood sounds boomy. Metal rings, and concrete sounds dead.', practical_application='The artist chooses a hollow riser for a boardwalk boom or a solid slab for a dead concrete thud, tuning resonance to the scene.', purpose_function='It determines the low end and tonal character of footsteps and impacts, helping sell the size and material of a space.', related_terms=ARRAY['Surface Texture','Surface Match','Surface Substitution','Surface Scatter','Sole Slap']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Creating the hollow boom of footsteps on a wooden pier','Matching the dead thud of steps in a concrete parking garage']::text[] WHERE id='3d74770e-dcf9-4a5a-9f6d-0ade00f59c48';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley surfaces', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Forgetting scatter so loose ground sounds hard and flat','Overdoing scatter so it masks the footstep itself','Using material that does not match the ground shown']::text[], definition='Surface scatter is the sound of loose surface material such as gravel, sand, leaves, or debris being displaced and scattered by footsteps or movement, adding a granular, shifting texture beneath the main contact.', plain_english='Surface scatter is the sound of loose bits moving underfoot. Think gravel, sand, or leaves shifting as you step. It adds a grainy texture.', practical_application='The artist steps into a tray of gravel or dry leaves and drags and shifts the material so it scatters in time with the footfalls.', purpose_function='It adds realism to walks on loose ground, conveying material that gives way and scatters with each step.', related_terms=ARRAY['Surface Texture','Surface Match','Surface Resonance','Surface Substitution','Sole Slap']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Footsteps kicking gravel on a country road','Leaves scattering as a character walks through autumn woods']::text[] WHERE id='1a9f2ff8-556b-4206-9dd1-f6474b59c194';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Substituting a surface whose sound does not read as the on-screen material','Choosing convenience over believability','Failing to test the substitute against the picture']::text[], definition='Surface substitution is the deliberate use of a Foley surface different from the one shown on screen because it produces a more convincing, controllable, or practical sound than the literal material would.', plain_english='Surface substitution means using a stand-in floor for Foley. The chosen surface sounds better than the real one. It fools the ear even though it differs from the picture.', practical_application='To create convincing snow crunch the artist walks on cornstarch or a similar material rather than real snow, because it records more reliably.', purpose_function='It lets the artist get the right sound when the literal surface is impractical, unsafe, or simply records poorly.', related_terms=ARRAY['Surface Match','Surface Texture','Surface Resonance','Surface Scatter','Shoe Modification']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Using cornstarch to fake footsteps in fresh snow','Recording body impacts on a padded surface instead of hard ground']::text[] WHERE id='3b8fb270-59df-40e5-9669-849cb97234fa';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley acoustics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Producing clean steps that miss a surface''s natural grit','Adding scuffs that are too heavy for the material','Matching resonance but forgetting texture']::text[], definition='Surface texture is the fine-grained tonal and frictional detail a Foley surface adds, such as grit, roughness, or smoothness heard as scrapes, scuffs, and micro-contacts under footsteps and moves.', plain_english='Surface texture is the fine detail a floor adds to a step. It includes grit, scrapes, and scuffs. It tells the ear if the ground is rough or smooth.', practical_application='The artist drags and scuffs the foot to bring out grit on a rough surface, or keeps contacts clean for a smooth one.', purpose_function='It conveys the feel of a surface and adds richness that separates, for example, smooth marble from rough concrete.', related_terms=ARRAY['Surface Resonance','Surface Match','Surface Scatter','Surface Substitution','Shoe Squeak']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Bringing out grit as boots cross a dusty warehouse floor','Keeping steps clean and smooth on a polished ballroom floor']::text[] WHERE id='c0ca4db2-d8e9-4741-b4a9-ed9f0f9fc8fb';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound editing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Aligning to the wrong peak in a sound that has several transients','Trusting the waveform over what the picture actually shows','Forgetting that a soft sound may have no clear transient to sync to']::text[], definition='A sync transient is a sharp, clearly defined attack in the audio, such as a heel strike, clap, or impact, used as a visual and audible reference point for aligning a sound precisely to picture.', plain_english='A sync transient is a sharp spike in the sound. Editors use it to line sound up to picture. A heel hit or clap makes a clear point.', practical_application='An editor slides a footstep so its heel transient sits on the frame the foot lands, using the waveform''s sharp peak as the anchor.', purpose_function='It gives editors an unambiguous marker to lock audio to the exact frame of an on-screen action.', related_terms=ARRAY['Stride Matching','Sound-Effects Cut','Sound Carryover','Toe-Heel Articulation','Transport lock']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Lining a footstep''s heel spike to the exact frame of contact','Syncing a hammer hit to the frame the hammer lands']::text[] WHERE id='50788049-1285-45b8-8913-b747d65b874c';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Landing the whole foot flat so steps lose natural detail','Using the same articulation for every character and mood','Reversing heel and toe order for the wrong kind of walk']::text[], definition='Toe-heel articulation is the Foley skill of performing the separate toe and heel contacts within a single footstep, controlling their order, timing, and weight to shape the feel of a step, from a firm heel-first walk to a toe-first sneak or a rolling stride.', plain_english='Toe-heel articulation is shaping the two parts of a footstep. A step has a heel hit and a toe hit. The artist controls their order and timing.', practical_application='For a confident walk the artist plants a firm heel then rolls to the toe; for a sneak they touch toe first with light pressure.', purpose_function='It gives footsteps natural detail and lets the artist express walk style, mood, and surface interaction within each step.', related_terms=ARRAY['Stride Matching','Sneak Step','Weight Shift','Sole Slap','Stagger Step']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A firm heel-to-toe walk for a confident executive','A toe-first creep for a character sneaking past a guard']::text[] WHERE id='d7091cce-d366-48ed-b25d-8e6cb8048063';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Post sync', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording before the transport has actually locked, which causes drift','Confusing lock to timecode with simply pressing play together','Mismatched frame rates that prevent a stable lock']::text[], definition='Transport lock is the state in which a DAW, recorder, or deck''s transport is synchronized and locked to a master timecode or reference, so playback and recording chase and stay frame-accurately in sync with picture and other machines.', plain_english='Transport lock keeps machines playing in sync. The recorder follows a master timecode. Timecode is a running clock stamped on the recording. It chases and locks so sound stays lined up with picture.', practical_application='Before a Foley pass the engineer sets the DAW to chase timecode and confirms transport lock so the recording lines up with the film.', purpose_function='It guarantees audio stays frame-accurate to picture across systems, which is essential for editing, recording Foley, and mixing to picture.', related_terms=ARRAY['Sync Transient','Scene Pass','Stride Matching','Sound-Effects Cut','Surface Match']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Locking the DAW to the picture editor''s timecode before a Foley session','Chasing an external deck so all machines start and stay in sync']::text[] WHERE id='dfcb87aa-6dde-430a-bc1c-8f94d49ca532';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley moves', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using fabric that sounds heavier or lighter than the costume','Adding cloth so loud it distracts from footsteps and dialogue','Ignoring the specific leg action being performed']::text[], definition='Trouser movement is a Foley moves cue that reproduces the rustle and swish of trousers as the legs walk, sit, or shift, adding cloth detail to lower-body motion.', plain_english='Trouser movement is the rustle of pants as the legs move. Foley artists make it with matching fabric. It adds detail to walking and sitting.', practical_application='The artist rubs or swishes trouser-like fabric near the mic in time with the character''s leg movements, matching the costume''s weight.', purpose_function='It fills in the cloth sounds of leg movement, working with footsteps to make a walk feel complete and clothed.', related_terms=ARRAY['Sleeve Movement','Seat Movement','Weight Shift','Surface Match','Skin Contact']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding the swish of stiff jeans as a character strides','Covering corduroy trousers rubbing as a character sits']::text[] WHERE id='0ab8e3f3-a631-470e-9e6d-0c4ed3e316b7';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Walking with the artist''s own weight instead of the character''s','Overdoing heaviness so every step sounds like a giant','Ignoring how footwear and surface change perceived weight']::text[], definition='Weight matching is the Foley skill of performing footsteps and moves with a force and heaviness that matches the character''s size, build, and physicality on screen, so a heavy character sounds heavy and a light one sounds light.', plain_english='Weight matching means making steps sound as heavy as the character. A big person''s steps sound heavy. A child''s steps sound light.', practical_application='For a large man the artist steps with more force and low end; for a small child they use light, quick contacts.', purpose_function='It makes footsteps believable for a specific body, conveying size and mass through the force of each step.', related_terms=ARRAY['Weight Shift','Stride Matching','Surface Match','Toe-Heel Articulation','Stagger Step']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Giving a heavyset guard slow, weighty footsteps','Making a small child''s steps light and quick across a floor']::text[] WHERE id='4b511762-c144-48e7-9fd2-1561c5ddffaf';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Foley moves', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Ignoring weight shifts so standing and leaning feel weightless','Adding shifts that are too loud for the subtle motion','Mistiming the shift so it does not match the body''s movement']::text[], definition='Weight shift is a Foley movement detail capturing the transfer of body weight from one foot or position to another, including the subtle creaks, scuffs, and pressure changes that occur as a character leans, rises, or moves their mass.', plain_english='Weight shift is the sound of a body moving its weight. You hear small creaks and scuffs as someone leans or stands. It shows the mass moving.', practical_application='As a character rises from a chair the artist creates the creak and scuff of weight transferring, timing it to the on-screen motion.', purpose_function='It adds subtle realism to standing, leaning, and settling, conveying that a real body with mass is moving.', related_terms=ARRAY['Weight Matching','Seat Movement','Toe-Heel Articulation','Stagger Step','Surface Resonance']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A heavy character shifting weight as they lean on a railing','Capturing the creak of weight moving as someone stands from a couch']::text[] WHERE id='c3d5fc0e-9fa5-4391-8403-13a2c38c3e6f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='AI spatial processing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming it recovers real spatial information when it only synthesizes plausible width','Pushing width so far the sound collapses or cancels when summed to mono','Applying it to material that must stay strictly mono-compatible for broadcast']::text[], definition='The use of machine-learning models to derive a plausible stereo signal from a mono source, generating a second channel or synthesizing width so the result sounds spatially spread rather than centered on a single point.', plain_english='This uses AI to turn a mono track into stereo. The software builds a fake left and right side. The sound then feels wider than one center point.', practical_application='A technician runs a mono archival vocal or interview through an AI stereoizer to add modest width, then checks the result summed to mono before delivery.', purpose_function='Adds apparent width and a sense of space to mono recordings without a true multi-microphone capture, letting old or single-channel material sit better in modern stereo mixes.', related_terms=ARRAY['AI upmixing','Stereo-to-surround','Pseudo-stereo','Mono compatibility','Stereo image']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Widening a mono vintage vocal stem for a stereo reissue','Giving a mono podcast interview a little space','Prepping a mono sample for a modern stereo production']::text[] WHERE id='641e97a8-3171-42d0-987e-7d30fbfe64f4';
UPDATE glossary SET category='AI spatial processing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Treating an AI upmix as equal to a true discrete surround master','Letting separation artifacts leak into the surround channels','Ignoring downmix and fold-down checks so the surround collapses badly to stereo']::text[], definition='Using machine-learning source separation and spatialization to convert a mixed stereo or mono recording into a larger channel format such as 5.1, 7.1, or immersive, by estimating component sources and distributing them across the added channels.', plain_english='This uses AI to turn stereo into surround sound. The tool splits the mix into parts. It then spreads those parts across more speakers.', practical_application='A post engineer upmixes a stereo music bed to 5.1 for a film, routing reverb and ambience to the surrounds while keeping vocals centered.', purpose_function='Lets legacy stereo material be delivered in surround or immersive formats without the original multitrack, by intelligently placing separated elements into rear and height channels.', related_terms=ARRAY['Stereo-to-surround','Stereo-to-Atmos','AI source separation','Upmix','Immersive audio']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Creating a 5.1 version of a stereo-only catalog track','Delivering surround for a documentary from stereo stems','Filling height channels from a stereo bed']::text[] WHERE id='444c2215-f1fa-45a8-8ccd-0165f412dc70';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Restoration processing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing recovered ambience with the original captured space','Over-denoising first and relying on synthetic ambience to rescue the result','Using a room-tone patch that does not match the level or spectrum of the surrounding audio']::text[], definition='A restoration process that rebuilds or matches the natural background ambience of a recording, typically to disguise edits or to replace room tone that was removed by aggressive noise reduction.', plain_english='This puts back the natural room sound of a recording. Heavy noise removal can strip that away. The tool fills gaps so edits sound seamless.', practical_application='After denoising dialogue, an editor generates matching room tone to fill a silent edit point so the cut is inaudible in the scene.', purpose_function='Keeps a recording sounding natural after cuts or strong denoising by generating room tone that matches the surrounding audio, avoiding dead, unnatural-sounding gaps.', related_terms=ARRAY['Room tone','Noise reduction','De-reverb','Region healing','Restoration workflow']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Filling an edit gap in denoised dialogue','Rebuilding room tone stripped by strong noise reduction','Smoothing an ADR insert into a location scene']::text[] WHERE id='536103e6-0190-4131-8d92-0e01d138df16';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Restoration processing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Expecting it to reveal true lost detail rather than plausible synthesized content','Pushing recovery so far it sounds harsh or artificial','Using it to excuse a poor original capture']::text[], definition='A restoration process that regenerates high-frequency content and harmonics missing from a band-limited recording, such as audio degraded by low sample rate, telephone bandwidth, or lossy codecs, by synthesizing plausible upper harmonics from the surviving spectrum.', plain_english='This rebuilds the missing high sounds in a dull recording. Phone or old audio often loses the highs. The tool guesses them and adds them back.', practical_application='An engineer applies spectral recovery to a phone-quality voice memo, extending its bandwidth so it blends with full-range studio dialogue.', purpose_function='Restores brightness and intelligibility to recordings that have lost their top end, making band-limited sources such as phone calls and old codecs sound fuller and clearer.', related_terms=ARRAY['Spectral recovery','Bandwidth extension','Band-limited audio','Codec artifacts','Restoration workflow']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Improving a phone-recorded interview for broadcast','Restoring highs on a bandwidth-limited archival source','Extending the top end on codec-degraded audio']::text[] WHERE id='f3594b33-a8a0-4456-9160-05422869641d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Corrective processing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing polarity inversion (a 180-degree flip) with true time alignment','Fixing phase on one section but ignoring drift across the track','Judging phase only by ear without checking the mono sum or a correlation meter']::text[], definition='The process of detecting and fixing timing or polarity misalignments between related audio signals, such as multi-microphone captures or the two sides of a stereo pair, so their waveforms sum constructively rather than partially cancelling.', plain_english='This lines up signals that are out of step. When two mics disagree, sound can cancel. Fixing the phase brings back a full, solid sound.', practical_application='A technician time-aligns a DI and a mic''d amp, or nudges one channel of a stereo drum overhead, to remove hollow comb-filtered cancellation.', purpose_function='Prevents comb filtering, thin sound, and low-end loss caused by misaligned or inverted signals, restoring a coherent stereo image and full frequency balance.', related_terms=ARRAY['Polarity inversion','Comb filtering','Time alignment','Mono compatibility','Stereo balance correction']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Aligning DI and amp tracks on a bass','Correcting an out-of-phase stereo file that cancels in mono','Time-aligning multi-microphone drum captures']::text[] WHERE id='4898f667-602e-49e0-8bce-d48a0d0b58b2';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Restoration processing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Healing a region too large for the surroundings to plausibly reconstruct','Expecting perfect results over long or complex defects','Forgetting that healed content is synthesized, not truly recovered']::text[], definition='A repair technique in which a bounded selection (region) of audio, in the waveform or spectrogram, is reconstructed by interpolating or resynthesizing from the surrounding material, so a defect within the region is replaced by plausible content that matches its neighbors.', plain_english='This fixes a marked spot by using the sound around it. The software fills the spot to match its neighbors. The defect inside it disappears.', practical_application='In a spectrogram, an editor boxes a phone-ring bleed over speech and heals it, letting the tool resynthesize the covered speech from adjacent frames.', purpose_function='Removes short defects such as clicks, dropouts, coughs, or noises by rebuilding just the damaged area from nearby audio, leaving the surrounding material untouched.', related_terms=ARRAY['Spectral repair','Interpolation','De-click','Spectrogram editing','Restoration workflow']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Removing a cough over a sustained note','Repairing a brief dropout in a vocal','Erasing a chair squeak from a quiet passage']::text[] WHERE id='433468b9-b8c4-4d1d-b8c9-b1e544e4534d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Restoration workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Comparing at mismatched loudness so the processed version seems better only because it is louder','Judging only short clips instead of full passages','Listening to the output but never soloing what is being removed']::text[], definition='The practice of critically A/B comparing processed audio against the original unprocessed source, and between successive processing stages, during restoration, to confirm that repairs improve the audio without introducing new artifacts or removing wanted content.', plain_english='This means listening to the fixed audio next to the original. You switch back and forth. It checks that your repair helped and added no new problems.', practical_application='After denoising, the technician toggles bypass and solos the noise being removed to verify that only noise, not program material, is affected.', purpose_function='Guards against over-processing by making damage removal audible relative to the source, ensuring artifacts, dulling, or lost detail are caught before delivery.', related_terms=ARRAY['A/B comparison','Restoration verification','Bypass','Noise reduction','Restoration workflow']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Checking that denoise did not dull a voice','Comparing two de-click settings','Auditioning the removed residue for wanted sound']::text[] WHERE id='b54deb2a-896c-45c9-af58-c138272a069d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Archival practice', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Documenting only the final settings, not the source condition and full chain','Storing notes separately so they become detached from the file','Skipping documentation on quick jobs that later need to be trusted']::text[], definition='The recorded metadata and processing history describing what was done to an audio object during restoration: source condition, equipment, settings, processing chain, and personnel, kept so the work is transparent, repeatable, and in principle reversible.', plain_english='This is a written record of every restoration step. It notes the gear, settings, and choices made. Others can then understand or repeat the work.', practical_application='An archivist logs the playback deck, calibration tape, sample rate, and each noise-reduction setting in a preservation metadata sheet stored with the master.', purpose_function='Provides provenance and accountability for archival work, letting future users judge how a file was altered and preserving the option to redo or reverse the treatment.', related_terms=ARRAY['Provenance','Preservation metadata','Flat transfer','Restoration verification','Archive transfer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Logging processing for a library preservation master','Recording the deck and EQ used on a tape transfer','Noting reversible versus baked-in processing']::text[] WHERE id='c4d9ddcf-6bf3-4b9c-8a8e-473c6f1e345b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Restoration workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Verifying on only one playback system','Trusting meters without a full critical listen','Skipping a check against the original source and the brief']::text[], definition='The quality-control step in which restored audio is checked, by critical listening and often by measurement, against defined targets and the source, to confirm that defects are adequately reduced, no new artifacts were introduced, and the result meets the project''s fidelity requirements.', plain_english='This checks that the finished restoration is actually good. You listen closely and measure. It confirms the repairs worked and nothing new broke.', practical_application='Before delivery, the technician does a full-length critical listen on reference monitors and headphones, checks levels and true-peak, and confirms against the brief.', purpose_function='Ensures deliverables meet quality standards before sign-off, catching residual noise, artifacts, level errors, or over-processing that automated tools can miss.', related_terms=ARRAY['Quality control','Restoration comparison','Critical listening','Restoration documentation','Restoration workflow']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Final QC before an archive delivery','Confirming no artifacts after aggressive denoise','Checking levels and peaks on a restored master']::text[] WHERE id='c72ab612-c70a-45a7-84dc-489fcd308153';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Corrective processing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Correcting balance while ignoring an underlying phase problem','Over-correcting and pulling the image off-center the other way','Judging balance on untrusted or asymmetric monitoring']::text[], definition='Adjusting the relative level, and sometimes spectral energy, difference between the left and right channels of a stereo recording so the image is centered and even, correcting an off-center or lopsided stereo field.', plain_english='This evens out the left and right sides of a stereo track. One side may be louder by mistake. Fixing it re-centers the sound.', practical_application='A mastering engineer nudges channel balance a fraction to re-center a mix that leans left, checking on a balance or correlation meter and by ear.', purpose_function='Corrects channel imbalance from miking, transfer, or gain errors, restoring a stable, centered stereo image so the mix does not pull to one side.', related_terms=ARRAY['Stereo image','Channel balance','Phase correction','Mid-side processing','Stereo width']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Centering a mix that leans to one channel','Fixing a lopsided stereo transfer','Balancing a stereo microphone pair capture']::text[] WHERE id='e87aaa88-9c82-49c5-868f-425314959ad2';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Immersive audio', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming an Atmos upmix equals a native Atmos mix built from multitracks','Letting separation artifacts sit exposed in isolated objects','Neglecting the binaural and stereo re-render checks']::text[], definition='The process of converting a stereo mix into a Dolby Atmos immersive presentation, typically by separating or re-creating elements and placing them as beds and objects across surround and height channels, often aided by AI source separation.', plain_english='This turns a stereo song into a Dolby Atmos mix. Parts of the song are spread around and above the listener. It builds an immersive version from stereo.', practical_application='An immersive engineer uses stem separation on a stereo master, then places vocals, drums, and reverb as Atmos objects and beds in a renderer.', purpose_function='Lets stereo-only material be delivered as immersive Atmos for streaming and cinema when the original multitrack is unavailable, distributing content into a three-dimensional soundfield.', related_terms=ARRAY['Dolby Atmos','AI upmixing','Stereo-to-surround','AI source separation','Immersive audio']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Creating an Atmos version of a legacy stereo single','Delivering immersive audio from a stereo-only catalog','Prepping stereo stems for object placement']::text[] WHERE id='bbcd5b63-33a9-4f1c-9adc-75a44a19d211';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Immersive audio', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Sending too much correlated content to the surrounds and smearing the image','Ignoring fold-down and downmix compatibility','Treating an upmix as a discrete surround master']::text[], definition='Upmixing a two-channel stereo signal into a multichannel surround format such as 5.1 or 7.1 by deriving centre, surround, and low-frequency content from the stereo mix through matrix or source-separation methods.', plain_english='This turns stereo into surround sound. Extra channels like center and rears are made from the stereo signal. It fills more speakers than two.', practical_application='A post engineer upmixes a stereo music cue to 5.1, sending reverb and ambience to the surrounds and anchoring dialogue-adjacent content to the centre.', purpose_function='Provides a surround deliverable from stereo-only material for film, broadcast, and home theatre, spreading ambience and elements to the surround field while keeping key content forward.', related_terms=ARRAY['Upmix','AI upmixing','Stereo-to-Atmos','5.1 surround','Matrix decoding']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Making a 5.1 bed from a stereo cue','Surround delivery from a stereo archive','Filling rears for a documentary mix']::text[] WHERE id='835ffccc-dd45-4948-8258-10b0702c635d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='AI speech editing tool', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Accepting all automatic cuts without review, which can clip wanted words or breaths','Expecting it to fix noise and tone, when it targets disfluencies rather than restoration','Assuming detection is perfect across accents and overlapping speech']::text[], definition='Cleanvoice is an AI-based online audio editing service that automatically detects and removes filler words, stutters, mouth sounds, and long silences from spoken-word recordings such as podcasts, reducing manual editing time.', plain_english='Cleanvoice is an AI tool for spoken audio. It finds ''um'', stutters, and mouth noises. Then it removes them so editing is faster.', practical_application='A podcast editor uploads an interview, lets Cleanvoice mark filler words and silences, reviews the suggested cuts, then exports the tightened edit.', purpose_function='Speeds up podcast and voice editing by auto-flagging and cutting disfluencies and dead air, letting creators publish cleaner speech without hand-editing every filler.', related_terms=ARRAY['Filler-word removal','Podcast editing','Krisp','Silence trimming','Disfluency detection']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Cleaning filler words from a podcast interview','Trimming dead air from a voice recording','Speeding up a rough edit of spoken content']::text[] WHERE id='666551e4-47b1-431a-b1fd-ce938a11f28c';
UPDATE glossary SET category='AI noise-cancellation tool', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming it restores audio quality, when heavy noise removal can add artifacts to speech','Running it alongside the app''s own noise suppression, causing double processing','Expecting studio-grade results from real-time low-latency cleanup']::text[], definition='Krisp is an AI-powered noise-cancellation application that processes microphone and speaker audio in real time to remove background noise, room echo, and other unwanted voices from voice and video calls.', plain_english='Krisp is an app that cleans up call audio live. It removes background noise and echo. Your voice comes through clearer on calls.', practical_application='A remote worker enables Krisp as their microphone device so keyboard clatter and room noise are removed before the conferencing app receives the signal.', purpose_function='Improves speech clarity on live calls and recordings by stripping ambient noise and reverb at the driver level, working across most conferencing apps without special hardware.', related_terms=ARRAY['Noise cancellation','Real-time processing','Waves Clarity Vx','Echo cancellation','Speech enhancement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Removing background noise on a video call','Cleaning echo in an untreated home office','Suppressing side conversations on a live call']::text[] WHERE id='008bef9c-8872-447b-9ba1-a44a1252807d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='AI amp and tone modeling tool', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Capturing with a poor DI or microphone setup, which bakes flaws into the model','Confusing a captured Tone Model with a static impulse response','Expecting a single capture to respond identically to the amp at all gain settings']::text[], definition='ToneX is IK Multimedia''s amp- and tone-modeling platform that uses machine learning (marketed as AI Machine Modeling) to capture the sonic behaviour of real guitar and bass amplifiers, cabinets, and pedals as reusable Tone Model profiles for playback in software or hardware.', plain_english='ToneX copies the sound of real amps and pedals. It uses AI to learn how they sound. You can then play through those captured tones.', practical_application='An engineer captures a favourite tube amp into a Tone Model, then reamps DI guitar tracks through it in the mix instead of re-tracking the amp.', purpose_function='Lets players and engineers model and reuse the sound of physical gear, capturing an amp''s response into a profile that can be reamped or played through without the original hardware.', related_terms=ARRAY['Amp modeling','Tone capture','Reamping','Impulse response','Machine learning']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Modeling a borrowed amp before returning it','Reamping guitars through a captured tone','Using captured tones live from a hardware pedal']::text[] WHERE id='76c99a1a-7c05-4f1e-aa4b-af3b1043cac5';
UPDATE glossary SET category='AI voice-conversion tool', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming output is broadcast-clean, when conversion can add artifacts, especially on noisy input','Ignoring consent and rights issues when cloning a real person''s voice','Underestimating latency and CPU load in a live chain']::text[], definition='Voice.ai is a real-time AI voice-changing and voice-cloning application that transforms a user''s live microphone input into a different target voice using machine-learning voice models.', plain_english='Voice.ai changes your voice as you speak. It uses AI to make you sound like someone else. This happens live through your microphone.', practical_application='A streamer routes their microphone through Voice.ai to speak in a chosen character voice during a live broadcast, monitoring latency and clarity.', purpose_function='Enables live voice transformation for streaming, gaming, and content creation, letting a speaker adopt a different vocal identity in real time without re-recording.', related_terms=ARRAY['Voice conversion','Voice cloning','Real-time processing','Speech synthesis','Voice changer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Changing voice for a live stream','Creating a character voice for content','Experimenting with voice conversion on a microphone']::text[] WHERE id='ff557b12-f78f-45b3-a419-cd65790ca68d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='AI noise-reduction plugin', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Over-reducing so the voice becomes thin or gains artifacts','Expecting it to replace full spectral repair for complex, localized noises','Relying on it to fix distortion or clipping, which it does not address']::text[], definition='Waves Clarity Vx is a real-time noise-reduction plug-in that uses a neural-network engine to separate a voice from its background, removing ambient sound, hum, and other unwanted noise while preserving speech.', plain_english='Clarity Vx is a plugin that cleans up voices. It uses a neural network, a smart computer model that learns to tell speech from noise. It removes the noise and keeps the voice.', practical_application='A dialogue editor inserts Clarity Vx on a noisy interview track and dials in the amount of noise reduction, using it live rather than doing offline spectral repair.', purpose_function='Provides low-latency, AI-driven vocal noise removal for dialogue, podcasts, and streaming, isolating the voice so background noise can be reduced without heavy manual spectral work.', related_terms=ARRAY['Noise reduction','Krisp','Real-time processing','Dialogue restoration','Neural network']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Cleaning a noisy interview in real time','Reducing room noise on a podcast vocal','Live noise removal on a streaming microphone']::text[] WHERE id='6ed52ade-97aa-4ca2-ab1d-2eb543c18877';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Archival playback', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Transferring before verifying speed and azimuth alignment','Using the wrong EQ standard, such as NAB versus IEC/CCIR, for the tape','Skipping level calibration so transfers cannot be compared']::text[], definition='The process of aligning an analog playback machine, such as a tape deck or turntable, to a known reference so it reproduces recorded media accurately, setting parameters such as speed, azimuth, playback level, and equalization using calibration tapes or test records.', plain_english='This tunes an old playback machine to play correctly. Speed, head angle, level, and EQ are set to a standard. It uses special test tapes or records.', practical_application='Before transferring a reel, the engineer plays a reference calibration tape to set reproduce level and EQ, then adjusts azimuth for maximum high-frequency output.', purpose_function='Ensures a transfer reflects what is on the media rather than machine error, so archival captures are accurate, repeatable, and comparable across systems.', related_terms=ARRAY['Playback equalization','Azimuth','Calibration tape','Transfer reference tone','Archive transfer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Aligning a tape deck before an archival transfer','Setting turntable speed and level for a disc transfer','Calibrating azimuth for the best high-frequency response']::text[] WHERE id='66a5ca4e-f965-44b4-9b4d-c7e83dcb6187';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Archival practice', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Applying restoration processing during the preservation transfer instead of keeping it flat','Transferring at too low a resolution to meet preservation standards','Failing to document the source and signal chain']::text[], definition='The controlled process of playing back and capturing content from an original archival carrier, such as tape, disc, or wire, into a digital preservation file, aiming to reproduce the source as accurately and completely as possible.', plain_english='This means copying old recordings into digital files. The original is played and captured carefully. The goal is a faithful, lasting copy.', practical_application='An archivist cleans and calibrates the source and deck, captures a flat, high-resolution file with reference tones, and logs full documentation.', purpose_function='Migrates fragile or obsolete carriers into stable digital preservation masters before the originals degrade or their playback machines disappear.', related_terms=ARRAY['Flat transfer','High-resolution transfer','Preservation master','Analog playback calibration','Restoration documentation']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Digitizing a deteriorating reel collection','Capturing a preservation master from a lacquer disc','Transferring wire or cassette originals']::text[] WHERE id='e9992769-87f2-484b-b1ab-848a8aaec187';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Archival restoration', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Cleaning a disc with a solvent unsuitable for its material, which can damage shellac or lacquer surfaces','Using a stylus size that does not match the groove','Over-declicking so that transients and detail are lost']::text[], definition='The cleaning, careful playback, and digital repair of disc-based recordings such as 78-rpm shellac, vinyl, and instantaneous lacquer discs, aiming to recover the best possible signal while managing surface noise, clicks, and physical damage.', plain_english='This restores sound from old records. The disc is cleaned and played with the right stylus. Then clicks and noise are cleaned up digitally.', practical_application='A technician cleans a 78, selects a stylus matched to the groove, applies the appropriate playback curve, then declicks and decrackles the capture.', purpose_function='Recovers audio from disc carriers that suffer wear, warping, and surface noise, using correct playback and gentle processing to reveal the recorded content.', related_terms=ARRAY['Shellac restoration','De-click','Playback equalization','Surface noise','Groove wear']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Restoring a scratched 78-rpm record','Transferring a fragile lacquer disc','Cleaning surface noise from a worn LP']::text[] WHERE id='04177df5-b7a8-4577-9540-0ddb2f808140';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Archival practice', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing ''flat'' with bypassing the required playback EQ standard','Baking noise reduction into what should be the preservation master','Discarding the flat master after making processed copies']::text[], definition='A transfer made without applying restoration processing, noise reduction, or corrective equalization beyond the standard playback EQ required to decode the carrier, capturing the source signal as-is for preservation.', plain_english='This copies a recording with no clean-up added. Only the basic playback EQ is used. It keeps the raw sound for the archive.', practical_application='An archivist captures a tape flat, with correct reproduce EQ only and no denoise, as the preservation master, then makes processed access copies separately.', purpose_function='Preserves an unaltered, faithful representation of the source so future restoration can start from original material and processing choices are not baked in irreversibly.', related_terms=ARRAY['Preservation master','Archive transfer','Playback equalization','High-resolution transfer','Restoration documentation']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Making an unprocessed preservation master','Capturing raw audio before restoration','Archiving originals for future re-restoration']::text[] WHERE id='928fa2ee-44ce-4e10-b87b-7e71e00fe466';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Archival practice', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming higher numbers add detail the source never contained','Setting levels too hot and losing the 24-bit headroom advantage','Using high resolution but skimping on playback calibration']::text[], definition='An archival transfer captured at a high sample rate and bit depth, commonly 96 kHz or higher at 24-bit, to preserve fine detail and headroom, in line with preservation recommendations that favour resolution well above consumer formats.', plain_english='This copies old audio at very high quality settings. A high sample rate and bit depth catch fine detail. It also gives extra room for later work.', practical_application='An archivist transfers a master tape at 96 kHz and 24-bit, giving headroom and resolution for later noise reduction without compromising the source.', purpose_function='Captures the full detail and dynamic range of a source, including its noise floor, so the preservation file supports high-quality restoration and future formats.', related_terms=ARRAY['Preservation master','Sample rate','Bit depth','Archive transfer','Flat transfer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Transferring a master tape at 96 kHz / 24-bit','Preserving a disc with full resolution and headroom','Meeting an archive''s preservation format specification']::text[] WHERE id='352488df-1a21-4000-96c8-957cc5879a09';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Tape maintenance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using ordinary or acidic adhesive tape instead of proper splicing tape','Splicing so the leader adhesive contacts and contaminates the oxide','Removing original leader that carries useful annotations without noting it']::text[], definition='The archival repair task of removing degraded or missing leader tape, the non-magnetic tape spliced to the ends and between sections of a reel, and splicing in fresh leader to protect the recording and mark sections.', plain_english='Leader tape is the plain tape at the start and end of a reel. Old leader gets brittle or falls off. This means splicing in new leader tape.', practical_application='Before transfer, a technician cuts away perished leader and splices new leader using a splicing block and proper splicing tape.', purpose_function='Protects the magnetic tape ends from damage, provides a clean threading start, and can colour-code or separate program sections without risking the recorded oxide.', related_terms=ARRAY['Tape splice repair','Splicing block','Reel-to-reel tape','Archive transfer','Splicing tape']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Replacing perished leader before a transfer','Re-leadering a reel with brittle ends','Adding fresh leader to protect tape ends']::text[] WHERE id='f2c82afa-8e37-42f9-a622-d89caf0bac78';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Archival playback', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using the wrong tape standard, NAB versus IEC/CCIR, so the high end is wrong','Assuming all 78s use one curve when early discs varied widely','Confusing standardized playback EQ with tonal restoration choices']::text[], definition='The standardized equalization applied on reproduction to undo the complementary recording equalization built into a medium, such as RIAA for vinyl, NAB or IEC/CCIR for analog tape, and various early curves for 78s, so the played-back signal has a flat, correct response.', plain_english='Recordings are made with the tone changed on purpose. Playback EQ reverses that change. Using the right curve makes the sound come out correct.', practical_application='For a tape, the engineer selects NAB or IEC/CCIR reproduce EQ to match the recording standard; for a 78, they choose the era-appropriate curve.', purpose_function='Restores flat frequency response by matching the reproduce curve to the one used at recording, without which playback sounds too bright, dull, or tonally wrong.', related_terms=ARRAY['RIAA curve','NAB equalization','IEC/CCIR equalization','Analog playback calibration','Turnover frequency']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Selecting NAB versus IEC on a tape deck','Choosing a 78-rpm playback curve','Applying RIAA on a disc transfer']::text[] WHERE id='589baecd-29c4-4cb6-b56a-1f5b2d4e73d7';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Restoration workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Applying broadband denoise before removing clicks, which smears the defects','Over-processing in one heavy pass instead of gentle stages','Working on a processed copy with no flat master to return to']::text[], definition='The planned, ordered sequence of steps used to restore a recording, typically transfer, then repair of discrete defects such as clicks and crackle, then broadband noise reduction, then tonal and level work, arranged so each stage does the least harm and sets up the next.', plain_english='This is the step-by-step order for cleaning up audio. You fix clicks first, then broad noise, then tone. A good order avoids making things worse.', practical_application='The technician transfers flat, removes clicks and crackle, applies gentle broadband denoise, then finishes with EQ and level, comparing to the source throughout.', purpose_function='Produces consistent, high-quality results and avoids artifacts by sequencing repairs sensibly and keeping a flat master, so processing is controlled and defensible.', related_terms=ARRAY['De-click','Noise reduction','Flat transfer','Restoration comparison','Restoration verification']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Planning steps for a noisy tape restoration','Ordering de-click before denoise','Restoring a disc from transfer to final master']::text[] WHERE id='1c59ed81-f79a-4289-92b6-2a7db0a34c31';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Archival restoration', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: Handling brittle shellac carelessly, since cracked discs can shatter into sharp fragments','Cleaning shellac with alcohol, which can dissolve or dull the surface','Assuming a single RIAA curve fits all early shellac recordings']::text[], definition='The restoration of recordings on shellac discs, chiefly 78-rpm records, involving careful cleaning, playback with a groove-matched stylus and appropriate, often pre-RIAA, equalization, and digital reduction of the heavy surface noise typical of the format.', plain_english='Shellac discs are old, brittle 78-rpm records. Restoring them means gentle cleaning and the right stylus. Then heavy crackle and noise are cleaned up.', practical_application='A technician selects a stylus matched to the worn groove, applies an era-appropriate playback curve, and uses de-click and denoise tuned for shellac hiss and crackle.', purpose_function='Recovers early recorded sound from a fragile, noisy medium by combining correct physical playback with restoration processing suited to shellac''s high surface noise.', related_terms=ARRAY['Disc restoration','78-rpm record','Playback equalization','Surface noise','De-crackle']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Restoring a 1920s 78-rpm disc','Reducing crackle on shellac transfers','Choosing a stylus for a worn shellac groove']::text[] WHERE id='d5965ca4-4d4d-4932-b83b-e373083c40a8';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Tape maintenance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using household or pressure-sensitive tape whose adhesive bleeds onto the oxide','Splicing over the magnetic side or leaving adhesive exposed','Making a ragged cut that causes dropouts or jams']::text[], definition='The physical repair of broken or failed joints in magnetic tape, including old splices whose adhesive has dried or bled, by trimming the ends and rejoining them with proper splicing tape on a splicing block so the tape can be safely played and transferred.', plain_english='Tapes are often joined with splices that can fail. This means cutting the ends cleanly and rejoining them. Proper splicing tape holds them together.', practical_application='The technician places the tape in a splicing block, trims a clean diagonal cut, and joins the ends with fresh splicing tape before transfer.', purpose_function='Lets damaged reels be threaded and played without breaking, and repairs old splices that have gone sticky or brittle, protecting both the tape and the machine.', related_terms=ARRAY['Splicing block','Leader tape replacement','Reel-to-reel tape','Archive transfer','Splicing tape']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Repairing a snapped reel before transfer','Replacing a failed old splice','Rejoining tape that broke during playback']::text[] WHERE id='e584a117-76f1-4b0d-a626-24ee7fc92fc1';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Archival practice', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Ignoring line-up tones already on the tape when setting playback level','Recording a reference tone at an undocumented level','Treating the tone as program material and normalizing it away']::text[], definition='A calibration tone, or set of tones such as 1 kHz and a multi-frequency line-up, recorded onto or alongside an archival transfer to document reference level and, where present on the original, to align playback level, azimuth, and equalization.', plain_english='This is a steady test tone kept with a transfer. It marks the reference level. It helps set and check level and alignment later.', practical_application='The engineer records the tape''s head line-up tones, or inserts a 1 kHz reference at a known level on the digital file, and logs it in the documentation.', purpose_function='Provides a fixed level and alignment reference so transfers can be calibrated, compared, and correctly reproduced across systems, and so later users know the intended level.', related_terms=ARRAY['Alignment tone','Reference level','Analog playback calibration','Transfer documentation','Archive transfer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Using head tones to set reproduce level','Adding a 1 kHz reference to a transfer file','Documenting reference level for an archive']::text[] WHERE id='dbfc0b67-b99e-4783-8559-823e58f0041f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Restoration processing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Cutting so much low end that the voice sounds thin','Relying on processing instead of a windscreen at the point of capture','Treating steady wind and sudden gusts with the same fixed setting']::text[], definition='The reduction of low-frequency rumble and turbulence caused by wind hitting a microphone, using a combination of high-pass filtering and specialized spectral or model-based processing to remove wind energy while preserving the wanted signal.', plain_english='Wind hitting a mic makes a low rumbling noise. This removes that rumble. It uses filters and special tools to keep the voice clear.', practical_application='An editor applies a high-pass filter plus a dedicated de-wind process to gusty location dialogue, restoring intelligibility without hollowing out the voice.', purpose_function='Recovers usable dialogue or sound from outdoor recordings marred by wind, cutting the low-frequency buffeting that masks and overloads the wanted signal.', related_terms=ARRAY['High-pass filter','De-wind','Rumble','Noise reduction','Location recording']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Cleaning gusty outdoor dialogue','Removing wind rumble from field recordings','Rescuing a windy interview take']::text[] WHERE id='92578faf-0185-4940-b0fa-235f54710e83';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Control surface concept', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming several tracks can hold attention at once, when only one is attentioned, usually the last selected','Confusing attention with mere selection when multiple tracks are selected','Expecting the surface knobs to follow a track that was selected but not attentioned']::text[], definition='On an EUCON-based control surface, such as Avid systems, the attention track is the single currently attentioned channel, the track whose parameters the surface''s encoders, knobs, and focused controls act upon, indicated on screen and moving with track selection.', plain_english='On some control surfaces one track gets the surface''s focus. That focused track is the attention track. Its controls appear on the surface''s knobs.', practical_application='The engineer selects a channel; it becomes the attention track, and the surface''s knob section shows that track''s plug-in and EQ parameters for hands-on tweaking.', purpose_function='Gives the operator one clear target for detailed editing, so selecting a track hands its EQ, dynamics, and routing to the surface''s knobs and displays.', related_terms=ARRAY['EUCON','Control surface','Track selection','Focus','Channel strip']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Editing one track''s EQ from the surface','Following selection to control a channel','Switching attention between mixer channels']::text[] WHERE id='bd5bb4cb-d670-4116-9576-d201cd92fc47';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Machine control and synchronization', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a start-time offset with a frame-rate or pull-up/pull-down mismatch','Entering the offset with the wrong sign so material lands early or late','Forgetting to account for the offset when exporting or conforming']::text[], definition='A timecode offset applied when synchronizing or controlling one machine or session from another, shifting the controlled device''s start reference so its timecode aligns with the master''s, used in machine control and chase-lock synchronization.', plain_english='When one machine follows another, their clocks may not match. A start-time offset shifts one to line up. Then both play in step.', practical_application='An engineer sets a start-time offset so a video deck under machine control chases the DAW and both hit the same frame at the scene''s start.', purpose_function='Aligns a slaved machine or session to a master timecode reference, compensating for differing start points so recorded or controlled material locks to the correct position.', related_terms=ARRAY['Timecode offset','Chase lock','MIDI Machine Control','SMPTE timecode','Synchronization']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Locking a deck to a DAW at the right frame','Offsetting a session start to match a reel''s timecode','Aligning a slaved machine under chase lock']::text[] WHERE id='c10eb829-8328-418a-8814-f9177971b6df';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Arrangement and mix technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Overloading a transition with so many effects it becomes cluttered','Neglecting transitions so sections change abruptly and flatly','Automating effects without matching the arrangement''s energy']::text[], definition='The point and treatment where one section of a musical arrangement moves into another, for example verse to chorus, shaped in the mix with tools such as fills, risers, sweeps, filter and effect automation, and level moves to guide the listener across the change.', plain_english='A song moves from one part to the next, like verse to chorus. The transition is that hand-off. Mixers use effects and automation to smooth or lift it.', practical_application='Into a chorus, the engineer automates a riser, a filter sweep, and a short reverb throw on the last vocal word to lift energy across the transition.', purpose_function='Maintains momentum and clarity between sections, using production and mix moves to signal, smooth, or heighten changes so the arrangement flows and the listener stays engaged.', related_terms=ARRAY['Riser','Filter sweep','Automation','Reverb throw','Drum fill']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Building a lift into a chorus','Smoothing a bridge back into a verse','Adding a riser and sweep between sections']::text[] WHERE id='8f7eb2f1-f560-4e24-b14e-2e99419d166e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Song arrangement / structure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making the breakdown so long that listeners lose interest','Removing every element so the section loses all momentum','Forgetting to build energy back up before the drop']::text[], definition='A breakdown is a section of a track, common in electronic and dance music, where the arrangement is stripped back—often removing drums or bass—to lower the energy before rebuilding toward a drop or chorus. It creates tension and contrast within the song''s overall dynamic shape.', plain_english='A breakdown is a part where most instruments drop out. The energy gets quiet for a while. It builds tension before the big part returns.', practical_application='A mix engineer preparing a club edit lengthens the breakdown, automating a filter and reverb so tension rises smoothly into the drop.', purpose_function='It resets the listener''s ear and builds anticipation, giving the following high-energy section more impact by contrast.', related_terms=ARRAY['Drop section','Section transition','Energy development','Density change','Extended intro']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Extending a breakdown for a DJ-friendly club mix','Automating a filter sweep through the breakdown into the drop']::text[] WHERE id='d49d9fe7-4955-40a5-9775-9c68d61ffb3f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Song arrangement / structure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Mixing the chorus at the same energy as the verse so it lacks lift','Overcrowding the chorus so the hook gets buried','Changing the melody each time and weakening recognition']::text[], definition='The chorus is the recurring central section of a song that carries its main hook, usually the highest-energy and most memorable part, returning several times with consistent lyrics and melody.', plain_english='The chorus is the main, catchy part of a song. It comes back again and again. It usually has the biggest sound and the hook.', practical_application='When mixing, the engineer lifts the chorus with a wider stereo image, added layers, and slightly higher level so it feels bigger than the verse.', purpose_function='It delivers the song''s core hook and emotional payoff, anchoring the listener and shaping the track''s energy peaks.', related_terms=ARRAY['Verse','Half chorus','Double chorus','Section transition','Instrumental section']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Automating level and width so the chorus opens up','Deciding which layers enter only at the chorus']::text[] WHERE id='bfa2ecae-6980-4985-b471-acf797de76ac';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Song arrangement / structure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Peaking too early so the real climax feels flat','Leaving no dynamic room, so the climax cannot sound bigger','Adding so many layers that the peak turns muddy']::text[], definition='A climactic section is the point of greatest intensity in a song''s arrangement, where energy, density, and dynamics reach their peak before resolving. It is the emotional and sonic high point the arrangement builds toward.', plain_english='This is the biggest, most intense part of a song. Everything builds up to it. After it, the song usually calms down.', practical_application='A mixer keeps the final chorus fullest and loudest, holding back headroom and layers in earlier sections so the peak lands hardest.', purpose_function='It provides the arrangement''s emotional peak and reward, giving the build-up a clear destination.', related_terms=ARRAY['Energy development','Chorus section','Density change','Drop section','Section transition']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Reserving the widest arrangement for the song''s climax','Planning automation so energy rises into the peak']::text[] WHERE id='bf971079-2249-409a-863a-83c7b4221ad3';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Song arrangement / structure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making the contrast so extreme it feels like a different song','Adding no contrast, so sections blur together','Forgetting to lead the ear back to the main section']::text[], definition='A contrast section is a passage deliberately arranged to differ from the surrounding material—in instrumentation, harmony, rhythm, or energy—so it stands out and refreshes the listener. A bridge is a common example.', plain_english='A contrast section sounds different from the parts around it. It might change the instruments, chords, or feel. This keeps the song interesting.', practical_application='The engineer may pan, filter, or re-voice a bridge so it clearly contrasts the choruses, then opens back up when the final chorus returns.', purpose_function='It breaks repetition and adds variety, preventing listener fatigue and setting up the return of familiar sections.', related_terms=ARRAY['Instrumentation change','Texture change','Harmonic transition','Section transition','Breakdown section']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Treating a bridge to contrast the choruses','Using a filtered, sparse section for relief before the final hook']::text[] WHERE id='3eefc9e0-beea-4fa7-ae7b-c1ef524f2018';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Arrangement dynamics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Keeping density constant so nothing feels dynamic','Piling on layers until the mix masks itself','Confusing loudness with density']::text[], definition='Density change refers to varying the number of simultaneous musical elements or the fullness of an arrangement over time, thinning or thickening the mix to shape energy and movement.', plain_english='Density is how full a mix sounds at once. A density change adds or removes parts. This makes the song feel like it moves.', practical_application='A mixer thins the arrangement in verses—muting pads and doubles—then thickens it in the chorus to make the hook feel larger.', purpose_function='Controlling density guides listener energy, creates contrast between sections, and keeps arrangements from feeling static or cluttered.', related_terms=ARRAY['Texture change','Instrumentation change','Energy development','Breakdown section','Chorus section']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Thinning a verse to set up a fuller chorus','Muting doubles to reduce clutter in a busy section']::text[] WHERE id='940c15ea-c4d8-4f37-b3bd-ad164c59f414';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Song arrangement / structure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Repeating the chorus identically so it feels static','Making the section too long and losing momentum','Not adding variation on the second pass']::text[], definition='A double chorus is an arrangement choice where the chorus is played twice back-to-back, or extended to double its usual length, to emphasize the hook—commonly at the end of a song.', plain_english='A double chorus is the chorus played two times in a row. It makes the catchy part last longer. Songs often do this at the end.', practical_application='For a final section, the engineer arranges two choruses in a row, often adding ad-libs or extra layers on the second to keep it building.', purpose_function='It maximizes the hook''s impact and gives a strong, memorable ending or peak.', related_terms=ARRAY['Chorus section','Half chorus','Tag ending','Climactic section','Extended outro']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Building a big finish with two choruses back to back','Adding ad-libs on the repeated chorus']::text[] WHERE id='a77b3f3a-5ca1-46b6-b1b0-c701e721688c';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Song arrangement / structure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['No contrast before the drop so it lacks impact','Over-limiting so the drop is loud but lifeless','Cluttering the low end so the bass loses punch']::text[], definition='The drop is the high-energy release section in electronic dance music that follows a build-up, where the full arrangement—typically heavy bass and drums—enters to deliver the track''s main impact.', plain_english='The drop is the big, high-energy part in dance music. It hits after a build-up. The bass and drums come in full force.', practical_application='The engineer maximizes impact at the drop with full-range bass and punchy drums, often adding a brief silence or filter right before it lands.', purpose_function='It delivers the payoff the build-up promises, providing the track''s main dance-floor moment.', related_terms=ARRAY['Breakdown section','Section transition','Energy development','Climactic section','Density change']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding a pre-drop silence for impact','Automating a filter build into the drop']::text[] WHERE id='23730017-b9d8-45cb-8d66-8730db9a6319';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Transitions', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Abrupt level jumps that feel jarring when unintended','Relying on volume alone with no build elements','Overusing risers until they lose effect']::text[], definition='A dynamic transition moves between two sections mainly through a change in energy or loudness—such as a build, a drop in level, or a sudden cut—rather than through melody or harmony alone.', plain_english='A dynamic transition connects two parts by changing the energy. It might get louder, softer, or cut out. This signals a new section.', practical_application='A mixer automates a swell of reverb and rising level to carry a quiet verse into a loud chorus.', purpose_function='It smooths or dramatizes the move between sections and cues the listener that the song''s energy is shifting.', related_terms=ARRAY['Section transition','Rhythmic transition','Harmonic transition','Energy development','Breakdown section']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Automating a build from verse into chorus','Using a downlifter to drop into a breakdown']::text[] WHERE id='8e172b1f-b344-4175-926f-a2b9f10f445a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Arrangement dynamics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Flat energy that makes the song feel static','Peaking too soon with nowhere left to build','Ignoring the arrangement and relying only on level']::text[], definition='Energy development is the planned rise and fall of intensity across a song, achieved through arrangement, dynamics, density, and effects, giving the track a sense of direction and momentum.', plain_english='Energy development is how a song''s intensity changes over time. It builds up and eases off. This gives the song a shape and a journey.', practical_application='The engineer maps the song''s energy curve, then uses automation and layer entrances so each section is a step up or down as planned.', purpose_function='It keeps listeners engaged by shaping tension and release across the whole arrangement.', related_terms=ARRAY['Density change','Climactic section','Section transition','Dynamic transition','Breakdown section']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Mapping an energy curve before mixing','Staging layer entrances to build across sections']::text[] WHERE id='bc217d7d-9b94-4fb1-8279-09133ba1b9fd';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Song arrangement / structure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making the intro too busy for DJs to mix over','Adding vocals too early in a DJ intro','Making it so long it bores non-DJ listeners']::text[], definition='An extended intro is a lengthened opening section, often beat- or groove-based with few melodic elements, designed to give DJs time to mix the track in smoothly.', plain_english='An extended intro is a longer start to a song. It often has just drums or a simple groove. DJs use it to mix one track into another.', practical_application='For a club version, the engineer builds an intro of 16 or 32 bars using drums and a filtered element so DJs can beatmatch and blend.', purpose_function='It makes a track DJ-friendly and eases the listener in before the main material.', related_terms=ARRAY['Extended outro','Section transition','Breakdown section','Instrumental section','Drop section']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Producing a DJ-friendly club edit','Building a 32-bar drum intro for beatmatching']::text[] WHERE id='0ac611aa-3db7-421b-926c-3dd3808d1d24';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Song arrangement / structure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Ending too abruptly for DJ use','Leaving melodic parts that clash when mixing out','Fading manually instead of arranging a clean outro']::text[], definition='An extended outro is a lengthened closing section, usually stripping back to drums or groove elements, that lets DJs mix out of the track smoothly and gives the song a gradual ending.', plain_english='An extended outro is a longer ending. It often keeps just the beat going. DJs use it to mix out of the track.', practical_application='The engineer arranges an outro that removes vocals and melody, leaving drums and bass for a set number of bars so DJs can transition out.', purpose_function='It supports DJ mixing and provides a controlled wind-down instead of an abrupt stop.', related_terms=ARRAY['Extended intro','Tag ending','Section transition','Instrumental section','Breakdown section']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Making a DJ-friendly outro for club play','Stripping to drums for the last 32 bars']::text[] WHERE id='6a33c88f-7b34-457a-9488-4f3a96287cb4';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Song arrangement / structure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Cutting the chorus so short the hook does not register','Making an awkward edit that breaks the phrasing','Using it so often the full chorus loses impact']::text[], definition='A half chorus is a chorus shortened to roughly half its normal length, used to vary repetition or move the song forward more quickly.', plain_english='A half chorus is a chorus cut to about half its length. It gives a quick taste of the hook. Then the song moves on.', practical_application='The producer uses a half chorus after the first verse for a quick hook, saving the full chorus for later to build anticipation.', purpose_function='It keeps a song from feeling repetitive and can speed the arrangement toward the next section.', related_terms=ARRAY['Chorus section','Double chorus','Shortened verse','Section transition','Tag ending']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Editing a radio version to tighten length','Teasing the hook early with a half chorus']::text[] WHERE id='8520e4c1-2149-4f78-8957-3b15ea6d3df8';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Transitions', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Jarring key jumps with no pivot or lead-in','Ignoring the bass note through the change','Overusing modulation so it loses impact']::text[], definition='A harmonic transition connects two sections through chord movement—such as a pivot chord, modulation, or turnaround—so the harmony leads the ear naturally from one part to the next.', plain_english='A harmonic transition uses chords to move between parts. The chords lead your ear forward. It can also change the key.', practical_application='When editing a mashup, the engineer inserts a short chord passage or modulation so two sections in different keys join smoothly.', purpose_function='It creates smooth, musical connections between sections and can lift energy through a key change.', related_terms=ARRAY['Section transition','Rhythmic transition','Dynamic transition','Contrast section','Chorus section']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Modulating up for a final chorus','Joining two songs in different keys for a mashup']::text[] WHERE id='452b2c64-f121-4d1f-b4f3-01af61159073';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Song arrangement / structure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Leaving the section too static without variation','Failing to feature the lead instrument clearly','Making it so long the song loses focus']::text[], definition='An instrumental section is a passage with no lead vocals, featuring instruments alone—such as a solo, intro, or break—to add variety and showcase the arrangement.', plain_english='An instrumental section has no singing. Instruments carry the music instead. It can be a solo, an intro, or a break.', practical_application='The mixer features a guitar solo section by bringing the lead forward and pulling back rhythm parts so the instrument stands out.', purpose_function='It gives the listener a rest from vocals, highlights musicianship, and adds contrast to the arrangement.', related_terms=ARRAY['Breakdown section','Section transition','Contrast section','Extended intro','Texture change']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Featuring a solo in the mix','Arranging an instrumental break between verses']::text[] WHERE id='bbbccbb6-404d-4af9-9ac8-46a1dc57c533';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Arrangement dynamics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Changing too many parts at once so the song loses identity','Keeping instrumentation identical across all sections','Poor gain staging when new instruments enter']::text[], definition='An instrumentation change is the addition, removal, or swapping of instruments between sections to alter the arrangement''s color and energy.', plain_english='An instrumentation change means switching which instruments play. You add, remove, or swap parts. This changes the song''s feel between sections.', practical_application='The producer drops the full band in the verse to just piano and voice, then brings drums and bass back for the chorus.', purpose_function='It creates contrast and shapes energy, distinguishing sections and guiding the listener through the arrangement.', related_terms=ARRAY['Texture change','Density change','Contrast section','Instrumental section','Energy development']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Stripping to piano and voice in a verse','Adding strings only in the final chorus']::text[] WHERE id='08aef49f-4f9c-4752-b673-21df9bc5e729';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Arrangement', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Adding parts without a plan so sections compete','Trying to fix arrangement problems only by mixing','Cluttered arrangements that mask key elements']::text[], definition='A production arrangement is the way all recorded and programmed elements—instruments, vocals, effects, and sections—are organized across a track''s timeline to serve the song. It covers what plays, when, and in what combination.', plain_english='A production arrangement is the plan for a whole track. It sets what plays and when. It shapes the song from start to finish.', practical_application='Before mixing, the engineer reviews the arrangement, muting clutter and confirming each section''s parts so the mix serves the song.', purpose_function='It gives the song structure, flow, and energy, ensuring each section supports the whole.', related_terms=ARRAY['Section transition','Instrumentation change','Density change','Energy development','Chorus section']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Reviewing the arrangement before starting a mix','Deciding which parts play in each section']::text[] WHERE id='553c8584-904b-4e5b-a22e-4ad4ab433888';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Transitions', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Fills that clash with the tempo or feel','Overusing fills so they lose their signaling power','No transition so section changes feel abrupt']::text[], definition='A rhythmic transition uses drums or percussion—such as a fill, a break, or a change in groove—to lead from one section into the next.', plain_english='A rhythmic transition uses drums to move between parts. A drum fill is a common example. It signals that a new section is coming.', practical_application='The engineer places a drum fill in the last bar of the verse to launch into the chorus, sometimes adding a cymbal swell for lift.', purpose_function='It cues section changes and keeps momentum by carrying the groove across the join.', related_terms=ARRAY['Section transition','Dynamic transition','Harmonic transition','Energy development','Drop section']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding a drum fill before the chorus','Using a beat change to signal the bridge']::text[] WHERE id='39d96337-d4e3-4d3d-af9c-b121f95df20f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Transitions', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Hard cuts that feel jarring when smoothness is wanted','Overloading transitions with too many effects','Ignoring transitions so the song feels stiff']::text[], definition='A section transition is the moment and technique by which a song moves from one structural part to another—such as verse to chorus—using arrangement, dynamics, effects, or edits to make the change feel intentional.', plain_english='A section transition is where one part of a song becomes the next. Producers use fills, sweeps, or edits here. It makes the change feel smooth or dramatic.', practical_application='The mixer smooths a verse-to-chorus move with a reverb throw, a riser, and a drum fill so the sections join cleanly.', purpose_function='It connects the song''s parts so structure feels natural and the listener follows the flow.', related_terms=ARRAY['Dynamic transition','Rhythmic transition','Harmonic transition','Breakdown section','Energy development']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding a riser and reverb throw between sections','Editing a clean cut from breakdown to drop']::text[] WHERE id='fa8c526c-5fd7-4f0a-916a-d3242f77cba9';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Song arrangement / structure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Cutting so much the lyric or story stops making sense','Creating an edit that breaks the phrasing','Shortening every verse so the song feels rushed']::text[], definition='A shortened verse is a verse trimmed below its usual length, often used later in a song to reach the chorus faster and maintain momentum.', plain_english='A shortened verse is a verse made shorter than normal. It gets to the chorus faster. This keeps the song moving.', practical_application='For a radio edit, the engineer cuts the second verse in half so the song reaches the hook sooner and fits a time limit.', purpose_function='It sustains momentum and avoids repetition, especially in later sections.', related_terms=ARRAY['Half chorus','Chorus section','Section transition','Tag ending','Instrumental section']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Trimming a verse for a radio edit','Halving the last verse to reach the final chorus']::text[] WHERE id='90cfcd81-3c89-4129-b778-82ac3e354885';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Song arrangement / structure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Repeating the tag too many times so it drags','Keeping full energy so there is no sense of ending','Mismatching the tag''s key or timing when edited']::text[], definition='A tag ending is a closing device in which a final phrase—often the last line of the chorus or hook—is repeated one or more times to conclude the song.', plain_english='A tag ending repeats the last line of a song a few times to finish. It drives the hook home. Then the song ends.', practical_application='The producer repeats the final hook line three times, dropping instruments on each pass, so the song winds down on the tag.', purpose_function='It provides a memorable, satisfying conclusion and reinforces the hook.', related_terms=ARRAY['Extended outro','Double chorus','Chorus section','Section transition','Half chorus']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Repeating the last hook line to close a song','Thinning the arrangement across a tag ending']::text[] WHERE id='e8ede57e-1ae9-48e5-a688-d7952e11037a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Arrangement dynamics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing texture with loudness','Keeping one texture throughout so sections blur','Changing texture so drastically it feels disjointed']::text[], definition='A texture change alters the layering and character of sound—how many parts play, how they combine, and their timbral quality—to shift the feel of a section.', plain_english='Texture is how sounds layer and blend together. A texture change alters that mix of parts. It changes the mood of a section.', practical_application='The engineer moves from a dense, distorted texture in the chorus to a sparse, clean one in the bridge for contrast.', purpose_function='It creates contrast and interest, distinguishing sections and guiding emotional shifts.', related_terms=ARRAY['Density change','Instrumentation change','Contrast section','Energy development','Breakdown section']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Switching to a sparse texture in a bridge','Layering parts to thicken texture into the chorus']::text[] WHERE id='d8e1397d-a0ca-4568-a2be-da9efc64b708';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Asset management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Inconsistent metadata that makes searching unreliable','Treating it as mere storage and skipping tagging','No access controls, risking overwritten masters']::text[], definition='Digital asset management is the practice and software system for storing, organizing, cataloging, retrieving, and controlling access to digital files—such as audio, images, and documents—using metadata and a central repository.', plain_english='Digital asset management is a system for storing and finding digital files. It tags files with information so you can search them. It also controls who can use each file.', practical_application='A studio catalogs stems, samples, and masters in such a system with tags for project, tempo, and rights, so any engineer can locate and reuse them.', purpose_function='It keeps large libraries of files organized and searchable, protects master versions, and streamlines collaboration and reuse.', related_terms=ARRAY['Media asset management','Media server','High-resolution media','Low-resolution proxy','Metadata']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Cataloging sample libraries with searchable tags','Managing rights and versions across a studio''s projects']::text[] WHERE id='382aab38-4963-4c24-b353-061b4ef1dd40';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Media formats', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming higher resolution always sounds better regardless of the source','Running out of storage by keeping high-res copies everywhere','Editing high-res files on underpowered systems and hitting limits']::text[], definition='High-resolution media are audio or video files captured or stored at high sample rates, bit depths, or pixel resolutions, preserving maximum quality for editing and mastering—as opposed to lower-quality proxies.', plain_english='High-resolution media are files with lots of detail. Audio uses high sample rates and bit depth. These big files keep the best quality for editing.', practical_application='An engineer edits and masters from 24-bit, 96 kHz files, keeping them as the archive master while delivering smaller consumer formats.', purpose_function='They preserve full fidelity for critical editing, processing, and mastering, and future-proof archived masters.', related_terms=ARRAY['Low-resolution proxy','Digital asset management','Media asset management','Sample rate','Bit depth']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Archiving masters at high sample rate and bit depth','Choosing high-res files for the final master']::text[] WHERE id='0085beb2-17d2-4d4e-ad09-12baf25f4e8a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Media formats', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Forgetting to relink to high-res before final export','Deleting the original high-res files, leaving only proxies','Assuming proxy quality reflects the finished product']::text[], definition='A low-resolution proxy is a smaller, lower-quality stand-in file generated from high-resolution media, used to edit smoothly on limited hardware; the system relinks to the original high-resolution files for final output.', plain_english='A proxy is a small, lower-quality copy of a big file. You edit with the light copy so the computer runs smoothly. At the end, the software swaps back to the full-quality file.', practical_application='An editor generates proxies for large sessions, works quickly on a laptop, then switches back to high-resolution media to render the master.', purpose_function='It enables fast, responsive editing on modest systems while protecting final quality by relinking to the originals.', related_terms=ARRAY['High-resolution media','Media asset management','Digital asset management','Media server','Metadata']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Editing a large session on a laptop with proxies','Relinking to full-resolution media before final render']::text[] WHERE id='96c2c933-730e-4165-a2cb-8d9aa7fee957';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Asset management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Weak or missing metadata that breaks retrieval','Confusing simple storage with a managed workflow','No versioning, causing confusion over the latest cut']::text[], definition='Media asset management is a specialized form of digital asset management focused on time-based media—audio and video—handling ingest, cataloging with metadata, versioning, and controlled retrieval, often integrating with editing and playout systems.', plain_english='Media asset management is a system for organizing audio and video. It logs each clip with details so you can find it fast. It is built for media used in editing and broadcast.', practical_application='A post house ingests all field recordings into such a system, tagging scene, take, and timecode so editors pull exactly the clips they need.', purpose_function='It centralizes large media libraries, tracks versions, and speeds retrieval for production, post, and broadcast workflows.', related_terms=ARRAY['Digital asset management','Media server','High-resolution media','Low-resolution proxy','Metadata']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Ingesting and tagging field recordings for a post team','Retrieving media by timecode and scene in a broadcast facility']::text[] WHERE id='8ff3e7a5-2eb0-40ac-8572-158192a199a2';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Infrastructure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Undersizing network bandwidth for high-res streaming','No backup or redundancy on a central server','Assuming a media server replaces proper asset management']::text[], definition='A media server is a networked computer or appliance that stores media files and streams or serves them to client systems on demand, providing centralized, shared access to audio and video content.', plain_english='A media server is a computer that stores media files and shares them over a network. Other machines pull audio or video from it. It keeps everything in one central place.', practical_application='A facility keeps all project media on a media server so multiple edit suites access the same files over the network without duplicating them.', purpose_function='It centralizes storage, enables shared access and collaboration, and offloads large media from individual workstations.', related_terms=ARRAY['Media asset management','Digital asset management','High-resolution media','Low-resolution proxy','Shared storage']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Sharing project media across multiple edit suites','Streaming assets to workstations from central storage']::text[] WHERE id='3e982b80-09cf-4e90-9ac1-aa84d6e1dd18';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Ableton Live recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Forgetting Fixed Length is on and getting clips cut to a set length unexpectedly','Setting a length that does not match the intended phrase','Assuming it applies to Arrangement recording as well']::text[], definition='Fixed-length recording is an Ableton Live option, available on Push and via clip record settings, that makes newly recorded Session clips a predetermined number of bars rather than recording open-ended until manually stopped.', plain_english='Fixed-length recording sets how long a new clip will be before you record. Live records exactly that many bars, then stops. You do not have to stop it by hand.', practical_application='A producer sets a fixed length of four bars on Push, arms a track, and records a loop that automatically stops and loops after four bars.', purpose_function='It lets performers capture loops of a known length hands-free, keeping recordings tidy and in phrase with the arrangement.', related_terms=ARRAY['Session recording','Loop brace','Global Quantization','Session View','Reduced latency when monitoring']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording four-bar loops hands-free on Push','Capturing set-length Session clips during a jam']::text[] WHERE id='44b8727d-c8f2-4ee5-b8a6-ae5878fe0911';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Ableton Live clip editing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the loop brace with the clip start marker','Moving the brace without enabling Loop, so no looping occurs','Setting loop points off the grid and causing timing drift']::text[], definition='In Ableton Live''s Clip View, the loop brace is the bracket in the sample or MIDI editor that marks the region a clip loops over. Its start and end can be dragged to set loop position and length, independent of the clip''s start marker.', plain_english='The loop brace is the bracket that shows a clip''s loop area in Ableton. You drag its ends to set where the loop starts and stops. The clip can still begin at a different start marker.', practical_application='An artist drags the loop brace around a two-bar groove so the clip loops that section, while the start marker leads in from an earlier point.', purpose_function='It defines exactly which part of a clip repeats, letting you loop a chosen region while starting playback elsewhere.', related_terms=ARRAY['Session recording','Fixed-length recording','Start marker','Warp Markers','Clip View']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Looping a chosen bar range inside a clip','Leading into a loop from an earlier start marker']::text[] WHERE id='81044de0-d357-475a-9fc4-f383ad0dc529';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Ableton Live recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Expecting it to remove all latency, including effects on the monitored track','Leaving it on and getting misaligned automation when latency plug-ins are in use','Not realizing it is saved per Set and disabled by default']::text[], definition='Reduced Latency When Monitoring is an option in Ableton Live''s Options menu that bypasses delay compensation for a monitored track, so a performer hears input with less delay while recording. Latency within the monitored signal''s own device chain still remains.', plain_english='This Ableton option lowers the delay you hear when monitoring a track you are recording. It skips the delay compensation Live normally adds. Effects on the track itself can still cause some delay.', practical_application='A player enables this option before tracking a soft-synth part so the keys respond quickly, accepting that other tracks may sit slightly out of alignment.', purpose_function='It reduces monitoring lag so playing software instruments or singing along feels more responsive during recording.', related_terms=ARRAY['Session recording','Fixed-length recording','Delay Compensation','Monitoring','Latency']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Tracking a soft-synth part with a responsive feel','Reducing monitoring lag while singing to a project']::text[] WHERE id='46276a05-0e81-4da8-9c69-075e326dd76f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Ableton Live recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording into the wrong slot or scene by mis-clicking','Forgetting to arm the track, so no record buttons appear','Confusing Session recording with Arrangement recording']::text[], definition='In Ableton Live, session recording is the capture of audio or MIDI into the clip slots of the Session View: arming a track reveals Clip Record buttons in empty slots, and the Session Record button or a Clip Record button starts recording into the selected slots.', plain_english='Session recording means recording clips into the grid of Ableton''s Session View. You arm a track, then empty slots show record buttons. Pressing one records a new clip into that slot.', practical_application='A producer arms several tracks, hits clip record buttons to lay down drum, bass, and synth loops into a scene, then launches them together to build a groove.', purpose_function='It lets you build and capture loops and ideas as independent clips you can launch, layer, and rearrange non-linearly.', related_terms=ARRAY['Fixed-length recording','Loop brace','Session View','Global Quantization','Reduced latency when monitoring']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Laying down loop ideas into Session slots','Building a scene of clips during a live jam']::text[] WHERE id='85da7fdc-6d3f-47e7-a268-1df5fe0f2f3c';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Warping / time-stretch setting', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming it changes pitch or tempo itself; it only fills the gaps between transients','Confusing it with Warp Modes such as Complex or Texture','Blaming clicks on it when the real fix is the Transient Envelope fade']::text[], definition='Transient Loop Mode is a per-clip setting within Ableton Live''s Beats Warp Mode. It controls what happens to the audio in each slice between transient markers whenever that slice has to stretch beyond the length of the underlying sample, most often when you drop the tempo below the recording''s own. Three choices are offered. With Loop Off, the slice sounds a single time and then goes quiet for any leftover duration. Loop Forward fills the extra time by repeating part of the slice, running from an interior zero-crossing point through to the slice''s end. Loop Back-and-Forth also repeats, but alternates direction, playing the slice forward and then in reverse over and over.', plain_english='This is a Beats Warp Mode setting in Ableton Live. Live cuts a clip into chunks at each transient. A transient is the sharp attack at the start of a sound. When a chunk must last longer than the sound, this setting fills the gap. It can stay silent, loop forward, or bounce back and forth.', practical_application='A technician slowing a drum loop picks Loop Forward to sustain each hit''s tail, or Loop Off for a gated, choppy feel.', purpose_function='Controls how the gap between transients is filled when warping, so slowed beats sound smooth or deliberately gated instead of clicky or empty.', related_terms=ARRAY['Beats Warp Mode','Transient markers','Warp markers','Transient Envelope','Time-stretching']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Slowing a sampled break for a half-time section','Cleaning up gaps when warping a live drum take','Designing a gated, stuttering percussion texture']::text[] WHERE id='0591c905-a184-409f-9d47-4eadbbaf4c9e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Latency / delay compensation control', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Leaving it on during mixdown, which can change how delay-heavy plug-ins sit','Expecting it to remove all latency; only plug-ins over the threshold are affected','Confusing it with the audio interface buffer-size setting']::text[], definition='In Cubase and Nuendo, this is a command that cuts the delay-compensation latency you feel while tracking. Switching it on tells the program to hold back the timing offset of any VST plug-in on a recorded or monitored channel once that plug-in''s own delay passes a set limit. Playing then responds close to real time, and the tool aims to disturb the overall sound as little as it can. Engineers switch it off again when they move from recording to mixing.', plain_english='Some plug-ins add delay. Cubase shifts tracks to line them back up, but that adds latency when you play live. This button cuts that latency for recording. It quiets high-delay plug-ins on the tracks you are using. You turn it off again when mixing.', practical_application='A technician clicks Constrain Delay Compensation before overdubbing a soft-synth part through a latency-heavy chain, then switches it off to check the full mix.', purpose_function='Lets performers record and monitor VST instruments or live audio in near real time without the lag that full delay compensation would otherwise add.', related_terms=ARRAY['Plug-in delay compensation','Latency','VST instrument','Direct monitoring','ASIO-Guard']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Overdubbing a keyboard part over a heavily processed mix','Recording a vocal while monitoring through look-ahead plug-ins','Reducing lag before a live software-instrument take']::text[] WHERE id='f3a16158-ae83-4e1d-9fbd-163bd2699663';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Quantize / groove tool', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Thinking it copies the actual notes rather than only their timing','Extracting a groove from poorly timed material and spreading its errors','Forgetting that audio needs hitpoints before a usable groove can be made']::text[], definition='A Cubase/Nuendo function that generates a groove quantize preset from the timing of selected material. The rhythmic feel of MIDI notes, or of audio hitpoints, is extracted and stored as a groove template that can then be applied to quantize other events to that same feel.', plain_english='This makes a feel template from a part you like. Cubase reads the timing of the notes or beats. It saves that timing as a groove. You can then push other parts onto that same feel. It copies swing and human timing, not the notes.', practical_application='A technician selects a well-played drum part, chooses Create Groove, then applies the new groove preset to a stiff bass line so it locks to the drummer''s feel.', purpose_function='Captures the timing feel of a performance or loop so the same groove can be imposed on other tracks for a unified rhythmic feel.', related_terms=ARRAY['Groove Quantize','Quantize Panel','Hitpoints','Quantize preset','Swing']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Matching a bass line to a drummer''s groove','Reusing the swing of a favorite loop across a project','Building a custom groove library from live takes']::text[] WHERE id='4656d3a4-d2eb-41ca-81c2-f3c67d71d60a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording feature', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Setting the pre-record time to zero and expecting recovery','Confusing it with MIDI Retrospective Record','Assuming buffered audio survives after you close the project']::text[], definition='A Cubase/Nuendo feature, set by Audio Pre-Record Seconds in the record preferences, that continuously captures incoming audio into a buffer during stop or playback, so that up to one minute of material played before you pressed Record can be recovered by dragging it out into a recorded event.', plain_english='Cubase can listen even before you hit record. It keeps the last bit of what you played in memory. If you catch a great take too late, it is not lost. You can pull that audio onto the track. You set how many seconds it keeps.', practical_application='A technician who forgot to arm recording drags the just-played guitar phrase out of the pre-record buffer instead of asking the player to repeat it.', purpose_function='Prevents losing spontaneous performances or warm-up takes that happen before recording is armed and started.', related_terms=ARRAY['Audio Pre-Record Seconds','Recording','Buffer','Retrospective Record','Punch in']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Catching a warm-up take the player nailed by accident','Recovering a phrase played before Record was pressed','Capturing a spontaneous moment during rehearsal']::text[] WHERE id='b692abd7-2f23-477f-bd3e-e889fdb6e425';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Quantize tool / dialog', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using full-strength quantize when iterative would keep a natural feel','Ignoring catch range, so distant notes snap to the wrong beat','Confusing panel settings with the quick Quantize command''s fixed value']::text[], definition='A Cubase/Nuendo dialog that centralizes quantize settings, letting you define how audio or MIDI is quantized to the grid or to a groove. It exposes controls such as grid value, swing, catch range, non-quantize, iterative strength and randomize, and lets you manage and create quantize presets.', plain_english='This is the window that holds all quantize controls. You set the grid, swing, and how strong the snap is. It works for MIDI and for audio. You can save your settings as presets. It is the one place to shape timing.', practical_application='A technician opens the Quantize Panel to add swing and set iterative strength to about 60 percent so a drum part tightens without sounding robotic.', purpose_function='Provides fine control over how tightly and in what feel events are pulled to musical positions, beyond a simple one-click quantize.', related_terms=ARRAY['Quantize','Groove Quantize','Iterative Quantize','Swing','AudioWarp']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding controlled swing to a hi-hat pattern','Tightening a sloppy MIDI take by a set percentage','Setting up a custom quantize preset for a session']::text[] WHERE id='d6a829ea-ba7d-4c30-9b4d-e3f2c9e0ad44';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Audio/MIDI editing action', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Expecting to slip audio beyond the source clip''s ends','Confusing slipping content with moving the whole event','Forgetting the modifier keys and accidentally resizing instead']::text[], definition='A Cubase/Nuendo editing action that shifts the audio or MIDI content inside an event left or right without moving the event''s start and end borders. The visible window stays fixed while the underlying material slides, and audio content cannot slip past the boundaries of its source clip.', plain_english='This moves the sound inside a clip, not the clip. The clip''s edges stay put on the timeline. The audio inside slides left or right under them. It is like moving film behind a fixed frame. Audio cannot slide past the ends of the real file.', practical_application='A technician holds the slip modifier and drags so the downbeat inside a fixed loop region lands exactly on the bar line.', purpose_function='Lets you re-time what plays within a fixed region, useful for aligning a performance to a cut without changing the region''s placement.', related_terms=ARRAY['Audio event','Audio clip','Sizing Applies Time Stretch','Snap','Trim']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Nudging a phrase inside a locked-length region','Aligning dialogue under a fixed picture edit','Fixing timing without disturbing surrounding events']::text[] WHERE id='524b2056-c8a2-4cbe-8f14-81fd9314cb40';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Loop recording window', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Forgetting to transfer POLAR layers into the sequence before moving on','Confusing POLAR loop recording with ordinary cycle recording on a track','Overwriting a keeper layer by not saving passes']::text[], definition='In MOTU Digital Performer, POLAR (Performance Oriented Loop Audio Recording) is a dedicated window for continuous, cycle-based loop recording that overdubs each pass as a new layer, letting you build up stacked vocal comps, beatboxing or layered loops in real time before transferring the results into the main sequence.', plain_english='POLAR is a loop-recording window in Digital Performer. It records over and over around a loop. Each new pass stacks on the last one. You can build thick layers of vocals or beats fast. When done, you send the layers into your song.', practical_application='A technician loop-records a singer stacking harmonies in POLAR, keeps the best layers, then transfers them to tracks in the main project.', purpose_function='Enables fast, hands-free loop layering and comping so ideas can be captured and stacked without stopping to manage tracks.', related_terms=ARRAY['Loop recording','Overdub','Comping','Cycle recording','Layering']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Stacking backing-vocal harmonies quickly','Building a layered beatbox groove','Capturing rapid-fire guitar loop ideas']::text[] WHERE id='2ee11954-c88f-49b1-9633-8e4cf228f9be';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Comping / take editing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a comp section with a whole take','Creating tiny sections that cause audible jumps between takes','Forgetting to flatten the comp before exporting stems']::text[], definition='In Logic Pro''s take folders, a comp section is one region of the comp, a span selected from a particular take, shown in the comp area at the top of the take folder. Adjacent comp sections come from different takes, and the vertical lines between them mark the transitions in the assembled composite.', plain_english='Logic stacks your recording takes in a folder. A comp section is one slice of the final mix-and-match take. Each slice is chosen from one take. The finished comp is made of several sections. Lines show where one take hands off to the next.', practical_application='A technician drags across the second take for the chorus, creating a comp section there, while the verses stay pulled from take one.', purpose_function='Lets you assemble one best performance by choosing which take plays during each part of the timeline.', related_terms=ARRAY['Take folder','Take region','Quick Swipe Comping','Comp','Crossfade']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Building a lead vocal from several sung takes','Choosing the tightest drum fill from multiple passes','Swapping one word from an alternate take']::text[] WHERE id='3efa9b3b-5c3e-4b92-92d7-7d73ce942f85';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Channel strip control', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Leaving a mono source on a stereo format and losing metering accuracy','Confusing the input format with the physical input-source assignment','Not knowing left/right formats hide under a long-click']::text[], definition='In Logic Pro, the input format, set by the channel strip''s Format (Channel Mode) control, determines whether a channel strip processes its signal as mono, stereo, left-only, right-only or surround. Long-clicking the control opens a menu to choose among these input formats.', plain_english='This control sets how a channel handles its input. It can be mono or stereo. It can also take just the left or just the right side. Surround is an option too. You click and hold it to choose the format.', practical_application='A technician long-clicks the format control and chooses the right channel to use only one side of a stereo interface input feeding a mono mic.', purpose_function='Matches the channel strip''s processing and metering to the incoming signal, so mono, stereo or single-side sources are handled correctly.', related_terms=ARRAY['Channel strip','Mono','Stereo','Right input channel (Logic)','Level meter']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting a vocal channel to mono','Using only the right side of a stereo input','Switching a track to surround for a film mix']::text[] WHERE id='46b65d7e-3b8a-4bdd-9d3d-879852c6a4c6';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Comping / take editing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Editing take boundaries with Quick Swipe on when you meant to cut','Making too many short swipes that cause clicks','Forgetting to toggle it off to move or trim the take folder']::text[], definition='A Logic Pro take-folder mode in which dragging (swiping) across takes with the Pointer or Pencil selects the swept span from that take for the comp. Selecting a section in one take automatically deselects the same span in the others, so swiping quickly assembles a composite performance.', plain_english='This is Logic''s fast way to build a comp. You drag across the take you want for each part. That part is added to the final take. The same spot in other takes turns off. A few swipes make a full comp.', practical_application='With Quick Swipe on, a technician swipes the strongest phrase from each vocal take to build a finished lead in seconds.', purpose_function='Speeds up comping by letting you paint the best sections into a composite instead of cutting and switching regions by hand.', related_terms=ARRAY['Take folder','Comp section','Comp','Take region','Flatten']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Comping a lead vocal from many takes','Assembling a solo from several passes','Quickly auditioning which take wins each phrase']::text[] WHERE id='87fbaddc-fa03-4dc1-8840-03d9548980d2';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording setting', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using it to mask latency that a correct buffer setting would fix','Setting a large delay that throws off correctly aligned takes','Confusing it with the monitoring latency you hear while playing']::text[], definition='A Logic Pro recording setting that applies a fixed offset, measured in samples, to newly recorded material, shifting it earlier or later to compensate for a consistent timing discrepancy between the input and the project, for example latency that an interface does not report accurately.', plain_english='Recorded audio can land a little early or late. This setting nudges new recordings in time. You set it in samples. A minus value pulls audio earlier; a plus value pushes it later. It fixes a steady timing offset.', practical_application='After noticing recordings land two milliseconds late, a technician sets a negative Recording Delay so future takes sit on the grid.', purpose_function='Corrects a repeatable recording timing offset, so takes line up with the click and existing tracks without hand-nudging every region.', related_terms=ARRAY['Latency','Low Latency Mode','Plug-in delay compensation','I/O buffer','Recording settings']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Fixing takes that consistently land off the grid','Compensating for an interface with unreported latency','Aligning overdubs to a click precisely']::text[] WHERE id='8cd4e511-6df2-4493-b4de-10628feadd1e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Channel strip input format', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording silence because the source is actually on the left channel','Confusing right-channel format with panning hard right','Leaving it set and wondering why half a stereo file is missing']::text[], definition='In Logic Pro, the right channel input format is a channel-strip setting, shown as two circles with the right one filled, in which only the right channel of a stereo audio source is played back and processed, isolating that single side of the stereo signal.', plain_english='A stereo signal has a left and a right side. This setting uses only the right side. Logic ignores the left channel. It is shown by two circles with the right one filled. It helps when your source is on the right input only.', practical_application='A technician sets the channel to the right input format so a mic patched into the right side of a stereo interface input records cleanly as mono.', purpose_function='Lets a channel take just the right side of a stereo input or file, useful when a mono source arrives on the right channel of a stereo pair.', related_terms=ARRAY['Input format selector','Channel strip','Mono','Stereo','Left channel']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Capturing a mono mic wired to the right input','Splitting a stereo file to use only its right side','Fixing a source that only appears on one channel']::text[] WHERE id='a77f9268-77f2-4f67-a24b-644efe924dfa';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Comping / take editing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a take region with the whole take folder','Deleting a take region you may still want for comping','Editing take regions without realizing comps reference them']::text[], definition='In Logic Pro, a take region is one of the individual recorded regions contained within a take folder. Each pass of a cycle or multi-take recording is stored as a separate take region that can be selected, cut, edited or comped inside the folder.', plain_english='When you record several tries, Logic saves each try in a folder. Each try is a take region. You can pick, cut, or edit these regions. Comping chooses parts from different take regions. Together they form the take folder.', practical_application='A technician cuts a take region at a breath point to swap just that phrase from another take during comping.', purpose_function='Stores each recorded pass as an editable unit, so multiple performances can be compared and combined into a comp.', related_terms=ARRAY['Take folder','Comp section','Quick Swipe Comping','Cycle recording','Flatten']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording multiple vocal passes in cycle mode','Cutting a take region to fix one line','Comparing take regions to pick the best drum fill']::text[] WHERE id='44b6c876-6993-4bb3-9686-4f94a9c04003';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording / file management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording to a slow or nearly full drive and getting dropouts','Losing audio by not knowing where files were written','Assuming files sit inside the project when assets are not copied in']::text[], definition='In Logic Pro, the recording path is the file-system location (recording folder) where the audio files created when you record onto audio tracks are written. It is defined in the project''s recording and asset settings and determines where new track recordings are stored on disk.', plain_english='When you record audio, Logic writes a file to disk. The recording path is where those files go. You can set this folder for the project. New track recordings are saved there. Knowing it helps you find and back up your audio.', practical_application='A technician points the recording path to a fast external drive so multitrack sessions record and stream reliably.', purpose_function='Defines where recorded audio is stored, so files stay organized, findable and safely backed up with the project.', related_terms=ARRAY['Recording settings','Project assets','Audio file','Bit depth','Sample rate']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting a session to record to an external SSD','Locating recorded files for backup','Keeping a project''s audio organized on disk']::text[] WHERE id='f4cd3a71-3ff8-4c5e-b82f-6e9b105f1834';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Editing mode', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Leaving it on and accidentally shifting the entire project during a small edit','Confusing it with per-track ripple, which moves only one track','Forgetting that unselected items still move with it']::text[], definition='A REAPER editing mode, Ripple editing all tracks, in which moving, inserting or deleting items shifts all later items on every track by the same amount, keeping the relative timing across the whole project aligned as edits open or close gaps.', plain_english='Turn this on and edits push everything after them. It moves items on all tracks together. Delete a section and the rest slides left to fill it. Insert time and all tracks move right. Everything stays lined up.', practical_application='A technician deletes an unwanted chorus with all-track ripple on, and the rest of the song closes the gap across every track at once.', purpose_function='Keeps a whole arrangement in sync when you cut or insert sections, so all tracks move together instead of drifting out of alignment.', related_terms=ARRAY['Per-track ripple editing','Ripple editing','Time selection','Insert/remove time','Item']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Removing a section from a full arrangement','Tightening gaps across a podcast with music beds','Rearranging song sections while keeping tracks aligned']::text[] WHERE id='3a327c93-5d12-4d3a-8a28-4abee49aeb98';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Rendering / item processing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Choosing a mono variant and collapsing a stereo effect','Forgetting the dry take remains and thinking the original is gone','Applying before the FX settings are final and having to redo it']::text[], definition='A REAPER command that renders a track''s FX chain into the selected media items, printing the processed audio as a new take on each item (with mono, stereo and multichannel variants), so the effect processing becomes part of the audio.', plain_english='This bakes a track''s plug-ins into the audio. REAPER renders the sound through the FX. The result is saved as a new take on the item. The old dry take stays underneath. It makes effects permanent on those clips.', practical_application='A technician applies a heavy amp-sim chain to the guitar items as a new take, freeing CPU while keeping the dry take to revert if needed.', purpose_function='Commits FX processing to audio to save CPU, freeze a sound, or prepare items for transfer to another system.', related_terms=ARRAY['Take','Track FX','Render','Freeze','Glue items']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Printing an amp sim to save CPU','Committing a resampled, effected drum loop','Preparing processed stems for another studio']::text[] WHERE id='15c49407-382e-47d6-800e-e2f190394eed';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Editing / audio slicing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Setting the threshold too high and cutting off quiet note tails','Removing gaps that contained wanted room tone','Creating hundreds of tiny items that are hard to manage']::text[], definition='A REAPER feature (Dynamic split items) that analyzes item amplitude and automatically splits the item at points defined by threshold and timing controls, for example at note onsets or in silences, optionally removing quiet sections, to gate, clean up or prepare material for quantizing.', plain_english='This cuts a clip based on how loud it is. You set a volume threshold. REAPER splits where sound starts or where it goes quiet. It can also delete the quiet bits. It is great for cleaning up gaps or slicing beats.', practical_application='A technician runs Dynamic split on a tom track to cut and remove the silent gaps, killing cymbal bleed between hits.', purpose_function='Automates splitting audio by level, so you can remove noise in gaps, gate bleed, or create slices at transients for timing edits.', related_terms=ARRAY['Transient','Threshold','Split items','Quantize items','Noise gate']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Removing bleed between drum hits','Slicing a loop at transients for quantizing','Cleaning silence out of a dialogue track']::text[] WHERE id='1d706bd3-501d-4088-af53-b947c1eec8e1';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Editing mode', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Expecting other tracks to follow, which requires all-track ripple','Losing sync with other tracks after a per-track edit','Confusing the two ripple modes and choosing the wrong one']::text[], definition='A REAPER editing mode, Ripple editing per-track, in which moving, inserting or deleting items shifts only the later items on the same track, leaving all other tracks unchanged, so a gap opens or closes within one track without affecting the rest of the project.', plain_english='This ripples only one track. Move or delete an item and later items on that track slide. Other tracks stay put. It keeps one track''s clips in order. The rest of the song is untouched.', practical_application='A technician deletes a bad word from a voiceover track with per-track ripple on, closing the gap on that track without moving the music.', purpose_function='Lets you rearrange or tighten a single track''s items while leaving the timing of every other track alone.', related_terms=ARRAY['All-track ripple editing','Ripple editing','Item','Time selection','Trim']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Editing a single voiceover track','Tightening gaps in one instrument''s items','Removing a flub without shifting the whole mix']::text[] WHERE id='bb6e7440-d130-44e2-9fc5-976e80e5c135';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording / delay compensation option', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing this monitoring-delay option with the audio buffer setting','Leaving it in the wrong state and finding takes shifted from where they were played','Assuming it removes latency rather than preserving the monitored offset']::text[], definition='A REAPER recording option (Preserve PDC delayed monitoring in recorded items) that governs how plug-in delay compensation (PDC) monitoring latency is handled when you record while monitoring through FX that add delay. When enabled, the monitoring delay offset is kept in the recorded item so the recording stays aligned with the delayed signal the performer actually heard, rather than being automatically shifted.', plain_english='Some plug-ins delay the sound you monitor while recording. This option keeps that same delay in the new recording. So the take matches what the player heard. REAPER does not auto-shift it back. It helps when the performer played to the delayed sound.', practical_application='A technician recording through a high-latency monitoring chain enables the option so the take lands where the player felt it, matching the delayed monitor.', purpose_function='Controls whether the latency the performer heard while monitoring through delay-inducing FX is retained in the recorded item, so recordings match the monitored timing.', related_terms=ARRAY['Plug-in delay compensation','Latency','Input monitoring','Record arm','Media item']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording through a latency-heavy monitoring chain','Aligning a take to the delayed sound a player heard','Troubleshooting recordings that land off from monitoring']::text[] WHERE id='0926e672-d525-4112-85e0-933dc6a9a849';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording mode', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Forgetting to select the items first, so nothing punches','Confusing it with time-selection punch, which uses a range instead','Overwriting good audio by selecting the wrong items']::text[], definition='A REAPER auto-punch recording mode (Auto-punch selected items) in which recording is captured only over the time spans occupied by currently selected items, punching in and out automatically at each selected item''s edges while leaving audio outside those spans intact.', plain_english='This records only over the clips you selected. REAPER punches in at each clip''s start. It punches out at its end. Audio outside those clips is not touched. It is great for fixing just certain regions.', practical_application='A technician selects three flubbed items on a bass track and uses this mode to re-record only those spots in one pass.', purpose_function='Automates punch-in and punch-out, so re-recording is confined to chosen items, protecting the surrounding performance.', related_terms=ARRAY['Punch time selection','Auto-punch','Punch in','Record arm','Item']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Fixing several bad notes across a track','Re-recording only tagged problem regions','Punching multiple spots in one take']::text[] WHERE id='2a8bffe7-098c-42b5-89fc-a10a573f8a88';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording mode', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Setting the time selection loosely and overwriting good adjacent audio','Confusing it with item-based auto-punch','Forgetting the range must be set before recording']::text[], definition='A REAPER auto-punch recording mode (Time selection auto punch) in which recording is captured only within the current time selection. Playback can start before the range, and REAPER automatically punches in at its start and out at its end.', plain_english='This records only inside the time range you set. You can roll in from before it. REAPER punches in at the range''s start. It punches out at the end. Only that stretch is re-recorded.', practical_application='A technician sets a time selection over a bad bar, rolls in from earlier, and REAPER punches in and out to fix only that bar.', purpose_function='Confines recording to a defined time range, so you can pre-roll, drop in, and drop out automatically without touching audio outside the range.', related_terms=ARRAY['Punch selected items','Auto-punch','Time selection','Pre-roll','Punch in']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Dropping in to fix one bar','Punching a corrected phrase with pre-roll','Re-recording a defined section hands-free']::text[] WHERE id='aeb05c8b-bc77-4c46-8cad-97748c4719d7';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording mode', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording output to mono when the source is genuinely stereo','Forgetting output recording ignores the track input','Not using the latency-compensated variant and getting a timing offset']::text[], definition='A REAPER track record mode (Output - mono) that records the track''s post-FX output, summed to a single channel, as a new mono audio file rather than recording the track''s input. A latency-compensated variant is also available.', plain_english='This records what comes out of the track, not what goes in. It captures the sound after the plug-ins. It saves it as one mono file. It is a way to print a processed track. A version fixes latency automatically.', practical_application='A technician sets a heavily processed mono synth track to record its output, printing the effected sound as a clean mono file.', purpose_function='Prints a track''s processed output to a mono file, useful for committing effects, resampling, or creating a mono stem.', related_terms=ARRAY['Record stereo output','Track FX','Resample','Stem','Render']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Printing a mono synth''s processed sound','Resampling a mono effect chain','Creating a mono stem inside the project']::text[] WHERE id='a32c3c5e-6187-4170-a993-f4002de1036a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording mode', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Printing to stereo a source that is actually mono and doubling data needlessly','Forgetting output recording ignores the input signal','Skipping the latency-compensated variant and misaligning the print']::text[], definition='A REAPER track record mode (Output - stereo) that records the track''s post-FX output as a new two-channel stereo audio file instead of recording its input. A latency-compensated variant is also available.', plain_english='This records the track''s output in stereo. It captures the sound after the plug-ins. It saves it as a two-channel file. Use it to print a processed stereo track. A version corrects latency for you.', practical_application='A technician records the stereo output of a reverb-heavy pad track to print the wet sound and free up CPU.', purpose_function='Prints a track''s processed stereo output to a file, useful for committing effects, resampling, or bouncing a stereo stem in place.', related_terms=ARRAY['Record mono output','Track FX','Resample','Stem','Render']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Printing a wet stereo pad','Bouncing a stereo effect chain in place','Resampling a stereo bus sound']::text[] WHERE id='3379a8a4-72d9-4ded-87b0-5651fbf2c70e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Rendering / export tool', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Forgetting to define regions first, leaving the matrix empty','Checking the wrong output and getting incomplete stems','Overwriting files by not using region names in the render pattern']::text[], definition='In REAPER, the Region Render Matrix is a grid view that assigns project regions to render outputs (the master and/or specific track or stem outputs), so that batch rendering produces separate files per region and per chosen output, enabling automated stem and section exports.', plain_english='This is a grid that maps regions to outputs. Each region can render to the master or chosen tracks. Check a box to route it. Then REAPER renders many files at once. It is a fast way to make stems per section.', practical_application='A technician uses the Region Render Matrix to export separate stems for each song section in one render pass for a mixing client.', purpose_function='Automates rendering of multiple regions to multiple outputs at once, greatly speeding up stem and multi-section exports.', related_terms=ARRAY['Region','Render','Stem','Master track','Batch export']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Exporting per-section stems for a mixer','Delivering multitrack stems by region','Batch-rendering cues from one project']::text[] WHERE id='159125d7-cda0-417e-ac4b-69dfbe15a335';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Time-stretch editing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Expecting a linear ramp when a curved slope is set, or vice versa','Adding extreme slopes that create audible artifacts','Confusing stretch-marker slope with clip playback-rate automation']::text[], definition='In REAPER, a stretch marker''s slope controls the shape of the playback-rate transition around that marker. Instead of a straight, linear change in the time-stretch ratio between markers, an adjusted slope curves the transition, allowing smooth speed ramps such as accelerando, ritardando or tape-stop effects.', plain_english='Stretch markers change speed inside a clip. Normally the speed changes in a straight line between markers. The slope bends that change into a curve. This makes speed ramps sound smooth. You can build tape-stop or slow-down effects with it.', practical_application='A technician adjusts a stretch marker''s slope to create a smooth tape-stop slowdown at the end of a drum loop.', purpose_function='Shapes how the stretch rate eases between markers, so speed changes sound natural or deliberately curved rather than abrupt.', related_terms=ARRAY['Stretch marker','Time-stretching','Playback rate','Transient','Tape stop']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Creating a tape-stop effect','Smoothing a tempo ramp inside a loop','Fine-tuning a gradual speed change']::text[] WHERE id='1ad9aeeb-8ff5-495c-b03a-ee7fab409904';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Take management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing it with exploding takes across new tracks that change position','Cluttering the project with many items and losing track of the comp','Exploding before comping when lanes would have sufficed']::text[], definition='A REAPER command (Explode takes of items in place) that separates the multiple takes stacked within an item into individual items occupying the same timeline position, typically laid out across lanes, so each take can be viewed and edited on its own without leaving its original location.', plain_english='An item can hold several takes stacked up. This command splits them apart. Each take becomes its own item. They stay at the same spot in time. Now you can edit each take on its own.', practical_application='A technician explodes takes in place to spread three guitar passes across lanes and compare them side by side.', purpose_function='Unstacks layered takes into separate items, so each performance can be handled, muted or edited independently while keeping timing.', related_terms=ARRAY['Take','Take lanes','Comping','Item','Show all takes in lanes']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Spreading takes to compare them','Separating passes for individual editing','Preparing stacked takes for independent edits']::text[] WHERE id='282d96aa-c913-47a6-9344-939dcacacce4';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Arranging tool', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing arranger sections with plain timeline markers','Reordering sections and forgetting automation may need checking','Leaving gaps or overlaps when sections do not align to bars']::text[], definition='In Studio One, an arranger section is a named part of the song defined on the Arranger track (for example Intro, Verse or Chorus) that spans a range of the timeline and can be selected, reordered, duplicated or removed to restructure the arrangement, moving all contained material together.', plain_english='The Arranger track splits your song into blocks. Each block is an arranger section, like Verse or Chorus. You name and color them. You can drag them to reorder the song. Moving a section moves all its audio and MIDI.', practical_application='A technician duplicates the Chorus arranger section and drags it to the end to add a final chorus without re-selecting every track.', purpose_function='Lets you restructure a song by working with whole labeled sections instead of selecting and moving raw events by hand.', related_terms=ARRAY['Arranger track','Scratch Pad','Song structure','Event','Marker']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Trying a different song order quickly','Duplicating a chorus for a final repeat','Blocking out song structure before arranging']::text[] WHERE id='c3bdaa88-2287-48fa-83ee-77eaa286f0fd';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Rendering / bouncing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing it with Bounce Selection, which renders in place','Forgetting the muted originals still occupy the source track','Expecting events to be merged when they stay separate']::text[], definition='A Studio One command that renders each selected event through its track''s insert effects to new audio on a new track, keeping the events separate rather than merging them, while the source events remain in place and are muted after the bounce.', plain_english='This prints your events as new audio. It runs them through the track''s effects first. The new audio lands on a fresh track. The originals stay but go muted. Each event stays separate, not glued together.', practical_application='A technician bounces a processed vocal to a new track to lock in the sound, muting the original chain but keeping it for recall.', purpose_function='Commits processed audio to a new track for CPU savings, safe archiving of a sound, or handing off printed audio while keeping the originals.', related_terms=ARRAY['Bounce Selection','Merge Events','Event FX','Track Transform','Render']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Printing a processed vocal to a new track','Committing effects while keeping the dry source','Creating a rendered copy for handoff']::text[] WHERE id='a497173e-d6e8-4072-be54-d01ec5410889';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Audio file / Pool management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming ''remove unused files'' also clears orphaned clip versions','Confusing a clip version with a track layer or take','Duplicating events and not realizing they share one clip version']::text[], definition='In Studio One, an event references an underlying audio clip in the Pool, and a clip version is a distinct saved state of that clip''s audio. Operations such as bouncing or certain destructive edits create new clip versions while the original audio and its edit history are retained, so different events can reference different versions.', plain_english='Each audio event points to a clip in the Pool, Studio One''s store of a project''s audio files. A clip version is one saved state of that clip. Some edits or a bounce make a new version. The old audio is kept too. Different events can use different versions.', practical_application='A technician checks the Pool to see which clip versions are still in use and removes stale ones to shrink the project''s audio footprint.', purpose_function='Preserves original audio and prior edit states, so processing stays non-destructive and versions can be tracked and managed in the Pool.', related_terms=ARRAY['Pool','Event','Audio clip','Bounce Selection','Merge Events']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Managing project size in the Pool','Tracking edit states of processed audio','Cleaning up unused clip versions']::text[] WHERE id='a30cbe7a-9dd4-480e-8eb4-53244d053165';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mastering / export', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Forgetting to enter metadata before release','Confusing Digital Release with burning a physical Disc Image','Exporting at the wrong format or sample rate for the distributor']::text[], definition='In Studio One''s Project page (mastering), Digital Release is a publishing option that renders the sequenced masters to digital audio files with embedded metadata for online distribution, producing deliverables suited to streaming and download rather than a physical disc image.', plain_english='The Project page holds your finished masters. Digital Release exports them as audio files. It adds track info like artist and title. The files are made for streaming or download. It is the online version of burning a CD.', practical_application='After sequencing an album on the Project page, a technician uses Digital Release to export tagged masters for the client''s distributor.', purpose_function='Produces distribution-ready digital masters with metadata, so an album or single can be delivered to stores and streaming services.', related_terms=ARRAY['Project page','Mastering','Disc Image','Metadata','Master file']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Delivering album masters to a distributor','Exporting tagged singles for streaming','Preparing digital deliverables after mastering']::text[] WHERE id='dc9c7049-1d5a-40f2-80ef-5c7df457704d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Event editing / automation', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing event envelopes with track automation lanes','Fighting a track automation move with a clip envelope','Forgetting envelope points travel with the event if it moves']::text[], definition='In Studio One, an event envelope is a per-event volume (gain) envelope drawn directly on an audio event, letting you add level breakpoints inside that single event independently of track automation, for clip-level gain shaping and ride corrections.', plain_english='This is a volume line drawn on one audio clip. You add points to raise or lower its level. It works inside that clip only. It is separate from track automation. Use it to fix loud or quiet spots fast.', practical_application='A technician draws an event envelope to duck a loud breath inside one vocal clip while leaving the track''s automation alone.', purpose_function='Provides clip-level volume control, so you can tame or boost parts of a single event without touching track automation.', related_terms=ARRAY['Event','Automation','Event FX','Fade','Gain']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Riding a word''s level inside one clip','Ducking a stray loud note','Shaping gain before track automation']::text[] WHERE id='e1a2d078-3136-4168-8fad-d5eefe988863';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Event editing / effects', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Adding Event FX to every clip when a track insert would be simpler','Forgetting Event FX affect only the one event','Not bouncing before handoff, so the per-event processing is missed']::text[], definition='In Studio One, Event FX are insert effects applied directly to an individual audio event rather than to a whole track, so processing such as EQ or a plug-in affects only that one event''s audio.', plain_english='These are effects on a single clip. You drop a plug-in right onto the event. Only that clip is processed. The rest of the track stays clean. It is handy for fixing one problem sound.', practical_application='A technician drops a de-esser as Event FX on one harsh vocal phrase without adding it to the entire vocal track.', purpose_function='Lets you process one event in isolation, so a fix or effect applies only where it is needed instead of across the whole track.', related_terms=ARRAY['Event','Insert effects','Event envelope','Bounce to New Track','Channel']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['De-essing one harsh phrase','Fixing a click on a single clip','Adding an effect to just one hit']::text[] WHERE id='894711a5-3946-42e7-883c-43d5911a7f73';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Time-stretch / tempo', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Turning it on for audio with no tempo information and getting odd stretching','Expecting pitch to change with tempo, which it does not','Leaving it off and having loops drift when tempo changes']::text[], definition='In Studio One, Follow Tempo is an audio event and track option (a Timestretch mode) that makes an audio event automatically time-stretch to track the song tempo, so loops and events stay in time when the project tempo changes.', plain_english='Turn this on and a clip follows the song tempo. If you change the tempo, the clip stretches to match. Loops stay in time. You do not have to re-time them by hand. It uses time-stretching to fit.', practical_application='A technician enables Follow Tempo on a drum loop so it stretches automatically when the song''s tempo is raised for the chorus.', purpose_function='Keeps audio events locked to project tempo, so loops and recordings stay in sync when the tempo is changed.', related_terms=ARRAY['Timestretch','Tempo','Audio Bend','Musicloop','Stretch']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Keeping a loop in time as tempo changes','Fitting an imported loop to the song','Adjusting project tempo without re-timing audio']::text[] WHERE id='c209b968-3e33-4b55-8038-c6fc529a2ea3';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Track processing / CPU management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing transform with a permanent bounce; it is reversible','Editing rendered audio and losing sync with the original instrument state','Forgetting to transform back before changing MIDI notes']::text[], definition='In Studio One, transforming an instrument track (via Track Transform) renders the virtual instrument and its insert effects to audio to save CPU, while Transform to Instrument Track reverses the process and restores the editable instrument. The transform is non-destructive and can be switched back and forth.', plain_english='A software instrument uses CPU. Transforming it renders the track to audio. This frees your computer''s power. You can turn it back into the live instrument later. Nothing is lost when you switch back.', practical_application='A technician transforms finished virtual-instrument tracks to audio to lighten the CPU load, transforming them back only when an edit is needed.', purpose_function='Frees CPU by rendering an instrument track to audio while keeping the option to return to the live, editable instrument.', related_terms=ARRAY['Track Transform','Transform to Rendered Audio','Bounce to New Track','Freeze','Virtual instrument']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Freeing CPU on a heavy instrument track','Rendering instruments to audio for a big session','Returning to a live instrument to tweak a part']::text[] WHERE id='6248e01c-5d63-49af-a9c1-10964ec7dc7e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Editing tool', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the Listen tool with soloing a whole track','Forgetting to switch back to the Arrow tool for normal editing','Expecting it to change persistent solo states, which it does not']::text[], definition='In Studio One, the Listen tool is an Arrange-view tool that lets you audition audio by clicking or dragging over events to hear them in isolation, soloing what you click, so you can preview material without changing the mix''s solo and mute states.', plain_english='This tool lets you hear a clip by clicking it. It plays just what you point at. Other tracks stay quiet while you listen. It does not change your solo buttons. Use it to preview parts fast.', practical_application='A technician uses the Listen tool to click through takes and comps, hearing each in isolation before deciding which to keep.', purpose_function='Provides quick auditioning of events in place, so you can preview or scrub material without altering solo and mute settings.', related_terms=ARRAY['Arrow tool','Scrub','Solo','Event','Audition']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Previewing events without soloing','Auditioning takes quickly','Checking a clip in isolation during editing']::text[] WHERE id='a2bef08f-60e1-4043-9693-066cbef0f460';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Studio One recording interface', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the Record Panel with the Console or the Inspector','Forgetting to set the desired record mode in the panel before loop recording, so takes are not stored as expected']::text[], definition='In Studio One, the Record Panel is a panel (opened from the View menu) that gathers a track''s recording options in one place — including record-mode and loop-recording behavior, precount/metronome settings, and input configuration — so the engineer can decide how the next take is captured before recording.', plain_english='The Record Panel is a small window in Studio One. It holds the recording settings. You open it before you record. It lets you choose how takes are captured.', practical_application='An engineer opens the Record Panel (Shift+Alt+R) to turn on loop recording and set a metronome precount before tracking several vocal or guitar passes.', purpose_function='Gathers a track''s recording preferences and record-mode options in one place so the engineer can decide how the next take is captured before hitting record.', related_terms=ARRAY['Loop recording','Record mode','Precount','Track layer','Takes to Layers']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Enabling loop recording and a precount before tracking a guitar solo','Reviewing record-mode settings before capturing multiple vocal passes']::text[] WHERE id='0dcb7e46-c7f3-4a84-9685-8cc7ea52ce06';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Studio One loop recording and comping', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Expecting each pass to replace the previous one (that is Replace mode) instead of stacking on layers','Not choosing this record mode before looping, so passes are merged rather than layered']::text[], definition='In Studio One, Record Takes to Layers is a loop-recording behavior in which each pass captured during loop recording is placed on its own separate track layer instead of overwriting the previous pass, preserving every take for comping.', plain_english='This is a way to record in Studio One. You play a part in a loop. Each pass is saved on its own layer. Later you pick the best parts.', practical_application='A singer loops a chorus several times; each pass lands on its own layer, and the engineer later comps a composite from the best phrases.', purpose_function='Sets loop recording so every pass is preserved on a separate track layer instead of overwriting the last, making all takes available for comping.', related_terms=ARRAY['Takes to Layers','Track layer','Loop recording','Comping','Record Panel']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Loop-recording several vocal passes so each is kept on its own layer','Capturing multiple guitar solo attempts for later comping']::text[] WHERE id='f1e5b423-fc95-4af3-9b08-ffbf2a5161c4';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Studio One comping', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing takes (stacked passes) with layers (separate lanes) and losing track of which pass is which','Deleting unused layers before the comp is finalized']::text[], definition='In Studio One, Takes to Layers is a function that unpacks recorded takes onto individual track layers, placing each pass on its own layer so the takes can be auditioned and comped into a single composite performance.', plain_english='This spreads recorded takes onto separate layers in Studio One. Each take sits on its own layer. That makes it easy to compare them and build one good take.', practical_application='After loop-recording, the engineer applies Takes to Layers so each pass is on its own layer, then swipes the best phrases into a comp.', purpose_function='Unpacks stacked takes onto individual track layers so passes can be auditioned side by side and comped into a single composite performance.', related_terms=ARRAY['Record Takes to Layers','Track layer','Comping','Loop recording','Record Panel']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Distributing several looped vocal passes onto layers before comping','Organizing multiple drum takes into layers for editing']::text[] WHERE id='42363442-8d2b-48af-85c1-247038a3cc4b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Studio One track structure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming inactive layers still play back — only the active layer is heard','Deleting a layer thinking it is merely muted, permanently losing that take']::text[], definition='In Studio One, a track layer is one of several stacked lanes belonging to a single track, each able to hold its own audio or note events; only the active layer plays back, and layers are used to store alternate takes for comping without adding extra tracks.', plain_english='A track layer is like an extra lane hidden inside one track. Each layer can hold a different take. Only one layer plays at a time. Layers let you keep and compare takes.', practical_application='An engineer keeps three vocal takes as layers on the lead-vocal track and comps the best parts from across those layers.', purpose_function='Stores multiple takes or alternates within a single track and enables comping without creating extra tracks in the arrangement.', related_terms=ARRAY['Takes to Layers','Record Takes to Layers','Comping','Loop recording','Record Panel']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Storing alternate guitar takes as layers on one track','Comping a lead vocal from several layered passes']::text[] WHERE id='0a0e04cd-67c7-4d2e-bccd-c6a1b44ee961';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Studio One mastering (Project page)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Forgetting to update after editing the Song, so the master is cut from an outdated mix','Assuming Song edits appear in the Project automatically without re-rendering']::text[], definition='In Studio One''s Project (mastering) page, Update Mastering File is a command that re-renders a linked Song''s mixdown into the mastering project, replacing the previously imported audio so that changes made in the Song page are carried through to the master.', plain_english='Studio One can link a mix to a master. If you change the mix, the master is out of date. Update Mastering File makes a fresh mixdown. It puts the new mix into the master.', practical_application='After correcting a fader level in the Song page, the engineer uses Update Mastering File so the Project page master is built from the corrected mix.', purpose_function='Keeps a mastering project in sync with the source Song by re-rendering the Song''s mixdown and replacing the previously imported audio in the Project page.', related_terms=ARRAY['Project page','Song page','Export Mixdown','Mastering chain','Mixdown']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Refreshing a master after revising the mix of a linked Song','Updating mastering files when plug-in or outboard settings changed']::text[] WHERE id='51067efc-f930-448f-9534-839a0a97a67f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Editing workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Two people editing the same file without version control, overwriting each other''s changes','Not documenting what was changed, making edits hard to reconcile']::text[], definition='Collaborative editing is a workflow in which more than one person contributes to editing a project''s audio — by sharing project files and revisions in turn or by working together in real time — with changes tracked and reconciled so edits stay consistent and none are lost.', plain_english='Collaborative editing means people work on the same audio project together. They may pass files back and forth. Or they may edit online at the same time. Every change must be kept in order.', practical_application='An assistant cleans and comps takes and shares the session; the lead editor finalizes, using version notes to avoid overwriting each other''s work.', purpose_function='Lets a team divide editing work — or edit together in real time — while tracking and reconciling changes so edits stay consistent and nothing is lost.', related_terms=ARRAY['Comping','Version control','Session documentation','Engineer handoff','Revision pass']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Handing a session between a day editor and a night editor','Splitting dialogue and music editing across two editors on a deadline']::text[] WHERE id='869304f6-47d5-46b8-801d-d19701a82bc6';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mastering evaluation and listening', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Comparing without matching levels, so the louder version wins by default','Treating a quick sighted A/B as unbiased when expectation still colors the result']::text[], definition='In mastering, a blind comparison is an evaluation in which the listener judges two or more versions of audio without knowing which is which, usually with playback levels matched, so the decision reflects sound quality rather than expectation, branding, or loudness bias.', plain_english='A blind comparison hides which version you are hearing. You judge them without knowing which is which. The levels are matched so the louder one does not just seem better. This keeps your choice honest.', practical_application='A mastering engineer level-matches two chain settings and switches between them without looking, choosing the cleaner one; the method also compares a master against a reference.', purpose_function='Removes bias — especially loudness bias and expectation — from subjective quality judgments so decisions reflect real sound quality.', related_terms=ARRAY['Level matching','A/B comparison','Client reference','Loudness bias','Reference track']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Choosing between two limiter settings by switching blind at matched loudness','Blind-checking a finished master against a commercial reference']::text[] WHERE id='ec8df41a-1049-49a3-a044-19d509ee56ae';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mastering workflow and references', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Matching the reference''s loudness at the expense of the song''s dynamics','Using a reference from a different genre or playback context','Comparing to the reference without level-matching first']::text[], definition='A client reference is a recording supplied by the client to communicate the sound they want a master to achieve — its tonal balance, loudness, or overall character — which the mastering engineer uses as a target to aim toward or discuss.', plain_english='A client reference is a song the client gives you. It shows the sound they want. You listen to it. Then you try to get their master close to that feel.', practical_application='A client sends a commercial track they love; the engineer level-matches it and compares tonal balance and loudness while mastering.', purpose_function='Communicates the client''s sonic goal — tonal balance, loudness, or character — so the engineer and client share a target and revisions are reduced.', related_terms=ARRAY['Reference track','Blind comparison','Tonal balance','Revision pass','Final approval']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Using a client''s chosen commercial track as a tonal target','Discussing loudness expectations by comparing to the client''s reference']::text[] WHERE id='e3f6e6a4-1a15-4c7d-967f-ee4d995d9c20';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Vinyl mastering and disc cutting', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the cutting engineer with the mastering engineer — cutting is a distinct final stage','Assuming a loud digital master transfers to vinyl unchanged']::text[], definition='A cutting engineer is a specialist who transfers a finished master to a lacquer disc on a cutting lathe for vinyl production, adjusting level, equalization, groove spacing, and other parameters so the program plays back cleanly within the physical limits of the format.', plain_english='A cutting engineer makes the master for vinyl records. They use a machine called a lathe. It cuts grooves into a soft disc. They set levels and spacing so the record plays well.', practical_application='The cutting engineer tames sibilance and bass, sets groove spacing for the side length, and cuts a lacquer that goes to the plating plant.', purpose_function='Transfers a finished master to a lacquer disc, adapting it to the physical limits of vinyl so records track cleanly and sound their best.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cutting lathe','Lacquer','De-essing for vinyl','Mastering engineer','Groove spacing']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Cutting a lacquer master for a vinyl pressing run','Adjusting a master''s low end and sibilance so the lathe can track it']::text[] WHERE id='59662c82-05fa-41eb-a953-f8e9dbb70016';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Vinyl mastering and disc cutting', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: Touching the heated cutter head or the lathe''s drive mechanism while it is running, risking burns or injury','Overdriving the cutter head with excessive level or high-frequency energy, causing distortion or cutter damage']::text[], definition='A cutting lathe is the electromechanical machine used in vinyl mastering to inscribe an audio program as a modulated spiral groove in a lacquer-coated master disc, driving a heated cutter head from the audio signal while advancing it across the rotating blank.', plain_english='A cutting lathe is a machine that makes vinyl masters. A disc spins on it. A tiny heated cutter carves a groove. The groove wiggles with the sound. That disc is used to make records.', practical_application='The engineer loads a lacquer blank, feeds the master through the lathe''s cutter head, and monitors groove depth and pitch as the cut proceeds.', purpose_function='Converts an electrical audio signal into the physical modulated groove of a lacquer master, which becomes the source for pressing vinyl records.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cutting engineer','Lacquer','Cutter head','Groove spacing','De-essing for vinyl']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Cutting a lacquer master on a Neumann lathe for pressing','Monitoring groove pitch and depth while cutting a long album side']::text[] WHERE id='6a49ae40-1794-412e-9650-92fa7e47952f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Vinyl mastering and disc cutting', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Over-de-essing so vocals sound lisping and dull','Ignoring sibilance until the cut mistracks or the cutter head overheats']::text[], definition='De-essing for vinyl is the reduction of excessive sibilance and concentrated high-frequency energy in a master before disc cutting, because sharp ''ess'' sounds and loud treble can overheat the cutter head, distort, or cause the playback stylus to mistrack.', plain_english='De-essing for vinyl means taming harsh ''sss'' sounds before cutting a record. Too much high sound can hurt the cutter. It can also make the needle jump. So engineers soften it first.', practical_application='Before cutting, the engineer applies a de-esser to vocals and controls treble so the lacquer cuts cleanly and plays without spitting or breakup.', purpose_function='Reduces excessive sibilance and concentrated high-frequency energy before cutting so the cutter head is protected and the groove plays back without distortion or mistracking.', related_terms=ARRAY['De-esser','Sibilance','Cutting lathe','Cutting engineer','High-frequency limiting']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Taming vocal sibilance on a master destined for vinyl','Reducing bright cymbal or ''ess'' energy before cutting a lacquer']::text[] WHERE id='60150006-a606-4037-8cc5-518d6d67da71';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mastering delivery and quality control', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Delivering without listening back to the actual exported file','Not checking sample rate, bit depth, or dither on the export','Missing a clip or glitch that appears only in the render']::text[], definition='Export verification is the quality-control step of checking a rendered master file after export — confirming format, sample rate, bit depth, channel count, fades, absence of clipping or glitches, and that it matches what was heard in the session — before delivery.', plain_english='Export verification means checking a finished file before you send it. You make sure the format is right. You listen for clicks or clipping. You confirm it sounds like the session. Then you deliver it.', practical_application='After bouncing, the engineer reopens the exported WAV, checks sample rate, bit depth, and levels, and listens top-to-tail before uploading it.', purpose_function='Catches export errors — wrong format, clipping, silence, glitches, or wrong metadata — before a master file is delivered to the client or manufacturer.', related_terms=ARRAY['Bounce','Dither','Loudness normalization','Final approval','Deliverables']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Auditioning a bounced master before client delivery','Verifying a batch of exports all have correct format and no clipping']::text[] WHERE id='16e3ddac-67cc-4d8f-b6a0-c0dec7d510b0';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mastering workflow and sign-off', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Sending files to manufacturing before written approval','Treating a casual ''sounds good'' as final without confirming all changes are closed']::text[], definition='Final approval is the client''s formal sign-off that a master is complete and acceptable for release or manufacturing, confirming no further changes are requested and authorizing the engineer to prepare and deliver the final files.', plain_english='Final approval is when the client says the master is done. They agree to no more changes. This lets the engineer send the final files. It also marks the job as finished.', practical_application='After a revision pass, the client signs off in writing; the engineer then delivers production-ready files and archives the session.', purpose_function='Closes the revision loop and formally authorizes delivery or manufacturing, protecting both engineer and client by confirming no further changes are requested.', related_terms=ARRAY['Revision pass','Client reference','Export verification','Deliverables','Sign-off']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Getting written sign-off before pressing vinyl','Confirming the client approves the master before delivery to distribution']::text[] WHERE id='04b8a5bc-ff4f-48b6-9452-9056e413ad93';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mastering workflow and revisions', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Not versioning files, so revisions get confused','Making unrequested changes during a revision pass','Reviewing a revision without level-matching it to the prior version']::text[], definition='A revision pass is a round of adjustments a mastering engineer makes to a master in response to client feedback, producing an updated version for review; several passes may occur before final approval.', plain_english='A revision pass is one round of changes from client notes. The client asks for tweaks. The engineer makes them. Then a new version is sent for review.', practical_application='The client asks for more low end and less brightness; the engineer makes a revision pass, re-exports, and sends the new version for review.', purpose_function='Incorporates client feedback in tracked rounds, moving the master toward final approval while keeping versions organized.', related_terms=ARRAY['Final approval','Client reference','Export verification','Recall','Deliverables']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adjusting tonal balance after a client review round','Producing a second version after feedback before final sign-off']::text[] WHERE id='e82837f3-b1bb-4423-8faa-d2271bf9a589';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Neural amp modeling / capture', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a Tone Model (full nonlinear capture) with a static impulse response of a cabinet','Expecting a Tone Model to capture time-based effects such as reverb or delay','Capturing with a noisy or misaligned reference file, which degrades the model']::text[], definition='In IK Multimedia''s TONEX system, a Tone Model is a neural-network capture of a specific piece of guitar gear — an amplifier, cabinet, combo, or drive/EQ pedal — created by AI machine modeling from recorded audio, so the captured rig''s sound and dynamic response can be reproduced in software or hardware.', plain_english='A Tone Model is a copy of a real amp or pedal''s sound. An AI makes it from recordings of the gear. Then you can play through the copy like the real thing. Users can share Tone Models online.', practical_application='A guitarist captures a favorite amp into a Tone Model, loads it on a TONEX pedal for a gig, and downloads community Tone Models from ToneNET.', purpose_function='Lets players capture and reuse the sound and dynamic response of a real amp, cabinet, or drive pedal in software or hardware without the physical gear.', related_terms=ARRAY['Neural Amp Modeler','Amp capture','Impulse response','Profile','AI machine modeling']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Capturing a borrowed vintage amp as a Tone Model before returning it','Loading a downloaded Tone Model for a live gig on a compact pedal']::text[] WHERE id='dbd05d43-e7a7-4e25-a795-a6e72cf10852';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Preamplifier / location recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming batteries can supply full 48V phantom for long sessions without checking charge','Not carrying spare batteries, risking failure mid-take','Mistaking a low battery for a gain or cabling fault']::text[], definition='A battery-powered preamplifier is a microphone or instrument preamp that runs on internal batteries instead of AC mains, providing gain in locations without reliable power and avoiding mains-borne hum and ground-loop noise, at the cost of limited runtime.', plain_english='A battery-powered preamp boosts a mic signal using batteries. It does not need a wall socket. This helps when recording outdoors or far from power. It can also cut hum from the mains.', practical_application='A location recordist uses a battery preamp for a remote interview so there is no AC hum and no dependence on venue power.', purpose_function='Provides clean microphone or instrument gain in locations without reliable AC power and avoids mains-borne hum and ground-loop noise.', related_terms=ARRAY['Microphone preamplifier','Phantom power','Ground loop','Field-production rack','Portable monitor controller']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a remote acoustic session with no clean mains power','Running preamps off batteries to avoid ground-loop hum on location']::text[] WHERE id='fba8a94a-5844-4e5a-b8f7-e3fbd8d4255a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Studio communication / monitoring', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Leaving talkback open so private control-room chat is heard by performers','Setting talkback so loud it startles players in headphones','Forgetting the performer cannot hear you when talkback is off']::text[], definition='Headphone communication is the use of the studio''s headphone (foldback/cue) system together with talkback to speak with and guide performers during a session, letting the control room give direction, counts, and feedback to musicians wearing headphones in the live room.', plain_english='Headphone communication means talking to players through their headphones. The engineer speaks into a talkback mic. The players hear it in their cans. This is how the control room guides the take.', practical_application='The engineer counts the band in and gives retake notes over talkback into the players'' headphones between takes.', purpose_function='Enables clear direction and coordination between the control room and performers who are isolated in the live room wearing headphones.', related_terms=ARRAY['Talkback','Cue mix','Foldback','Headphone mix verification','Talkback etiquette']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Counting a band in through their headphones','Giving a singer direction between takes over talkback']::text[] WHERE id='a2802816-0e78-45ba-a37e-31607e32b347';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Cue and monitor setup', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: Setting headphone level dangerously high, risking hearing damage to the performer','Checking only your own headphones, not the performer''s actual cue mix','Forgetting to include the click or a key reference in the cue']::text[], definition='Headphone mix verification is the practice of checking, before and during recording, that the cue (foldback) mix delivered to a performer''s headphones is correct — right sources, balance, level, and any needed effects — so the performer can play or sing comfortably and in time.', plain_english='This means checking the sound in a performer''s headphones. You confirm they hear the right parts at a good level. A bad cue mix (the custom headphone blend they hear) hurts timing and pitch. So you verify it before recording.', practical_application='Before a vocal take, the engineer confirms the singer hears enough of their own voice and the click, then adjusts the cue send.', purpose_function='Ensures the cue (foldback) mix each performer hears is correct — right sources, balance, and level — so the performer is comfortable and the takes stay in time.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cue mix','Foldback','Headphone communication','Click track','More-me mix']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Checking a singer''s headphone balance before a take','Confirming each musician''s cue mix during a live-band tracking date']::text[] WHERE id='f19ca80b-b42b-48fd-aab7-b235e4b4e0e2';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Session documentation', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Taking blurry or distant photos that do not show angle or distance','Not labeling which mic and source each photo shows','Relying on memory instead of documenting placement']::text[], definition='Microphone setup photographs are reference photos taken of mic placements, angles, and distances during a session and kept with the session documentation, so the exact setup can be recalled, matched on another day, or reproduced for overdubs and future sessions.', plain_english='These are photos of where the mics are placed. You take them during setup. Later they help you rebuild the same sound. This matters if you must record more on another day.', practical_application='The engineer photographs each drum mic''s position so an overdub next week matches the original tone and imaging.', purpose_function='Documents mic placement, angle, and distance so a setup can be recalled and reproduced for overdubs, continuity, or future sessions.', related_terms=ARRAY['Recall sheet','Track sheet','Session documentation','Mic placement','Overdub']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Photographing drum mic positions for a later overdub','Documenting a room-mic setup to reproduce it on a future date']::text[] WHERE id='305e5018-4983-46a6-af6b-c31f68c773d6';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Location recording / monitoring', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Placing monitors against a wall or in a corner, coloring the low end','Judging a mix only on unfamiliar headphones without a reference','Ignoring strong room reflections when choosing a listening spot']::text[], definition='A mobile monitoring position is the temporary listening location an engineer establishes for a remote or location recording, arranging monitors or headphones and seating so the balance heard is as reliable as possible in an untreated or unfamiliar space.', plain_english='This is where the engineer sits and listens on location. There is no fixed control room. So you set up monitors or headphones in the best spot you can. The goal is a sound you can trust.', practical_application='At a venue, the engineer places nearfields on stands at ear height and away from walls, or trusts calibrated headphones, to judge the recording.', purpose_function='Establishes the most dependable listening reference possible for a remote recording, away from a treated, familiar control room.', related_terms=ARRAY['Nearfield monitor','Portable monitor controller','Room acoustics','Reference headphones','Field-production rack']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting up nearfields in a hotel room for a remote session','Choosing a monitoring spot backstage during a live recording']::text[] WHERE id='f8999b83-fa61-4a64-b494-7155fa94dba8';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Location recording / signal routing (role)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Not labeling patch points, making faults slow to trace','Confusing mic-split feeds between front-of-house, monitors, and the record rig','Skipping a line check after patching']::text[], definition='A mobile patch technician is a crew member on a remote or location recording who handles signal routing and patching — connecting mics, stage boxes, splits, preamps, and recorders through patchbays — and troubleshoots connections so signal reaches the recording rig cleanly.', plain_english='This person handles the cables and patching on location. They connect mics, splitters, and recorders. They fix signal problems fast. Their job keeps every channel flowing to the recorder.', practical_application='At a remote concert recording, the patch technician wires the stage split to the mobile rig, labels lines, and traces any dead channel during line check.', purpose_function='Ensures correct, reliable signal routing in complex remote setups and provides fast fault-finding when a channel goes wrong.', related_terms=ARRAY['Patchbay','Remote patch panel','Stage box','Remote line check','Field-production rack']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Wiring a stage split to a mobile recording truck','Tracing a dead channel through the patchbay during setup']::text[] WHERE id='66f2f6ee-30ee-4591-99ce-754c692de491';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Monitoring hardware', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming the volume knob is calibrated — note a reference level yourself','Forgetting a passive controller can load the interface output differently','Not checking mono and mute states before critical listening']::text[], definition='A portable monitor controller is a compact device placed between an audio interface''s outputs and the monitors/headphones that provides level control, source and speaker selection, mono/dim/mute, and headphone feeds, giving consistent monitoring control in mobile or small setups.', plain_english='This is a small box that controls your speaker volume. It sits between the interface and the monitors. It lets you switch sources, mute, or go mono. It is easy to carry for mobile work.', practical_application='On location, the engineer uses a portable monitor controller to set a repeatable listening level and to check the mix in mono.', purpose_function='Provides repeatable monitoring control — level, source and speaker selection, mono, dim, and mute, plus headphone feeds — without a large console, in a portable form.', related_terms=ARRAY['Monitor controller','Mobile monitoring position','Nearfield monitor','Dim and mute','Audio interface']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting a repeatable monitoring level on a remote session','Switching between two monitor pairs during a mobile mix check']::text[] WHERE id='26428e57-346e-4568-a756-5b76d63c2f20';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Location recording / setup check', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Checking only some channels and assuming the rest are fine','Not verifying that labeling matches the actual source','Skipping polarity or phase checks on multi-mic sources']::text[], definition='A remote line check is the systematic pre-session verification, at a location or remote recording, that every input line carries the correct source at healthy level and polarity — testing each channel from mic through split and preamp to the recorder before tracking begins.', plain_english='A remote line check tests every input before recording on location. You go channel by channel. You confirm each mic reaches the recorder at a good level. This catches problems before the take.', practical_application='Before a live recording, the engineer has each mic tapped or spoken into and confirms signal, label, and level on every recorder input.', purpose_function='Confirms the whole signal path works on every channel — source, level, labeling, and polarity — so no input fails once recording starts.', related_terms=ARRAY['Line check','Soundcheck','Mobile patch technician','Remote patch panel','Signal flow']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Verifying every input on a mobile rig before a concert recording','Confirming split feeds to the record truck during setup']::text[] WHERE id='1862adb1-a4f2-4ea6-86ad-289e883f4bce';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Location recording / signal routing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Leaving patch points unlabeled, slowing fault-finding','Misunderstanding normalled connections and losing signal','Overloading a single panel without documenting the routing']::text[], definition='A remote patch panel is a patchbay deployed at a remote or location recording that centralizes the connection points between stage sources, splits, preamps, and recorders, letting the crew route, re-route, and troubleshoot signals from one organized point away from the main studio.', plain_english='A remote patch panel is a patchbay used away from the studio. It gathers all the connections in one place. You plug and re-route signals there. It makes location setups tidy and easy to fix.', practical_application='The patch technician routes stage-box returns and preamp outputs through the remote patch panel to the recorder inputs, re-patching as needs change.', purpose_function='Centralizes and organizes the connection points between stage sources, splits, preamps, and recorders so routing and troubleshooting happen from one place on location.', related_terms=ARRAY['Patchbay','Mobile patch technician','Stage box','Remote line check','Normalling']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Routing a stage split through a patch panel to a mobile rig','Re-patching inputs quickly between sets at a remote recording']::text[] WHERE id='2fb06079-ffdd-48f5-b2c5-75ffbef76814';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Session planning / scheduling', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Underestimating patch and line-check time, causing a late start','Not scheduling buffer for troubleshooting','Scheduling performer arrival before the rig is tested']::text[], definition='A setup schedule is the planned timeline for preparing a session before recording — covering load-in, equipment placement, patching, mic setup, and line check — so everything is ready and tested by the time performers arrive and tracking is due to start.', plain_english='A setup schedule is a plan for getting ready before a session. It lists when to load in, place gear, and patch. It leaves time for a line check. The goal is to be ready when the artists arrive.', practical_application='The engineer allots two hours for load-in and patching and thirty minutes for line check before the band''s call time.', purpose_function='Sequences and times the preparation tasks — load-in, placement, patching, mic setup, line check — so the rig is ready and tested before tracking is due to start.', related_terms=ARRAY['Call time','Load-in','Remote line check','Engineer call time','Setup']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Planning load-in and setup before a full-band tracking day','Allotting time for patching and line check ahead of a remote recording']::text[] WHERE id='4708e2c2-e855-46be-8b90-b2259ea5aa51';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Studio communication / conduct', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Leaving talkback latched open, broadcasting private conversation','Being blunt or negative on an open mic, hurting a performer''s confidence','Talking over a take because you forgot the mic is live']::text[], definition='Talkback etiquette is the set of professional conventions for using the control-room talkback microphone: being aware of when it is live, keeping communication clear and concise, and avoiding remarks that could demoralize or startle performers hearing it in their headphones.', plain_english='Talkback etiquette is good manners on the talkback mic. Always know when it is on. Keep your words short and clear. Never say something rude that the artist will hear in their cans.', practical_application='The engineer holds talkback only while speaking, gives concise notes, and keeps private control-room chat off the performers'' headphones.', purpose_function='Keeps control-room communication clear, concise, and professional, protecting the session atmosphere and the performer''s confidence.', related_terms=ARRAY['Talkback','Headphone communication','Cue mix','Control room','Session workflow']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Giving concise, encouraging notes between vocal takes','Keeping control-room chatter off the artist''s headphones']::text[] WHERE id='c3a7e52c-4c2d-4971-abc6-a3d241475814';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Studio space / production workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Neglecting monitoring and acoustics because it is ''just for beats''','Not organizing samples and backing up project files','Assuming a beat room needs no calibrated reference']::text[], definition='A beat-production room is a studio space set up for creating instrumental tracks and beats — typically centered on a computer/DAW, a keyboard or pad controller, and monitoring — where a producer programs drums, samples, and parts rather than tracking a live ensemble.', plain_english='A beat-production room is a space made for making beats. It usually has a computer and a DAW. It has a keyboard or pads to play parts. A producer builds tracks here instead of recording a live band.', practical_application='A producer builds a hip-hop instrumental on pads and a DAW in the beat room; artists then record vocals to it there or elsewhere.', purpose_function='Provides a focused environment centered on a DAW and controllers for programming and arranging beats and instrumentals rather than tracking a live ensemble.', related_terms=ARRAY['Pad controller','DAW','Sampling','Project studio','Creative approval session']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Programming an instrumental before a vocal session','Setting up pads and a DAW for a producer''s beat-making workflow']::text[] WHERE id='bc61d5a8-1979-4e1b-b58e-bb85d2713a02';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production workflow / approvals', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Not documenting the decisions made in the meeting','Presenting unfinished work without context, inviting confusion','Treating creative approval as final technical sign-off']::text[], definition='A creative approval session is a scheduled review in which the artist, producer, label, or client listens to work in progress or a candidate version and decides whether the creative direction is approved or needs changes before the project moves forward.', plain_english='A creative approval session is a listening meeting. The client or artist hears the work so far. They say if the direction is good. Or they ask for changes before the project moves on.', practical_application='A producer plays rough mixes for the label A&R, notes requested changes, and gets the go-ahead to proceed to final mixing.', purpose_function='Secures stakeholder buy-in on creative direction at defined checkpoints, reducing costly changes later in the project.', related_terms=ARRAY['Final approval','Revision pass','Rough mix','Client reference','Session documentation']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Playing rough mixes for label approval before the final mix','Reviewing candidate arrangements with the artist to lock direction']::text[] WHERE id='773d447f-5496-45a6-8d07-7df79c8e0ce1';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Session direction / performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Giving an unclear or late cue, causing a missed entry','Cueing over talkback while the take is being recorded','Assuming the performer heard the cue without confirming']::text[], definition='A cue instruction is a direction given to a performer that signals when or how to play or sing — such as a count-in, a hand or verbal cue to enter, or a marked instruction on a chart — coordinating the performance with the recording.', plain_english='A cue instruction tells a player when to come in. It can be a count-in or a hand signal. It can be a note written on the music. It keeps the performance in time with the recording.', practical_application='The producer counts a singer in, or the arranger marks ''enter at bar 9'' so an overdub lands in exactly the right spot.', purpose_function='Coordinates precise performer entries and actions with the take through counts, gestures, or marked directions.', related_terms=ARRAY['Count-in','Click track','Punch-in','Talkback','Lead sheet']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Counting a vocalist in for a punch-in','Signaling a horn section to enter on a specific bar']::text[] WHERE id='aa31e69d-1b6c-4a86-9e4f-89f0f82b89b6';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Session backup / documentation', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Keeping only one copy on the working drive','Backing up the audio but not the project or session file','Never testing that the backup actually restores']::text[], definition='An end-of-day archive is the routine of backing up a session''s project files, recorded audio, and documentation to secondary storage at the close of each working day, protecting against drive failure, corruption, or loss before work resumes.', plain_english='An end-of-day archive is a daily backup. At the end of the day you copy the session to another drive. This protects the work if a drive fails. Then you can start again safely tomorrow.', practical_application='The engineer copies the day''s session and audio to a second drive, and often a cloud or off-site copy, before leaving — keeping at least two copies.', purpose_function='Safeguards each day''s project files, recorded audio, and documentation with backups to secondary (and ideally off-site) storage, following redundancy best practice.', related_terms=ARRAY['Backup','Session documentation','3-2-1 backup rule','Engineer handoff','Version control']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Copying a tracking day''s session to a backup drive before leaving','Making an off-site copy at day''s end during a multi-day project']::text[] WHERE id='0a1faa54-db6c-48ae-a7cd-5761dbd3da5c';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Session scheduling', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Setting call time too close to the artist''s arrival, leaving no setup margin','Not accounting for load-in and line-check time','Confusing the engineer''s call time with the artist''s call time']::text[], definition='Engineer call time is the scheduled time the engineer is required to be present and ready to work — typically before the artists'' arrival — allowing time for setup, patching, and line check so recording can start on time.', plain_english='Engineer call time is when the engineer must arrive. It is usually before the artists come. This gives time to set up and patch. Then recording can start on schedule.', practical_application='With a noon downbeat, the engineer''s call time is set for 10 a.m. to place mics, patch, and line-check before the band arrives.', purpose_function='Schedules the engineer to arrive early enough to set up, patch, and line-check so recording can begin on time when performers arrive.', related_terms=ARRAY['Call time','Setup schedule','Downbeat','Load-in','Engineer handoff']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Arriving early to prep before a band''s downbeat','Coordinating an assistant''s earlier call time for setup']::text[] WHERE id='fd118a8e-5567-4b13-a853-f25e716b5b17';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Session workflow / continuity', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Handing off without written notes, forcing the next engineer to reverse-engineer the session','Not confirming file locations and versions','Omitting client preferences and pending revisions']::text[], definition='An engineer handoff is the transfer of an in-progress session from one engineer to another — for a shift change or when a different specialist takes over — including passing session files, track sheets, settings, and notes so work continues seamlessly and nothing is lost.', plain_english='An engineer handoff is passing a session to another engineer. It happens at a shift change. You share the files, notes, and settings. This lets the next person carry on smoothly.', practical_application='The night engineer documents recorded takes, routing, and pending edits and briefs the incoming engineer so the project continues seamlessly.', purpose_function='Preserves continuity and context when responsibility for a session moves between engineers, so work continues without rework or lost information.', related_terms=ARRAY['Track sheet','Session documentation','Recall sheet','Collaborative editing','End-of-day archive']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Passing a session between a day shift and a night shift','Transferring a project to a mixing specialist after tracking']::text[] WHERE id='33ed6e19-d53e-4532-b3ff-1154890f8ba8';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Location recording / equipment', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: Overloading the rack''s power distribution or daisy-chaining power strips, risking overheating or fire','Not securing gear for transport, risking damage or injury','Forgetting spare cables, power, and backup media in the rack']::text[], definition='A field-production rack is a portable, ruggedized equipment rack assembled for location or field recording that houses the core signal chain — such as preamps, converters, a recorder, power distribution, and monitoring — in a transportable, quickly deployable form.', plain_english='A field-production rack is a portable rack of gear for recording on location. It holds preamps, a recorder, and power. It is built to travel and set up fast. You take it where the session is.', practical_application='The recordist rolls the field rack into a venue, powers it, patches the stage split, and records a live performance from it.', purpose_function='Packages the essential recording chain — preamps, converters, recorder, power distribution, and monitoring — into a rugged, transportable unit for on-location work.', related_terms=ARRAY['Portable monitor controller','Battery-powered preamplifier','Remote patch panel','Stage box','Mobile monitoring position']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Rolling a portable rack into a venue for a live recording','Deploying a field rack for a remote multitrack session']::text[] WHERE id='104fcd15-8b69-4811-89ed-a3a5de0200f5';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Review and sign-off', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Listening only in short jumps instead of playing the whole piece through','Judging on tired ears or at the very end of a long day','Using unfamiliar speakers so problems are missed','Skipping it and delivering straight from the mix']::text[], definition='A concentrated critical-listening pass at the end of a project in which the finished mix or master is played through in full, on trusted monitoring, to confirm it is ready before it is approved and delivered.', plain_english='This is a last, careful listen to the finished audio. You play the whole thing from start to end. You use monitors you trust. You listen for any problem left in it. If it sounds right, it is ready to send.', practical_application='The engineer plays the full master uninterrupted at a moderate level, sometimes on more than one set of speakers, noting anything to revisit, then confirms the version to be delivered.', purpose_function='Catches faults, weak spots, and doubts while there is still time to fix them, and gives everyone confidence that the finished audio meets the project''s goals before sign-off.', related_terms=ARRAY['Final technical review','Producer playback','Reference take','Mastering','Technical approval session']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A mastering engineer auditioning a finished album before making the delivery files','A mix engineer doing a final full-length pass before client approval','A team gathering to confirm a track is ready to release']::text[] WHERE id='a02f56a1-3832-4705-a7d8-4250a0cb659b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Quality control', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confirming loudness but missing an inter-sample clip or short dropout','Delivering the wrong sample rate or bit depth for the target','Leaving incorrect or blank metadata on the file','Trusting a plugin meter without an independent check']::text[], definition='A quality-control check of a finished deliverable that verifies technical specifications such as levels, headroom, absence of clipping, dropouts, or unwanted noise, correct file format, sample rate, bit depth, and metadata before release.', plain_english='This is a last check of the technical side of a file. You confirm levels and format are correct. You listen and look for clicks, clipping, or gaps. You check the file details match the specs. Only then is it cleared.', practical_application='The engineer checks peak and loudness readings, scans for clipping and dropouts, confirms sample rate, bit depth, and file type against the delivery spec, and verifies metadata before exporting the final files.', purpose_function='Prevents technically faulty or non-conforming files from being delivered, protecting the release from rejected masters, failed QC, and costly re-delivery.', related_terms=ARRAY['Final listening session','Technical approval session','Mastering','Loudness normalization','Delivery specification']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Checking a master against a streaming platform''s technical spec','Verifying broadcast deliverables against a loudness standard','A post-production QC pass before handing files to distribution']::text[] WHERE id='25e618bb-cd77-4ad2-b0c2-eecc80d03ca6';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Data management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming an incremental sync captured a file that was open and locked during the copy','Never verifying that the synced copy actually restores','Relying on one sync target with no second, offline backup','Confusing a mirror that deletes with a true versioned backup']::text[], definition='A file-syncing method that copies only the data that has changed since the previous sync, rather than the whole session, keeping local and remote or backup copies of a project aligned quickly and with minimal transfer.', plain_english='This keeps two copies of a project in step. It moves only the parts that changed since last time. It does not copy the whole session again. This makes each update fast. It saves time and storage.', practical_application='During a long session the engineer lets sync software or the DAW''s cloud service push only new and edited files to a backup drive or server, so an up-to-date copy always exists.', purpose_function='Makes frequent backups and remote transfers practical by moving far less data, so session copies stay current without long waits or wasted bandwidth and storage.', related_terms=ARRAY['Portable archive system','Secure session transfer','Session download','Remote asset management','Session merge']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Auto-backing up a session to a NAS between takes','Keeping a cloud copy of a project current for a remote collaborator','Syncing an on-location recording to a studio server overnight']::text[] WHERE id='431b2084-12d6-44d7-b4f7-86c2038f6cd7';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Tracking workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Reviewing at a very different level than it was tracked at, skewing the judgment','Reviewing every single take and killing the session''s momentum','Deleting a take right after a quick listen instead of keeping it','Judging tone on poor headphones during a quick check']::text[], definition='Immediate playback of a take right after it is recorded, letting the engineer and performers evaluate the just-captured performance without leaving the recording workflow or reconfiguring the system.', plain_english='This is playing back a take the moment it ends. You do not stop to set anything up. The players and engineer hear it at once. They decide if it is good. Then they keep it or record again.', practical_application='After a take the engineer drops the transport back and plays it in the control room or over headphones, and the artist and producer quickly decide whether to move on or do another.', purpose_function='Speeds decisions during tracking by letting everyone judge a take while the performance is fresh, so the session keeps momentum and problems are caught early.', related_terms=ARRAY['Producer playback','Reference take','Safety take request','Punch-in','Comping']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Checking a vocal take before moving to the next line','A drummer listening back to confirm timing on a pass','A producer approving a solo immediately after it is played']::text[] WHERE id='ed7b973c-d25e-4827-860b-fa0554f19fd4';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mobile and remote gear', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: loading the cart top-heavy so it can tip when wheeled over a threshold or ramp','UNSAFE: leaving gear and cables unsecured so items fall during transport','Running everything from one outlet and overloading the circuit','Not managing cable strain relief, so connectors work loose in transit']::text[], definition='A wheeled cart or rolling rack that holds a DAW computer, audio interface, and often monitoring, letting an engineer move a complete workstation between rooms or to a location and set it up quickly.', plain_english='This is a rolling cart that carries a whole workstation. It holds the computer, interface, and often speakers. You wheel it where it is needed. You plug it in and work. It makes a setup easy to move.', practical_application='The engineer wires the computer, interface, and monitors into the cart once, then rolls it into a tracking room, edit bay, or venue and connects power and inputs to start working.', purpose_function='Lets a working setup be relocated and deployed fast, supporting sessions in different rooms or on location without rebuilding the rig each time.', related_terms=ARRAY['Remote equipment cart','Portable machine room','Temporary control room','Touring recording system','Remote audio interface']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Rolling an edit rig between two studio rooms','Bringing a workstation to a rehearsal space to record','Setting up a portable tracking rig at a client''s office']::text[] WHERE id='7dc8b8b7-3a5b-4a3f-99b9-9bfaf0a656e7';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Studio space', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Treating the room so dead that the piano loses its natural bloom','Ignoring mechanical noise from the piano action or a nearby HVAC vent','Poor mic placement that captures an uneven, boxy tone','Assuming any spare room works without checking its acoustics']::text[], definition='A dedicated, often acoustically treated isolation room within a studio built to house and record an acoustic piano, chosen for its size, ambience, and separation from the main tracking space.', plain_english='This is a room set aside for a piano. It is built to sound good for that instrument. It keeps the piano apart from other players. It is treated for the right room sound. Mics there capture the piano cleanly.', practical_application='The engineer places the grand piano in the room, sets mics inside and at a distance for room tone, and records it while other musicians play in separate spaces without spill.', purpose_function='Gives a large acoustic instrument a suitable, controlled space and keeps it isolated from other sources, so the piano can be miked cleanly and captured with the desired room character.', related_terms=ARRAY['Isolation booth','Programming room','Production suite','Live room','Machine room']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Tracking a grand piano for a ballad while the band plays elsewhere','A studio offering a well-tuned piano room as a selling point','Recording solo piano for a classical or jazz session']::text[] WHERE id='7564b234-eb62-4226-8f8f-ac817925449a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Data management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Archiving without verifying the copy actually reads back','Keeping only one archive copy with no second location','Forgetting to include plugin data or media the session depends on','Storing drives in heat or without protection during transport']::text[], definition='A transportable storage setup, such as external drives, a small NAS, or LTO tape, used to back up and archive session files on location or move an archived project safely between facilities.', plain_english='This is storage you can carry to keep sessions safe. It may be drives, a small NAS, or tape. You copy finished projects onto it. You can move it between places. It protects the work long term.', practical_application='At the end of a project the engineer copies the full session and its media to the archive drives or tape, verifies the copy, labels it, and stores or ships it for safekeeping.', purpose_function='Provides safe, movable long-term storage for completed or in-progress sessions, protecting against drive failure and allowing projects to be handed off or stored off-site.', related_terms=ARRAY['Incremental synchronization','Secure session transfer','Remote asset management','Session download','Mobile workstation cart']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Backing up a day''s tracking on location before travel','Archiving a finished album to LTO for long-term storage','Handing a project archive to another studio for a mix']::text[] WHERE id='6bcb5885-a275-4ebd-811c-05991bc74c8d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mobile and remote gear', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: enclosing gear so tightly that it overheats without airflow','Placing the noisy rack within mic range of the performers','Running signal cables alongside power without regard for noise pickup','Forgetting the extra cable length needed to keep the rack remote']::text[], definition='A transportable rack or enclosure that houses noisy equipment such as computers, converters, and recorders, kept away from the listening position, used to recreate a studio machine room in a remote or temporary setup.', plain_english='This is a movable box for noisy gear. It holds computers and recorders. It is placed away from where you listen. That keeps fan and drive noise out of the room. It copies a studio machine room on the go.', practical_application='For a location session the engineer places the racked computers and converters in an adjacent space or isolated case and runs cabling to the temporary control position, keeping fan noise away from mics and monitors.', purpose_function='Keeps heat and mechanical noise from computers and drives out of the recording and monitoring space during remote or temporary sessions, preserving quiet and clean acoustics.', related_terms=ARRAY['Machine room','Temporary control room','Remote equipment cart','Mobile workstation cart','Remote audio interface']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A remote classical session with computers racked in a side room','Keeping a loud workstation out of a quiet acoustic tracking space','A location shoot where recorders sit away from the set']::text[] WHERE id='2e13aee6-7b14-434d-bc11-0a63552e94a7';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Session documentation', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Writing notes so vague that a take can''t be identified later','Not tying comments to specific take or timecode references','Keeping notes only in one person''s head or private device','Losing the notes because they were never saved with the session']::text[], definition='Written notes kept by the producer during a session that document creative decisions, preferred takes, arrangement changes, direction given, and tasks still to be done, forming part of the session''s documentation.', plain_english='These are notes the producer writes during a session. They record which takes are best. They list changes and choices made. They note what still needs doing. Later, everyone can follow the plan.', practical_application='As the session runs the producer marks favored takes, notes edits, tuning, or arrangement ideas, and lists follow-ups, then shares these notes with the engineer for editing and the next session.', purpose_function='Captures the producer''s decisions and intentions so the session can be continued, recalled, or handed off accurately without relying on memory.', related_terms=ARRAY['Track sheet','Take log','Production recap','Revision request log','Reference take']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A producer marking keeper takes during a vocal session','Passing decision notes to an editor for comping','Reviewing yesterday''s notes to plan the next day''s work']::text[] WHERE id='075e4c14-8cc7-4441-a629-7a73bc1aec50';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Tracking workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Playing back far louder than the tracking level, flattering the take','Playing an unbalanced rough that misleads the decision','Not resetting monitoring after a soloed check','Rushing the playback so the producer can''t judge properly']::text[], definition='A monitored playback of recorded material staged for the producer, at an appropriate level and on the control-room monitors, so they can evaluate the performance and make production decisions.', plain_english='This is playing recorded parts back for the producer. It is done on the main speakers. It is set at a good level. The producer listens and judges the take. Then they decide what to do next.', practical_application='The engineer sets a suitable monitoring level, solos or plays the relevant tracks, and runs the passage for the producer, who signals whether to keep it, fix it, or record again.', purpose_function='Gives the producer a clear, controlled listen so they can approve takes, request changes, or guide the next step in the session.', related_terms=ARRAY['Instant review','Producer notes','Reference take','Final listening session','Comping']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Playing a comped vocal for the producer to approve','A band gathering to hear the last take with the producer','Reviewing a solo before deciding on another pass']::text[] WHERE id='44de42eb-f7a5-4aba-8a5c-ced5149c18f0';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Session documentation', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recapping so briefly that key decisions are lost','Failing to list outstanding tasks for the next session','Not filing the recap where the team can find it','Recording opinions but not the actual take numbers used']::text[], definition='A summary compiled at or after a production session that records what was accomplished, which takes were kept, decisions made, and outstanding tasks, used for the project''s records and for handoff to the next stage.', plain_english='This is a short summary of a session''s work. It lists what got done. It notes the takes that were kept. It records choices and open tasks. It helps the next session pick up smoothly.', practical_application='At the end of a day the producer or engineer writes up which songs and parts were tracked, the keeper takes, decisions reached, and the to-do list, then files it with the session.', purpose_function='Provides a clear record of session outcomes so progress is tracked, nothing is forgotten, and anyone continuing the project knows exactly where it stands.', related_terms=ARRAY['Producer notes','Track sheet','Take log','Revision request log','Final technical review']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Wrapping a tracking day with a written summary','Handing a project to a mix engineer with a status recap','Updating a client on what was achieved in a session']::text[] WHERE id='1843562e-3542-4c1d-8ba0-a97823c9de93';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Studio space', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Ignoring room acoustics because the space is small','Overfilling the room so monitoring position suffers','Assuming a suite can double as a full live tracking room','Poor monitor placement in an untreated near-field setup']::text[], definition='A room or set of connected rooms equipped for music and audio production, typically combining writing, programming, and mixing capability in a compact, integrated space rather than a large commercial tracking studio.', plain_english='This is a room set up for making music. It suits writing, programming, and mixing. It is smaller than a big studio. Everything needed is in one place. One person or a small team can work there.', practical_application='A producer works in the suite with a DAW, controllers, monitors, and instruments to build arrangements, program parts, record overdubs, and mix, often handling a whole project in that one space.', purpose_function='Provides a focused, self-contained space for production and mixing work, giving producers an efficient environment without the scale or cost of a full tracking facility.', related_terms=ARRAY['Programming room','Piano room','Temporary control room','Machine room','Mobile workstation cart']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A producer building tracks in an in-house production suite','A songwriter demoing ideas in a compact suite','A facility renting suites for individual producers']::text[] WHERE id='dd1db7fc-1e22-4c15-8f46-bcfce015b79d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Studio space', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Neglecting monitoring accuracy because no acoustic tracking happens','Assuming the room needs no treatment at all','Cramming gear so the working position is compromised','Confusing it with a full tracking room for live drums']::text[], definition='A studio space set up primarily for MIDI sequencing, beat and drum programming, and virtual-instrument production rather than live acoustic tracking, centered on a DAW, controllers, and sound sources.', plain_english='This is a room made for programming music. It centers on a computer and keyboards. It is used for beats and MIDI parts. It is not built for loud live tracking. A producer builds tracks there.', practical_application='The producer sequences drums, plays in synth and sampler parts from a MIDI controller, edits the MIDI, and builds the arrangement in the DAW, monitoring on near-field speakers.', purpose_function='Gives producers a comfortable, well-monitored space to program and arrange electronic and sampled parts, where quiet, hands-on work matters more than acoustic isolation for loud sources.', related_terms=ARRAY['Production suite','Piano room','Temporary control room','Mobile workstation cart','Machine room']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Programming a beat and bassline before a vocal session','A composer sequencing a cue in a dedicated programming room','Building electronic arrangements for later overdubs']::text[] WHERE id='2af9e401-adfd-4b58-815e-144e34af4644';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Collaboration workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Two people editing the same track without any locking, causing conflicts','Assuming a poor connection will still keep everyone in sync','Not agreeing who owns which parts before starting','Forgetting that large media still takes time to upload for others']::text[], definition='A workflow in which multiple users work on the same project over a network with changes shared as they happen, typically through a cloud-based DAW service that lets collaborators see and hear each other''s updates live.', plain_english='This is when people work on one project at once. They are in different places. Changes show up for everyone right away. A cloud service links their DAWs. They build the track together in real time.', practical_application='Using a service such as a DAW''s cloud collaboration feature, each user opens the shared project, and as one adds or edits tracks the changes upload and appear in the others'' sessions.', purpose_function='Lets separated collaborators contribute to the same session together, speeding up shared work and removing the need to keep emailing files back and forth.', related_terms=ARRAY['Remote asset management','Session lockout','Session merge','Secure session transfer','Session download']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Two producers in different cities building a track together','A vocalist adding parts to a shared cloud project','A remote mixer working on the same session as the producer']::text[] WHERE id='57e6f7f9-224e-4cd2-9c0d-1251193e9d22';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Takes and comping', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Treating the reference as final and not improving on it','Building all overdubs on a reference that later gets replaced, causing timing drift','Not labeling which take is the reference','Deleting the reference before the comp is finished']::text[], definition='A recorded take kept as a benchmark or guide for comparison, used to judge later performances, to hold an arrangement in place, or to serve as a scratch reference for tracking other parts.', plain_english='This is a take kept as a guide. Other takes are compared to it. It sets the feel or the arrangement. Players can track against it. It may be replaced later by a better one.', practical_application='The engineer keeps an early strong take as the reference, then plays it back while later takes are recorded or compared, and the producer judges new passes against its feel and timing.', purpose_function='Gives the session a fixed point to measure and align other performances against, keeping arrangement, timing, and feel consistent as parts are built up.', related_terms=ARRAY['Scratch track','Guide track','Safety take request','Comping','Producer playback']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Keeping a strong early vocal as a reference for comping','Tracking overdubs against a reference rhythm take','Comparing new solo passes to an agreed reference']::text[] WHERE id='0fc222e6-b63a-41b0-8a13-976bf85afe52';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Collaboration workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Judging tone over a lossy stream that misrepresents the audio','Not confirming what the client''s playback system sounds like','Failing to log the changes and approvals agreed on the call','Assuming spoken approval without written confirmation']::text[], definition='A session in which a client or producer reviews and signs off on material from another location, typically through streamed playback or a video link, instead of being physically present in the room.', plain_english='This is a review done from far away. The client is not in the room. They hear the audio over a link. They give notes or approve it. The engineer changes things or moves on.', practical_application='The engineer streams the mix to the remote client over a monitoring or video service, plays the material, takes their feedback live, and confirms approval or notes changes to make.', purpose_function='Lets clients and producers approve work without traveling, keeping projects moving when key people cannot attend in person.', related_terms=ARRAY['Remote asset management','Technical approval session','Producer playback','Revision request log','Real-time collaboration']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A client approving a mix from their office by streamed playback','A producer giving notes on a session over a video link','Signing off a master remotely before delivery']::text[] WHERE id='3761f053-3b01-446e-9cae-09368fdec4c0';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Data management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Uploading files with vague names so versions can''t be told apart','Assuming the managed copy removes the need for a backup','Poor access controls exposing assets to the wrong people','Not tracking which version is the current, approved one']::text[], definition='The organizing, cataloging, and controlling of a project''s media assets such as session files and recordings through a system that can be accessed and administered remotely, often a cloud or media-asset-management platform.', plain_english='This is keeping track of project files from afar. All the media is organized in one system. People can reach it over a network. It shows what exists and who can use it. It keeps assets in order across a team.', practical_application='The team stores sessions and media on a managed cloud or asset system, tags and versions them, and each collaborator pulls the files they need without duplicating or misplacing assets.', purpose_function='Keeps a project''s growing library of files organized, findable, and access-controlled across a distributed team, reducing lost work and version confusion.', related_terms=ARRAY['Secure session transfer','Session download','Incremental synchronization','Real-time collaboration','Portable archive system']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A studio keeping all client sessions in a managed cloud library','A team pulling shared stems from a central asset system','Tracking versions of a project across several collaborators']::text[] WHERE id='2ba9240a-2cd2-4ba5-b867-9e2ad8a0f423';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mobile and remote gear', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: powering a stage unit without proper grounding, risking shock and hum','Exceeding the network''s channel or latency limits','Using an unreliable link that drops audio mid-session','Forgetting the remote unit needs its own stable power']::text[], definition='An audio interface or converter unit placed away from the host computer, such as a stagebox or networked converter, connected to the system over a digital or network link so inputs and outputs can sit close to the sources.', plain_english='This is an interface kept away from the computer. It sits near the players or stage. It links back over a cable or network. Mics plug into it close by. This keeps the analog cable runs short.', practical_application='The engineer places the remote unit near the performers or on stage, plugs mics and lines into it, and carries the audio back to the workstation over a network or digital snake.', purpose_function='Puts conversion and connections close to the sources rather than at the computer, shortening analog cable runs and letting inputs be placed where they are needed.', related_terms=ARRAY['Portable machine room','Remote equipment cart','Mobile workstation cart','Temporary control room','Touring recording system']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A stagebox feeding a recording rig at a live venue','Placing converters near a drum kit to shorten mic runs','A networked interface at a remote location session']::text[] WHERE id='f4217ffb-21af-4240-aa3d-731f0388173a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mobile and remote gear', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: overloading the cart so it becomes top-heavy and can tip on a ramp','UNSAFE: failing to strap gear down, so items fall during transport','Packing it disorganized so setup is slowed anyway','Ignoring cable management, leaving trip hazards on the floor']::text[], definition='A wheeled cart used to carry and organize recording, monitoring, and support gear for use at remote or location sessions, letting a crew move and deploy equipment quickly outside a fixed studio.', plain_english='This is a rolling cart for location gear. It carries recorders, cables, and tools. A crew wheels it to the job. It keeps the gear together and ready. It speeds setup away from the studio.', practical_application='The crew loads the cart with the interface, cables, stands, and support gear, wheels it into the venue, and works from it, keeping everything at hand during the session.', purpose_function='Keeps location gear organized and mobile so a remote setup can be transported and deployed efficiently, reducing setup time and lost items on the job.', related_terms=ARRAY['Mobile workstation cart','Portable machine room','Remote audio interface','Temporary control room','Touring recording system']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Wheeling recording gear into a concert hall for a location session','A crew moving support equipment between venue rooms','Transporting a portable rig from truck to stage']::text[] WHERE id='398ee634-e1c2-4b24-aff7-afacc99ec979';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Session documentation', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Logging vague requests without a timecode or section reference','Not marking which requests are done, so some are repeated or missed','Keeping the log only in email threads that get lost','Closing revisions without checking every logged item']::text[], definition='A running document that records each change requested by a client or producer during mix or master revisions, along with its status and how it was resolved, keeping the revision process organized and traceable.', plain_english='This is a list of change requests. Each note from the client goes on it. It shows if a change is done. It records how each one was handled. Nothing gets missed or forgotten.', practical_application='As feedback arrives the engineer logs each request with the timecode or section, marks it done or pending as it is addressed, and reviews the log to confirm all changes were made.', purpose_function='Ensures every requested change is captured, tracked, and confirmed, preventing missed notes and disputes over what was asked for during revisions.', related_terms=ARRAY['Producer notes','Production recap','Remote approval session','Technical approval session','Final technical review']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Tracking a client''s mix notes across several revision rounds','Confirming all requested master changes were made before delivery','Managing feedback from multiple stakeholders on one project']::text[] WHERE id='b5cd48c1-1e7b-4894-a5fc-3a7542b47337';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Takes and comping', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Skipping the safety and later finding a flaw in the only take','Not labeling the safety take, so it is confused with the keeper','Asking for so many safeties that the performance tires and drops','Deleting safeties before editing is finished']::text[], definition='A request to record one more take after an acceptable performance has been captured, kept as insurance so a backup exists in case a fault is discovered later in the approved take.', plain_english='This is asking for one extra take. A good take is already done. The extra one is a backup. It guards against a problem found later. It costs little to grab while everyone is ready.', practical_application='After a keeper take the producer asks for a safety, the performer runs it once more while warm and set up, and the engineer records and labels it as a backup.', purpose_function='Provides an insurance take so the session is protected if the chosen performance turns out to have a hidden flaw, avoiding a costly recall of the performer.', related_terms=ARRAY['Reference take','Instant review','Comping','Producer playback','Punch-in']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Grabbing an extra vocal pass after a keeper take','A safety take before releasing a session musician','Recording a backup of a difficult solo just in case']::text[] WHERE id='4099033d-a6cf-43a2-af1a-46a5a28de670';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Data management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Sending unreleased material over an unprotected, public link','Sharing the password over the same channel as the files','Not verifying the files arrived complete and uncorrupted','Assuming a service is secure without checking how it protects data']::text[], definition='The moving of session files between parties using encrypted, authenticated methods that protect the files'' confidentiality and integrity in transit, guarding unreleased material against interception or tampering.', plain_english='This is sending session files safely. The files are protected in transit. Only the right person can open them. It stops others from seeing or changing them. It keeps unreleased work private.', practical_application='The engineer sends the session through an encrypted transfer service or password-protected package, confirms the recipient''s identity, and the recipient verifies the files downloaded intact.', purpose_function='Protects sensitive or unreleased material as it moves between collaborators, preventing leaks and ensuring the files arrive complete and unaltered.', related_terms=ARRAY['Session download','Remote asset management','Incremental synchronization','Session lockout','Portable archive system']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Delivering an unreleased artist''s session to a mix engineer','Sending stems to a remote collaborator under an NDA','Transferring master files securely to a distributor']::text[] WHERE id='0a100895-1a25-45b5-b285-ee1926b0397e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Collaboration workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Opening a session before all its media has finished downloading','Downloading to a drive too slow or too full for playback','Grabbing an outdated version instead of the current one','Assuming plugins and settings transfer with the media']::text[], definition='Retrieving a stored or cloud-hosted session''s files onto a local workstation so the project can be opened and worked on, pulling the media and session data from a server or shared storage.', plain_english='This is getting a session onto your computer. The files live on a server or cloud. You download them locally. Then you can open the project. Now you can work on it.', practical_application='The engineer opens the cloud project, downloads the session and its media to a local drive, waits for large files to finish, and then opens the fully populated session in the DAW.', purpose_function='Lets a collaborator obtain the current project files from shared storage so they can open and work on the session locally with everything it needs.', related_terms=ARRAY['Secure session transfer','Remote asset management','Incremental synchronization','Real-time collaboration','Session merge']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Pulling a cloud project to a local rig to start mixing','A collaborator downloading shared stems to add parts','Retrieving an archived session from server storage']::text[] WHERE id='727ca5a4-d9bc-4e9c-ae2d-26f64475fe73';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Collaboration workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Holding a lock long after finishing and blocking teammates','Assuming no lock is needed and overwriting another''s edits','Forgetting to release or check a track back in','Not knowing who currently holds the lock on a part']::text[], definition='A control that prevents others from editing a session, or a checked-out part of it, while one user has it open, so simultaneous conflicting changes cannot occur in a shared or collaborative project.', plain_english='This stops two people editing the same thing at once. One user opens or checks out a part. It is locked for others while they work. This avoids clashing changes. When done, they release it.', practical_application='In a collaborative DAW a user checks out or opens a track, which locks it for others; they make edits and then release or check it back in so a teammate can take over.', purpose_function='Prevents edit conflicts and lost work in shared projects by ensuring only one person can change a given session or track at a time.', related_terms=ARRAY['Real-time collaboration','Session merge','Remote asset management','Session download','Secure session transfer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Checking out a track in a cloud session to edit it','Preventing two mixers from editing the same automation','Locking a master session while final tweaks are made']::text[] WHERE id='c3dd53be-02cc-484e-a95f-295d858ed297';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Editing workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Merging without matching timecode so imported parts land out of place','Importing duplicate tracks and creating confusion','Overwriting existing automation by importing carelessly','Not checking sample rate and format compatibility before merging']::text[], definition='Combining data from one session into another by importing tracks, regions, automation, or settings from a separate session file, as with a DAW''s import-session-data function, to consolidate work into a single project.', plain_english='This joins two sessions into one. You bring tracks from one into another. Regions and automation can come across too. It uses the DAW''s import feature. Now the work sits in one project.', practical_application='The engineer uses the DAW''s import-session-data command to pull chosen tracks, playlists, or automation from another session into the current one, matching timecode so everything lines up.', purpose_function='Lets separately recorded or edited work be brought together into one session, consolidating overdubs, alternate versions, or a collaborator''s parts into the main project.', related_terms=ARRAY['Session download','Real-time collaboration','Session lockout','Comping','Remote asset management']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Importing a vocalist''s overdub session into the main project','Consolidating parts recorded in two separate sessions','Bringing a collaborator''s tracks into the master session']::text[] WHERE id='2ab6df39-1c44-4565-8de5-8af2ea666cb8';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Quality control', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Approving on a subjective listen without checking the actual specs','Missing a wrong sample rate or format in the deliverable','Signing off without confirming metadata is correct','Not recording who approved and when']::text[], definition='A review in which finished deliverables are checked and signed off against technical specifications, confirming that formats, levels, and file requirements are met before the material is released or handed on.', plain_english='This is a check to approve the technical side. The files are compared to the required specs. Levels and formats are confirmed. If all is correct, it is signed off. Then the work can be delivered.', practical_application='The team reviews the finished files against the delivery spec, verifying loudness, format, and metadata, and formally approves them for delivery once every requirement is confirmed.', purpose_function='Confirms that deliverables meet the required technical standards before release, preventing rejected or non-conforming files from reaching the client or platform.', related_terms=ARRAY['Final technical review','Final listening session','Revision request log','Remote approval session','Delivery specification']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Signing off broadcast deliverables against a loudness spec','Approving master files against a distributor''s requirements','A final technical sign-off before archiving a project']::text[] WHERE id='1af6d8f2-e8f1-4f26-b557-21a3021a29e6';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mobile and remote gear', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Trusting monitoring in an untreated, unfamiliar room without checks','UNSAFE: running power for the rig without regard for load and grounding','Placing monitors poorly so the balance is misjudged','Not allowing time to tune the setup before recording']::text[], definition='A control and monitoring position set up on a short-term basis outside a permanent facility, such as in a remote truck, hotel room, or location space, to run a specific session before being struck.', plain_english='This is a control room set up just for a job. It is not a permanent studio. It may be in a truck or a spare room. The engineer monitors and records there. It is taken down after the session.', practical_application='The engineer sets up monitors, the workstation, and cabling in the chosen space, checks the acoustics and monitoring, runs the session, and then strikes the setup afterward.', purpose_function='Provides a working monitoring and control position wherever a session must happen, letting an engineer record and mix on location without a built studio.', related_terms=ARRAY['Portable machine room','Mobile workstation cart','Remote equipment cart','Production suite','Touring recording system']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting up a control position in a remote recording truck','A temporary mix room in a hotel for an on-tour session','Running a location session from a converted side room']::text[] WHERE id='7a90b013-3f49-4b48-836d-6cf9664dff86';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mobile and remote gear', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: powering the rig without proper grounding across varying venue power, risking shock and hum','Recording to a single drive with no redundant backup','Skipping a line check so a channel is lost for the whole show','Underestimating heat and vibration on gear in the road case']::text[], definition='A portable, ruggedized multitrack recording rig carried on tour to capture live performances from night to night, built to be transported, set up, and struck repeatedly while reliably recording the show.', plain_english='This is a recording rig that travels with a tour. It is built tough to move each day. It records the live show every night. It is set up and torn down often. It must work reliably on the road.', practical_application='The engineer splits the stage feeds to a rugged multitrack recorder in a road case, records every show to redundant drives, and packs the system for transport to the next venue.', purpose_function='Lets a touring act capture consistent multitrack recordings of each performance for live releases, archives, or mixing, using gear that survives constant transport and quick setup.', related_terms=ARRAY['Remote equipment cart','Remote audio interface','Temporary control room','Mobile workstation cart','Portable archive system']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording every night of a tour for a live album','Capturing multitrack feeds from a festival stage','Archiving a band''s shows across a run of dates']::text[] WHERE id='0348eb09-204f-4470-8f71-382bdb269c6f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Live-event AV production role', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Loading an outdated version of the deck so the wrong slides appear on screen','Not testing embedded video, audio, and fonts before doors, causing playback errors live','Advancing slides on the wrong cue or drifting out of sync with the speaker']::text[], plain_english='A presentation engineer runs the slides and video during big events. They take cues from the show caller. They make sure the right image reaches the screen on time.', practical_application='Loads decks and clips into a playback system such as PowerPoint, Keynote, ProPresenter, or a media server, builds a cue list, and advances slides or plays clips on the show caller''s cues.', purpose_function='Ensures on-screen presentation content plays back accurately and on cue, so speakers'' visuals support the show without errors or delays.', related_terms=ARRAY['Show caller','Video operator','Media server','ProPresenter','LED wall','Front-of-house engineer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Running keynote slides at a corporate conference','Playing sponsor video clips between speakers','Cueing lower-thirds and graphics during a livestreamed general session']::text[] WHERE id='da88e705-46b6-4e7f-b43b-b00aedc4b8b5';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Sound-reinforcement system role', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: Flying loudspeaker arrays without verifying rigging hardware, load ratings, and safeties','Confusing the systems role with mixing the show at front of house','Skipping measurement and tuning, leaving uneven coverage and tonal imbalance']::text[], plain_english='A systems manager builds and tunes the main speaker system. They set up the PA for even, clear sound. This lets the front-of-house engineer work on a known rig.', practical_application='Specifies and rigs loudspeakers and amplifiers, distributes signal and power, then uses measurement software such as Smaart to align delays, set crossovers, and equalize the system before the show.', purpose_function='Delivers even coverage, correct level, and consistent tone across the audience, so the front-of-house engineer mixes on a predictable, optimized system.', related_terms=ARRAY['Front-of-house engineer','Line array','Smaart','System tuning','Loudspeaker alignment','Amplifier']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Deploying and aligning a line-array PA for an arena tour','Tuning coverage zones so the balcony matches the floor','Handing a tuned system to the front-of-house engineer before soundcheck']::text[] WHERE id='0393d7b8-64fa-4b63-9129-fd2ac302a2e6';
UPDATE glossary SET category='DJ set / event type', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Playing peak-time crowd-pleasers when the after-hours room wants a deeper, slower journey','Treating after-hours as simply ''late'' and ignoring its different energy and pacing','Burning out early instead of pacing a long set']::text[], definition='In club and DJ culture, an after-hours event or set is one that continues into the early morning after a venue''s normal closing time, and by extension the deeper, more hypnotic style of music suited to that late slot and its committed crowd.', plain_english='An after-hours is a party that runs late into the morning. It starts after the normal club closing time. The music is often deeper and more hypnotic.', practical_application='A DJ booked for an after-hours slot programs slower-building, deeper tracks and extended mixes, reading a smaller committed crowd over several hours.', purpose_function='Gives dancers a continued space past normal closing hours and lets DJs play longer, deeper, more experimental sets for a dedicated late crowd.', related_terms=ARRAY['Warm-up set','Peak time','Deep house','Extended set','Residency']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Playing a 4am-to-8am after-hours room at a club','Programming a deeper set for a late warehouse party','Extending a festival night into a smaller after-hours space']::text[] WHERE id='45d805ff-8c58-4d0e-93f0-5addb515b081';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Track type / crowd dynamics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Playing anthems too early and leaving nothing bigger for the peak','Overusing obvious anthems so a set feels predictable','Assuming every big track works as an anthem for every crowd']::text[], definition='In dance-music DJ culture, an anthem is a track that reliably unites a crowd — an emotionally euphoric, widely recognized record that produces a peak, hands-in-the-air or sing-along moment on the dancefloor.', plain_english='An anthem is a track that the whole crowd loves. It creates a big, emotional peak moment. People sing along or put their hands up.', practical_application='A DJ saves a known anthem for peak time, mixing into it as energy builds so the crowd erupts on the drop or vocal hook.', purpose_function='Gives a DJ a dependable emotional high point to lift the room and create a shared, memorable moment.', related_terms=ARRAY['Peak time','Drop','Crowd-pleaser','Vocal hook','Set programming']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Dropping a festival anthem at the main-stage peak','Closing a set with a well-known anthem','Reading the room before committing to an anthem moment']::text[] WHERE id='e5bf4e8f-be83-4ba4-bf53-b71f4618decb';
UPDATE glossary SET category='DJ event type', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Programming heavy peak-time material as if it were a late-night club','Ignoring daylight and a sober, chatty crowd''s different energy','Starting too hard with nowhere left to build']::text[], definition='A day party is a social music event or DJ gig held during daytime hours rather than late at night — typically running through the afternoon into early evening, often outdoors or at a rooftop or bar — with programming pitched to a relaxed, sociable daytime crowd.', plain_english='A day party is a party held in the daytime. It runs in the afternoon, not late at night. The vibe is relaxed and social.', practical_application='For a rooftop day party, a DJ plays sunlit, groove-driven house and classics, keeping the energy sociable rather than pushing constant peak-time drops.', purpose_function='Offers a lighter, earlier alternative to nightclubbing and shapes how a DJ programs, favoring warmer, groovier, less peak-intensity music.', related_terms=ARRAY['Warm-up set','Brunch set','Open format','Rooftop event','Set programming']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['DJing a summer rooftop day party','Programming a relaxed afternoon set for a bar day party','Playing a brunch-into-afternoon day party']::text[] WHERE id='9b1e01c9-c6fb-4385-95e6-a1800cdd4fb2';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mixing technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Misjudging phrasing so the two drops land out of sync','Clashing keys or basslines, producing a muddy or dissonant collision','Overusing the trick until it loses impact']::text[], definition='A mixing technique in which a DJ aligns two tracks so both reach their drop — the main rhythmic and energy climax — at the same moment, layering the two peaks together for heightened impact.', plain_english='A double drop is when a DJ lines up two songs. Both tracks hit their big drop at the same time. This stacks two peaks into one bigger moment.', practical_application='The DJ beatmatches and phrase-aligns both tracks, counts the bars to each drop, and releases the crossfade so the two drops land together.', purpose_function='Creates a dramatic, high-energy surprise by combining the climaxes of two tracks, rewarding an attentive crowd.', related_terms=ARRAY['Drop','Beatmatching','Phrasing','Crossfader','Harmonic mixing']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Aligning two drum-and-bass tracks for a double drop at peak time','Layering two builds so both drops land together','Planning a crowd-pleasing double drop in a prepared set']::text[] WHERE id='5373172f-e477-499d-b601-e1fa5e77751a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Arrangement / transition element', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making the fill too long or busy so it clutters the groove instead of bridging it','Placing the fill off the phrase boundary so the transition feels early or late','Relying on the same fill every time until it becomes predictable','Confusing a fill (a short bridging figure) with the main groove or a full breakdown']::text[], definition='In beatmatching, mixing, and FX, a fill is a short passage that bridges two sections of a track — typically a brief percussive figure (such as a drum fill) or an FX gesture like a roll, riser, or sweep — inserted at a phrase boundary to fill a momentary gap and signal the coming transition, often the lead-in to a drop.', plain_english='A fill is a short bit of sound between two parts of a track. It is often a quick drum run or an effect like a roll or sweep. It fills a small gap at the join. It also warns the crowd a change is coming, like a drop.', practical_application='Approaching a phrase change, a DJ or producer drops in a drum fill or an FX roll or riser over the last bar or two, using it to smooth the join and lead the crowd into the next section or the drop.', purpose_function='Marks the boundary between two sections, keeps energy moving across a gap, and cues both dancers and the incoming mix that a transition — often a drop — is about to happen.', related_terms=ARRAY['Roll effect','Riser','Drop','Transition','Breakdown','Sweep']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Dropping a drum fill over the last bar before a drop','Using an FX riser as a fill to bridge into a new section','Adding a quick fill to cover a momentary gap in the mix']::text[] WHERE id='3a441925-7a0d-4d80-84be-bd7eea69fe68';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Edit / remix technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming a flip clears the original''s copyright for release','Confusing a flip with a full official remix commissioned by the rights holder','Reworking a track so heavily that its recognizable hook is lost']::text[], definition='In music production and DJ culture, a flip is a reworked version of existing material — a track, sample, or acappella transformed into a new edit, bootleg, or remix — that repurposes the original into something with a fresh arrangement, tempo, or genre.', plain_english='A flip is a new version made from an existing track or sample. The producer reworks it into something fresh. It can change the beat, tempo, or style.', practical_application='A DJ makes a flip of a well-known acappella over a new beat, then drops it in sets as an exclusive edit the crowd recognizes but has not heard this way.', purpose_function='Lets producers and DJs create original-feeling, exclusive material and DJ tools from familiar records.', related_terms=ARRAY['Edit','Bootleg','Remix','Acappella','Sample','Mashup']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Making a flip of a classic vocal for peak-time sets','Producing a genre flip that speeds a track into a new style','Testing an unreleased flip on the dancefloor']::text[] WHERE id='ba18e0b3-3747-49f9-82ac-6951b35bb331';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Event / performance role', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Talking over the music so much it disrupts the DJ''s mix and the crowd','Confusing the host or MC with the DJ who selects and mixes the tracks','Poor mic technique or levels causing feedback or an unclear voice']::text[], definition='In DJ, club, and party settings, the host (or MC) is the person who addresses the crowd on the microphone — introducing the DJ or acts, making announcements, and hyping and guiding the room — working alongside the DJ rather than selecting the music.', plain_english='The host is the person on the mic at a party. They talk to the crowd and introduce the DJ. They hype the room but do not pick the music.', practical_application='At a club night the host greets the crowd, introduces each DJ, shouts out moments over the music, and relays announcements between sets.', purpose_function='Keeps a crowd engaged and informed, bridges moments between acts, and adds energy and personality to a set or event.', related_terms=ARRAY['MC','Hype man','DJ','Warm-up','Announcements']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Hosting a club night and introducing each DJ','MCing announcements between sets at an event','Hyping the crowd on the mic during a warm-up']::text[] WHERE id='05bf9c80-bcbe-4754-b6a5-5e55a7c10362';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Tracklist / labeling convention', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Thinking ''ID'' is an actual song or artist name rather than a placeholder','Expecting every ID to be released or identifiable','Confusing an unreleased ID with a bootleg or edit']::text[], definition='In DJ culture, ''ID'' is the placeholder label used for an unidentified, unreleased, or as-yet-unnamed track — typically an exclusive dubplate or promo — that appears in tracklists and listener requests as ''ID'' until the record is identified or released.', plain_english='ID means a track no one has named yet. It is often unreleased or a secret exclusive. Tracklists write ''ID'' until the song is known.', practical_application='When posting a set''s tracklist, a DJ marks an unreleased exclusive as ''ID - ID'', and listeners ask for the ID hoping to identify it.', purpose_function='Lets DJs share and log sets containing unreleased or secret music without stating a title that does not yet exist publicly.', related_terms=ARRAY['Dubplate','Promo','Tracklist','White label','Exclusive']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Listing an unreleased exclusive as ''ID'' in a festival set tracklist','Fans asking ''track ID?'' after a set','A producer testing an unreleased ID on the dancefloor']::text[] WHERE id='023a53ea-afb3-4b5b-bdff-37b3468d6b1e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='DJ effect (beat FX)', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Setting the beat division wrong so the roll falls out of time','Holding the roll too long until it becomes tiring or loses impact','Confusing a roll (repeats a captured slice) with a standard loop of the track''s playback']::text[], definition='A beat-synced DJ effect that captures a short slice of the playing audio and repeats it as a rapid loop at a chosen beat division (such as 1/4, 1/8, or 1/16), producing a stuttering ''roll'' without moving the track''s underlying playback position; it appears as the ROLL Beat FX on Pioneer DJM mixers and as a loop roll in Serato and rekordbox.', plain_english='A roll effect grabs a tiny piece of the track. It repeats that slice fast, in time with the beat. This makes a stutter that builds tension.', practical_application='Approaching a drop, the DJ engages a 1/8 then 1/16 roll to ramp up energy, then releases it as the next track''s drop lands.', purpose_function='Adds tension and a build-up or fill during transitions and breakdowns without stopping the music.', related_terms=ARRAY['Beat FX','Loop roll','Beat division','Breakdown','Transition']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Using a 1/16 roll to build into a drop','Adding a roll fill over the last bar before a transition','Stuttering a vocal phrase with a roll effect for emphasis']::text[] WHERE id='6a203e36-d896-4ace-a7a5-a470d956d9b8';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Broadcast production / station imaging', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the imaging voice with the on-air DJ or presenter','Using inconsistent voices so the station brand feels disjointed','Over-processing the voice until it sounds unnatural against the music bed']::text[], definition='In radio production, the imaging voice is the voiceover talent — and by extension the recognizable voice — used across a station''s imaging elements such as sweepers, station IDs, promos, and liners, giving the station a consistent sonic brand and personality between songs and segments.', plain_english='The imaging voice is the voice on a station''s short branding clips. It says the station name and slogans between songs. It gives the station one clear identity.', practical_application='A producer records the imaging voice reading sweepers and IDs, then processes and layers them with music beds in production software for on-air use.', purpose_function='Reinforces the station''s brand and format identity by keeping a consistent, recognizable voice across all identification and promotional elements.', related_terms=ARRAY['Sweeper','Station ID','Liner','Voiceover talent','Production bed','Air chain']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a new imaging voice package for a format refresh','Producing sweepers with the imaging voice over a music bed','Swapping the imaging voice to reposition a station''s brand']::text[] WHERE id='a44a2347-5c72-454c-b48f-6e4e0ed62125';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring ensemble', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Writing dense, full-orchestra textures that overwhelm a small ensemble','Expecting a chamber group to deliver the power of a full orchestra','Under-booking players for the parts written, leaving lines uncovered']::text[], definition='In film scoring, a chamber scoring ensemble is a small instrumental group — a reduced number of players, often a handful of strings, winds, or mixed instruments — used to record a cue, offering an intimate, transparent, and cost-effective alternative to a full orchestra.', plain_english='A chamber scoring ensemble is a small group of players. It records music for a film instead of a big orchestra. It gives an intimate, close sound and costs less.', practical_application='A composer scores a tender scene for a chamber ensemble of a few strings and winds, records them in a scoring session, and may layer the result with samples.', purpose_function='Provides an intimate, detailed sonic character and lower cost and logistics than a full orchestra, suiting smaller films, delicate scenes, or hybrid scores.', related_terms=ARRAY['Orchestral score','Scoring session','Cue','String quartet','Hybrid score','Orchestration']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording an intimate indie-film score with a small string group','Using a chamber ensemble for a delicate emotional scene','Choosing a chamber ensemble to fit a limited music budget']::text[] WHERE id='abddb9f5-0a68-4377-a1bd-c1fb21e0b339';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring documentation', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the conductor''s full score with an individual player''s part','Leaving out the timing marks and sync points needed to conduct to picture','Poor page layout or turns that trip up the conductor in session']::text[], definition='In film scoring, the conductor score is the full notated master score — showing all instrumental parts aligned in the ensemble''s standard order — that the conductor reads during a scoring session, often annotated with timings, streamers, and sync points to picture.', plain_english='The conductor score is the full written score for a cue. It shows every instrument''s part on the page. The conductor reads it while recording to picture.', practical_application='The conductor works from the conductor score marked with timecodes and streamers, cueing entrances and controlling tempo to hit the film''s key moments.', purpose_function='Gives the conductor a complete view of every part and its timing so the ensemble can be led accurately in sync with the film.', related_terms=ARRAY['Full score','Instrumental part','Streamers and punches','Click track','Cue','Spotting']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Conducting an orchestra from the marked conductor score at a scoring stage','Adding timecodes and streamers to the conductor score before a session','Checking the conductor score against the parts for errors']::text[] WHERE id='d3d840f8-ca9a-4a89-999d-3154d03b9143';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring timing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a cue''s duration with its start or end timecode','Writing music longer or shorter than the scene requires','Forgetting to account for a reverb tail when logging duration']::text[], definition='In film scoring, cue duration is the total length of a musical cue — the elapsed time from its start to its end — set by the timing of the picture it accompanies and logged on the cue sheet and session paperwork.', plain_english='Cue duration is how long a piece of film music lasts. It is measured from where the cue starts to where it ends. The length of the scene sets it.', practical_application='During spotting, the composer notes each cue''s start and end, calculates its duration, and writes music to fill exactly that length to picture.', purpose_function='Defines how much music must be written and recorded for a scene, and feeds timing, budgeting, and music-cue-sheet paperwork.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cue','Spotting','Cue sheet','Timecode','Click track','Hit point']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Logging each cue''s duration during a spotting session','Reporting total scored minutes from summed cue durations','Timing a cue so its duration matches the edited scene']::text[] WHERE id='577353b3-3fd6-4cc2-9954-5eb5fbd2b675';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring timing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the end timecode with the cue''s total duration','Ending a cue a beat late so it steps on the next scene or dialogue','Not accounting for a reverb tail past the notated end']::text[], definition='In film scoring, the cue end time is the timecode position in the picture at which a musical cue finishes; together with the start time it fixes the cue''s placement and length within the film.', plain_english='Cue end time is the exact spot where a piece of film music stops. It is written as a timecode. With the start time, it fixes where the cue sits.', practical_application='In the spotting notes, the composer records each cue''s end timecode so the music is written and recorded to conclude exactly at that frame.', purpose_function='Pins the precise moment music must end relative to picture, ensuring the cue resolves on the intended edit, action, or line of dialogue.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cue start time','Cue duration','Timecode','Spotting','Hit point','Cue sheet']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Marking each cue''s end timecode during spotting','Aligning a cue''s end time to a scene cut','Checking the cue end time against the picture before recording']::text[] WHERE id='21bba6a6-6829-4cf9-af88-8371adc0e4ba';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a cue revision with a brand-new cue and assigning a new cue number','Failing to update version tracking so the wrong revision is recorded','Not conforming the revised cue to the current picture edit']::text[], definition='A revised version of a specific film music cue, created after feedback from the director, composer, or music editor, in which the music, timing, or orchestration is changed while the cue keeps its identity within the score.', plain_english='A cue is one piece of music for a scene. A cue revision is a changed version of it. The composer edits it after notes. The music or timing may change. It is still the same cue.', practical_application='When a director asks for a lighter feel, the composer produces a revision of that cue, logs it as a new version, and the music editor conforms it to the latest cut.', purpose_function='Lets the score respond to editorial and creative feedback without renaming or renumbering the cue, keeping the music aligned with the current picture.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cue version','Cue take','Music editor','Spotting session','Picture lock']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Director requests changes after a screening','Picture edit changes and the cue must be reworked','Composer refines orchestration before the scoring session']::text[] WHERE id='18bb23f7-b7b2-42f3-98df-81ff6a18f6e1';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Slating the wrong cue or take number','Not matching the spoken slate to the written log','Recording without slating, making takes hard to identify later']::text[], definition='The verbal and written identification recorded at the head of each take during a scoring session, naming the project, cue number and title, and take number, so recorded takes are properly labeled.', plain_english='Before a take is recorded, someone announces its details. That is the cue slate. It gives the cue number, title, and take number. This lets the team find each take later. It labels the recording clearly.', practical_application='The scoring mixer or music editor slates ''2M3, take 2'' before the orchestra plays, and the same information is written on the session log and the take name.', purpose_function='Ensures every recorded take is uniquely identified for editing, selection, and archiving, preventing mix-ups among many similar takes.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cue take','Cue title','Scoring-session log','Music editor','Take']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Start of each orchestra take on a scoring stage','Logging takes for later selection','Archiving session recordings']::text[] WHERE id='ba062fe1-6f61-49c0-94eb-73e015f0485b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring sync', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing cue start time with the reel or film start time','Using the wrong frame rate so the start time drifts','Not updating start time after a picture recut']::text[], definition='The exact timecode position in the picture where a film music cue begins, defining the sync point at which the cue''s first note or downbeat lands relative to the edited picture.', plain_english='Each cue starts at a set moment in the film. The cue start time is that moment. It is given as a timecode. The music must begin exactly there. This keeps music in sync with picture.', practical_application='The music editor notes a cue''s start time, sets it in the session, and builds the click and streamers from that reference so the conductor hits it precisely.', purpose_function='Anchors the cue to picture so the music lines up with the scene, and forms the basis for click tracks, streamers, and placement in the session.', related_terms=ARRAY['Timecode','Cue title','Streamer preparation','Click track','Spotting session']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Spotting a film and logging where each cue begins','Setting up a click track to a cue','Conforming cues after a new picture cut']::text[] WHERE id='81345765-dfc2-45ba-85b1-e6bc611ae35e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Not slating each take, causing confusion in selection','Assuming the last take is always the best','Failing to log preferred takes during the session']::text[], definition='A single recorded performance of a film music cue captured during a scoring session; multiple takes of the same cue are recorded so the best performance can be selected or edited together.', plain_english='A cue take is one recording of a cue. The orchestra may play a cue several times. Each try is a take. Later the team picks the best one. Takes can also be edited together.', practical_application='The composer records three takes of a cue, the music editor notes which is preferred, and the final master is comped from the best sections of two takes.', purpose_function='Provides options for the best performance and allows editing between takes to assemble a final version of the cue.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cue slate','Cue revision','Comping','Music editor','Take']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording an orchestra on a scoring stage','Selecting the best performance after a session','Editing several takes into one master']::text[] WHERE id='2a8df20b-ea7c-45b4-ad6f-ee7882cc6392';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring organization', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using inconsistent titles across score, parts, and logs','Relying only on titles without cue numbers','Changing a cue title without updating all documents']::text[], definition='The descriptive name assigned to a specific film music cue, usually alongside its cue number, identifying that piece of music within the film''s score.', plain_english='Every cue has a name. That name is the cue title. It sits next to the cue number. It helps everyone talk about the right music. It appears on scores and logs.', practical_application='A cue numbered 3M2 might be titled ''Escape from the Tower''; the copyist prints the title on parts and the music editor uses it in the cue sheet.', purpose_function='Gives each cue a memorable, human-readable label to complement the numeric cue reference, aiding communication and organization.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cue number','Cue slate','Cue sheet','Music preparation','Score recording schedule']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Naming cues during spotting','Printing parts for the orchestra','Preparing a cue sheet for the studio']::text[] WHERE id='d1eaeab4-3c3a-4d7f-bfb3-2b0fa3cf073f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Overwriting an approved version without archiving the earlier one','Ambiguous version labels that do not indicate which is current','Delivering an outdated version to the dub stage']::text[], definition='One of several iterations of the same film music cue, tracked so that different states of the cue - such as original, revised, or alternate - can be distinguished throughout production.', plain_english='A cue can exist in more than one form. Each form is a cue version. Versions are numbered or dated. This shows which one is current. It stops old versions being used by mistake.', practical_application='The music editor labels files ''2M1 v3'' and keeps a log noting v3 as approved, so the mixing stage receives the right version.', purpose_function='Enables version control of cues as they change through revisions and picture edits, so the team always uses the correct current material.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cue revision','Cue title','Scoring-session log','Music editor','Picture lock']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Tracking cue changes through revisions','Delivering approved cues to the mix','Managing alternates for a scene']::text[] WHERE id='525ec2c4-77ab-4fe7-a8ed-329488827747';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring production', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Not clocking or syncing the live recording to the electronic track','Failing to keep live and programmed elements on separate stems','Ignoring latency between playback and the live players']::text[], definition='A scoring session that combines recorded live musicians with pre-produced electronic or sampled elements, blending an acoustic performance with a programmed track to create the final score.', plain_english='Some scores mix live players with computer sounds. A hybrid session records the live parts. These play along with the electronic tracks. The two blend into one score. This is common in modern films.', practical_application='A composer plays back a programmed synth and drum bed on the stage while a string section records over it, and the recordist keeps live and electronic elements on separate stems.', purpose_function='Allows composers to combine the realism of live performance with the control and texture of electronic production, common in contemporary film scoring.', related_terms=ARRAY['Stem','Click track','Music preparation','Orchestration preparation','Score recording schedule']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording strings over a synth mockup','Blending sampled percussion with live drums','Delivering separate acoustic and electronic stems']::text[] WHERE id='64ff6878-f180-4d7e-8f6b-5bcc9bc129ad';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring pre-production', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Leaving awkward page turns that disrupt players','Errors in transposition for transposing instruments','Delivering parts that do not match the current cue version']::text[], definition='The process of producing accurate, legible, performance-ready conductor scores and instrumental parts from a composer''s or orchestrator''s manuscript, including copying, transposition, formatting, and page layout for a recording session.', plain_english='Music prep turns a composer''s score into clean parts. Each player needs a readable part. The prep team copies and formats the music. They fix page turns and errors. This gets the music ready to record.', practical_application='A music preparation team uses notation software to extract parts from the orchestrated score, checks transpositions and page turns, and delivers printed parts to each stand before the session.', purpose_function='Ensures musicians have clear, error-free parts so the scoring session runs efficiently and costly studio time is not wasted on mistakes.', related_terms=ARRAY['Part preparation','Orchestration preparation','Score recording schedule','Copyist','Session part']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Extracting parts before a scoring session','Correcting a score after orchestration','Formatting parts for fast reading on the stand']::text[] WHERE id='79dccd08-f968-48b5-998d-5f131d9377a3';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring pre-production', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Writing outside an instrument''s practical range','Ignoring balance so lines are masked','Not matching the orchestration to the approved mockup']::text[], definition='The stage of film-score production in which a composer''s sketch or mockup is developed into a full orchestral score by assigning material to specific instruments and setting voicings, ranges, doublings, and dynamics ready for copying.', plain_english='A composer often writes a sketch. Orchestration prep turns it into a full score. The orchestrator picks which instruments play each line. They set ranges and dynamics. Then the parts can be copied.', practical_application='An orchestrator takes the composer''s mockup and sketch, voices the chords across strings and winds, checks instrument ranges, and hands the full score to the copyist for part preparation.', purpose_function='Translates the composer''s intent into a playable, well-balanced orchestral score, ensuring idiomatic writing for each instrument before parts are prepared.', related_terms=ARRAY['Music preparation','Part preparation','Orchestrator','Mockup','Score recording schedule']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Turning a MIDI mockup into a live score','Voicing a theme for full orchestra','Preparing the score before copying parts']::text[] WHERE id='fa9ca6bd-8523-4ee3-8358-349974efa963';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring pre-production', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Bad page turns that force players to stop','Missing rehearsal marks or bar numbers','Failing to update parts after a cue revision']::text[], definition='The task of extracting and formatting individual instrumental parts from the full orchestral score so each player receives a clear, correctly transposed, well-laid-out part for the recording session.', plain_english='Each musician reads their own part. Part prep makes those parts. The team pulls each instrument''s music from the score. They set clear page turns and rests. Every player gets a clean part.', practical_application='The copyist extracts the first violin part from the score, adds cue notes and sensible page turns, checks multirests, and prints it for the session.', purpose_function='Gives each performer exactly the music they need in readable form, supporting an efficient, accurate scoring session.', related_terms=ARRAY['Music preparation','Session part','Orchestration preparation','Copyist','Cue title']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Extracting parts from a finished score','Adding cue notes for long rests','Reprinting parts after a revision']::text[] WHERE id='656c4d25-e82f-4b98-8e59-260c518378e4';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring sync', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Placing punches on the wrong frame so hits miss','Relying on punches without a streamer lead-in','Not updating punches after a picture change']::text[], definition='The process of placing punches - brief visual flashes on the picture at precise frames - to give a conductor exact sync points, such as a cue''s downbeat, when recording to picture.', plain_english='A punch is a quick flash on the screen. It marks an exact moment for the conductor. Punch prep sets these flashes at the right frames. The conductor sees the flash and hits the beat. This keeps music in sync.', practical_application='The music editor places a punch at the cue''s downbeat frame and further punches at key hits, so the conductor sees each flash and lands the orchestra exactly on the action.', purpose_function='Provides precise visual timing cues so a conductor can align the orchestra with picture without relying only on a click track.', related_terms=ARRAY['Streamer preparation','Click track','Cue start time','Music editor','Timecode']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Marking a downbeat for a free-timed cue','Cueing a conductor to a specific hit','Recording to picture without a click']::text[] WHERE id='2fd86913-d8e9-4960-92a3-8d981efcaebb';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring production management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Scheduling more cues than fit the booked hours','Not grouping cues by instrumentation, wasting player time','Ignoring union break and overtime rules']::text[], definition='The planned timetable for a film''s scoring sessions, listing which cues are recorded in what order, on which dates and stages, and with which musicians, to make efficient use of booked studio time.', plain_english='Recording a score takes careful planning. The schedule lists which cues record when. It sets the order and the dates. It books the players and rooms. This uses costly studio time well.', practical_application='The music contractor and producer build a schedule grouping cues by instrumentation, so string players are called only for their cues and the brass are released early.', purpose_function='Coordinates cues, musicians, and stage time so the score is recorded on budget and on deadline.', related_terms=ARRAY['Scoring session call','Musician scheduling','Music preparation','Cue title','Session budget']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Planning a two-day orchestral recording','Grouping cues to release players early','Fitting recording into a fixed budget']::text[] WHERE id='ef93a4be-755c-4595-84c3-3c892c5897e4';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring production management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Giving the wrong call time or location','Not specifying instrumentation, so wrong players attend','Confusing arrival time with downbeat time']::text[], definition='The formal notice given to musicians for a scoring session, specifying the call time, location, instrumentation, and expected duration so players know when and where to report.', plain_english='Players must be told when to arrive. The session call gives that notice. It states the time and place. It lists the instruments needed. Musicians report as the call says.', practical_application='The contractor issues a call for a 10 a.m. downbeat with a 60-piece orchestra, and the players arrive early to warm up before the first cue.', purpose_function='Ensures the right musicians arrive at the right time and place, supporting an organized start to the session and correct payment.', related_terms=ARRAY['Score recording schedule','Musician scheduling','Scoring-session log','Musician contractor','Session budget']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Booking an orchestra for a scoring date','Notifying players of call time and place','Coordinating arrival before downbeat']::text[] WHERE id='1dd07aaf-8898-4fe0-a7d4-466b85e42aec';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring documentation', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Notes that do not match the slated take numbers','Failing to mark preferred takes during the session','Illegible or incomplete logs that slow editing']::text[], definition='The running record kept during a scoring session that documents each cue, take, and comment, noting preferred takes, timing, and technical issues for later editing and selection.', plain_english='During a session someone keeps notes. That is the session log. It lists each cue and take. It marks the good takes and problems. Editors use it later to pick takes.', practical_application='The music editor logs ''2M3, take 2 - preferred, slight rush in bar 12,'' so the editing team can quickly assemble the best master.', purpose_function='Provides an accurate reference of what was recorded and which takes were preferred, guiding editing and avoiding lost or confused material.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cue slate','Cue take','Music editor','Scoring session call','Cue version']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Tracking takes during an orchestra session','Marking preferred performances for editing','Noting technical issues for the mix']::text[] WHERE id='d2dd4a9d-a040-4f85-a000-1904f3935cbe';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring materials', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Placing the wrong part on a stand','Parts missing rehearsal marks or bar numbers','Using an outdated part after a revision']::text[], definition='The individual, performance-ready instrumental part a musician reads at a scoring session, produced through part preparation and containing that player''s music with cues, rehearsal marks, and page turns.', plain_english='A session part is the sheet a player reads. It holds only their instrument''s music. It has bar numbers and page turns set. The player uses it at the session. It comes from the prep team.', practical_application='Before downbeat, the librarian places each session part on the correct stand, and players mark bowings and notes directly on their parts.', purpose_function='Delivers each performer the exact music they need in a clear format so the session runs smoothly.', related_terms=ARRAY['Part preparation','Music preparation','Cue title','Copyist','Score']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting parts on stands before a session','A player marking bowings on their part','Distributing corrected parts after a change']::text[] WHERE id='a5cb9266-a2b3-4d17-9db3-a9bd48bf433a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Film scoring sync', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Streamer that ends on the wrong frame','Too short a streamer to give useful warning','Not pairing the streamer with a punch at the hit']::text[], definition='The process of creating streamers - lines that travel across the picture over a set number of seconds and end at a specific frame - to lead a conductor smoothly into a sync point when recording to picture.', plain_english='A streamer is a line that moves across the screen. It takes a few seconds to cross. It ends at an exact frame. It warns the conductor a hit is coming. Streamer prep sets these up.', practical_application='The music editor sets a streamer that ends in a punch at the cue''s downbeat, giving the conductor a countdown to lead the orchestra in.', purpose_function='Gives the conductor advance visual warning of an upcoming sync point so the orchestra arrives together on the beat.', related_terms=ARRAY['Punch preparation','Click track','Cue start time','Music editor','Timecode']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Leading a conductor into a downbeat','Cueing a free-timed cue to picture','Recording hits without a click track']::text[] WHERE id='cd2d98fc-73f6-4486-b1cb-a3b0c6c21f93';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Interactive audio design', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming audio behavior is fixed like a linear track','Creating behaviors so repetitive they sound looped','Not testing behaviors against real gameplay states']::text[], definition='In interactive game audio, the runtime logic and rules that determine how a sound or music asset responds to game state, events, and parameters, including when it plays, how it varies, and how it changes with player actions.', plain_english='In games, sounds react to what happens. Audio behavior is the set of rules for that. It decides when a sound plays. It decides how the sound changes. Player actions can trigger it.', practical_application='A sound designer sets an audio behavior in the middleware so footstep sounds change with surface type and randomize pitch, avoiding an obviously looping effect.', purpose_function='Defines how audio adapts to gameplay so the soundtrack feels responsive and non-repetitive, a core goal of interactive audio design.', related_terms=ARRAY['Middleware','Game parameter','Adaptive music','Sound event','Randomization']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Making footsteps vary with surface','Changing music with player health','Randomizing weapon sounds to avoid repetition']::text[] WHERE id='a07b4512-392f-42a8-9163-fa4df2bbe34f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Stereo microphone technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing RAI with coincident XY, which uses no capsule spacing','Using the wrong angle or spacing and losing the intended image','Assuming it is a close-miking method rather than a stereo array']::text[], definition='A near-coincident stereo microphone array developed by the Italian broadcaster RAI, using two cardioid microphones angled at 100 degrees with their capsules spaced about 21 cm apart, capturing both level and time differences between channels.', plain_english='RAI is a way to record in stereo. It is named after the Italian broadcaster RAI, which created it. It uses two cardioid (front-facing, directional) mics. They point apart at a wide angle. Their capsules sit about 21 cm apart. This captures a natural stereo image.', practical_application='An engineer sets up a RAI pair in front of an ensemble, angling the two cardioids at 100 degrees with the capsules spaced 21 cm apart to capture a wide, natural stereo image.', purpose_function='Provides a stereo image with a balance of time and level cues, one of several broadcaster-defined near-coincident arrays used for realistic stereo capture.', related_terms=ARRAY['Near-coincident pair','ORTF','NOS','Cardioid microphone','Stereo recording angle']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording an ensemble in stereo','Capturing room and instrument together','Choosing among near-coincident arrays']::text[] WHERE id='196aeb7d-c499-43f2-8c64-c8e85b56efed';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production role', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Treating associate producer as equal to co-producer','Granting the credit without a written fee or royalty agreement','Assuming the title carries the same rights everywhere']::text[], definition='A supporting production credit in a music project given to someone who assists the main producer, typically handling specific creative or logistical tasks without holding full production responsibility for the record.', plain_english='An associate producer helps the main producer. They handle set tasks, not the whole record. The role can be creative or organizing. The credit shows their contribution. It ranks below the lead producer.', practical_application='A collaborator who arranges several tracks and coordinates sessions is credited as associate producer and paid a smaller agreed share than the lead producer.', purpose_function='Recognizes a meaningful but secondary contribution to a recording, clarifying credit and, where agreed, a share of production fees or royalties.', related_terms=ARRAY['Co-producer','Record production','Production assistant','Creative producer','Producer royalty']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Crediting a helper on an album','Negotiating a smaller production share','Recognizing coordination work on a project']::text[] WHERE id='87e2e2da-f0fe-4bc5-a426-61329e33fe64';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production role', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Not defining how the producer royalty is split','Confusing co-producer with associate producer','Assuming equal credit means equal legal rights without a contract']::text[], definition='A production credit shared by two or more people who jointly take on the producer''s role for a recording, dividing creative direction and, typically, the production fee or royalty between them.', plain_english='Sometimes two people produce a record together. Each is a co-producer. They share the creative decisions. They also share the pay. Their credit is roughly equal.', practical_application='A songwriter and a studio producer share creative control over a single and are credited as co-producers, splitting the producer royalty as agreed in their contract.', purpose_function='Reflects genuinely shared production responsibility, allocating credit and compensation among the collaborators.', related_terms=ARRAY['Associate producer','Record production','Creative producer','Producer royalty','Production approval']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Two producers sharing a track','Splitting a producer royalty','Crediting joint creative direction']::text[] WHERE id='69143402-723d-4fa7-8bec-bda2caee16dd';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Business and rights', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Not defining who holds creative approval in the contract','Confusing creative approval with final commercial approval','Assuming approval rights without them being written down']::text[], definition='The contractual right of an artist, label, or other stakeholder to review and approve creative decisions on a project - such as mixes, artwork, or musical direction - before the work is finalized or released.', plain_english='Someone must sign off on creative choices. That right is creative approval. It covers things like mixes and artwork. The holder can accept or reject them. It is set in the contract.', practical_application='An artist''s deal gives them creative approval over the final mix, so the label cannot release a version the artist has not signed off on.', purpose_function='Ensures the party holding it controls the artistic outcome, protecting brand, vision, or contractual standards before release.', related_terms=ARRAY['Label approval','Mix approval process','Production approval','Artistic control','Creative producer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Signing off on a final mix','Approving album artwork','Resolving artistic disputes in a deal']::text[] WHERE id='0da2adcc-c5d6-4e4c-9880-ad0531282d95';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production role', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the creative producer with the recording engineer','Assuming they handle budgets and logistics too','Overlooking that the role still needs a clear credit and deal']::text[], definition='A producer whose role centers on the creative and artistic direction of a recording - shaping arrangements, performances, and overall sound - as distinct from primarily technical or budgetary responsibilities.', plain_english='A creative producer shapes the art of a record. They guide the arrangement and sound. They focus on the vision, not the wiring. They push for the best performance. Their job is creative direction.', practical_application='A creative producer reworks a song''s arrangement and coaches the vocalist toward a stronger performance, leaving the signal routing to the engineer.', purpose_function='Provides artistic leadership that defines the identity and quality of the recording, guiding the artist and team toward a shared vision.', related_terms=ARRAY['Co-producer','Associate producer','Record production','Creative approval','Record producer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Shaping a song''s arrangement','Coaching an artist''s performance','Setting the artistic vision for an album']::text[] WHERE id='64befb1e-ebea-4609-a66d-dc7e65ba7d91';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production process', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Overspending on a demo meant to be rough','Confusing a demo with a finished master','Pitching a demo with poor sound that hides the song']::text[], definition='The process of creating a demo - a preliminary, lower-budget recording made to demonstrate a song or artist - used to pitch material to labels, publishers, or collaborators rather than for commercial release.', plain_english='A demo shows what a song could be. Demo production is making that recording. It is quick and low cost. It is not the final release. It helps sell the song or artist.', practical_application='A songwriter records a rough demo at home to pitch a song to a publisher, who decides whether to fund a full master recording.', purpose_function='Lets songwriters and artists present ideas cheaply to decision-makers, testing material before committing to a full production budget.', related_terms=ARRAY['Pre-production session','Record production','Session budget','Songwriter','Production timeline']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Pitching a song to a publisher','Testing an arrangement cheaply','Deciding whether to fund a master']::text[] WHERE id='cd6fa32e-e4bf-4635-9bd6-c824fa38c34e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Booking instruments without confirming player availability','Forgetting setup and teardown time between instruments','Not arranging rentals early enough']::text[], definition='Planning when specific instruments are recorded and ensuring the required instruments, backline, and rentals are available at the right sessions within a production''s timeline.', plain_english='A project needs the right instruments ready. Instrument scheduling plans this. It sets when each instrument is recorded. It makes sure gear and rentals arrive. This keeps sessions on track.', practical_application='A producer schedules drums for day one and horns for day two, and arranges a rented vintage keyboard to arrive the morning it is needed.', purpose_function='Coordinates instrument availability and recording order so sessions run efficiently and no time is lost waiting on gear or setup.', related_terms=ARRAY['Musician scheduling','Production timeline','Session budget','Pre-production session','Backline']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Planning tracking order for a session','Arranging backline rentals','Sequencing instruments across studio days']::text[] WHERE id='3ee7aba0-5002-4495-89a9-1ef1a42aadec';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Business and rights', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Manufacturing before label approval is granted','Assuming artist sign-off replaces label approval','Not building approval time into the schedule']::text[], definition='The record label''s contractual right to review and approve elements of a project - such as final masters, budgets, artwork, or release plans - before the label commits to manufacturing, marketing, or release.', plain_english='A label often must approve the work. That right is label approval. It covers masters, budgets, and artwork. The label can accept or ask for changes. It happens before release.', practical_application='A producer submits the final masters for label approval, and the release date is confirmed only after the label signs off.', purpose_function='Protects the label''s investment and standards by giving it sign-off over key deliverables and spending before release.', related_terms=ARRAY['Creative approval','Mix approval process','Production approval','Master','Production milestone']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Submitting final masters to a label','Getting budget sign-off','Confirming a release date after approval']::text[] WHERE id='99bf8180-3e2c-4159-975e-dc4a54752afd';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Moving to mastering before written mix sign-off','Collecting vague feedback that causes endless revisions','Not tracking which mix version is approved']::text[], definition='The structured sequence by which a mix is reviewed, revised, and formally signed off by the artist, producer, or label, moving from rough mixes through revisions to an approved final mix ready for mastering.', plain_english='A mix must be checked and approved. The mix approval process guides this. The team reviews the mix. They ask for changes if needed. A final version is then signed off.', practical_application='The mixer sends a rough mix, collects notes, delivers a revised mix, and only after written sign-off does the track move to mastering.', purpose_function='Ensures the mix meets everyone''s expectations before mastering, capturing feedback in an orderly way and preventing costly late changes.', related_terms=ARRAY['Creative approval','Label approval','Production revision','Mastering','Production milestone']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Sending mixes for client notes','Revising a mix from feedback','Signing off before mastering']::text[] WHERE id='0ff5c791-8cbf-4845-9c35-5008e7ef0d89';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Double-booking a player across sessions','Not confirming availability before booking studio time','Forgetting to send clear call times']::text[], definition='The planning and booking of musicians for recording sessions, coordinating their availability, call times, and roles so the right players are present when their parts are recorded.', plain_english='Sessions need the right players present. Musician scheduling books them. It matches their free dates to the sessions. It sets call times. This keeps recording on schedule.', practical_application='A producer books a session drummer for Tuesday and a string trio for Thursday, sending each a call time so tracking flows without gaps.', purpose_function='Ensures needed performers are available at the right times, avoiding wasted studio time and keeping the production on track and on budget.', related_terms=ARRAY['Instrument scheduling','Scoring session call','Production timeline','Session budget','Pre-production session']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Booking session players for an album','Coordinating availability across a week','Setting call times for tracking']::text[] WHERE id='d1248174-4775-46cb-8fd2-b2792f5eac4f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production process', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Skipping pre-production and solving problems on expensive studio time','Not locking tempos or keys before tracking','Leaving arrangements undecided until recording']::text[], definition='A working session held before recording begins, in which the artist and producer rehearse, refine arrangements, set tempos and keys, and plan the recording so studio time is used efficiently.', plain_english='Work happens before the main recording. That is a pre-production session. The team rehearses and plans. They fix arrangements, keys, and tempos. This saves costly studio time later.', practical_application='In pre-production, a band rehearses arrangements, locks tempos to a click, and agrees keys, so tracking days move quickly.', purpose_function='Prepares songs and plans before expensive studio time, reducing indecision and revisions during recording.', related_terms=ARRAY['Demo production','Production timeline','Musician scheduling','Record production','Session budget']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Rehearsing before tracking','Setting tempos and keys','Planning the recording approach']::text[] WHERE id='58213f18-254c-4ad6-b3da-b5cc8f9483a3';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Proceeding to the next stage without documented approval','Unclear who holds approval authority','Confusing informal praise with formal sign-off']::text[], definition='Formal sign-off that a stage or element of a music production meets the required standard and may proceed, given by the artist, producer, label, or client at defined points in the project.', plain_english='Each stage of a project needs a green light. Production approval is that sign-off. It says the work is good to go. The right person grants it. Then the project moves on.', practical_application='After tracking is complete, the client gives production approval so the project can move into mixing without ambiguity.', purpose_function='Provides clear decision points that authorize the project to advance, aligning stakeholders and preventing later disputes.', related_terms=ARRAY['Label approval','Creative approval','Mix approval process','Production milestone','Production timeline']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Approving tracking before mixing','Authorizing a stage to proceed','Aligning stakeholders at a checkpoint']::text[] WHERE id='d9578a7b-4ff0-4723-b440-a7d7d81b0418';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production role', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a production assistant with an assistant producer','Expecting creative decision-making from the role','Overlooking their part in keeping accurate logs and receipts']::text[], definition='A person in an entry-level support role in music production, who handles logistical, administrative, and setup tasks - such as coordinating schedules, preparing the studio, and running errands - to keep sessions running smoothly.', plain_english='A production assistant supports the team. They handle setup and errands. They help with schedules and admin. They keep sessions running. It is often an entry-level job.', practical_application='A production assistant sets up the live room, orders lunch, tracks receipts, and keeps the session log so the producer can concentrate on the recording.', purpose_function='Frees the producer and engineer to focus on creative and technical work by handling logistics and support tasks.', related_terms=ARRAY['Associate producer','Record production','Session budget','Musician scheduling','Production timeline']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting up a studio before a session','Coordinating logistics during tracking','Tracking session expenses']::text[] WHERE id='6398ec51-c29c-46de-8b2f-623532fda5f6';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Setting vague milestones that cannot be clearly met','Tying payments to milestones without defining completion','Ignoring milestones once the project runs late']::text[], definition='A defined checkpoint in a music production''s timeline marking the completion of a key stage or deliverable - such as finished tracking, an approved mix, or a delivered master - used to track progress.', plain_english='A project has key points to reach. Each is a production milestone. It marks a stage done, like tracking finished. Teams use it to track progress. It shows the project is on time.', practical_application='A producer sets milestones for pre-production done, tracking complete, and final master delivered, checking each off to keep the project on schedule.', purpose_function='Breaks a project into measurable stages so progress, deadlines, and payments can be tracked against a plan.', related_terms=ARRAY['Production timeline','Production approval','Mix approval process','Session budget','Pre-production session']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Tracking progress on an album','Tying payments to deliverables','Reviewing whether a project is on schedule']::text[] WHERE id='1d786bbb-006c-43e8-8a97-98d986602002';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Not tracking which version reflects the latest revision','Allowing endless revisions without a limit','Confusing a small revision with a full re-production']::text[], definition='A change made to produced material - such as a re-recorded part, altered arrangement, or reworked mix - carried out in response to feedback before the production is finalized.', plain_english='Sometimes produced work must change. That change is a production revision. It follows notes from the team. A part or mix gets reworked. It happens before the final version.', practical_application='After the artist''s notes, the producer re-records a chorus vocal and logs the change as a revision so everyone knows which version is current.', purpose_function='Allows the production to be improved in response to feedback, keeping changes tracked and controlled before finalization.', related_terms=ARRAY['Mix approval process','Production approval','Production milestone','Demo production','Production timeline']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Re-recording a part after notes','Reworking a mix from feedback','Tracking changes before finalizing']::text[] WHERE id='d3fe2f0c-e912-4d90-9f7f-f01494add8a9';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Building a timeline with no slack for revisions','Not updating the timeline when dates slip','Ignoring dependencies between stages']::text[], definition='The overall schedule of a music production from start to delivery, laying out stages, milestones, and dates - such as pre-production, tracking, mixing, and mastering - so the project is completed on time.', plain_english='A project runs on a plan of dates. That plan is the production timeline. It lists each stage and deadline. It runs from start to delivery. It keeps the project on time.', practical_application='A producer maps a timeline with tracking in week one, mixing in week three, and mastering in week five, sharing it so everyone hits their dates.', purpose_function='Gives the whole team a shared plan of stages and deadlines, coordinating work and keeping the project on schedule and budget.', related_terms=ARRAY['Production milestone','Score recording schedule','Musician scheduling','Session budget','Pre-production session']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Planning an album from start to delivery','Scheduling stages and deadlines','Coordinating the team around key dates']::text[] WHERE id='ca534a22-01a2-4e1f-b82d-a02f2d8a8c2c';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production process', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing record production with only the recording step','Assuming production is purely technical','Starting production without clear pre-production planning']::text[], definition='The overall process of overseeing the making of a recording - guiding song selection, arrangement, performance, recording, and mixing - to turn musical ideas into a finished, releasable record.', plain_english='Record production is making a finished record. It guides the whole process. It covers arrangement and recording. It shapes the sound and performance. The result is a release-ready track.', practical_application='A producer leads record production by choosing songs, refining arrangements, running sessions, and steering the mix toward a cohesive album.', purpose_function='Coordinates the creative and technical work needed to realize an artist''s music as a polished recording ready for release.', related_terms=ARRAY['Creative producer','Co-producer','Pre-production session','Production timeline','Session budget']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Producing a single or album','Guiding an artist''s sound','Overseeing sessions from tracking to mix']::text[] WHERE id='7bd5f7ab-f64a-451d-8023-57468174f64a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production management', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Leaving no contingency for overruns','Underestimating studio and overtime costs','Not tracking actual spend against the budget']::text[], definition='The planned allocation of money for a recording session or project, covering costs such as studio time, musicians, engineers, rentals, and materials, used to control spending and keep the production affordable.', plain_english='A session costs money. The session budget plans that spending. It covers studio time and players. It also covers gear and staff. It keeps costs under control.', practical_application='A producer builds a session budget covering two studio days, three musicians, and an engineer, then tracks actual costs against it to avoid overspending.', purpose_function='Controls and forecasts spending so the project stays financially viable and choices are made within available funds.', related_terms=ARRAY['Production timeline','Musician scheduling','Instrument scheduling','Record production','Production milestone']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Planning costs for an album','Booking studio days within funds','Tracking spending during a project']::text[] WHERE id='684ea228-9124-414e-b014-82e049623469';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Studio business / pre-production', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Underestimating setup and soundcheck time','Not sharing charts or keys with players in advance','Booking more expensive tracking time than the budget supports']::text[], definition='The pre-production process of organizing a recording session''s logistics - booking studio time, scheduling musicians and personnel, preparing arrangements and gear, and setting goals and a running order - so that costly studio hours are used efficiently.', plain_english='This means getting a recording session ready before it starts. You book the room and the players. You plan which songs to record and in what order. Good planning saves money. Studio time is costly.', practical_application='A producer builds a session sheet listing songs, keys, tempos, call times, and gear, then shares it with the engineer and players.', purpose_function='Ensures limited, paid studio time is used productively and everyone arrives prepared, cutting wasted hours and cost overruns.', related_terms=ARRAY['Session producer','Studio budget','Pre-production','Session musician','Running order']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Planning a one-day band tracking session','Scheduling session players around studio availability','Listing the songs to record before an expensive booking']::text[] WHERE id='67a93cde-a3f4-4866-9687-53521c2a9cbb';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Studio role / personnel', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the producer''s creative role with the engineer''s technical role','Assuming the producer only picks songs, not performances','Letting sessions run over budget by not tracking time']::text[], definition='The person who oversees a recording session''s creative and practical direction, guiding performances, arrangement, and sound while managing time, personnel, and budget, and taking responsibility for delivering the agreed recordings.', plain_english='This is the person in charge of a recording session. They guide the players and shape the sound. They keep the session on time and on budget. They decide when a take is good enough.', practical_application='The session producer calls takes, gives performance notes, decides comping choices, and watches the clock so the day finishes on budget.', purpose_function='Provides creative leadership and time/budget control so a session yields usable recordings that match the artist''s and label''s goals.', related_terms=ARRAY['Session planning','Record producer','Session musician','Studio budget','Comping']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Directing a vocalist toward the right emotional take','Deciding which sections to re-record','Managing session pace to hit the studio budget']::text[] WHERE id='08e41db9-88c7-4c48-8a49-aa05ce89c7d1';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Songwriting / pre-production', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording a song before the writing is finished','Locking an early draft too soon and ignoring feedback','Confusing arrangement work with the underlying song''s development']::text[], definition='The iterative process of taking a song from an initial idea or demo toward a finished, arranged work by refining melody, lyrics, harmony, structure, and arrangement through drafts, feedback, and revision.', plain_english='This is how a rough song idea grows into a finished song. You improve the tune, words, and chords. You try new versions and get feedback. The song changes until it works.', practical_application='A writer and producer workshop a demo, rewrite a weak second verse, adjust the chorus melody, and test the new version before booking time.', purpose_function='Turns raw ideas into strong, well-crafted songs, improving the material before time and money are spent recording it.', related_terms=ARRAY['Song mapping','Song structure planning','Pre-production','Demo','Arrangement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Reworking a demo before a label pitch','Co-writing revisions in a workshop session','Strengthening a chorus during pre-production']::text[] WHERE id='e0d7d761-eaf0-4ca8-9523-09be4711377d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Arrangement / pre-production', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the section map with a full notated chart','Leaving out bar counts, causing confusion at punch-ins','Changing the map mid-session without telling everyone']::text[], definition='Laying out the sections of a song in order - such as intro, verse, pre-chorus, chorus, bridge, and outro - often with bar counts, key, and tempo, to create a clear roadmap of the arrangement for writing, rehearsal, or recording.', plain_english='This means drawing out a song''s parts in order. You mark the intro, verses, and chorus. You may add bar counts and the key. It gives everyone a simple map of the song.', practical_application='A producer sketches a section map such as intro-verse-chorus-verse-chorus-bridge-chorus with bar numbers so the band and engineer follow the same arrangement.', purpose_function='Gives players, arrangers, and engineers a shared roadmap of the song''s form, making rehearsal and recording faster and clearer.', related_terms=ARRAY['Song structure planning','Song development','Arrangement','Running order','Chart']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Charting a song''s form before a band rehearsal','Marking sections for editing and comping','Communicating an arrangement to session players']::text[] WHERE id='08d6f6c6-ff2d-4062-9ebd-e35d027d273a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Songwriting / arrangement', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using too many sections and losing focus','Placing the chorus too late for a radio edit','Confusing structure with production choices like instrumentation']::text[], definition='Deciding the arrangement of a song''s sections and their order, lengths, and relationships - for example how many verses, where the chorus and bridge fall, and how the song builds - to shape its overall form and pacing.', plain_english='This is choosing how a song is built. You decide how many verses to use. You pick where the chorus and bridge go. You plan how the song grows and holds interest.', practical_application='A writer shortens an intro, adds a pre-chorus, and delays the final chorus''s big lift to improve the song''s flow.', purpose_function='Shapes a song''s pacing and impact so it holds attention and the sections build in a satisfying way.', related_terms=ARRAY['Song mapping','Song development','Arrangement','Bridge','Pre-chorus']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Reworking a song''s form for a single edit','Deciding whether to add a bridge','Planning how sections build toward a climax']::text[] WHERE id='e41aa0c1-12c1-47e7-b2f6-6008b94e1efd';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Music business / project finance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Forgetting mixing, mastering, and contingency costs','Underbooking studio time and paying rush rates later','Not tracking spend against the plan during the project']::text[], definition='The planned allocation of money for a recording project, covering studio hire, engineers and producers, session musicians, gear rental, mixing, and mastering, used to control spending and keep a project financially viable.', plain_english='This is the money plan for a recording project. It covers the room, the staff, and the players. It also covers mixing and mastering. The budget keeps spending under control.', practical_application='A producer estimates tracking days, mixing, and mastering costs, adds a contingency, and tracks spending against the budget as work proceeds.', purpose_function='Keeps a recording project financially controlled so costs like studio time, personnel, and mastering do not exceed available funds.', related_terms=ARRAY['Session planning','Session producer','Recording contract','Mastering','Contingency']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Costing an EP before approaching a label','Allocating funds across tracking, mixing, and mastering','Deciding how many studio days a project can afford']::text[] WHERE id='e05defd9-c784-44b1-9e3c-2789ac6e8edf';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Classical recording aesthetic / perspective', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Over-using spot mics, which pulls the balance too close','Adding artificial reverb that fights the natural hall sound','Confusing it with a dry, multitracked pop balance']::text[], definition='A mixing and miking aesthetic that reproduces an orchestral or choral performance as heard from a good seat in the audience - a blended, hall-influenced balance with natural depth - rather than an up-close, spotlit balance.', plain_english='This is a mix that sounds like sitting in the audience. The instruments blend together. You hear the room around them. It feels natural and a little distant, not right on top of the players.', practical_application='An engineer leans on the main and hall microphones over close spots to keep an orchestral mix sounding like a seat in the hall.', purpose_function='Delivers a natural, immersive result that matches how audiences actually hear a concert, valued in classical recording.', related_terms=ARRAY['Concert-hall perspective','Concert realism','Hall-microphone balance','Main microphone array','Direct-to-ambient ratio']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Mixing a symphony for a natural concert feel','Choosing a main-mic-led balance over close spots','Recreating the sound of a good concert seat']::text[] WHERE id='dd212676-643a-4e2b-9fef-838bdfadacc5';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Ensemble mixing / classical', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Over-miking a small ensemble like a pop multitrack','Letting spot mics dominate and flatten the natural blend','Ignoring the room, which is a key part of the sound']::text[], definition='Balancing and blending a small orchestra, in which individual instruments and sections are more exposed than in a full symphony, typically relying on a main microphone array with limited, carefully placed spot microphones and the room''s natural acoustics.', plain_english='This is mixing a small orchestra. Fewer players means each part is easier to hear. You lean on a main mic pair and the room. You add only a few close mics. Balance and blend matter a lot.', practical_application='An engineer captures a chamber orchestra with a main array plus a few spots on winds, then blends the spots gently under the mains.', purpose_function='Achieves a clear, natural balance for smaller ensembles where every instrument is exposed and heavy spot miking would sound unnatural.', related_terms=ARRAY['Main microphone array','Spot-microphone balance','Section blend','Concert-hall perspective','Decca tree']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a string orchestra in a church acoustic','Balancing exposed woodwinds against strings','Blending light spot mics under a main pair']::text[] WHERE id='d90e28eb-6a8a-4822-996e-5b18ba916f8d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Classical recording aesthetic', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Adding too much artificial reverb or compression','Spotlighting instruments so they sound unnaturally close','Narrowing the image so the ensemble loses its real width']::text[], definition='The aesthetic goal in classical recording and mixing of reproducing a performance so it convincingly resembles a live concert, with natural balance, believable depth and imaging, and the ambience of the performing space rather than an artificially close or hyped sound.', plain_english='This is making a recording sound like a real live concert. The balance feels natural. Instruments sit in believable places. You hear the real room. Nothing sounds fake or too close.', practical_application='An engineer favors natural hall ambience and restrained spot mics so the finished mix feels like a real concert, not a studio construction.', purpose_function='Guides mixing choices toward a believable, natural result that satisfies listeners expecting the sound of a real performance.', related_terms=ARRAY['Concert-hall perspective','Audience perspective','Hall-microphone balance','Instrument localization','Direct-to-ambient ratio']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Mixing a live symphony recording','Judging whether a mix sounds convincingly live','Balancing spots and ambience for a natural result']::text[] WHERE id='ca36bde6-a092-462d-be8e-8c1e46ef2f06';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Classical recording / spatial perspective', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Too much direct sound, making it feel unnaturally close','Too much hall, washing out clarity and detail','Mismatched spot-mic distance breaking the sense of depth']::text[], definition='The sense in a recording of the listener''s position and distance within a concert hall, created by the balance of direct sound, early reflections, and reverberant hall sound, plus the depth and imaging of the ensemble on stage.', plain_english='This is where the recording seems to place the listener in a hall. It comes from mixing direct sound with the room''s echo. It sets how close or far you feel. It also sets the stage in front of you.', practical_application='An engineer sets the balance of main, hall, and spot mics to place the listener a realistic distance back, with the orchestra spread ahead.', purpose_function='Establishes a believable listening viewpoint and depth, key to natural-sounding orchestral and choral recordings.', related_terms=ARRAY['Audience perspective','Direct-to-ambient ratio','Early-reflection balance','Hall-microphone balance','Stage imaging']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting listener distance in an orchestral mix','Balancing hall and main mics for depth','Recreating a mid-hall seat perspective']::text[] WHERE id='5cc9c36b-a126-40f7-9b10-3a07ed359853';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mix automation / dynamics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Riding levels so hard the natural performance dynamics are lost','Automating too fast, making the swell sound unnatural','Fighting a well-played crescendo instead of supporting it']::text[], definition='The use of mix automation, typically fader or gain moves, to support or shape a musical crescendo (a gradual increase in loudness) so that a swelling orchestral or choral passage builds smoothly and stays balanced.', plain_english='A crescendo is music that gets louder. This is using automation to help that swell. You raise faders slowly as the music grows. It keeps the build smooth and balanced.', practical_application='An engineer draws a slow fader rise on the string bus through a building passage so the crescendo swells evenly into the climax.', purpose_function='Helps a recorded crescendo build convincingly and stay balanced, fixing spots where natural dynamics or mic balance fall short.', related_terms=ARRAY['Decrescendo automation','Orchestral dynamics','Section ride','Fader automation','Dynamic range']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Shaping a building orchestral climax','Supporting a choir''s swell into a forte','Smoothing an uneven recorded crescendo']::text[] WHERE id='5199369c-a5d2-4c94-8002-f5790670385a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mix automation / dynamics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Pulling levels down so far quiet detail disappears','Automating too fast for a natural taper','Overriding a well-performed diminuendo']::text[], definition='The use of mix automation, usually fader or gain moves, to support or shape a musical decrescendo or diminuendo (a gradual decrease in loudness) so that a fading passage tapers smoothly and stays balanced across the ensemble.', plain_english='A decrescendo is music that gets quieter. This is using automation to help that fade. You lower faders slowly as the music softens. It keeps the fade smooth and even.', practical_application='An engineer automates a gentle fall on the orchestra bus so a passage settles smoothly from forte to pianissimo without losing inner parts.', purpose_function='Helps a recorded diminuendo taper convincingly and keep its balance, fixing spots where the natural fade is uneven.', related_terms=ARRAY['Crescendo automation','Orchestral dynamics','Section ride','Fader automation','Dynamic range']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Shaping a fade into a quiet passage','Smoothing an uneven recorded diminuendo','Tapering a choir''s release into silence']::text[] WHERE id='e2729a74-44dd-4369-94e2-b79f5b3deae5';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Spatial balance / acoustics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Too much ambient level, blurring clarity','Too little ambience, making it sound dry and close','Confusing added plate reverb with real hall ambience']::text[], definition='The balance between direct sound, captured by close or main microphones, and ambient or reverberant sound, captured by room and hall microphones, which controls the perceived distance, depth, and spaciousness of an acoustic ensemble recording.', plain_english='This is the balance of close sound versus room sound. Close mics give clear, direct sound. Room mics give echo and space. The mix of the two sets how far away it feels.', practical_application='An engineer raises the hall mics against the mains to push an orchestra farther back and more spacious, or lowers them for a closer sound.', purpose_function='Controls perceived distance and spaciousness, letting the engineer place the ensemble nearer or farther and set the natural room feel.', related_terms=ARRAY['Hall-microphone balance','Concert-hall perspective','Early-reflection balance','Reverberation','Instrument localization']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting depth in an orchestral mix','Balancing hall mics against main mics','Dialing spaciousness for a choral recording']::text[] WHERE id='d255d147-4aee-480e-851b-d4ac5884de20';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Acoustics / spatial balance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing early reflections with the reverb tail','Strong early reflections causing a boxy, comb-filtered sound','Ignoring their effect on perceived distance']::text[], definition='The management of early reflections - the first sound reflections arriving shortly after the direct sound from nearby surfaces - relative to direct and reverberant sound, shaping the perceived size, intimacy, and clarity of an acoustic recording.', plain_english='Early reflections are the first echoes off nearby walls. They arrive just after the direct sound. Balancing them shapes how big or close a room feels. Too many can blur clarity.', practical_application='An engineer positions or blends mics and adjusts early-reflection timing in a reverb to keep an ensemble clear yet convincingly placed in a space.', purpose_function='Influences perceived room size, intimacy, and clarity, helping a recording sound natural without becoming muddy or boxy.', related_terms=ARRAY['Direct-to-ambient ratio','Reverberation','Concert-hall perspective','Hall-microphone balance','Reflection']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Reducing boxiness in a small-hall recording','Shaping room size with a reverb''s early reflections','Balancing first reflections against the reverb tail']::text[] WHERE id='dd3eae1c-4632-4e4b-933b-24b1a8e1a931';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Ambience control / classical mixing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Letting bloom swell until detail is lost in loud passages','Cutting ambience so hard the hall''s warmth disappears','Confusing bloom with the late reverb tail']::text[], definition='Managing the bloom of a hall''s reverberation - the way reverberant energy swells and develops after loud or sustained passages - so it enhances warmth and richness without swamping clarity or masking detail in an ensemble mix.', plain_english='Bloom is how a hall''s echo swells after loud notes. It adds warmth and richness. Controlling it keeps that swell pleasing. Too much bloom hides detail and muddies the sound.', practical_application='An engineer eases hall-mic level or gently controls ambience through big tuttis so the reverb bloom stays lush but does not smother the orchestra.', purpose_function='Preserves the rich, enveloping character of a good hall while stopping its swell from burying detail during loud or dense passages.', related_terms=ARRAY['Hall-tail preservation','Hall-microphone balance','Reverberation','Direct-to-ambient ratio','Hall equalization']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Taming reverb swell in a loud orchestral tutti','Keeping a hall''s warmth without muddiness','Balancing bloom against clarity in a choral mix']::text[] WHERE id='d87215fc-8296-4b6a-82a9-d0e2ef75ae6f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Equalization / ambience', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Leaving low-frequency rumble in the hall mics','Over-EQing ambience until it sounds unnatural','Boosting highs and exposing hiss in distant mics']::text[], definition='The tonal shaping of hall or ambient microphone signals - for example rolling off low rumble or taming harsh reflections - so the captured room sound blends naturally with the direct microphones and supports a clean, warm ensemble balance.', plain_english='This is EQ applied to the hall mics. You might cut low rumble or harsh highs. It makes the room sound blend with the close mics. The goal is a clean, natural room tone.', practical_application='An engineer high-pass-filters the hall mics to remove air-handling rumble and dips a harsh frequency so the ambience blends with the mains.', purpose_function='Cleans and shapes captured ambience so the hall sound supports the mix rather than adding rumble, harshness, or mud.', related_terms=ARRAY['Hall-microphone balance','Hall bloom control','Equalization','Direct-to-ambient ratio','High-pass filter']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Cleaning rumble from distant hall mics','Blending ambient mic tone with the mains','Taming a harsh reflection in a bright hall']::text[] WHERE id='a7e50384-9e1d-4cb0-957a-39b1b39a8a92';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Spatial imaging / ambience', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Narrow hall mics that fail to envelop','Hall image wider than or offset from the stage image','Phase problems between spaced hall mics collapsing the width']::text[], definition='The stereo or surround placement and width of the reverberant hall sound in a mix, which sets how the ambience surrounds and envelops the listener and how well it matches the stage image of the ensemble.', plain_english='This is where the hall''s echo sits in the stereo picture. It sets how wide and surrounding the room feels. Good hall imaging wraps around the listener. It should match the stage in front.', practical_application='An engineer pans and widens the hall mics so ambience spreads around the listener while the orchestra stays imaged across the front.', purpose_function='Creates a convincing sense of envelopment and space, making the recorded hall feel three-dimensional around the ensemble.', related_terms=ARRAY['Stage imaging','Hall-microphone balance','Direct-to-ambient ratio','Section imaging','Concert-hall perspective']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Widening ambience in a stereo orchestral mix','Matching hall width to the stage image','Placing surround ambience in a multichannel mix']::text[] WHERE id='14f367d8-72d3-44bc-a1d3-4de5b6300f4e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Ambience balance / classical mixing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Too much hall level, washing out clarity','Too little, leaving the mix dry and lifeless','Ignoring the time delay between hall and main mics']::text[], definition='The relative level and blend of distant hall or ambience microphones against the main and spot microphones, controlling how much of the room''s reverberant character appears in the final ensemble mix.', plain_english='This is how loud the far room mics are in the mix. They capture the hall''s echo and space. Balancing them sets how much room you hear. It works with the close and main mics.', practical_application='An engineer rides the hall-mic level down under loud tuttis and up in quiet passages to keep a consistent, natural sense of space.', purpose_function='Sets the amount of natural room sound in the mix, controlling depth, spaciousness, and the sense of the performing space.', related_terms=ARRAY['Direct-to-ambient ratio','Hall imaging','Hall equalization','Main microphone array','Concert-hall perspective']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Dialing room level in an orchestral mix','Riding ambience across loud and soft passages','Blending hall mics with a Decca tree']::text[] WHERE id='071cff4d-00e3-4bff-b8ab-23c1848f4cb1';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Editing / ambience preservation', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Hard edits that chop off the reverb tail','Gating ambient mics and killing the decay','Fading out too early and truncating the hall']::text[], definition='Retaining the natural reverberant decay, or tail, of a hall through editing, mixing, and processing - for example avoiding abrupt cuts, aggressive gating, or premature fades - so the recording keeps its authentic sense of space.', plain_english='The hall tail is the echo that lingers after a note stops. This means keeping that decay intact. You avoid cutting or gating it off. It keeps the recording sounding like a real space.', practical_application='An engineer crossfades edits over the reverb tail and avoids gating the ambient mics so each phrase''s natural decay carries through.', purpose_function='Maintains a recording''s natural ambience and sense of space, preventing edits or processing from stripping the hall''s decay.', related_terms=ARRAY['Hall bloom control','Reverberation','Hall-microphone balance','Crossfade','Direct-to-ambient ratio']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Editing between takes without cutting the tail','Preserving decay when trimming a movement''s end','Avoiding gates that swallow the hall']::text[] WHERE id='8a5cd3a3-4422-4254-a2ed-c4dfa406e7ce';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Spatial imaging / psychoacoustics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Panning spots away from the real stage positions','Localization from spots clashing with the main-mic image','Over-narrow imaging that clumps instruments together']::text[], definition='The perceived position of an individual instrument within the stereo or surround image - its left-to-right placement and sense of depth - established by microphone technique, panning, and level so listeners can locate each instrument on the stage.', plain_english='This is where each instrument seems to sit in the sound field. You hear it left, right, or center. You also sense how far back it is. Mic setup, panning, and level create this.', practical_application='An engineer pans spot mics to match the players'' real stage positions so violins, cellos, and winds localize where the audience would expect them.', purpose_function='Lets listeners place each instrument on the stage, giving an orchestral or ensemble mix a clear, believable spatial layout.', related_terms=ARRAY['Stage imaging','Section imaging','Spot-microphone balance','Panning','Concert-hall perspective']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Panning spot mics to match stage seating','Placing a soloist clearly in the image','Aligning spot and main-mic positions']::text[] WHERE id='f3dad770-be2c-4576-9979-1e3f7776ed52';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Dynamics / classical recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Over-compressing and flattening the dynamic contrast','Setting gain so quiet passages sink into noise','Clipping loud tuttis by leaving too little headroom']::text[], definition='The range and shaping of loudness in an orchestral performance, from pianissimo to fortissimo including crescendos, accents, and sudden contrasts, and how that dynamic range is captured and preserved through recording and mixing.', plain_english='Dynamics are how loud or soft the music is. An orchestra can play very quiet or very loud. This term covers that range and its changes. Recording it well keeps those contrasts alive.', practical_application='An engineer sets gain to capture fortissimos without clipping while keeping pianissimos above the noise floor, using minimal compression to protect the range.', purpose_function='Preserving wide dynamic contrast is central to orchestral realism and impact, so recordings must capture soft and loud extremes faithfully.', related_terms=ARRAY['Crescendo automation','Decrescendo automation','Dynamic range','Section ride','Headroom']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting levels for a wide-dynamic symphony','Protecting quiet passages above the noise floor','Preserving contrast between soft and loud sections']::text[] WHERE id='c822e65b-fc0d-48c3-84a9-66a76fd607e4';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Microphone technique / stereo imaging', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Spreading outriggers so wide the center hollows out','Phase issues between outriggers and the main tree','Raising outrigger level until the image loses focus']::text[], definition='The level and blend of outrigger, or flanking, microphones - a widely spaced pair placed outside a main array such as a Decca tree - used to broaden the stereo image and capture the ensemble''s outer edges, balanced against the main and spot mics.', plain_english='Outrigger mics are a wide pair set outside the main mics. They capture the far left and right of the orchestra. Balancing them widens the stereo picture. Too much can make it hollow.', practical_application='An engineer blends outrigger mics under a Decca tree to extend the width so first violins and basses reach fully to the edges without a hole in the middle.', purpose_function='Widens and fills out the stereo image of a large ensemble, capturing outer players that a central main array under-represents.', related_terms=ARRAY['Decca tree','Main microphone array','Stage imaging','Section imaging','Spot-microphone balance']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Widening a Decca tree image for a large orchestra','Filling out the sides of a wide string section','Balancing flanking mics against the main array']::text[] WHERE id='2ea3291b-340a-424c-bdf5-6e65d28800d8';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Section mixing / classical', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Letting cymbal transients spike over the orchestra','Panning percussion away from its real stage position','Over-close timpani spots losing the hall blend']::text[], definition='Balancing and placing the orchestral percussion section - timpani, cymbals, snare, mallets, and auxiliary instruments - within an ensemble mix, controlling level, transient impact, and stereo position so it adds power and color without overwhelming the orchestra.', plain_english='This is mixing the orchestra''s percussion. It includes timpani, cymbals, and drums. You set their loudness and place in the stereo image. They should add punch and color, not drown the music.', practical_application='An engineer blends timpani spots under the mains, controls cymbal peaks, and pans percussion to match the back-of-stage seating.', purpose_function='Integrates powerful, transient-rich percussion into the ensemble so it adds impact and color while staying balanced and correctly placed.', related_terms=ARRAY['Section blend','Spot-microphone balance','Orchestral dynamics','Stage imaging','Transient']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Balancing timpani in a loud tutti','Controlling cymbal peaks in a film-score mix','Placing percussion at the back of the stage image']::text[] WHERE id='0d32a2be-8c44-488b-9e21-ac849df8c306';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Ensemble mixing / blend', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Close spots that expose individual players and break blend','Uneven level between desks within a section','Ignoring the room''s role in fusing a section']::text[], definition='The degree to which the players within an orchestral or choral section fuse into a single, unified sound rather than being heard as separate individuals, achieved through microphone choice and placement, level balance, and ambience.', plain_english='A section is a group like all the violins. Blend is how well they sound like one voice. Good blend fuses them together. Mic placement, balance, and room all help this.', practical_application='An engineer positions section mics far enough back and leans on the mains so a string section fuses into one rich sound instead of exposing individual players.', purpose_function='Creates a cohesive, unified section sound, essential to a natural orchestral or choral tone rather than a set of separate voices.', related_terms=ARRAY['Section imaging','Section ride','Spot-microphone balance','Chamber-orchestra mixing','Instrument localization']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Fusing a string section into one voice','Blending choir voices within a part','Balancing section spots under the mains']::text[] WHERE id='8b27e503-88b4-49ef-b8ec-fef7a392a3d3';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Spatial imaging / classical', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Panning a section opposite its real stage position','Section image conflicting with the main-mic layout','Sections overlapping so the image loses clarity']::text[], definition='The stereo or surround placement and width of a whole instrumental or vocal section within the ensemble image, positioning each section to reflect the stage layout and give the mix a clear spatial structure.', plain_english='This is where a whole section sits in the stereo picture. First violins might sit left, cellos right. It follows the real stage layout. It gives the mix a clear, organized space.', practical_application='An engineer pans section spots to mirror the seating so the mix''s left-to-right layout matches how the orchestra actually sits on stage.', purpose_function='Places each section correctly in the stereo image so the ensemble''s layout is clear and matches the real stage arrangement.', related_terms=ARRAY['Stage imaging','Instrument localization','Section blend','Hall imaging','Panning']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Panning sections to match the seating plan','Setting string layout across the stereo field','Aligning section spots with the main image']::text[] WHERE id='22f12791-6736-4427-bdfc-2574130682b5';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mix automation / level balance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Riding so aggressively the natural dynamics vanish','Riding a whole section when only one part needs it','Jerky moves that draw attention to the ride']::text[], definition='The practice of riding, or continuously adjusting, the fader or gain of an instrumental or vocal section during a mix to keep its level balanced through changing dynamics so the section sits correctly against the rest of the ensemble.', plain_english='Riding means moving a fader by hand as music plays. A section ride does this for a whole group. You raise it when too quiet and lower it when too loud. It keeps the section balanced.', practical_application='An engineer rides the woodwind fader up under a busy string passage so an important melody stays audible, then eases it back afterward.', purpose_function='Keeps a section balanced against the ensemble as dynamics change, fixing moments where it is too loud or too quiet.', related_terms=ARRAY['Solo ride','Crescendo automation','Orchestral dynamics','Fader automation','Section blend']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Lifting winds over dense strings','Balancing a section through a crescendo','Keeping a countermelody audible']::text[] WHERE id='a04d9ff6-14d1-4c82-a0b1-69b6f6c300e1';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Mix automation / level balance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Riding so hard the soloist floats unnaturally above the hall','Late moves that let the ensemble briefly bury the solo','Changing the soloist''s tone by over-riding the spot mic']::text[], definition='Riding the fader or gain of a soloist or solo instrument during a mix to keep it clearly audible and correctly balanced above the accompaniment as its dynamics and the ensemble''s density change.', plain_english='This is moving a soloist''s fader by hand during the mix. You raise it when the soloist is covered and ease it back when clear. It keeps the solo on top of the group.', practical_application='An engineer rides a violin soloist''s spot mic up as the orchestra swells so the solo line stays present, then lowers it in quieter passages.', purpose_function='Keeps a soloist audible and well balanced against the ensemble throughout a passage, following the music''s changing dynamics.', related_terms=ARRAY['Section ride','Spot-microphone balance','Orchestral dynamics','Fader automation','Instrument localization']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Keeping a concerto soloist above the orchestra','Lifting a solo entry over an accompaniment','Balancing a vocal soloist against a choir']::text[] WHERE id='962eabd5-70be-4aa3-adc6-d00c4920a662';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Phase / microphone coherence', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Blending spots without time-aligning them to the mains','Reversed spot polarity thinning the sound','Ignoring comb filtering between spots and main mics']::text[], definition='The phase and timing agreement between spot, or accent, microphones and the main microphone array, so that when spots are blended in they reinforce rather than smear or comb-filter the direct sound, keeping the image and tone coherent.', plain_english='Spot mics are close mics on single instruments. Coherence means they line up in time with the main mics. When they line up, blending them sounds clean. When they do not, the sound smears or thins.', practical_application='An engineer time-aligns each spot to the mains so blending a spot adds clarity and body without hollow, comb-filtered tone.', purpose_function='Ensures spot mics reinforce the main array cleanly, preserving clear imaging and natural tone instead of phase smear or comb filtering.', related_terms=ARRAY['Spot-delay compensation','Spot-microphone balance','Comb filtering','Phase','Instrument localization']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Checking spot phase against the mains','Fixing hollow tone when a spot is added','Time-aligning accent mics in an orchestral mix']::text[] WHERE id='ef9d0be1-6f09-4a21-a48d-59a647aa5ec0';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Equalization / spot mics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Boosting a spot so hard it stands apart from the blend','Leaving proximity bass boom on a close spot','EQing spots without checking them against the mains']::text[], definition='Tonal shaping applied to individual spot, or accent, microphones to correct their close-up sound - for example taming proximity bass, harshness, or bleed - so each spot blends naturally with the main array and the overall ensemble tone.', plain_english='This is EQ on a single close mic. Close mics can sound too bassy or harsh. EQ fixes that tone. Then the spot blends well with the main mics.', practical_application='An engineer high-passes a cello spot to cut proximity boom and dips a harsh upper-mid so the spot sits naturally under the mains.', purpose_function='Corrects the unnatural close-up tone of spot mics so they blend smoothly with the main array rather than sounding thin, boomy, or harsh.', related_terms=ARRAY['Spot-microphone balance','Spot coherence','Equalization','Proximity effect','Main microphone array']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Taming proximity bass on a close spot','Matching a spot''s tone to the mains','Reducing harshness on a close woodwind mic']::text[] WHERE id='25fdaf67-6a82-4e4f-ab2c-18d7990d6447';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Time alignment / microphone technique', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Blending spots with no delay, smearing transients','Applying the wrong delay, pulling the instrument forward or back','Guessing distances instead of measuring for the delay']::text[], definition='Delaying spot, or accent, microphone signals so their arrival time matches that of the more distant main microphone array, aligning the close mic''s direct sound with the main pickup to avoid smearing, phase problems, and a doubled or forward image.', plain_english='A close spot mic hears an instrument before the main mics do. This adds a small delay to the spot. Now both mics line up in time. That stops smearing and phase problems.', practical_application='An engineer measures the distance from an instrument to the main mics, then delays its spot by roughly three milliseconds per metre so the arrivals coincide.', purpose_function='Time-aligns close spots with the main array so blended spots reinforce the sound cleanly and keep the ensemble''s depth and imaging intact.', related_terms=ARRAY['Spot coherence','Spot-microphone balance','Main microphone array','Comb filtering','Instrument localization']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Aligning a spot mic to a Decca tree','Fixing smear when a close mic is added','Setting delay by distance to the mains']::text[] WHERE id='57e6f160-3b8c-482b-9f6c-144cfef63062';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Microphone balance / classical mixing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Pushing spots so far the mix sounds close and multitracked','Adding spots without time-aligning them','Losing the natural blend by overusing accent mics']::text[], definition='The level and blend of spot, or accent, microphones against the main array, adding presence, clarity, or reinforcement to individual instruments or sections while keeping the main-mic sound as the foundation of the ensemble balance.', plain_english='Spot mics are close mics on single instruments. This is how loud they are in the mix. They add clarity and presence to those parts. The main mics stay the foundation.', practical_application='An engineer nudges a woodwind spot up just enough to bring a melody forward, keeping the mains dominant so the ensemble still sounds natural.', purpose_function='Adds definition and reinforcement to chosen instruments while keeping the natural main-mic balance as the mix''s foundation.', related_terms=ARRAY['Main microphone array','Spot coherence','Spot-delay compensation','Spot equalization','Section blend']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Bringing a solo line forward with its spot','Reinforcing a weak inner part','Balancing accent mics under the mains']::text[] WHERE id='009baef4-ee3c-4b53-9830-4dde1355a989';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Spatial imaging / classical', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Over-widening the mains so the center hollows','Spot pans that contradict the real seating','Depth collapsing so everything sounds equally close']::text[], definition='The reproduction of an ensemble''s left-to-right layout and front-to-back depth as it sat on stage, so the recorded image places each section and instrument where it physically was, giving a believable picture of the performance.', plain_english='This is the picture of the stage in the recording. It shows where each section sat, left to right. It also shows how far back they were. A good image matches the real stage.', practical_application='An engineer sets main-mic width and pans spots to reproduce the seating so the recorded stage matches where the players actually sat.', purpose_function='Recreates a believable stage picture so listeners perceive the ensemble''s real spatial layout and depth.', related_terms=ARRAY['Section imaging','Instrument localization','Hall imaging','Outrigger balance','Concert-hall perspective']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Reproducing an orchestra''s seating in the mix','Setting stereo width for a believable stage','Matching spot pans to the layout']::text[] WHERE id='fa2ac2ee-c9c3-479b-bfe1-42adaec02277';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Voice recording / session workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Not slating which take is the alternate, confusing the edit','Changing the words instead of just the delivery','Recording so many alternates the session runs long']::text[], definition='An additional recorded take of the same scripted line or passage performed with a different delivery - such as a change in tone, pace, emphasis, or emotion - giving the producer or editor options to choose from during editing.', plain_english='This is another take of the same line. The performer reads it a new way. They might change the tone or speed. The editor then picks the best version.', practical_application='A voice actor records a straight read, then an alternate read with a warmer, slower delivery so the producer can choose later.', purpose_function='Gives editors and producers a choice of deliveries for the same line, improving the final edit without re-booking the talent.', related_terms=ARRAY['Voiceover','Pickup','Take','Script','Comping']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Capturing a warmer version of a narration line','Recording upbeat and neutral reads of an ad','Giving the director delivery options']::text[] WHERE id='0138499d-18dc-42e8-9ef8-2bc839e4f2bb';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Voice recording / production', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording flat, under-energized takes unsuited to animation''s expressive style','Not capturing pickups and alternates for the animators','Letting mouth noise or plosives spoil close-mic takes']::text[], definition='The process of recording voice actors performing character dialogue for animated productions, typically capturing highly expressive, character-driven performances, and in many workflows recorded before animation so the visuals are timed to the voice.', plain_english='This is recording voices for cartoons. Actors play characters and say their lines. The acting is big and expressive. Often the voice is recorded first. Then the animation is drawn to match.', practical_application='An engineer records an animation actor performing several character voices, capturing clean, expressive takes and alternates for the animators and editors.', purpose_function='Captures expressive character performances that drive an animated production and, in pre-recorded workflows, provide timing the animation is built around.', related_terms=ARRAY['Voiceover','Alternate read','Character voice','Dialogue recording','Voice booth']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording character voices for a cartoon','Capturing pre-animation dialogue for timing','Voicing multiple characters in one session']::text[] WHERE id='d9cac637-9c29-4e54-9537-c48203c3d4d7';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Spoken-word recording / production', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Inconsistent level or tone across long sessions','Noise floor above the platform''s required limit','Uncorrected mouth clicks and breaths in long narration']::text[], definition='The process of recording a narrator reading a book aloud for spoken-word release, producing long-form, consistent narration that meets distributor technical standards such as the level, peak, and noise-floor targets required by audiobook platforms.', plain_english='This is recording someone reading a book out loud. The result is a long spoken-word file. The sound must stay even for hours. It also must meet the store''s loudness and noise rules.', practical_application='A narrator records chapters in a treated booth while the engineer keeps levels within ACX targets: about minus 23 to minus 18 dB RMS, minus 3 dB peak, and a noise floor below minus 60 dB.', purpose_function='Produces clear, consistent long-form narration that meets platform technical standards so the audiobook is accepted and comfortable to hear.', related_terms=ARRAY['Narration','Voiceover','Noise floor','RMS level','Loudness normalization']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a novel to ACX standards','Keeping tone consistent across many chapters','Meeting a platform''s noise-floor requirement']::text[] WHERE id='153f84df-b1b4-4781-bc89-42e3cbe9f0fa';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Voice performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making two characters sound so similar the listener cannot tell them apart','Losing a character''s voice or accent partway through a long recording','Overacting to the point that the delivery feels forced and unnatural']::text[], definition='The performance choices a narrator or voice actor makes to convey a character''s personality, emotion, and intent through voice, giving distinct life to that character within a scripted or narrated podcast.', plain_english='This is how a reader gives a character its own voice. The actor chooses tone, mood, and feeling. It makes the character sound like a real person.', practical_application='A narrator studies the script, decides on each character''s pace, pitch, and attitude, then keeps those choices consistent across every scene and episode.', purpose_function='Lets listeners tell characters apart and feel their emotions, turning flat script text into a believable performance that supports the story.', related_terms=ARRAY['Character voice recording','Performance read','Script read','Voice producer','Podcast narration']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Voicing several roles in a fiction or drama podcast','Deciding on a narrator persona for a scripted series','Keeping a character consistent across many recording sessions']::text[] WHERE id='ab95a21f-8ec4-41c9-b736-ee4f4352efd6';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Voice recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Changing mic distance or level between characters so voices do not match later','Not labeling takes by character, making the edit slow and confusing','Recording all characters in one pass, leaving no room to fix a single role']::text[], definition='The process of capturing a voice actor performing one or more distinct characters, often recording each character or role separately so the parts can be edited and mixed into a scripted audio production.', plain_english='This means recording an actor playing a character. Each role may be recorded on its own. The clips are then edited together into the show.', practical_application='The engineer sets a consistent mic and level, records the actor''s character lines in takes, labels each role, and captures pickups to fix mistakes.', purpose_function='Provides clean, well-performed character audio for fiction podcasts and audio dramas, giving the editor separate material to place, balance, and process.', related_terms=ARRAY['Character interpretation','Performance read','Pickup line','Multi-host recording','Voice producer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording an actor who voices multiple roles in an audio drama','Building separate character tracks for a scripted fiction podcast','Capturing pickups for a mispronounced or flubbed character line']::text[] WHERE id='8bfd93f2-be7e-4a90-bb74-ed68d3d1a101';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Voice performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Reading with a dramatic tone that undercuts a factual, credible feel','Recording narration at a level that does not match the interview audio','Pacing the read too fast for listeners to absorb the information']::text[], definition='Spoken narration recorded for factual, documentary-style audio content, in which a narrator guides the listener through real events, evidence, and interviews using a measured, informative delivery.', plain_english='This is the voice that guides a true-story show. The narrator explains facts and events. The tone is calm, clear, and trustworthy.', practical_application='The narrator records a clean script read at a steady pace, and the editor weaves that narration between interview clips and sound to build the episode.', purpose_function='Ties together interviews, archive audio, and scenes into a clear story, giving the listener context and a steady voice to follow through the material.', related_terms=ARRAY['Podcast narration','Script read','Long-form narration editing','Interview recording','Performance read']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Narrating a true-crime or investigative podcast','Linking archive tape and interviews in a documentary episode','Recording a steady voiceover for a factual audio series']::text[] WHERE id='da38160b-662f-4eec-97a5-982c75554e13';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Podcast metadata', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Leaving the description blank or reusing the same generic text every episode','Pasting unsupported HTML that some podcast apps strip or display badly','Burying the actual topic below a wall of promotional links']::text[], definition='The text field in a podcast''s RSS feed and directory listing that summarizes a single episode''s content, shown to listeners in podcast apps and carried in the feed''s item-level tags.', plain_english='This is the short write-up for one episode. It tells listeners what the episode covers. Podcast apps show it under the episode title.', practical_application='A producer writes each episode''s description with a summary, key topics, guest names, and links, then enters it in the hosting platform that builds the RSS feed.', purpose_function='Helps listeners decide whether to play an episode and helps apps and search index it, so a clear, accurate description improves discovery and engagement.', related_terms=ARRAY['Episode number','RSS feed audio','Podcast chapter marker','Sponsor message','Podcast guest']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Writing show notes and a summary before publishing an episode','Adding guest names and timestamps to an episode listing','Editing a description to improve how the episode appears in apps']::text[] WHERE id='e891878f-6739-4f99-a537-bd2ed10542db';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Podcast metadata', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Skipping or duplicating numbers so the series order becomes confusing','Confusing episode number with season number in the feed settings','Relying on numbers for trailers or bonuses that should stay unnumbered']::text[], definition='A numeric value assigned to a podcast episode, carried in the RSS feed''s episode-level tag, used by podcast apps to order and label episodes within a show or season.', plain_english='This is the number given to each episode. It tells apps the play order. Listeners see it next to the episode title.', practical_application='The producer sets the episode number in the hosting platform, often alongside a season number, so the feed presents episodes in the intended sequence.', purpose_function='Lets podcast apps sort episodes correctly and lets listeners follow a series in order, which matters most for serial or seasonal shows.', related_terms=ARRAY['Episode description','RSS feed audio','Podcast narration','Sponsor message','Podcast host']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Numbering a serialized podcast so apps play it in order','Setting season and episode numbers for a new season','Fixing an out-of-order feed by correcting episode numbers']::text[] WHERE id='724df053-5192-4a3e-b4cb-e83e2225abfa';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Podcast advertising', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Reading advertiser copy so stiffly it loses the natural host feel','Omitting required disclosure that the segment is a paid ad','Baking a dated offer into evergreen episodes that keep running it']::text[], definition='An advertisement performed by the podcast''s own host in their voice and style, either baked into the recording or inserted dynamically, rather than a separately produced studio spot.', plain_english='This is an ad read by the show''s host. It uses the host''s own voice and style. It feels like part of the show, not a separate spot.', practical_application='The host records the sponsor''s talking points in their normal delivery, and the producer places the read as a baked-in segment or as a dynamically inserted ad.', purpose_function='Uses the host''s credibility and rapport with listeners to make ads feel personal and trusted, which advertisers value more than a generic pre-produced spot.', related_terms=ARRAY['Sponsor message','Promo voice recording','Podcast host','Episode description','Trailer voice recording']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a sponsor read in the host''s own voice mid-episode','Choosing between baked-in and dynamically inserted host ads','Updating a host read when a sponsor''s promo code changes']::text[] WHERE id='80985300-b92b-47ae-97fd-b5326b0c54e8';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Podcast recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording everyone onto one mixed track, leaving no way to fix one voice','Trusting only the internet stream instead of local backups for remote guests','Letting guest and host levels drift far apart during a long talk']::text[], definition='The capture of a conversation between a host and one or more guests for a podcast, done in person or remotely, typically recording each participant so the audio can be edited and balanced afterward.', plain_english='This means recording a talk between a host and a guest. It can be in a room or online. Each voice is captured so it can be edited later.', practical_application='The engineer sets a mic and clean level for each person, records a backup, and for remote guests captures a local track from each end to avoid connection artifacts.', purpose_function='Provides the raw conversation that many podcasts are built on, and recording participants separately gives the editor control over levels, edits, and cleanup.', related_terms=ARRAY['Local podcast recording','Multi-host recording','Roundtable recording','Podcast guest','Podcast host']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a remote guest interview over a call platform','Capturing an in-studio conversation on separate mics','Setting up backup tracks before a one-time guest session']::text[] WHERE id='4dd84f87-7804-4685-b3b0-88b8927a2d7f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Podcast recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Forgetting to start each local recorder so a track is lost','Judging quality by the call audio instead of the local files','Not capturing a sync clap or marker to line up the separate tracks']::text[], definition='A remote-podcast method in which each participant''s audio is recorded on their own device at full quality and later combined, rather than capturing the compressed audio of a live internet connection.', plain_english='Here each person records their own voice on their own device. The clean files are sent in later. This avoids the poor quality of a live call.', practical_application='Using a double-ender or a recording platform, each speaker records locally while they talk over a call, then uploads their track for the editor to sync and mix.', purpose_function='Preserves full audio quality for every participant regardless of connection problems, so the finished show sounds like everyone was in the same room.', related_terms=ARRAY['Interview recording','Multi-host recording','Roundtable recording','RSS feed audio','Podcast guest']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a remote co-host with a double-ender setup','Collecting local tracks from guests in different cities','Avoiding dropouts by recording each end locally during a call']::text[] WHERE id='182a5580-5e21-4b07-bef1-3421b4d2f2c2';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Audio editing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Cutting pauses so tightly the read sounds rushed and unnatural','Letting tone or level drift between sessions recorded on different days','Missing swallowed words or clicks that only show up on close listening']::text[], definition='The editing of extended spoken-word recordings such as audiobooks or long narrated episodes, involving removal of errors and noises, retake assembly, and pacing so hours of narration play as a smooth, continuous listen.', plain_english='This is editing long spoken recordings, like audiobooks. The editor cuts out mistakes and noises. The goal is a smooth listen over many hours.', practical_application='The editor removes flubs and swaps in pickups, tightens gaps between sentences, cleans mouth noises, and checks level and tone match across an entire long recording.', purpose_function='Turns raw hours of narration into a clean, evenly paced program, keeping the tone and level consistent so long-form listening stays comfortable.', related_terms=ARRAY['Punch-and-roll recording','Pickup line','Mouth-noise reduction','Podcast narration','Documentary narration']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Editing a full audiobook narration to a clean master','Assembling pickups into a long narrated episode','Smoothing pacing across hours of a single narrator''s read']::text[] WHERE id='4ad59a26-0d55-480d-bb83-33a8853a0f58';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Audio restoration', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Over-processing so the voice loses natural detail and sounds lisped','Treating mouth noise only in editing instead of also fixing it at recording','Missing quiet clicks between words that reveal themselves on headphones']::text[], definition='The reduction or removal of unwanted mouth sounds in a voice recording, such as lip smacks, clicks, and saliva noises, using targeted editing or specialized de-click and mouth-noise processing.', plain_english='This removes small mouth sounds from a voice track. Clicks, lip smacks, and saliva noises are cut. The voice then sounds clean and clear.', practical_application='The editor uses a mouth de-click tool or manual edits to catch clicks and smacks, and the talent stays hydrated to reduce the noises at the source.', purpose_function='Cleans up close-mic voice recordings so distracting mouth sounds do not pull the listener''s attention away from the words, which matters most for narration and audiobooks.', related_terms=ARRAY['Long-form narration editing','Punch-and-roll recording','Podcast narration','Pickup line','Engineering best practice']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Cleaning lip smacks from a close-mic audiobook narration','Removing clicks before mastering a voiceover','Fixing dry-mouth noises on a long interview track']::text[] WHERE id='4cca6257-c9f2-46cd-aecf-9e0ccbedb3f3';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Podcast recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Sharing one mic between hosts, which ruins independent control','Ignoring mic bleed so one host is heard on another''s track','Letting host levels drift apart over a long conversation']::text[], definition='A recording session with two or more hosts, in which each host is captured on a separate microphone and track so their voices can be edited and balanced independently in the mix.', plain_english='This is a session with two or more hosts. Each host gets their own mic and track. That lets the editor balance every voice on its own.', practical_application='The engineer gives each host a dedicated mic into its own channel, sets matched levels, manages bleed between mics, and records everyone to separate tracks.', purpose_function='Gives a conversational show multiple voices while keeping each on its own track, so the editor can control levels, cut cross-talk, and fix any one host.', related_terms=ARRAY['Single-host recording','Roundtable recording','Interview recording','Local podcast recording','Podcast host']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a two-host chat show on separate mics','Managing bleed between several hosts around one table','Balancing co-hosts who speak at very different volumes']::text[] WHERE id='af851a09-8bd3-4c61-8c41-700491c64fcd';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Voice performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Pushing energy so hard the read becomes shouty and tiring','Applying a high-energy read to content that needs a calm, factual tone','Losing consistency of energy across separate takes and pickups']::text[], definition='A voice delivery given with deliberate energy, emotion, and pacing to engage listeners, as opposed to a flat or purely informational read, common in trailers, ads, and dramatized narration.', plain_english='This is a lively, expressive way of reading. The voice uses energy and feeling. It grabs the listener instead of sounding flat.', practical_application='A voice producer coaches the talent on energy, emphasis, and pacing, and the engineer captures several performed takes to choose the most engaging delivery.', purpose_function='Adds emotion and momentum to spoken content so it holds attention, which is why trailers, promos, and story narration rely on it rather than a neutral read.', related_terms=ARRAY['Script read','Character interpretation','Trailer voice recording','Promo voice recording','Voice producer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording an upbeat read for a podcast trailer','Directing talent for an engaging ad delivery','Choosing the most expressive take for a promo']::text[] WHERE id='2a309a82-7250-476d-9c45-d0e92675fdb2';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Voice recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording pickups at a different mic distance or level so they do not match','Changing energy or pace so the pickup stands out from the original','Waiting until the talent has left, making a matching pickup impossible']::text[], definition='A short line or phrase re-recorded to replace a mistake, misread, or noise in an earlier take, captured under matching conditions so it can be edited seamlessly into the original recording.', plain_english='This is a small line recorded again to fix an error. It replaces a bad word or noise. It is dropped into the earlier take.', practical_application='The talent keeps the same mic, distance, and tone, re-reads the flubbed words, and the editor swaps the pickup in so the join is inaudible.', purpose_function='Lets an engineer repair a small flaw without re-recording a whole section, saving time while keeping the delivery and sound consistent.', related_terms=ARRAY['Punch-and-roll recording','Long-form narration editing','Script read','Character voice recording','Pronunciation check']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Re-recording a mispronounced name after a narration take','Fixing a single flubbed sentence in an ad read','Capturing pickups before the voice talent leaves the studio']::text[] WHERE id='cbfe82cf-9b8f-4367-b224-6b5d3e01ce38';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Podcast metadata', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming every podcast app displays chapters, when support varies','Setting chapter times that drift after the audio is re-edited','Confusing the embedded ID3 and podcast-namespace chapter formats']::text[], definition='A timestamped marker that divides a podcast episode into labeled sections, delivered either as embedded ID3 chapters in the audio file or via the podcast namespace, letting listeners jump between segments in supporting apps.', plain_english='This marks named sections inside an episode. Each marker has a time and a title. Listeners can jump to a part in apps that support it.', practical_application='The producer defines section start times and titles, then embeds them as ID3 chapters in the file or publishes a chapters file referenced from the RSS feed.', purpose_function='Improves navigation of longer episodes by letting listeners skip to topics, ads, or segments, and lets creators structure content clearly for the audience.', related_terms=ARRAY['Episode description','RSS feed audio','Episode number','Sponsor message','Podcast narration']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding jump points to a long interview episode','Marking the start of ad and content segments','Publishing chapters so supporting apps show a segment list']::text[] WHERE id='384829b7-bae1-42b0-8dce-246b301dac6a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Podcast roles', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Not prepping a remote guest''s mic and room before recording','Failing to confirm the guest''s name pronunciation and spelling','Relying on the call audio instead of a clean local guest track']::text[], definition='A person who appears on a podcast episode by invitation to be interviewed or take part in a discussion, rather than being a regular host of the show.', plain_english='This is a person invited onto an episode. They join to talk or be interviewed. They are not a regular host of the show.', practical_application='The producer briefs the guest on setup, captures a local track when the guest is remote, checks levels, and gathers the correct name spelling for the episode credits.', purpose_function='Brings new voices, expertise, or stories to a show, and from a production view the guest''s audio must be captured cleanly, often under less controlled conditions.', related_terms=ARRAY['Podcast host','Interview recording','Local podcast recording','Pronunciation guide','Roundtable recording']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Preparing a remote guest''s setup before an interview','Recording a guest''s clean local track for later editing','Confirming a guest''s name for the show credits']::text[] WHERE id='ebb2543e-a1ba-4519-ad17-c30f17c7f639';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Podcast roles', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Changing the host''s mic or chain so their sound differs between episodes','Balancing guests to a host level that itself drifts over time','Neglecting the host''s room treatment while focusing on guests']::text[], definition='The regular presenter of a podcast who leads the show, guides conversations, delivers narration or ad reads, and provides the consistent voice that listeners associate with the program.', plain_english='This is the main person who leads the show. They guide the talk and speak to the listener. Their voice is the one people know.', practical_application='The engineer dials in a reliable mic, level, and tone for the host, uses that as the mix reference, and records host segments, links, and any host-read ads.', purpose_function='Gives the show a consistent identity and direction, and from a production view the host''s voice is usually the reference the whole mix is balanced around.', related_terms=ARRAY['Podcast guest','Multi-host recording','Single-host recording','Host-read advertisement','Voice producer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting the host''s mic as the mix reference for an episode','Recording host links and intros between segments','Keeping the host''s sound consistent across a series']::text[] WHERE id='9f8130f7-6263-4744-ab5e-bc857ed489bf';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Voice performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording narration in an untreated room so it clashes with clean scenes','Pacing the read without leaving space for edits and sound','Letting the narration tone shift between separate recording days']::text[], definition='Scripted spoken content in a podcast delivered by a narrator to tell a story or present information, as distinct from unscripted conversation, forming the backbone of storytelling and documentary-style shows.', plain_english='This is scripted speaking in a podcast. A narrator reads to tell a story or explain facts. It is planned, not casual chat.', practical_application='The narrator records the script in a treated space at a steady level, and the editor cleans and paces the narration to sit between interviews, music, and effects.', purpose_function='Carries the story or information in scripted shows, giving a clear, controlled voice that the producer can pace and edit precisely around other audio.', related_terms=ARRAY['Documentary narration','Script read','Long-form narration editing','Performance read','Podcast host']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording the scripted spine of a storytelling podcast','Voicing links that connect interviews in a documentary','Pacing narration to leave room for music and effects']::text[] WHERE id='27efad7e-5021-44d3-a5bc-209d3b5e8bc6';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Voice recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Recording a flat read that fails to stand out as a promo','Making the promo too long for the slots it will run in','Leaving the voice level too low to survive later music and mastering']::text[], definition='The recording of a short promotional voice segment used to advertise a podcast, episode, or partner show, typically delivered with an engaging read and produced to grab attention quickly.', plain_english='This is recording a short ad for a show. The voice promotes an episode or podcast. It is punchy and made to grab attention fast.', practical_application='The talent records an energetic read of the promo copy, and the producer edits it tightly, adds music or effects, and delivers it for cross-promotion or ad slots.', purpose_function='Creates the spoken element of a promo that draws new listeners, so the read must be engaging and the recording clean enough to sit in a polished spot.', related_terms=ARRAY['Trailer voice recording','Performance read','Sponsor message','Host-read advertisement','Script read']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a promo to swap with another podcast','Producing a short spot to advertise a new episode','Cutting an energetic promo read for an ad slot']::text[] WHERE id='74ae768d-d0e7-4759-bb30-f8e08977c1ac';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording preparation', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming a pronunciation instead of confirming it with the person named','Checking terms only after the talent has already recorded them wrong','Not writing pronunciations down for the talent to follow']::text[], definition='The step of confirming the correct pronunciation of names, places, and technical terms before or during recording, so the talent can deliver them accurately and avoid re-records.', plain_english='This is checking how to say hard words before recording. Names and terms are confirmed first. It stops mistakes that need a redo.', practical_application='The producer gathers tricky names and terms, confirms them with the guest or a reliable source, and shares the correct pronunciations with the talent before recording.', purpose_function='Prevents mispronunciations that would require pickups or damage credibility, saving time and keeping a professional, accurate delivery for the audience.', related_terms=ARRAY['Pronunciation guide','Pickup line','Script read','Podcast guest','Voice producer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Confirming a guest''s name before an interview','Checking foreign place names in a script','Clarifying technical terms with an expert before recording']::text[] WHERE id='59eb18b1-ebf2-4475-8150-65a68cef190b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording preparation', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Writing pronunciations in a way the talent cannot easily read','Leaving the guide incomplete so some hard words are still guessed','Not updating the guide when the script or names change']::text[], definition='A reference document that lists the correct pronunciations of names, places, and terms in a script, often written phonetically, so voice talent can deliver them consistently and accurately.', plain_english='This is a list showing how to say hard words. It often spells them out by sound. Talent uses it to say each word right.', practical_application='The producer builds a phonetic list of tricky terms with the script, shares it with the talent before the session, and updates it as new names come up.', purpose_function='Gives the talent a single trusted reference for difficult words, ensuring accurate and consistent delivery across takes, sessions, and multiple readers.', related_terms=ARRAY['Pronunciation check','Script read','Podcast narration','Voice producer','Documentary narration']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Preparing a phonetic list for an audiobook narrator','Sharing name pronunciations with a documentary voiceover artist','Keeping terms consistent across several narrators']::text[] WHERE id='479d36c3-b5d9-4759-ad82-48997333834f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Voice recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Punching in with a different tone or pace so the join is audible','Setting the punch point mid-word, leaving an obvious seam','Relying on it so heavily that natural read momentum is lost']::text[], definition='A narration technique in which the reader plays back the last few seconds of the previous take, then records over from a chosen point, seamlessly continuing the read; widely used for audiobooks and long-form voice work.', plain_english='The narrator hears the last few words, then records on from there. The new audio replaces the mistake. This keeps a long read smooth as they go.', practical_application='In the DAW the narrator sets a pre-roll, listens to the tail of the good take, and punches in to re-record from the last clean word onward without stopping the flow.', purpose_function='Lets a solo narrator fix errors during recording and finish with an almost-edited take, cutting editing time on long projects like audiobooks.', related_terms=ARRAY['Pickup line','Long-form narration editing','Podcast narration','Script read','Engineering workflow optimization']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A solo narrator self-recording an audiobook','Fixing misreads on the fly during long narration','Delivering a near-edited take to save editing time']::text[] WHERE id='f6ba22cc-b05b-4ea3-9a03-7301fb57a0e8';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Podcast recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using too few mics so voices share channels and cannot be separated','Ignoring heavy bleed that makes cutting cross-talk impossible','Failing to manage turn-taking so overlaps ruin usable audio']::text[], definition='A recording session capturing several participants in an open group discussion, each ideally on a separate microphone, presenting the challenge of managing overlap, cross-talk, and mic bleed among many voices.', plain_english='This records a group talking together. Each person should have a mic. The hard part is people talking over each other.', practical_application='The engineer mics each participant, sets matched levels, controls bleed by mic choice and placement, and records everyone to separate tracks for editing overlap.', purpose_function='Captures lively multi-person conversation for panel and discussion shows, while separate tracks give the editor control over the many voices and their overlap.', related_terms=ARRAY['Multi-host recording','Interview recording','Single-host recording','Local podcast recording','Podcast guest']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a four-person panel discussion','Managing cross-talk in a lively group debate','Micing several guests around one table']::text[] WHERE id='3e0a3a7b-47ff-486f-af72-c8b714d517a0';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Podcast distribution', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Changing an episode''s audio URL, which breaks the enclosure for apps','Setting a wrong MIME type or file size in the enclosure entry','Delivering a bitrate too low for a program that needs clean speech']::text[], definition='The audio file for a podcast episode referenced in the RSS feed''s enclosure element, which podcast apps read to download or stream the episode; the feed carries the file URL, type, and size.', plain_english='This is the episode audio linked in the podcast feed. Apps read the feed to find and play it. The feed lists the file''s web address, type, and size.', practical_application='The host platform uploads the episode audio and writes an enclosure entry in the RSS feed with the correct URL, MIME type, and byte length for apps to fetch.', purpose_function='Is how podcast apps actually get each episode: the enclosure in the RSS feed points to the hosted audio so listeners can download or stream it.', related_terms=ARRAY['Episode description','Episode number','Podcast chapter marker','Sponsor message','Local podcast recording']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Publishing an episode so apps can fetch its audio','Troubleshooting an enclosure that podcast apps fail to load','Checking file type and size fields in a podcast feed']::text[] WHERE id='6c22d655-9a68-4e4a-8815-7f1b37c64f82';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Voice performance', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Reading so carefully it sounds stiff instead of conversational','Not marking the script for breaths and emphasis before recording','Rustling paper scripts into the mic during the read']::text[], definition='A voice recording delivered from written script rather than spoken spontaneously, used for narration, ads, and trailers, where the talent aims to sound natural and engaging while following the exact words.', plain_english='This is reading a written script out loud for recording. The talent says the exact words. The goal is to still sound natural.', practical_application='The talent rehearses the script, marks emphasis and breaths, and records takes at a steady level, while the producer captures pickups for any misreads.', purpose_function='Delivers exact, controlled wording for narration and ads, letting producers plan content precisely while the talent keeps the delivery sounding conversational.', related_terms=ARRAY['Performance read','Podcast narration','Pickup line','Pronunciation guide','Voice producer']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a scripted ad read','Voicing planned narration for an episode','Delivering trailer copy from a written script']::text[] WHERE id='1859832e-a727-479c-8b00-6974a1a586e4';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Podcast recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Neglecting room treatment because only one mic is in use','Letting the single voice''s level drift with no reference to compare','Recording plosives and breaths without a pop filter or placement fix']::text[], definition='A recording session with one host speaking alone, typically captured on a single microphone and track, common for solo commentary, narration, and monologue-style podcasts.', plain_english='This is a session with just one host. One mic and one track are used. It suits solo shows and narration.', practical_application='The engineer optimizes one mic, room, and chain for the single voice, sets a steady level, and records the host''s monologue or narration to one clean track.', purpose_function='Provides a simple, controlled setup for solo shows, letting the engineer focus on one voice, one mic, and consistent level and tone.', related_terms=ARRAY['Multi-host recording','Roundtable recording','Podcast narration','Podcast host','Script read']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a solo commentary episode','Capturing a monologue-style show on one mic','Voicing solo narration in a treated booth']::text[] WHERE id='07af2f74-c986-4641-8b21-6d7bffc8cb84';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Podcast advertising', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Omitting clear disclosure that the segment is a paid ad','Mixing the ad much louder or quieter than the show content','Leaving an expired offer in an evergreen episode']::text[], definition='An advertising segment within a podcast that promotes a sponsor''s product or service, delivered as a host read or a produced spot, and typically disclosed to listeners as paid promotion.', plain_english='This is an ad for a sponsor inside a podcast. The host or a voice reads it. Listeners are told it is paid.', practical_application='The producer records or inserts the sponsor read at a set point, balances it with the show, and includes required disclosure and any promo code or link.', purpose_function='Delivers the paid promotion that funds many podcasts, and it must be produced and disclosed so it meets advertiser expectations and disclosure rules.', related_terms=ARRAY['Host-read advertisement','Promo voice recording','Podcast chapter marker','Episode description','Trailer voice recording']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Placing a sponsor read into an episode','Balancing an ad segment against show levels','Adding disclosure and a promo code to a sponsor spot']::text[] WHERE id='58afde38-cfb6-47bb-85de-6f2059ff26f7';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Voice recording', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Making the trailer too long for its teaser purpose','Recording a flat read that fails to build interest','Forgetting to mark the item as a trailer in the feed settings']::text[], definition='The recording of voice content for a podcast trailer, a short piece that introduces a show or season and is often flagged in the RSS feed as a trailer, delivered with an engaging read to attract new listeners.', plain_english='This is recording the voice for a podcast trailer. A trailer is a short teaser for a show. The read is lively to pull in new listeners.', practical_application='The talent records an energetic read of the trailer copy, and the producer edits it with music and effects, then publishes it flagged as a trailer in the feed.', purpose_function='Creates the spoken hook for a trailer that markets a show or season, so the read and recording must be engaging and clean enough for a polished teaser.', related_terms=ARRAY['Promo voice recording','Performance read','Script read','Episode description','Sponsor message']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording the voice for a new-season trailer','Producing a short teaser to launch a podcast','Cutting an engaging trailer read with music']::text[] WHERE id='84474c94-9d3d-4920-8839-94d56cf433ec';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Podcast roles', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Directing so much the talent loses their natural delivery','Failing to catch a misread that then needs a costly pickup later','Not confirming all lines were covered before the talent leaves']::text[], definition='A person who directs voice talent during a recording session, guiding performance, pacing, and interpretation, and making creative and technical decisions to get the best usable read.', plain_english='This person directs the voice talent while recording. They coach tone, pace, and feeling. Their job is to get the best read.', practical_application='The voice producer briefs the talent, listens for tone and pace, calls for pickups on misreads or weak delivery, and confirms coverage before the session ends.', purpose_function='Shapes the vocal performance to fit the project, coaching delivery and catching errors in the moment so the recorded read meets the creative goal.', related_terms=ARRAY['Performance read','Character interpretation','Script read','Pronunciation check','Podcast narration']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Directing a narrator through an audiobook session','Coaching a voice actor on a character''s tone','Guiding talent for pace and energy on an ad read']::text[] WHERE id='f56b45a0-79f8-4a75-aa68-9ee10c43b103';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording methodology', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Treating a habit or preference as best practice without evidence it works','UNSAFE: Ignoring proper grounding and safe electrical practice, risking hum, damage, or shock','Chasing loud levels and clipping converters instead of leaving headroom']::text[], definition='In recording, the established, recommended methods for setting up and operating a signal chain, such as correct gain staging, proper grounding, level referencing, and consistent monitoring, that reliably produce clean, professional results.', plain_english='These are the proven ways to set up recording gear. They cover things like levels and grounding. Following them gives clean, reliable sound.', practical_application='An engineer applies best practice by setting proper gain at each stage, checking grounding to avoid hum, labeling signal paths, and monitoring at a controlled level.', purpose_function='Gives engineers a shared, tested baseline that avoids common problems like noise, distortion, and poor levels, so sessions are consistent and technically sound.', related_terms=ARRAY['Engineering workflow optimization','Mission-critical recording','Mouth-noise reduction','Multi-host recording','Interview recording']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting correct gain staging across a signal chain','Troubleshooting hum by checking grounding practice','Standardizing a reliable session setup for a studio']::text[] WHERE id='95486d11-0620-4fd0-9fcd-7dd86b8bada9';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording methodology', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Optimizing for speed in ways that skip essential quality checks','Building templates so rigid they cannot adapt to a new session','Not documenting a workflow so others cannot follow it']::text[], definition='The practice of streamlining recording and signal-chain procedures, through session templates, labeling, shortcuts, and repeatable routines, to work faster and more reliably without sacrificing audio quality.', plain_english='This means making the recording process faster and smoother. Engineers use templates and set routines. The goal is speed without losing quality.', practical_application='The engineer builds session templates, names tracks and inputs consistently, sets up routing once, and uses shortcuts so each new session starts ready to record.', purpose_function='Reduces wasted time and errors during sessions, letting an engineer focus on capture and creative decisions rather than repetitive setup and troubleshooting.', related_terms=ARRAY['Engineering best practice','Mission-critical recording','Punch-and-roll recording','Multi-host recording','Local podcast recording']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Creating a session template for recurring podcast episodes','Standardizing track naming and routing across sessions','Speeding repetitive setup so more time goes to capture']::text[] WHERE id='60606819-920e-41bf-8c3a-58d98e663d60';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording methodology', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Trusting a single recorder or path with no backup for a one-time event','UNSAFE: Skipping a pre-event equipment and power check, risking total capture loss','Not monitoring levels live, so clipping or dropouts go unnoticed']::text[], definition='A recording of an event that cannot be repeated, such as a live performance or one-time interview, where failure is unacceptable and redundant recording and careful preparation are used to guarantee a usable capture.', plain_english='This is recording something that happens only once. Failure is not an option. Engineers use backups to make sure it is captured.', practical_application='The engineer runs a redundant second recorder or backup track, checks batteries and media, tests the chain before the event, and monitors levels throughout.', purpose_function='Protects irreplaceable audio by planning for equipment or media failure, so a one-time event is still captured even if one recorder or path fails.', related_terms=ARRAY['Engineering best practice','Engineering workflow optimization','Interview recording','Local podcast recording','Multi-host recording']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a live concert that cannot be repeated','Running a redundant recorder for a one-time interview','Preparing backups before an irreplaceable event']::text[] WHERE id='caec1105-7b8c-45cb-b586-19d07c91d10a';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Cinematic / trailer sound design element', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Overusing braams so every cut sounds the same and the impact is lost','Layering so much sub-bass that the braam distorts or masks dialogue','Assuming a braam is purely orchestral rather than a designed brass-and-synth hybrid','Confusing a braam with a simple, undesigned orchestral brass stab']::text[], definition='A braam is a loud, low, brassy blast used as a dramatic accent in film trailers and cinematic scores. Popularized after Hans Zimmer''s Inception, it typically stacks distorted brass with synthesized sub-bass into a short, ominous swell that punctuates a cut, reveal, or title card.', plain_english='A braam is a big, low horn-like blast. You hear it a lot in movie trailers. It makes a moment feel huge and tense. Designers stack brass and deep bass to build it. It hits hard for a second, then fades.', practical_application='A trailer editor drops a braam on a hard cut or title card, then layers reverb and a sub-bass tail so it sits under picture and lands with the visual impact.', purpose_function='It delivers an instant sense of dread, scale, or impact, marking a cut, logo, or reveal in trailers and cinematic cues without needing a melody.', related_terms=ARRAY['Riser','Downer effect','Trailer sound design','Sub-bass','Impact hit','Brass stab']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Cutting a teaser where each title card lands on a braam','Designing a custom braam by distorting and pitching down brass samples','Mixing a braam under a logo sting so it does not bury the voiceover']::text[] WHERE id='46e3c0c9-2f63-4705-9ada-c83e60740272';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Cinematic / trailer transition effect', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a downer with a riser, which moves in the opposite direction','Timing the downer so its end misses the picture cut','Letting the descending sweep clash with a rising musical cue underneath','Using an overly long downer that drains momentum from the edit']::text[], definition='A downer is a transition sound effect whose pitch, energy, or filter descends over time, used to release tension or lead into an impact. It is the falling counterpart to a riser and is common in trailer, cinematic, and electronic-music transitions.', plain_english='A downer is a sound that falls in pitch or energy. It winds down instead of building up. It is the opposite of a riser. Editors use it to ease out of a scene. It often drops into a hit or into quiet.', practical_application='A sound editor places a downer at the tail of an action beat so the descending sweep carries the audience into the next, calmer scene, often crossing into an impact or into silence.', purpose_function='It smooths or dramatizes a transition by pulling energy downward, signaling a comedown, a scene change, or the settling that follows a big moment.', related_terms=ARRAY['Riser','Braam','Whoosh','Impact hit','Transition effect','Sweep']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Ending an intense trailer section with a downer into quiet dialogue','Layering a downer before an impact to accentuate the drop','Transitioning between two contrasting scenes with a descending sweep']::text[] WHERE id='bd68b7e8-9d5a-4eb7-a490-d25ca8028d3e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Modular / Eurorack CV utility module', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Expecting audio-rate fidelity from a recorder meant for slow modulation voltages','Forgetting to match playback clock or loop length to the rest of the patch','Confusing it with a step sequencer, since it records continuous voltage rather than discrete steps','Feeding it voltages outside the module''s rated input range']::text[], definition='A control-voltage recorder is a module or device that captures incoming control-voltage signals over time and plays them back, letting a performer record CV gestures and loop or replay them. It effectively provides motion or automation recording for a modular synthesizer system.', plain_english='A CV recorder saves the control voltages you send it. Later it plays them back the same way. Think of it like recording your knob moves. You can loop the recording so the motion repeats. It adds hands-free movement to a patch (a set of connected modules).', practical_application='A modular player records a slow filter sweep or a hand-drawn CV curve into the module, then loops it so the sweep repeats in time while they patch or perform other parts.', purpose_function='It lets a musician capture evolving modulation or hand-played voltage gestures and replay them exactly, freeing the hands and adding repeatable, complex movement to a patch.', related_terms=ARRAY['Control voltage','Modulation','Sequencer','Sample and hold','Envelope generator','Automation']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a live-performed filter sweep to loop during a set','Capturing a random CV source''s output so a ''happy accident'' can be replayed','Layering recorded modulation onto several destinations in a generative patch']::text[] WHERE id='4d24df74-7868-4c08-9ab5-d50a0776bd2b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Modular / CV gate signal type', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Patching a negative (S-trig) gate straight into a positive-gate input and getting no sound or stuck notes','Confusing the S-trigger with the different inverted gates used by Korg and Yamaha','Assuming every synth uses the same positive-going gate polarity','Forgetting that reversing polarity flips which voltage level means note-on']::text[], definition='A negative gate is a gate signal with inverted, active-low polarity: it rests at a high level and drops to a low or negative voltage to indicate the note-on or active state, the reverse of a standard positive gate that rests low and rises to trigger. It is conceptually related to the S-trigger and to the inverted gate signals used by some Korg and Yamaha instruments.', plain_english='A normal gate sits low and jumps high to play a note. A negative gate does the opposite. It sits high and drops low to trigger. Some old Moog, Korg, and Yamaha gear worked this way. You often need a converter to use it with modern synths.', practical_application='When connecting a vintage S-trig or inverted-gate synth to a modern positive-gate system, a technician inserts an S-trig/gate converter or a gate inverter so the negative gate correctly starts and stops notes.', purpose_function='It signals note-on or an active state using inverted voltage so gear built for active-low logic triggers correctly, and it lets a patch reverse normal gate behavior.', related_terms=ARRAY['Gate signal','S-trigger','V-trigger','Trigger','Envelope generator','Gate inverter']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Connecting a Moog with S-trig to a Eurorack sequencer through a converter','Inverting a gate so an envelope opens on release instead of on note-on','Troubleshooting silent notes caused by mismatched gate polarity']::text[] WHERE id='6ac53c22-da7b-41e5-8903-26d639d18eec';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Modular / CV clock and sync signal', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the run/stop line with the clock pulse itself','Wiring the wrong DIN-sync pin so a device clocks but never starts','Expecting a run signal alone to keep timing without a separate clock signal','Assuming every sequencer reads run polarity the same way']::text[], definition='A run signal is a gate-like control signal that starts and stops a clock or sequencer: while it is high the device runs, and while it is low it stops. In the DIN sync standard it is the run/stop line, held near 0 V for stop and about +5 V for run.', plain_english='A run signal tells a sequencer or clock when to go. High voltage means run. Low voltage means stop. It works like a start and stop switch made of voltage. DIN sync uses about five volts for run and zero for stop.', practical_application='A technician patches a master clock''s run output to a sequencer''s run input, so pressing play starts both in sync and pressing stop halts them together at the same point.', purpose_function='It provides transport control for a clock or sequencer so several devices start, stop, and stay aligned together instead of each running on its own.', related_terms=ARRAY['Clock','Reset','DIN sync','Gate signal','Sequencer','Start/stop']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Syncing a drum machine to a modular clock via the DIN sync run/stop line','Using a run gate to start and stop a sequencer from a master transport','Debugging why a slaved sequencer clocks but will not begin playing']::text[] WHERE id='f6ea5882-a8e7-4bdd-8a4e-f44d285554c3';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Magnetic tape format', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a broadcast NAB cart with the consumer 8-track cartridge','Forgetting the tape is an endless loop that cannot be rewound','Neglecting worn pressure pads or lubrication, causing wow, flutter, and dropouts']::text[], definition='A tape-recording method using a self-contained plastic cartridge that holds an endless loop of lubricated magnetic tape, so playback runs continuously without rewinding; used in broadcast ''cart'' machines (NAB carts) for jingles and spots and in the consumer 8-track music format.', plain_english='Cartridge recording uses tape sealed inside a plastic case. The tape forms an endless loop. It plays over and over without rewinding. Radio stations used carts for jingles and ads. Home 8-track players used a similar idea.', practical_application='A radio operator loads a jingle onto a cart, cues it with the machine''s automatic cue tone, and fires it instantly on air; the loop re-cues itself for the next play.', purpose_function='Provides instant-start, no-rewind playback of short segments, which made cartridges ideal for broadcast jingles, station IDs, and commercials, and for continuous consumer music playback.', related_terms=ARRAY['NAB cartridge','8-track tape','Endless-loop tape','Broadcast cart machine','Cue tone']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Firing station jingles from a cart machine on air','Playing back commercials in a radio automation system','Listening to an album on a car 8-track player']::text[] WHERE id='ba245422-ed13-45a5-a704-c44876255461';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Magnetic tape format', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the Elcaset with the ordinary compact cassette','Assuming Elcaset tape and cassette tape are interchangeable','Overlooking that the format was short-lived and commercially unsuccessful']::text[], definition='A recording method using the Elcaset, a large cassette format introduced by Sony in 1976 that housed quarter-inch tape running at 3.75 ips and drew the tape out of the shell for transport contact, aiming to combine open-reel sound quality with cassette convenience.', plain_english='Elcaset was a big cassette made by Sony. It held wider tape than a normal cassette. The tape ran faster, so it sounded better. The machine pulled the tape out of the shell to play it. The format did not sell well and faded fast.', practical_application='An enthusiast recorded from a tuner or turntable onto Elcaset, gaining higher quality than a standard cassette while avoiding threading open-reel tape by hand.', purpose_function='Sought to deliver near open-reel fidelity in a convenient loadable shell, offering better signal-to-noise and frequency response than the compact cassette of the era.', related_terms=ARRAY['Compact cassette','Open-reel tape','Quarter-inch tape','Tape speed','Signal-to-noise ratio']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Restoring a vintage Sony Elcaset deck','Comparing Elcaset fidelity to open-reel and cassette','Cataloguing an obsolete 1970s consumer tape format']::text[] WHERE id='4f4caf29-4a9c-442a-b0b7-96c7cbbaea26';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording history period', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming early records were multitracked when many were mono or two-track','Confusing higher track count with better audio quality','Overlooking tape-machine maintenance needs when transferring old multitracks']::text[], definition='The period in recording history — broadly from the late 1950s and 1960s onward — when multitrack tape machines (4-, 8-, 16-, then 24-track) became central to music production, letting parts be recorded separately, overdubbed, and mixed down afterward rather than captured live to one or two tracks.', plain_english='This was the time when studios used tape with many tracks. Each part could be recorded on its own track. Musicians could add parts one at a time. Engineers mixed all the tracks together later. It changed how records were made.', practical_application='Knowing the era helps a technician read old track sheets, service vintage multitrack machines, and transfer archived 16- or 24-track tapes for reissue.', purpose_function='Marks the shift to layered, overdubbed production, giving artists and engineers control to build recordings track by track and to set balances after the performance.', related_terms=ARRAY['Multitrack tape machine','Overdubbing','Mixdown','Track sheet','24-track recording','Tape recording era']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Transferring a 24-track master from the analog era','Studying how overdubbing changed 1960s production','Servicing a vintage multitrack machine for a reissue project']::text[] WHERE id='ad5ae86d-7103-488a-8a9a-31052bc0d435';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Analog mastering medium', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Playing a tape back with the wrong EQ standard (NAB vs IEC/CCIR)','Skipping alignment to reference tones, causing level and tonal errors','Ignoring print-through after long storage of the master reel']::text[], definition='The practice of recording a finished stereo or mono master onto open-reel magnetic tape — typically quarter-inch or half-inch — as the final production master from which records or other copies are made, using standardized record/playback equalization (NAB or IEC/CCIR).', plain_english='Open-reel mastering records the final mix onto reel-to-reel tape. This tape becomes the master copy. Records and other copies are made from it. Engineers follow set tape standards so playback sounds correct.', practical_application='A mastering engineer aligns the machine to reference tones, records the program to half-inch tape at 30 ips, then plays that master back to cut a lacquer or transfer for release.', purpose_function='Captures the completed mix on a high-quality analog master medium, giving a cohesive analog sound and a stable master for cutting vinyl or making further copies.', related_terms=ARRAY['Master tape','Tape equalization','NAB curve','IEC/CCIR equalization','Reel-to-reel machine','Tape bias']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recording a mastered mix to half-inch tape at 30 ips','Aligning a mastering deck before transferring a master','Baking and transferring an old open-reel master to digital']::text[] WHERE id='e49c2580-6971-417f-89bb-3bcb9593095e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Recording history period', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Treating all tape-era recordings as inherently low quality','Forgetting that editing meant physically cutting the tape','Underestimating how much machine maintenance tape work required']::text[], definition='The broad historical period — roughly from the late 1940s until digital recording became dominant in the 1980s and 1990s — during which magnetic tape was the primary medium for capturing, editing, and mastering professional audio.', plain_english='This was the time when tape ruled audio. Studios recorded onto magnetic tape. Editing meant cutting and splicing the tape. It lasted until digital took over. It shaped how music and radio were made.', practical_application='A technician restoring archives or vintage gear from this era must know tape formats, machine alignment, and splicing methods that were standard at the time.', purpose_function='Frames the decades when tape workflows — recording, razor-blade editing, and tape mastering — defined professional practice, before digital systems replaced them.', related_terms=ARRAY['Magnetic tape','Splice editing','Open-reel mastering','Multitrack recording era','Tape machine alignment']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Restoring recordings made during the analog tape era','Explaining razor-blade tape editing to students','Dating a recording by its tape format and workflow']::text[] WHERE id='25d5feb2-dc88-41a3-80df-fe2fbb44086b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Cable-management slang', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Leaving cables in a bird''s nest so faults are hard to trace','Pulling on tangled cables and stressing connectors or solder joints','Not labeling or dressing cables during setup']::text[], definition='Informal term for a chaotic, tangled mass of cables and connectors — for example a snarl of mic and patch cables behind a console or stage box — that is difficult to trace, dress, or unpick.', plain_english='A bird''s nest is a big tangle of cables. The wires twist together into a messy knot. It is hard to find where each cable goes. It happens when cables are not coiled or dressed neatly.', practical_application='When a channel drops out, an engineer traces it back to a bird''s nest under the stage, then re-dresses and labels the cables so faults are quicker to find next time.', purpose_function='Names a common, avoidable problem so crews can spot and fix messy cabling that slows troubleshooting, risks damaging connectors, and creates trip hazards.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cable dressing','Snake','Patch bay','Cable coiling','Strain relief']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Untangling a bird''s nest behind the console before a show','Tracing a dead channel through a snarl of patch cables','Re-dressing messy cabling to clear a walkway trip hazard']::text[] WHERE id='a1179c78-a2a3-4ae0-89c1-f1f679aa8eda';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Playback / show operation', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing house music (venue playback) with the house-music dance genre','Leaving house music running when the act starts','Playing walk-in music far louder or quieter than the show']::text[], definition='In live sound, house music is the recorded background music played to the audience through the main (house) PA before the show, during intermissions, and after the show, run from the front-of-house position rather than performed on stage. It is unrelated to the electronic dance-music genre of the same name.', plain_english='House music is recorded music played for the crowd. It plays before, between, and after acts. It comes through the main PA speakers. The sound engineer plays it from the mix position. It is not the dance-music genre.', practical_application='At front of house, the engineer plays a house-music playlist during walk-in, fades or stops it at showtime, and brings it back for walk-out, controlling it on a dedicated channel.', purpose_function='Sets the mood and covers quiet gaps for the audience while the stage is empty, and gives the engineer a familiar source to check and set the PA before doors.', related_terms=ARRAY['Walk-in','Walk-out','Front of house','Playback channel','Public address system']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Playing walk-in house music as doors open','Covering a set change with house music','Using house music to line-check the PA before the crowd arrives']::text[] WHERE id='ad4850e4-8e7a-4ad0-9778-c8963a4d3832';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Show operation / cue state', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing walk-in (pre-show) with walk-out (post-show)','Leaving stage mics open during walk-in','Setting walk-in music so loud the room cannot talk']::text[], definition='In live-event operation, the walk-in is the pre-show period as the audience enters and is seated, and by extension the system state during it — including walk-in (house) music, house lighting, and any pre-show console snapshot recalled before the performance begins.', plain_english='Walk-in is the time when the crowd comes in. It happens before the show starts. Recorded music often plays during it. The console and lights sit in a set pre-show state. Then the show begins.', practical_application='The engineer recalls a ''walk-in'' snapshot with house music up and stage channels muted, then moves to the first show cue when the act is announced.', purpose_function='Defines a known pre-show state so the crew can set music, levels, and looks for arriving guests and be ready to switch cleanly into the performance.', related_terms=ARRAY['Walk-out','House music','Snapshot','Doors','Front of house']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recalling the walk-in snapshot as doors open','Playing walk-in music while guests are seated','Handing off from the walk-in state to the first show cue']::text[] WHERE id='9fc53185-7ea4-4c1d-864f-506bdff06b1b';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Show operation / cue state', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing walk-out (post-show) with walk-in (pre-show)','Forgetting to mute stage channels before walk-out music','Cutting the music before the room has cleared']::text[], definition='In live-event operation, the walk-out is the post-show period as the audience leaves the venue, and by extension the system state during it — typically walk-out (play-off) music through the house PA and house lighting up, recalled after the performance ends.', plain_english='Walk-out is the time when the crowd leaves. It happens after the show ends. Recorded music usually plays them out. The house lights come up. The console returns to a safe end state.', practical_application='At the encore''s end, the engineer recalls a ''walk-out'' snapshot that brings up play-off music and house lights and mutes the stage, then powers down in order.', purpose_function='Gives a clean, planned end state that plays the audience out, restores house lighting, and mutes stage sources safely once the performance is over.', related_terms=ARRAY['Walk-in','House music','Play-off music','Snapshot','Front of house']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Recalling the walk-out snapshot after the encore','Playing the audience out with walk-out music','Restoring house lights and muting the stage at show''s end']::text[] WHERE id='63e18750-77b3-41ba-97af-a156ad8e216c';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Console illumination hardware', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: Servicing or replacing meter lamps with the console still powered and mains connected','Fitting an LED without checking polarity or supply voltage, so it fails to light','Assuming a dark meter is broken when only its lamp has failed']::text[], definition='A small illumination lamp mounted in the meter bridge of a mixing console or tape machine that lights the VU or PPM meter scales from behind or above, so the meter pointers and markings stay readable in dim studio or stage conditions.', plain_english='A meter bridge lamp lights up the console''s meters. The meters sit on a raised panel called the meter bridge. The lamp makes the scale easy to read in the dark. These small bulbs often burn out and get replaced. Many are now swapped for LEDs.', practical_application='When a VU meter goes dark, a technician powers down, opens the meter bridge, and replaces the failed grain-of-wheat bulb or fits an LED equivalent to restore illumination.', purpose_function='Keeps level meters clearly visible in low light so the operator can read signal levels accurately during recording, mixing, or a show.', related_terms=ARRAY['Meter bridge','VU meter','PPM meter','Grain-of-wheat lamp','Console illumination']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Replacing a burned-out lamp behind a console VU meter','Retrofitting meter bridge lamps with LEDs for reliability','Reading meters lit by the meter bridge lamps during a dim-stage show']::text[] WHERE id='bc254fb8-63c5-4330-9309-56b615719ba3';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Audio transformer hardware', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: Lifting the mains safety earth to cure hum instead of using a shielded isolation transformer','Leaving the transformer''s Faraday shield ungrounded, so it provides no benefit','Mounting the transformer next to a power supply where its magnetic shield is overwhelmed']::text[], definition='An audio or power transformer fitted with shielding to reduce unwanted coupling: an electrostatic Faraday shield between primary and secondary windings that blocks capacitive noise transfer, and/or a magnetic (mu-metal) can that blocks external hum fields, keeping induced noise out of the signal.', plain_english='A shielded transformer has extra shielding to block noise. One shield sits between the coils and stops electrical noise. A metal can blocks magnetic hum from outside. Together they keep the audio clean. The shields must be grounded correctly to work.', practical_application='To kill a ground-loop hum between two systems, a technician inserts a shielded isolation transformer, grounding the Faraday shield per the maker''s instructions to break the loop while keeping safety earth intact.', purpose_function='Prevents hum and noise from coupling into audio through a transformer, which matters for clean signal in interfaces, DI boxes, isolation transformers, and mic and line stages.', related_terms=ARRAY['Faraday shield','Mu-metal shield','Isolation transformer','Ground loop','Common-mode rejection']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Adding a shielded isolation transformer to break a ground loop','Choosing a mu-metal-shielded transformer for use near strong hum fields','Grounding a transformer''s Faraday shield to reject capacitive noise']::text[] WHERE id='4641ca46-8ebf-449b-af59-d971df2a2872';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Digital cabling slang', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using cheap unshielded patch cable for long, abused stage runs','Exceeding the roughly 100 m copper Ethernet distance limit without a switch or fiber','Assuming any RJ45 lead is roadworthy without locking etherCON shells']::text[], definition='Concert-audio slang for a run of Category (Cat5e/Cat6) twisted-pair cabling used to carry networked digital audio and control — such as a Dante or other audio-over-IP link, or a digital snake — between stage, console, and system devices.', plain_english='A cat line is an Ethernet-type cable run. It uses Cat5e or Cat6 network cable. It carries digital audio and control data. Crews use it for digital snakes and networks. It replaces heavy analog multicore cables.', practical_application='A crew runs a cat line from the stage box to the console for a Dante link, using shielded cable and locking etherCON connectors to survive stage abuse.', purpose_function='Carries many channels of audio and control over one lightweight network cable, replacing bulky analog multicore and simplifying stage-to-console connections.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cat5e/Cat6 cable','etherCON','Digital snake','Dante','Audio-over-IP']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Running a cat line from the stage box to a Dante console','Replacing an analog multicore snake with a single cat line','Terminating a cat line in etherCON connectors for touring use']::text[] WHERE id='bd97bc62-fe7a-4e7f-9b13-e84342fae7af';
UPDATE glossary SET category='FOH mixing workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Bringing the solo up late, so its first notes are buried','Pushing the solo so hard it feeds back or clips','Forgetting to pull the level back after the solo ends']::text[], definition='In front-of-house mixing, to ''catch the solo'' is to anticipate a musician''s featured passage and raise that channel''s level in time so the solo cuts clearly through the mix as it begins.', plain_english='To catch the solo means to turn up a solo in time. The engineer sees the solo coming. They push that channel''s fader up. The solo is then loud and clear. They pull it back when the solo ends.', practical_application='Watching the stage and set list, the engineer rides the guitar fader up as the solo starts, holds it above the mix, then returns it to rhythm level when the singer comes back.', purpose_function='Ensures featured passages — a guitar, sax, or keyboard solo — are heard clearly and at the right moment, keeping the mix musical and responsive to the performance.', related_terms=ARRAY['Fader riding','Miss the solo','Front of house','Mix balance','Set list']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Riding the guitar fader up for a solo at front of house','Following a set list to be ready for each solo','Featuring a sax solo cleanly over a busy mix']::text[] WHERE id='24687cc7-49cf-4c70-b4d5-51e6729ff62e';
UPDATE glossary SET category='FOH mixing workflow', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Not learning the arrangement, so solos arrive unexpectedly','Watching the console instead of the stage during the song','Reacting only after the solo is already audible and late']::text[], definition='In front-of-house mixing, to ''miss the solo'' is to fail to raise a featured passage in time, leaving a solo buried in the mix or unheard because the engineer did not catch it as it began.', plain_english='To miss the solo means to not turn it up in time. The solo starts but stays too quiet. The crowd cannot hear it well. It is the opposite of catching the solo. Good preparation helps avoid it.', practical_application='An engineer who does not know the arrangement misses a keyboard solo, then marks the set list so the next show''s solo is caught on time.', purpose_function='Names a common mixing error so engineers learn to anticipate and prepare for solos rather than reacting too late.', related_terms=ARRAY['Catch the solo','Fader riding','Front of house','Mix balance','Set list']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Realizing a solo was missed and marking the set list','Missing a solo because the arrangement was unfamiliar','Reviewing a show recording where a solo stayed buried']::text[] WHERE id='f121a4e1-a72e-4da8-a5f0-a9a320494418';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Monitor communication / talkback', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assigning talkback to the wrong mix so the message goes to the entire band','Leaving the talkback key latched open so stage talk bleeds into a live mix','Assuming talkback is private when it is actually routed to all mixes or to a broadcast feed']::text[], definition='A monitor-console talkback function that routes the engineer''s talk microphone to one specific performer''s monitor mix (a wedge or in-ear feed) rather than to every mix at once, allowing a discreet one-to-one conversation during a performance.', plain_english='Private talkback lets the engineer speak to just one performer. The engineer''s mic goes only to that person''s monitors. Other performers do not hear it. It keeps talk quiet and personal during a show.', practical_application='During a song the engineer presses a talk key assigned to the vocalist''s mix and asks if they need more of themselves, hearing back through a stage return microphone.', purpose_function='It lets a monitor engineer communicate with an individual musician without interrupting the rest of the band or leaking talk into the main mix.', related_terms=ARRAY['Talkback','In-ear monitors','Monitor mix','Interruptible foldback (IFB)','Wedge']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A monitor engineer quietly checks with a singer mid-set about their in-ear level','A band leader asks, over private talkback, to add keys to only the drummer''s mix','Troubleshooting one performer''s feed without distracting the others']::text[] WHERE id='2de57c2b-92b8-460b-b721-a244c5564fdb';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Production communications / intercom', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming the shout circuit is private - because it is always open, everyone on it hears every word','Confusing the always-live shout system with a keyed talkback or partyline that needs a talk button','Leaving a loud source or open microphone on the circuit so it distracts every position','Using the shout system for long conversations instead of quick cues, tying up the channel']::text[], definition='In live production and broadcast, a shout system is a dedicated, permanently open (always-live) voice circuit - a ''hoot-n-holler,'' squawk-box or shout-down circuit - that links key positions such as front-of-house, monitors, the stage and an outside-broadcast truck. Because the channel is always live, an operator can speak and be heard instantly without pressing a talk key, so urgent verbal cues pass with no delay.', plain_english='A shout system is an always-on voice line between crew positions. It never closes, so no one has to press a talk key. An operator just speaks and the others hear it right away. It links spots like front-of-house, monitors, and a broadcast truck. Crews use it for fast, urgent calls during a show. People also call it a hoot-n-holler or squawk box.', practical_application='During a live show a monitor engineer hears the front-of-house engineer call a change over the shout circuit and responds at once, without reaching for a talk key. A broadcast truck can shout a countdown to the stage the same way.', purpose_function='It gives the crew an instant, hands-free way to pass time-critical cues between positions. Because the circuit stays open, there is no delay from dialing or keying a microphone, which matters when a cue cannot wait.', related_terms=ARRAY['Partyline intercom','Talkback','Interruptible foldback (IFB)','Squawk box','Front-of-house (FOH)']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Front-of-house calls a two-minute warning and every position hears it instantly','A broadcast truck shouts a countdown to the stage manager without keying a microphone','Monitors and front-of-house trade a fast fix over the open circuit during a song']::text[] WHERE id='c242bc4e-e6fe-40e8-bd7d-a60d260c5bb7';
UPDATE glossary SET category='In-ear monitor accessories / eartips', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using a tip that is too small, breaking the seal and losing bass and isolation','Assuming universal tips isolate as well as custom molds','UNSAFE: Compensating for a poor seal by raising in-ear volume to unsafe levels, risking hearing damage']::text[], definition='Pre-molded, one-size-fits-many ear tips (typically foam or silicone) supplied with in-ear monitors that seal the ear canal without a custom impression, as opposed to individually molded custom tips.', plain_english='Universal tips are ready-made ear tips for in-ear monitors. They come in a few sizes, like small, medium, and large. You push them in to make a seal. They are not molded to your own ear.', practical_application='A performer trying stock in-ears swaps between foam and silicone universal tips to find the size that seals best and blocks the most stage noise.', purpose_function='They provide an acoustic seal and hold for generic-fit earphones, giving bass response and isolation without the cost and lead time of custom molds.', related_terms=ARRAY['Custom molds','In-ear monitors','Ear-seal performance','Isolation','Foam eartips']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Fitting a guest musician with loaner in-ears before a set','Choosing foam tips for maximum isolation in a loud stage environment','A drummer tests tip sizes to stop in-ears working loose']::text[] WHERE id='a5366f0f-a273-48b0-a2d7-7534bbff920d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='System design / speaker coverage', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the spatial (in-space) crossover with the electronic frequency crossover','Overlapping coverage too much, causing comb filtering and uneven level in the crossover zone','Ignoring phase offset at the overlap, which creates cancellation dips']::text[], definition='The region in space where the coverage of two adjacent loudspeakers overlaps at roughly equal level so their outputs combine; the acoustic transition zone between one speaker''s coverage and the next, as distinct from a frequency (spectral) crossover.', plain_english='A spatial crossover is where two speakers'' sound overlaps. Both speakers reach that spot at about the same loudness. Their sound adds together there. It is a crossover in space, not in pitch.', practical_application='Aligning two side-by-side tops, the engineer sets splay and delay so the spatial crossover lands at equal level and minimal phase offset, giving smooth coverage across the seats.', purpose_function='Understanding spatial crossovers lets a system engineer predict where speakers combine, so coverage stays even and level bumps or comb filtering at the overlap are controlled.', related_terms=ARRAY['Spectral crossover','Coverage overlap','Comb filtering','Splay angle','Array summation']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Splaying two point-source tops to cover a wide room','Tuning the overlap between a main array and a delay speaker','Diagnosing a level bump where two cabinets'' coverage meets']::text[] WHERE id='a5207e9e-0235-4237-baad-13758e956c65';
UPDATE glossary SET category='System design / signal division', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the spectral (frequency) crossover with the spatial crossover in coverage','Ignoring phase through the crossover region, causing a cancellation dip at the handover','Choosing a crossover frequency that pushes a driver outside its usable range']::text[], definition='A crossover in the frequency domain: the frequency at which signal is divided between drivers or between subsystems (for example subwoofer to mains) so each transducer reproduces its intended range, contrasted with a spatial crossover between overlapping speaker coverages.', plain_english='A spectral crossover splits sound by pitch. Low notes go to one driver and highs to another. The split point is a frequency. It divides the audio band, not the space.', practical_application='Setting the sub-to-tops crossover, the engineer picks the split frequency and slopes, then checks phase through the crossover so the two sum flat rather than cancel.', purpose_function='It assigns each transducer the frequency range it handles best, protecting drivers and giving a flat combined response through the handover region.', related_terms=ARRAY['Spatial crossover','Crossover frequency','Subwoofer alignment','Filter slope','Phase alignment']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Aligning subwoofers to main speakers at the crossover frequency','Configuring a processor''s high-pass and low-pass filters for a bi-amped cabinet','Checking summation through the sub-to-top handover']::text[] WHERE id='a394e201-4553-41a1-a7db-eee86a6a11cc';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Soundcheck / rehearsal procedure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming a top-and-tail check catches problems that only appear mid-song','Topping and tailing so fast the engineer cannot store scenes or level changes','Skipping songs entirely instead of at least checking their start and end']::text[], definition='To rehearse or check only the beginning and end of each song or cue, skipping the middle, so many items can be covered quickly; used in soundcheck and rehearsal to confirm starts, transitions and endings without playing full pieces.', plain_english='''Top and tail'' means play only the start and end of a song. You skip the middle part. It lets you check many songs fast. You confirm the intro and the ending work.', practical_application='With limited soundcheck time, the band tops and tails the setlist so the engineer captures each song''s opening levels and confirms clean endings before doors.', purpose_function='It saves time when many songs or cues must be checked, focusing on the parts most prone to mistakes: entrances, transitions and endings.', related_terms=ARRAY['Line check','Setlist','Cue','Virtual soundcheck','Run-through']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A festival changeover with only minutes to check a headliner''s set','A theatre tech rehearsal running the tops and tails of cues','Confirming song intros and endings when full run-throughs are not possible']::text[] WHERE id='2a92af0f-d5d7-4e94-bc7c-28eb7fe6dace';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Transducer motor design', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a copper cap''s electromagnetic role with simple heat-sinking of the motor','Assuming any conductive cap works equally; placement in the gap and conductivity matter','Overlooking that reducing inductance also shifts impedance and crossover behavior']::text[], definition='A copper sleeve or cap fitted over the pole piece of a loudspeaker or compression-driver motor that acts as a shorting ring, reducing voice-coil inductance and its modulation with position and current, thereby lowering distortion and extending high-frequency response.', plain_english='A copper cap is a copper cover over the speaker''s center pole. It lowers the voice coil''s inductance. That cuts distortion and helps high notes. It works like a shorting ring in the magnet gap.', practical_application='A driver designer specifies a copper cap on the pole piece to cut harmonic distortion and flatten the impedance rise, improving a woofer''s upper-band detail.', purpose_function='It stabilizes the magnetic field and reduces inductance-related nonlinear distortion (flux modulation), smoothing frequency response and improving clarity, especially at higher frequencies.', related_terms=ARRAY['Shorting ring','Faraday ring','Pole piece','Voice-coil inductance','Flux modulation']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Comparing distortion data of a driver with and without a copper pole-piece cap','A manufacturer citing low inductance from a copper shorting cap','Explaining why a woofer''s harmonic distortion drops at the top of its range']::text[] WHERE id='38d4059a-127d-40a3-9e16-4a2c8fd79127';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Headphone acoustics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Blaming a response peak on the driver when it is a cup cavity resonance','Assuming a larger ear cup always sounds better, ignoring the resonances it adds','Measuring without a proper ear simulator, missing seal-dependent resonances']::text[], definition='An acoustic resonance arising from the air volume and geometry enclosed between a headphone''s ear cup, its driver and the wearer''s ear, which colors the frequency response with peaks or dips at the resonant frequencies.', plain_english='Ear-cup resonance is a ringing in the space inside a headphone cup. The trapped air and cup shape resonate at certain pitches. This adds peaks or dips to the sound. It changes the headphone''s tone.', practical_application='A headphone engineer adds felt or foam damping inside the cup to suppress a midrange resonance measured on an acoustic ear simulator.', purpose_function='Managing ear-cup resonances lets designers control coloration and hit a target response, using damping, cup shape and venting to tame unwanted peaks.', related_terms=ARRAY['Ear-seal performance','Circumaural','Acoustic damping','Frequency response','Standing wave']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Voicing a closed-back headphone to remove a boxy midrange peak','Explaining tonal differences between two cup shapes','Damping an ear cup to smooth measured response']::text[] WHERE id='e95045a5-d8b4-400b-9d51-5162ce5377f5';
UPDATE glossary SET category='In-ear / headphone fit and isolation', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Blaming thin bass on the earphone rather than a broken seal','UNSAFE: Turning up in-ear level to restore lost bass from a bad seal, raising exposure toward hearing-damage levels','Assuming a seal that feels tight also isolates well without measuring']::text[], definition='How effectively an earphone or headphone maintains an airtight acoustic seal against the ear canal or around the ear, which governs low-frequency response, passive isolation and consistency of sound between wearers and re-insertions.', plain_english='Ear-seal performance is how well earphones seal your ear. A good seal blocks outside noise. It also keeps the bass strong. A leak makes the bass weak and lets noise in.', practical_application='A performer tests seal by inserting in-ears and checking that bass stays full and stage noise drops; if bass is thin, they change tip size for a better seal.', purpose_function='A stable seal sets the bass extension and isolation of in-ears; poor sealing loses low end and reduces noise blocking, so seal performance is central to fit and sound.', related_terms=ARRAY['Universal tips','Custom molds','Isolation','Occlusion effect','Bass extension']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Checking in-ear fit before a loud set','Diagnosing why a musician''s in-ears sound thin','Selecting tips for reliable isolation on a noisy stage']::text[] WHERE id='38d7cee5-6efa-492e-8108-1face1903c84';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Headphone ergonomics / mechanical design', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Bending the headband to loosen it and then losing the seal and bass','Assuming tight clamping alone guarantees a good seal regardless of pad shape','Ignoring that clamping force relaxes as headband materials age']::text[], definition='The clamping force a headphone''s headband exerts to hold the ear cups against the head; it affects the acoustic seal, low-frequency response, positional stability and long-term comfort.', plain_english='Headband tension is how hard the headphones squeeze your head. More tension gives a tighter seal and more bass. Too much tension hurts over time. Too little lets the cups slip.', practical_application='An engineer notes that a strong headband improves the bass seal but causes fatigue over a long session, while a loose one weakens the seal.', purpose_function='The clamping force balances a reliable seal and stable fit against comfort, directly affecting bass response and how long a listener can wear the headphones.', related_terms=ARRAY['Clamping force','Ear-seal performance','Circumaural','Ear pads','Comfort']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Choosing headphones for long mixing sessions where comfort matters','Explaining why a headphone loses bass after the headband is stretched','Comparing clamping force between two studio headphones']::text[] WHERE id='896ed79d-e5d5-47a6-a0a5-04d481b5e9fd';
UPDATE glossary SET category='In-ear monitor / transducer configuration', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming more drivers automatically means better sound regardless of crossover tuning','Confusing a hybrid (mixed driver types) with a set using only balanced armatures','Ignoring crossover integration, which can cause tonal discontinuities between drivers']::text[], definition='An in-ear earphone that combines two or more transducer types in one shell, typically a dynamic (moving-coil) driver for low frequencies with one or more balanced-armature drivers for mids and highs, using an internal crossover to blend their outputs.', plain_english='A hybrid earphone uses more than one kind of driver. A dynamic driver makes the bass. Balanced-armature drivers make the highs. These are tiny, precise drivers, like the ones in hearing aids. A crossover blends them. This aims for full, detailed sound.', practical_application='A musician picks a hybrid in-ear so kick and bass feel full from the dynamic driver while vocals and cymbals stay detailed from the balanced armatures.', purpose_function='Combining driver types plays to each one''s strength: dynamic drivers for bass impact, balanced armatures for detailed treble, widening bandwidth and controlling response.', related_terms=ARRAY['Balanced-armature driver','Dynamic driver','In-ear monitors','Crossover','Multi-driver earphone']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Selecting in-ears that need strong bass and detailed highs on stage','Comparing a hybrid to an all-balanced-armature model','Explaining why an earphone lists both dynamic and armature drivers']::text[] WHERE id='65268989-e70a-4f05-85d7-cdc18f4f3584';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Active noise control / earphone technology', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming ANC removes all noise, when high-frequency noise is mostly blocked by the passive seal','Confusing hybrid ANC with simple passive isolation','UNSAFE: Relying on noise cancellation as hearing protection in hazardous industrial noise, which it is not rated to provide']::text[], definition='An active noise-cancellation architecture that combines feedforward and feedback methods, using both an external microphone outside the earpiece and an internal microphone near the ear to generate the anti-noise signal, improving cancellation depth and bandwidth over either method alone.', plain_english='Hybrid noise cancellation uses two microphones per earpiece. One faces outside and one faces your ear. The earphone mixes both to cancel noise. This blocks more noise across more pitches.', practical_application='A traveler or performer uses hybrid-ANC earphones so low rumble and some higher noise are cancelled, with the inside mic correcting leftover noise at the ear.', purpose_function='Using both mic positions extends the frequency range and stability of noise reduction, correcting errors the outside mic misses and widening effective cancellation.', related_terms=ARRAY['Feedforward ANC','Feedback ANC','Active noise cancellation','Anti-noise','Passive isolation']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Choosing ANC earphones for noisy tour transit','Explaining why hybrid ANC outperforms feedforward-only designs','Understanding why ANC still needs a good seal to work']::text[] WHERE id='c5751c2e-ccbd-4905-a1e6-b1e40c367a0f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Transducer evaluation / psychoacoustics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Judging fatigue from a brief demo instead of extended listening','Blaming fatigue only on loudness, ignoring distortion and resonance','Treating fatigue as purely subjective and skipping controlled comparison']::text[], definition='The evaluation of how tiring a transducer or system is to listen to over an extended period, judging whether traits such as harsh resonances, high distortion or excessive high-frequency energy cause listener discomfort or fatigue over time.', plain_english='Listening-fatigue assessment checks how tiring a device sounds. Some headphones wear your ears out fast. Harsh peaks and distortion cause this. Testers listen for a long time to judge it.', practical_application='An engineer auditions monitor headphones over a full day, noting whether a bright treble peak causes fatigue before committing them to long editing sessions.', purpose_function='It helps designers and buyers judge long-session comfort, flagging tonal or distortion traits that make a product tiring even when short auditions sound impressive.', related_terms=ARRAY['Listening fatigue','Harmonic distortion','Frequency response','Harshness','Voicing']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Selecting headphones for all-day mixing work','Comparing two speakers over a long session for listener comfort','Explaining why a detailed-sounding headphone becomes tiring']::text[] WHERE id='6c33e1b5-7eac-484a-b2f2-b93f72457d60';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Transducer motor design', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing top-plate thickness (gap height) with total motor or magnet size','Assuming a thicker top plate always improves output, ignoring its effect on overhang and Xmax','Overlooking that gap height interacts with coil height to set linearity']::text[], definition='The thickness of the front (top) plate of a loudspeaker''s magnetic motor, which sets the height of the magnetic gap the voice coil sits in; together with the voice-coil winding height it determines coil overhang and the driver''s linear excursion (Xmax).', plain_english='The top plate is a steel disc in the speaker''s magnet. Its thickness sets how tall the magnetic gap is. That gap height and the coil height decide how far the coil can move cleanly. It limits linear travel.', practical_application='A driver designer chooses top-plate thickness and coil winding height so the overhang gives the target Xmax, trading efficiency against clean low-frequency excursion.', purpose_function='It defines the magnetic gap height, a key factor in a driver''s linear excursion and efficiency; designers set it against coil height to balance output, distortion and sensitivity.', related_terms=ARRAY['Magnetic gap','Voice-coil overhang','Xmax','Pole piece','Motor structure']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Designing a woofer motor for a target Xmax','Explaining how gap height relates to a driver''s clean excursion','Comparing two drivers'' motor geometry']::text[] WHERE id='8162d39f-435d-4581-9699-bbf53b54fa81';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Measurement display / analysis', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Comparing overlays taken with different smoothing or averaging, giving a misleading difference','Overlaying traces measured at different mic positions and treating the difference as an EQ effect','Forgetting an overlay is frozen and reading it as live data']::text[], definition='In audio measurement, a stored or captured trace (such as a frequency-response, transfer-function or spectrum curve) displayed superimposed over the live measurement so the two can be compared directly on the same graph.', plain_english='An overlay is a saved measurement drawn on top of a live one. You freeze a trace and keep it on screen. Then you compare it to the new reading. It shows what changed.', practical_application='While tuning a PA, the engineer captures the un-EQed response as an overlay, applies EQ, and compares the live trace against the stored overlay to confirm the change.', purpose_function='Overlays let an engineer compare measurements, such as before and after EQ, one position versus another, or a target curve against the result, to judge changes at a glance.', related_terms=ARRAY['Trace capture','Reference trace','Transfer function','Target curve','Frequency response']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Comparing PA response before and after equalization in Smaart','Overlaying a target curve while tuning a system','Saving reference traces from several seating positions for comparison']::text[] WHERE id='f11e6e7a-a89b-45c5-b804-d7b54a697ecc';
UPDATE glossary SET definition='A power resistor whose wirewound (or thick-film) element is sealed inside a rectangular ceramic case filled with a fireproof cement compound, giving rugged mechanical protection, high power dissipation, and flame-resistant operation; commonly used for current sensing, bleeder, and load duties and available in inductive or low-inductance windings.', scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Dissipating power as a load or bleeder resistor in an amplifier supply','Sensing current in a power-amp output or switch-mode supply','Replacing a burned-open resistor that ran hot on a repair bench']::text[] WHERE id='8d35e3f9-4324-4956-9b72-030734878f76';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Control surface behavior', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming every button latches when some are momentary','Ignoring the LED state and toggling a function twice by mistake','Confusing a press-and-hold shortcut with a normal single press']::text[], definition='The way a physical button or key responds to being pressed on a control surface or device - for example momentary (active only while held), latching/toggle (press to change state), press-and-hold, or double-press - together with any illumination or feedback that indicates its current state.', plain_english='This is how a button acts when you press it. Some buttons work only while held down. Others stay on until you press again. Some need a long press. A light often shows the button''s state.', practical_application='On a console, an engineer sets a mute button to latch and a talkback button to momentary, then checks the LED shows the live state.', purpose_function='Defines how each control maps a press to an action, so operators know what a button does and how its state is shown, cutting mistakes on consoles and hardware.', related_terms=ARRAY['Momentary switch','Latching switch','Toggle','Control surface','Illuminated pushbutton','Mute button']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Configuring mute and solo buttons on a mixing console','Setting a footswitch to momentary or latching','Reading a button''s LED to confirm its current state']::text[] WHERE id='deaa01c6-3e8f-4a47-98c5-20ff503a55d9';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Touring production planning', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming the festival supplies your specific console model','Not carrying a split or stagebox compatible with the house system','Failing to back up and test the show file before the gig']::text[], definition='A compact, self-contained set of an act''s own control equipment - typically their mixing console(s), a stage rack or digital stagebox, and input documentation - assembled so the act can drop into a festival''s shared house PA quickly and minimise changeover time between bands.', plain_english='This is a small kit a band brings to a festival. It holds their own mixing desk and stage box. The band plugs it into the festival''s main PA. This lets each act change over fast. It saves setup time.', practical_application='A monitor engineer rolls the festival package onstage, connects one multicore to the house split, loads the show file, and is mixing within the changeover window.', purpose_function='Lets touring acts keep their own console files and workflow while sharing a festival''s PA, so changeovers between bands are fast and predictable.', related_terms=ARRAY['Changeover','House PA','Stagebox','Input list','Control package','Line check']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Preparing a fly date at a summer festival','Making a fast changeover between bands on a shared PA','Bringing only control gear while the festival supplies the PA']::text[] WHERE id='c4714014-7f91-419a-a175-e54eb039015d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Touring logistics / scheduling', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a hotel day with a travel day that still ends in a show','Assuming a hotel day means no advancing or work gets done','Booking rooms too late to secure the whole party']::text[], definition='A day on a tour itinerary with no performance on which the crew and artists are booked into a hotel to rest or travel by day, rather than sleeping on the tour bus, used to manage fatigue, laundry, and logistics between shows.', plain_english='This is a day on tour with no show. The crew sleeps in a hotel, not the bus. It gives people rest and a real shower. It helps with tiredness on long tours. The tour manager plans it into the schedule.', practical_application='A tour manager blocks a hotel day after a run of shows, books rooms, and tells the crew there is no load-in so they can rest or do laundry.', purpose_function='Gives touring personnel proper rest and downtime, helping manage fatigue and morale across a long routing of shows and overnight drives.', related_terms=ARRAY['Day off','Tour itinerary','Routing','Tour manager','Bus call','Load-in']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Scheduling rest after several back-to-back shows','Planning laundry and downtime on a long tour','Booking hotel rooms instead of an overnight bus drive']::text[] WHERE id='0fe8711b-bda6-4b3f-b45d-c57e3e022f75';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Console furniture / ergonomics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Building side wings so deep that reach and sightlines suffer','Ignoring cable management behind a wraparound frame','Assuming any console fits a generic wraparound shell']::text[], definition='A mixing or control console (or the desk furniture housing it) built in a curved or angled layout that partly encircles the operator, placing side sections and outboard within easy reach for ergonomic operation in studios, broadcast, or large front-of-house positions.', plain_english='This is a mixing desk shaped to curve around the operator. Side panels wrap toward the person sitting there. It keeps controls and gear within easy reach. It suits big desks with many channels. It helps comfort during long sessions.', practical_application='A studio installs a wraparound desk so the engineer can reach the patchbay, monitor controller, and outboard racks without leaving the chair.', purpose_function='Keeps a large number of controls, meters, and outboard within arm''s reach of one operator, improving ergonomics and workflow on big consoles.', related_terms=ARRAY['Console','Studio furniture','Outboard rack','Patchbay','Front-of-house position','Ergonomics']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Fitting a large-format console into a control room','Laying out outboard racks around the operator','Designing an ergonomic FOH or broadcast position']::text[] WHERE id='f0efd722-fc1e-4325-abaf-06df322b4318';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Bench repair procedure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Fitting a replacement of the wrong value, voltage, or power rating','Reversing a polarised capacitor or diode','UNSAFE: Replacing parts in mains or high-voltage supply sections without unplugging and discharging the filter capacitors first, risking a severe or lethal shock']::text[], definition='The repair task of removing a failed or out-of-spec electronic part - such as a resistor, capacitor, transistor, or IC - and soldering in a correct replacement of matching type, value, rating, and footprint so the circuit returns to its intended operation.', plain_english='This means swapping a broken part for a new one. You remove the bad part with a soldering iron. You fit a new part of the same type and rating. Then the circuit should work again. The new part must match the old one.', practical_application='A tech desolders a bulged filter capacitor, fits one of equal or higher voltage rating and correct capacitance and temperature grade, then retests the supply.', purpose_function='Restores a faulty circuit to working order by replacing the specific part that failed, matching its electrical value and rating so the fix is safe and reliable.', related_terms=ARRAY['Desoldering','Soldering','Filter capacitor','Voltage tracing','Thermal failure','Capacitor discharge']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Swapping a failed output transistor in a power amp','Replacing bulged capacitors in a power supply','Fitting a new op-amp after a bench diagnosis']::text[] WHERE id='6afbb447-bd6a-4fd9-957d-f3304f8e435f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Bench repair safety procedure', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Skipping current limiting and applying full mains straight after a repair','Misreading a brightly lit dim-bulb lamp as normal rather than a fault','UNSAFE: Treating a variac as an isolation transformer - it is not isolated from mains, so its output can still deliver a lethal shock']::text[], definition='A repair technique for first energising freshly repaired mains equipment through a device that restricts inrush and fault current - such as a series incandescent ''dim-bulb'' tester or a variable transformer (variac) brought up slowly - so a remaining short circuit shows up harmlessly instead of destroying parts or starting a fire.', plain_english='This is a safe way to first switch on repaired gear. You feed it power through a limiter. A special bulb or dial keeps the current low. If a fault remains, nothing blows up. You watch for problems before full power. It protects the gear and you.', practical_application='After a repair, a tech powers the amp through a dim-bulb tester; a steadily glowing bulb warns of a short, so full mains is never applied to a fault.', purpose_function='Protects both the technician and the repaired equipment by catching a lingering short at low current, before full mains voltage and current are applied.', related_terms=ARRAY['Dim-bulb tester','Variac','Isolation transformer','Inrush current','Short circuit','Component replacement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['First power-up of a repaired tube or power amp','Finding a residual short after a component swap','Bringing a vintage unit up slowly on a variac']::text[] WHERE id='d18cf10a-b427-41d4-ad53-38dc846a4f2f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Fault type / repair', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Overlooking a fine crack and swapping healthy components instead','Blaming software or cables for what is a mechanical crack','UNSAFE: Probing or reflowing a cracked joint in a live mains section, where a hidden crack can arc or shift and expose dangerous voltages']::text[], definition='A very fine, often barely visible fracture - in a printed-circuit-board trace, a solder joint, a component body, or an enclosure - that can interrupt or intermittently break an electrical connection or weaken a part, and which frequently causes hard-to-find intermittent faults.', plain_english='This is a tiny crack you can barely see. It can appear in a circuit-board track or a solder joint. It may break the connection on and off. That makes faults come and go. These faults are hard to find.', practical_application='Chasing an intermittent output, a tech flexes the board under magnification, spots a hairline crack in a solder joint at a heavy connector, and reflows it.', purpose_function='Names a common cause of intermittent failures, prompting the tech to inspect closely and reflow or repair the fractured joint or trace instead of replacing good parts.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cold solder joint','Reflow','Intermittent fault','PCB trace','Dry joint','Voltage tracing']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Diagnosing an intermittent connection on a flexed PCB','Inspecting solder joints at heavy connectors and jacks','Finding a fractured trace after a unit was dropped']::text[] WHERE id='f5071da1-b477-4b17-ac64-d73bb591a193';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Failure mode / repair', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Replacing the burned part without fixing what caused the heat','Ignoring blocked vents, dead fans, or dried-out thermal paste','UNSAFE: Touching heatsinks, power resistors, or tubes right after operation - they can reach burning temperatures and cause serious burns']::text[], definition='The failure of a component or assembly caused by excessive heat - from overload, poor cooling, high ambient temperature, or thermal runaway - which can shift values, open or short a part, char the board, or trip protection, often leaving visible discoloration or burn marks.', plain_english='This is when heat destroys a part. Too much current or poor cooling makes it too hot. The part can burn, open, or short. You may see brown marks or melting. Heat is the root cause.', practical_application='Finding a charred resistor and a failed transistor, a tech checks bias and cooling, replaces both, and confirms the stage no longer overheats before returning the amp.', purpose_function='Identifies overheating as the failure cause, guiding the tech to fix the reason for the heat - not just the burned part - so the repair lasts.', related_terms=ARRAY['Thermal runaway','Heatsink','Thermal paste','Overload','Component replacement','Bias']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Diagnosing an amp that shuts down when it heats up','Finding a charred resistor and tracing its root cause','Checking cooling after repeated overheating failures']::text[] WHERE id='d0faf8da-f009-4730-8f1c-d899835d5850';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Diagnostic technique / repair', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Measuring without a schematic or known-good reference values','A poor ground reference or slipping probes shorting adjacent pins','UNSAFE: Probing live mains or high-voltage supply nodes without insulated probes, one-hand technique, and awareness of stored charge, risking a lethal shock']::text[], definition='A troubleshooting method that follows a fault by measuring DC (and where needed AC) voltages at successive points in a powered circuit and comparing them with the schematic''s expected values, so the technician can localise the stage or part where the reading goes wrong.', plain_english='This is finding a fault by measuring voltages. You check points along a live circuit with a meter. You compare each reading to the schematic. Where the numbers go wrong, the fault is near. It points you to the bad part.', practical_application='With the unit powered and the schematic open, a tech measures supply rails and stage-by-stage node voltages, narrowing a dead channel to one biased-off transistor.', purpose_function='Localises a fault to a specific stage or component by comparing measured operating voltages against the design values, focusing the repair.', related_terms=ARRAY['Multimeter','Schematic','Signal tracing','Supply rail','Bias','Component replacement']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Localising a dead channel in a power amp','Checking supply rails against the service manual','Following bias voltages through a preamp stage']::text[] WHERE id='b23efb58-3485-4ea9-8e0b-7783fa0eb313';
UPDATE glossary SET common_mistakes=ARRAY['Hooking up before the hardware rating and seating are checked','Side-loading a hook or leaving its safety latch open','UNSAFE: Connecting a load that exceeds the rated capacity of the hook, sling, or point, or omitting a required secondary/safety, risking a dropped load onto people below']::text[], definition='A rigger''s instruction to connect a load - such as a truss, motor chain, or point - to its lifting hook or pickup so it is ready to be raised, given only after the connecting hardware and its rating have been confirmed correct for the load.', plain_english='This means hook the load onto the lift. A rigger clips the truss or motor to the hook. It gets the load ready to go up. The rigger first checks the hardware is right. Only then is it safe to lift.', practical_application='On the deck, the crew chief calls ''hook it'' once the shackle and sling are rated and seated, so the crew connect the truss to the motor-chain hook before the lift.', purpose_function='Signals that a load should be attached to its lifting point, marking the step between assembling hardware and taking the weight onto the hoist.', related_terms=ARRAY['Chain hoist','Shackle','Pick point','Sling','Working load limit','Motor chain']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Connecting a truss to a motor chain before a lift','Attaching a point to its pickup on the deck','Calling the crew to hook up a hang once hardware is checked']::text[] WHERE id='ab79a3a2-06fe-4f87-b5e3-e9fad5b8e3e6';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Rigging task / slang', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Rigging to structure that has not been verified to take the load','Mixing mismatched or unrated hardware in a steel bridle','UNSAFE: Working under a live rigging area without hard hats, exclusion zones, or secondary safeties while steel and tools are overhead, risking struck-by injuries and dropped loads']::text[], definition='Crew slang for doing overhead rigging work - installing the steel components of a hang such as beam clamps, wire rope, shackles, chain hoists, and truss - to build the load-bearing points from which production equipment is flown.', plain_english='This means doing the up-high steel rigging. Crews fit clamps, shackles, and steel wire to the building. They hang motors and truss from these points. It is heavy overhead rigging work. It must follow strict load rules.', practical_application='The up-rigger ''throws steel'' to the structural beams, setting basket-hitched wire rope and shackles to hang chain motors before truss is pulled up.', purpose_function='Describes building the load-bearing steel points overhead, from which lighting, audio, video, and scenery are then flown.', related_terms=ARRAY['Bridle','Wire rope','Beam clamp','Chain hoist','Shackle','Working load limit']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Building overhead points in an arena before a load-in','Setting steel bridles to structural steel','Hanging chain motors for a flown PA and lighting rig']::text[] WHERE id='b6d2bc8e-f3fd-422c-a0a3-c4f8ec45a6d6';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Touring transport / storage', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Overloading one bay and exceeding the vehicle''s axle or bay limit','Leaving loose items that shift and jam the belly doors','UNSAFE: Reaching or crawling into a deep bay without care, where heavy sliding cases can crush hands or trap a limb']::text[], definition='The lower external cargo bays of a tour bus, or the underfloor luggage compartments of a truck or coach, used to store crew luggage, small cases, and gear that travels with the party rather than in the equipment trucks.', plain_english='This is the storage space under a tour bus or coach. Side doors open to it. Crew put luggage and small cases there. It travels with the bus. It is not the main gear truck.', practical_application='At bus call the driver opens the belly and the crew load their bunk bags and small cases before the overnight drive.', purpose_function='Provides secure travelling storage for luggage and small items directly under the bus or coach, kept separate from the production trucks.', related_terms=ARRAY['Tour bus','Bus call','Bunk','Truck pack','Road case','Load-out']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Loading crew luggage before an overnight drive','Storing small cases that travel on the bus','Finding gear stowed under the coach at the next stop']::text[] WHERE id='3768f669-812e-41a6-9155-6db90b355087';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Load-in / load-out logistics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Building a case line that blocks a walkway or fire exit','Staging cases out of pack order and jamming the truck load','UNSAFE: Leaving heavy wheeled cases unbraked on a ramp or slope, where a runaway case can strike crew or the public']::text[], definition='A row of road cases staged in order along a route or dock during load-in or load-out, arranged in loading sequence so crew can move them on and off the truck efficiently and in the correct pack order.', plain_english='This is a row of road cases set out in order. Crew line them up along the path to the truck. The order matches how they pack. It keeps the load-in or load-out moving. Nothing gets lost or jammed.', practical_application='During load-out the crew build a case line from stage to dock in truck-pack order, then wheel each case out so the truck loads tight and balanced.', purpose_function='Keeps load-in and load-out organised by staging cases in pack order, so the truck fills logically and gear reaches the stage in the right sequence.', related_terms=ARRAY['Road case','Load-out','Truck pack','Case park','Dock','Ramp']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Staging cases in pack order during load-out','Lining cases from stage to the loading dock','Organising a fast truck load after a show']::text[] WHERE id='709e2760-5453-492e-ad15-e1061ee0dbed';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Backstage logistics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Parking cases where they block fire exits or crossovers','Stacking cases too high or unbraked so they can topple','UNSAFE: Blocking a marked egress route or fire lane with parked cases, which can trap people during an emergency evacuation']::text[], definition='A designated area, usually offstage or in a back hallway, where empty road cases are stored neatly during a show after the gear is unpacked, keeping them out of working and escape routes until load-out.', plain_english='This is where empty cases are kept during the show. Crew wheel them to one spot offstage. They stay out of the way while gear is used. At load-out the cases come back. It keeps the stage clear.', practical_application='After setup, the crew wheel every empty case to the case park in a back corridor, stacked safely so the wings and exits stay clear during the show.', purpose_function='Keeps empty cases tidy and out of traffic and egress paths during a show, then holds them ready for a fast repack at load-out.', related_terms=ARRAY['Road case','Case parking','Case world','Load-out','Egress','Backstage']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Storing empty cases offstage during a concert','Choosing a back hallway to hold cases','Keeping the wings clear while cases wait for load-out']::text[] WHERE id='7834e1bf-4e4f-46e9-841f-d5aceb350c58';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Backstage logistics', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Letting cases creep into walkways and crossovers as more arrive','Mixing departments so load-out cases are hard to find','UNSAFE: Allowing parked cases to block a fire exit or fire lane, obstructing emergency escape']::text[], definition='The practice of moving and stowing empty road cases into a designated holding area during a show, and the ongoing management of that space, so cases stay orderly, accessible in load-out order, and clear of working and escape routes.', plain_english='This is the job of storing empty cases during a show. Crew move each case to a set area. They keep them neat and in order. Cases must not block exits. It makes load-out faster.', practical_application='The crew chief assigns case parking to a stagehand, who wheels empties to the holding area and keeps them sorted by department for a fast load-out.', purpose_function='Manages where empty cases go during a show so they do not clutter the stage or block exits, and so load-out repacking is quick and orderly.', related_terms=ARRAY['Case park','Case world','Road case','Load-out','Egress','Backstage']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Assigning a hand to manage empty cases during a show','Sorting parked cases by department for load-out','Keeping escape routes clear while cases are stored']::text[] WHERE id='dd159d12-3bf6-43db-982c-70fb4e0d49ee';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Backstage logistics / slang', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Letting case world spill into fire lanes or dock drive paths','Failing to group cases by truck or department for load-out','UNSAFE: Overstacking or blocking exits and dock doors in the case-storage area, creating crush and egress hazards']::text[], definition='Crew slang for the area - often a large back-of-house room or dock space - where all the empty road cases from a show are gathered and stored together during the performance before load-out; also called ''case city''.', plain_english='This is the big area where all empty cases are kept. Crew call it case world or case city. Every empty case goes there during the show. It stays out of the way of the stage. At load-out the cases are pulled from there.', practical_application='A stagehand directs empties to case world in the loading dock, grouping them by truck and department so the load-out repack runs smoothly.', purpose_function='Consolidates all empty cases in one managed space during a show, keeping the stage and backstage clear and making the load-out repack efficient.', related_terms=ARRAY['Case park','Case parking','Road case','Loading dock','Load-out','Egress']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Gathering all empty cases in a dock room during a show','Grouping cases by truck for load-out','Keeping dock exits clear while cases are stored']::text[] WHERE id='036d1e0a-ff71-49ac-9ecc-af27ba20b3c3';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Cueing terminology', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing a button with a fade-out ending - a button is a hard, definitive stop, not a gradual decay.','Firing the applause or blackout cue a beat late so it misses the button and the moment falls flat.','Assuming every number has a button; some are written to segue straight into the next scene with no applause point.']::text[], definition='In show cueing, a button is the crisp, definitive musical ending of a song or number - a final note, chord, or short phrase - that marks the piece as over and signals the audience that it is appropriate to applaud. Lighting and sound cues are frequently timed to land exactly on the button.', plain_english='A button is the sharp ending of a song. It is the last note or chord that clearly says the number is over. The audience hears it and knows it is fine to clap. The sound and light crew often time a cue to hit right on it.', practical_application='An operator watching the score or the conductor fires the applause-state look or sound cue exactly on the button, then restores or opens mics for the transition into the next scene.', purpose_function='Gives performers, the band, and operators a shared, unmistakable endpoint so a number finishes together and the audience knows to respond. It also provides a reliable timing anchor for coordinated lighting and sound cues.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cue','Vamp','Tag','Applause cue','Standby']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['A number ends on a loud final chord and the operator lands the applause look exactly on that button.','During tech the stage manager sets a standby so the sound cue restores house mics right after the button.','The music director asks for a ''cold button'' so the ending is abrupt and unmistakable.']::text[] WHERE id='55c449a5-d563-4c67-b3d6-fad7bb2d4490';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Scheduling / call terminology', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing show call with curtain time - show call is earlier, so crew are ready before the audience arrives.','Treating a show call like a work call and starting heavy setup that should already be finished.','Arriving exactly at show call with no margin to fix a dead channel or a flat mic battery before the house opens.']::text[], definition='A show call is the scheduled report time at which cast and crew must be present and ready to run a performance - typically around half an hour before the posted curtain - as distinct from a work call scheduled for load-in, setup, maintenance, or strike labor.', plain_english='A show call is the time the crew must be ready for a performance. It is usually about thirty minutes before the show starts. It is not the same as a work call, which is for setup or repair work. Missing your show call can delay the whole show.', practical_application='The audio crew arrives at show call to power up the system, run a line check, hand out and test wireless mics, and confirm comms before doors open.', purpose_function='Sets one posted deadline so every department is in position and checked before the house opens and the performance begins. It also separates paid performance labor from setup and strike labor for scheduling and payroll.', related_terms=ARRAY['Work call','Places','Half hour','Load-in','Curtain time','Call time']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Show call is 7:30 for an 8:00 curtain, so the A2 tests every radio mic in that window.','The stage manager posts the show call on the callboard and over comms.','A tech misreads the schedule, arrives at curtain instead of show call, and misses the line check.']::text[] WHERE id='45ca3318-496e-4545-9652-6dd68679a3b7';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Load-in / load-out operations', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: rushing a bump-out and manually lifting or rigging heavy cases, cabinets, or motors without enough crew, mechanical aids, or correct technique, risking crush and back injuries.','Confusing ''bump'' (a load-in or load-out) with ''bumping'' a chain hoist - briefly jogging the motor - which is a different action.','Underestimating bump-in time and leaving no margin for system tuning and a line check before doors.']::text[], definition='In live-production and touring usage, a bump is the load-in or load-out of a show - the coordinated moving in and setting up (bump-in) or tearing down and removing (bump-out) of sound, lighting, staging, and rigging. ''Bump-in'' and ''bump-out'' are common in Australian and British practice for what North American crews call load-in and load-out.', plain_english='In touring, a bump is a load-in or a load-out. Bump-in means bringing the gear in and setting it up. Bump-out means tearing it down and packing it out. These words are common in Australia and Britain for what Americans call load-in and load-out.', practical_application='A production manager schedules a six-hour bump-in for the PA, stage, and rigging, and a fast bump-out after the show so the venue can be cleared and the trucks loaded on time.', purpose_function='Names the whole get-in or get-out operation so crew, the venue, and production can schedule labor, trucks, and stage time around it. It is the window in which the system is deployed or de-rigged.', related_terms=ARRAY['Load-in','Load-out','Get-in','Strike','Turnaround','Advance']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['The advance sheet lists a 7am bump-in and a midnight bump-out for the one-night show.','During bump-in the crew flies the line arrays before focusing and tuning the system.','A tight bump-out means every case is labeled and staged for fast, safe loading.']::text[] WHERE id='4a132df8-1a5b-43ab-a477-f3a73284b2a7';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Theatrical sound role', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the programmer, who builds cues, with the operator, who runs them in performance','Not numbering cues to match the prompt book, so calling and running drift out of sync','Leaving routing or level settings unset, so a cue plays to the wrong outputs','Failing to back up the cue file, risking loss of the whole programmed show']::text[], definition='In theatrical sound, the playback programmer is the technician who builds and organizes a production''s sound-cue playback, most often in cueing software such as QLab. They import and edit audio and create, sequence, and configure cues (levels, routing, fades, and triggers) so an operator can run the show reliably.', plain_english='The playback programmer builds the show''s sound cues. They work in software like QLab. They load the audio and set each cue in order. They set levels, fades, and start points. This lets the operator run cues cleanly each night.', practical_application='Working from the designer''s cue sheet, the programmer imports files into QLab, builds each sound cue with fades and routing, numbers them in script order, and rehearses the sequence with the operator during tech.', purpose_function='It turns a sound designer''s plan into a working, ordered cue stack, so every sound fires correctly, in the right order, and at the right level during performances.', related_terms=ARRAY['Sound designer','QLab','Sound cue','Cue stack','Board operator','Playback operator']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Building a QLab cue stack from the sound designer''s cue sheet during tech','Adjusting cue levels and fades in a technical rehearsal','Handing the finished workspace to the board operator for the run']::text[] WHERE id='ee75a7a1-bc30-4c05-9227-c47e2af4741f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Theatrical sound system design / reliability', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Assuming one computer is enough for a high-stakes live show with no backup','Letting the backup drift out of sync so it holds an older version of the cue file','Not rehearsing the switchover, so the crew cannot fail over quickly under pressure','Backing up the computer but not the interface or storage, leaving other single points of failure']::text[], definition='A redundant playback system is a theatrical sound-playback setup that includes a backup machine or signal path mirroring the primary one, so if the main computer or device fails the show can continue from the backup with little or no interruption.', plain_english='This is a backup for the show''s sound computer. A second machine copies the main one. If the main system fails, the backup takes over. This keeps the show running. It protects a live performance from a crash.', practical_application='A sound team runs the show on a primary computer while a second, identical machine mirrors the same cue file, ready to be switched in if the primary crashes mid-performance.', purpose_function='It protects a live performance against a single point of failure, keeping sound running if the primary playback computer, drive, or interface stops working.', related_terms=ARRAY['Playback operator','QLab','Sound cue','Failover','Backup','System redundancy']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Running a long commercial run with a mirrored backup playback computer','Switching to the backup machine after a primary crash during a performance','Keeping both machines updated with the same cue file after every change']::text[] WHERE id='e3c389b2-ed94-448c-82d2-ef01e1a39809';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Themed/haunt ambient audio', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Using a loop with an audible seam or click at the repeat point','Setting the loop so loud it competes with scare cues instead of underscoring them','Looping a clip so short that the pattern becomes obvious and loses menace','Feeding it to a wide house system so it loses its close, personal feel']::text[], definition='A breathing loop is a seamlessly looped recording of breathing (slow, labored, or menacing) played continuously as an ambient bed in a themed or haunted scene. Because it repeats without an obvious seam, it fills a space with a constant, unsettling presence.', plain_english='A breathing loop is a breathing sound that repeats. It plays over and over with no clear seam. It fills a room with a creepy feeling. It suggests something living is near. It runs quietly under a scene.', practical_application='The designer sets a breathing recording to loop on a small hidden speaker in a dark room, so guests feel watched by something breathing nearby the whole time they pass through.', purpose_function='It sustains atmosphere and dread in a scene with no defined start or end, suggesting a lurking presence and keeping tension up between scares.', related_terms=ARRAY['Ambient loop','Sound bed','Whisper loop','Atmosphere','Localized speaker','Soundscape']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Running a breathing bed in a dark holding room','Placing a breathing loop near a hidden actor''s position','Layering breathing under a scene to hold tension between scares']::text[] WHERE id='221ebe4f-7e9d-4f02-9048-9015271efbe4';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Themed/haunt sound-system design', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Running long, unbalanced speaker lines that pick up noise or lose level','Ignoring bleed between rooms so scenes wash into each other','UNSAFE: Mounting overhead or concealed speakers without secure, rated rigging, so they can fall on guests','Setting all zones too loud, which tires guests and masks scare cues']::text[], definition='Distributed haunt audio is the use of many small loudspeakers spread throughout a haunted attraction, each covering a small area at moderate level, rather than a few loud central speakers. This gives even coverage, sound localized to each scene, and control zone by zone.', plain_english='This means using many small speakers around a haunt. Each speaker covers a small area. None has to be very loud. Sound seems to come from each scene. You can control each zone on its own.', practical_application='A designer places small speakers in each room and prop, feeding them by zone, so ambience and scares play locally and neighboring scenes do not bleed into each other.', purpose_function='It provides even, immersive sound throughout a walkthrough, lets each scene have its own local audio, and avoids one loud system that spills between rooms.', related_terms=ARRAY['Distributed sound system','Audio zone','Localized speaker','Ambience','Sound coverage','Show control']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Designing zoned audio for a multi-room haunted house','Feeding each scene its own local speaker and content','Balancing zone levels so rooms do not bleed together']::text[] WHERE id='3721a7dc-3e77-41a7-8bf0-5b2d254c95c5';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Themed/theatrical loudspeaker placement', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Hiding a speaker behind material that muffles or dulls the sound','Concealing the speaker so well that it cannot be reached for service','UNSAFE: Mounting concealed or overhead speakers without secure, rated fixings, so they can fall','Placing the speaker so its direction gives the hidden source away']::text[], definition='The hidden speaker effect uses concealed loudspeakers (behind set pieces, inside props, or within the structure) so sound seems to come from the environment or a character rather than from visible equipment. Hiding the source strengthens the illusion in themed and theatrical scenes.', plain_english='This hides the speakers from view. They sit behind sets or inside props. Sound then seems to come from the scene itself. Guests do not see where it comes from. This makes the effect feel more real.', practical_application='A tech mounts a small speaker inside a prop corpse or behind a wall grille, aims it toward the path, and plays voices or breathing so the object itself seems to make the sound.', purpose_function='It preserves the illusion of a themed environment by keeping audio sources out of sight, so voices and effects appear to come from objects, characters, or the space itself.', related_terms=ARRAY['Concealed loudspeaker','Localized speaker','Prop audio','Sound illusion','Set piece','Ambience']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Putting a speaker inside a talking prop so it seems to speak','Concealing speakers behind a set wall for room ambience','Aiming a hidden speaker so a voice appears to come from a portrait']::text[] WHERE id='e7ae6852-8c6d-496a-a7a4-d1168cfabbd3';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Themed/haunt loudspeaker placement', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Playing the same sound on the house system too, which smears the perceived location','Placing the speaker where its aim reveals it and breaks the illusion','UNSAFE: Fixing the speaker overhead or in a prop without secure, rated mounting, so it can fall','Setting it so loud that it dominates and no longer seems to come from the object']::text[], definition='A localized scare speaker is a dedicated point-source loudspeaker placed at a specific spot so a scare sound appears to originate exactly there. Because listeners localize to the nearest, first-arriving source, the sound seems to come from the prop, corner, or character at that location.', plain_english='This is one speaker set at a chosen spot. It makes a scare sound seem to come from there. People hear the closest speaker first. So they look toward that spot. It ties the sound to a prop or corner.', practical_application='The tech mounts a small speaker at a coffin and plays a growl only there, so guests turn toward the coffin instead of toward a distant house system.', purpose_function='It anchors a scare to a precise location, directing guests'' attention to a prop, doorway, or actor and making the fright feel like it comes from a real thing in the space.', related_terms=ARRAY['Point-source loudspeaker','Precedence effect','Hidden speaker','Sound localization','Scare cue','Audio zone']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Placing a growl speaker inside a specific prop','Using one local speaker so a whisper seems to come from a doorway','Keeping a scare sound off the house system so it stays localized']::text[] WHERE id='969134d7-1b8c-4356-ae26-395a43b29212';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Themed/haunt audio safety practice', common_mistakes=ARRAY['UNSAFE: Setting scares so loud that staff exposure over a shift risks permanent hearing damage','UNSAFE: Judging loudness by ear instead of measuring peak and average levels with a meter','Checking only average level and ignoring short, very loud peaks','Measuring at one spot and missing louder positions closer to the speakers']::text[], definition='A scare audio level check is the practice of measuring and setting the sound-pressure levels of scare and effect audio so they are impactful yet stay within safe hearing-exposure limits for guests and staff. It uses an SPL meter to verify peak and average levels against recognized noise-exposure guidance.', plain_english='This checks how loud the scare sounds are. A meter measures the sound level. The goal is loud enough to scare but still safe. It protects the hearing of guests and staff. Levels are compared to safe limits.', practical_application='Before opening, the tech walks the attraction with an SPL meter, plays each scare, lowers any that peak too high, and logs levels against recognized noise-exposure limits.', purpose_function='It balances a strong scare against hearing safety, ensuring loud stingers do not exceed exposure limits over a long shift for staff or across a visit for guests.', related_terms=ARRAY['Sound pressure level','SPL meter','Noise exposure','Hearing protection','Scare cue','Level setting']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Walking the maze with an SPL meter before opening night','Lowering a stinger that exceeds safe exposure for actors nearby','Logging scare levels to show they meet hearing-safety limits']::text[] WHERE id='749f8018-827d-4876-aa72-dbb4dd30c590';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Themed/haunt show-control cue', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Confusing the cue (the triggered event) with the sound file it plays','Firing the scare too early or too late so it misses the guests','UNSAFE: Setting scare sounds so loud they risk hearing harm to guests or actors over a long shift','Not testing sensor triggers, so the cue misfires or fails in the queue line']::text[], definition='In themed and haunted-attraction audio, a scare cue is a triggered event that plays a startling sound (often with synchronized effects) at a planned moment to frighten guests. Like any show-control cue, it can be fired manually by an operator or automatically by a sensor as guests reach a spot.', plain_english='A scare cue is a planned scary moment. It plays a sudden sound to startle guests. A person or a sensor can start it. It often fires as guests reach a certain spot. Other effects may fire with it.', practical_application='A haunt tech assigns a loud stinger to a cue, then triggers it from a button or a motion sensor so the sound hits exactly as guests turn a corner.', purpose_function='It delivers a scripted scare on demand, letting operators time frights to where guests are and coordinate sound with props, lighting, and actors.', related_terms=ARRAY['Cue','Trigger','Startle effect','Show control','Sound effect','Sensor trigger']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Triggering a screech as guests open a door in a haunted house','Linking a scare cue to a motion sensor in a dark hallway','Coordinating a scare cue with a lighting flash and an actor''s move']::text[] WHERE id='45292120-98c4-498f-b4e8-77d24ec2c711';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Themed/haunt show-control timing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Sending groups through faster than the effect can reset, causing weak or missed scares','Timing only the audio and forgetting the prop''s slower mechanical reset','UNSAFE: Re-firing a pneumatic or moving prop before it resets, which can damage it or strike someone','Confusing reset time with the electronic trigger lockout setting']::text[], definition='Scare reset time is the interval a scare effect needs to return to its ready state after firing (covering the audio playback, any moving prop, and the controller) before it can be triggered again reliably. It sets how quickly successive groups can be scared.', plain_english='This is how long a scare needs to reset. After it fires, the sound and prop must return to start. Only then can it fire again well. It sets how fast groups can follow each other. Too short a gap causes misfires.', practical_application='Knowing a pneumatic prop and its sound take a few seconds to reset, the tech spaces groups or sets a matching lockout so each group meets a fully reset scare.', purpose_function='It defines the safe minimum spacing between triggers so the sound, prop, and controller fully recover, keeping each scare clean and protecting the mechanism from being driven too fast.', related_terms=ARRAY['Scare trigger lockout','Prop controller','Cycle time','Trigger','Pneumatic prop','Show control']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Spacing guest groups to match a prop''s reset time','Setting controller timing so audio and prop reset together','Diagnosing weak scares caused by too-short reset intervals']::text[] WHERE id='e54f7185-6acc-49c2-8419-50bdaeae128d';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Themed/haunt show-control timing', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Setting no lockout, so a sensor re-fires and the scare stutters','Making the lockout longer than the gap between groups, so some guests get no scare','Confusing the lockout with the effect''s mechanical reset time, though they overlap','Testing only one trigger and missing chatter from a noisy sensor']::text[], definition='A scare trigger lockout is a timed inhibit that stops a triggered scare from firing again until a set period passes. After a sensor or button starts the effect, further triggers are ignored during the lockout, so the scare completes and does not chatter or re-fire on the same guests.', plain_english='A lockout blocks a scare from firing again too soon. After it fires once, the trigger is ignored for a set time. This lets the effect finish. It stops rapid repeats on the same guests. Then the trigger works again.', practical_application='On a prop controller, the tech sets a lockout of several seconds after a motion sensor fires, so one group gets one clean scare instead of a stuttering, repeating trigger.', purpose_function='It prevents rapid re-triggering, letting the effect and its audio run fully and reset before the next guest can set it off, which protects both the show and the equipment.', related_terms=ARRAY['Trigger','Sensor','Scare reset time','Prop controller','Debounce','Show control']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Setting a timeout on a PicoBoo-style controller after each trigger','Stopping a motion sensor from re-firing on a lingering group','Tuning lockout time to the flow of guests through a scene']::text[] WHERE id='2a696d02-9a26-4761-a08a-e2b75c745367';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Themed/haunt show-control cue', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Reusing the same scream so often that guests stop reacting','Timing the scream after the visual so the two do not match','UNSAFE: Playing screams at unsafe volumes that risk hearing damage across a long event','Choosing a scream that is muddy or too quiet to cut through background sound']::text[], definition='A scream cue is a scare-oriented playback cue whose triggered sound is a scream or shriek, used at a set moment to startle guests or heighten a scene. It is a specific case of a scare cue, defined by the vocal content it plays.', plain_english='A scream cue plays a scream sound. It fires at a planned moment. It is used to shock or scare guests. A person or sensor can start it. It is one kind of scare cue.', practical_application='The tech loads a screaming vocal onto a cue and fires it as a creature lunges, so the scream lands with the visual and startles the group.', purpose_function='It provides a sharp, human fright at a chosen moment, often marking a reveal, an attack, or a scene''s climax in a haunt or themed scene.', related_terms=ARRAY['Scare cue','Cue','Stinger','Trigger','Sound effect','Vocal effect']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Firing a scream as a hidden actor jumps into view','Adding a distant scream to build dread before a room','Matching a scream cue to a strobe and a prop drop']::text[] WHERE id='20e84177-307e-4f53-afca-9547de66d367';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Themed/haunt ambient audio', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Setting whispers loud or clear enough to sound like normal dialogue','Using an obvious short loop so guests notice the repeat','Placing it on a wide house system so the intimate, close feel is lost','Letting the whisper bed mask cue lines or safety announcements']::text[], definition='A whisper loop is a seamlessly repeating recording of whispered voices played as a continuous ambient layer in a themed or haunted scene. The low, indistinct speech creates unease and the sense of unseen figures speaking nearby.', plain_english='A whisper loop is whispering that repeats. It plays softly and never clearly. It makes guests feel unseen figures are near. It runs under a scene without stopping. The words stay just out of reach.', practical_application='The tech loops layered whispers on a hidden speaker near the path, kept low so guests half-hear voices around them without making out the words.', purpose_function='It builds creeping dread and a feeling of being surrounded by unseen presences, filling quiet moments with soft, unsettling voices between bigger scares.', related_terms=ARRAY['Ambient loop','Breathing loop','Sound bed','Hidden speaker','Atmosphere','Soundscape']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Running a whisper bed in a narrow, dark hallway','Placing whispers near a hidden speaker beside guests','Layering whispers under a scene to unsettle guests between scares']::text[] WHERE id='da6929de-78a9-45c1-92ed-2af1ac3f018f';
UPDATE glossary SET category='Worship audio role', common_mistakes=ARRAY['Sending the click track to the main speakers so the congregation hears it','Not learning the arrangement, so the operator misses a repeat or skip that the leader calls','Failing to test the setlist and file versions before the service','Relying on one device with no backup of the tracks']::text[], definition='In modern worship production, the tracks operator runs the pre-produced backing tracks, click, and guide cues that play alongside the live band. Using playback software such as Ableton Live or a dedicated app, they launch songs, follow arrangement changes, and route click and guides to the musicians'' in-ear monitors.', plain_english='The tracks operator plays the backing tracks for worship. These tracks add sounds the band is not playing live. They also run a click track and cue guides. The click keeps the band in time. Cues go to the players'' in-ear monitors.', practical_application='The operator loads the setlist in Ableton Live or a playback app, starts each song''s tracks, watches for arrangement jumps the worship leader calls, and makes sure the click reaches the band''s in-ears.', purpose_function='It lets a small worship band sound fuller by adding extra parts, and keeps everyone in time and on the same arrangement through a shared click and guide track.', related_terms=ARRAY['Click track','In-ear monitors','Backing tracks','Ableton Live','Multitracks','Music director']::text[], scenario_contexts=ARRAY['Running backing tracks and click for a Sunday worship set','Following the worship leader''s spontaneous arrangement changes live','Preparing and testing the setlist in playback software before the service']::text[] WHERE id='26bcc47a-1749-4e42-8004-9484c96d8f30';
COMMIT;
